window.CRISIS_DATA = {"stats":{"total":125,"fatalities":2896,"fulltext":112,"avg_body":3334,"countries":19,"sources":26,"by_category":{"conflict":63,"kidnapping":18,"terrorism":22,"other":22},"by_region":{"West Africa":31,"Southern Africa":5,"Horn of Africa":56,"Sahel":10,"Central Africa":14,"North Africa":9},"by_country":{"Nigeria":29,"Sudan":20,"Somalia":16,"Ethiopia":10,"South Sudan":7,"Cameroon":7,"DR Congo":6,"South Africa":5,"Niger":4,"Burkina Faso":4,"Unspecified":4,"Chad":2,"Libya":2,"Somaliland":2,"Morocco":2,"Sierra Leone":1,"Central African Republic":1,"Guinea":1,"Algeria":1,"Uganda":1},"by_source":{"Anadolu · Africa":11,"HumAngle":9,"Dabanga Sudan":9,"Somalia Guardian":9,"Radio Tamazuj":7,"ThisDay Live":6,"Africanews":6,"International Crisis Group":6,"Punch Nigeria":5,"Daily Post":5,"Radio Dalsan":5,"Journal du Cameroun":5,"Sudans Post":5,"Ethiopia Observer":5,"Vanguard Nigeria":4,"Sun News Online":4,"Horn Diplomat":4,"Daily Trust":3,"Channels TV":3,"North Africa Post":3,"FDD Long War Journal":3,"Sahel Intel":3,"Burkina 24":2,"IOL News":1,"Sahara Reporters":1,"Ethiopian Monitor":1},"by_day":{"2026-07-03":1,"2026-07-09":1,"2026-08-01":1,"2026-08-02":2,"2026-08-07":1,"2026-08-10":2,"2026-08-17":1,"2026-08-25":2,"2026-08-28":1,"2026-08-30":2,"2026-08-31":3,"2026-09-01":7,"2026-09-02":9,"2026-09-03":7,"2026-09-04":16,"2026-09-05":8,"2026-09-06":6,"2026-09-07":24,"2026-09-08":31},"updated_at":"2026-09-08T07:17:00.316122+00:00"},"items":[{"id":"c7275c35cae5","title":"Lagos family petitions IG, accuses police of shielding alleged land grabbers","summary":"Olarinde Royal Family petitions IG, accusing Lagos police of shielding alleged land grabbers and frustrating investigations into violent attacks in Ogunted Read More: https://punchng.com/lagos-family-petitions-ig-accuses-police-of-shielding-alleged-land-grabbers/","body":"The Olarinde Royal Family of Oguntedo Village, Satellite Town, Lagos State, has petitioned the Inspector-General of Police, Olatunji Disu, accusing some officers of the Lagos State Police Command of abuse of power, intimidation and alleged collusion with land grabbers. The petitioners also alleged that the officers frustrated investigations into violent attacks on members of the family and used police authority to harass and intimidate them. The family had staged a protest at The PUNCH Place in July 2025 over the alleged harassment and land dispute. In the petition, signed by Prince Yussuf Olarinde on behalf of the family and obtained by PUNCH Metro on Thursday, the petitioners accused officers attached to the Satellite Police Station, Area E Command, Tactical Team Area E and the Lagos State Criminal Investigation Department of allegedly aiding land-grabbing activities and frustrating complaints against persons they described as land grabbers. The petition was also addressed to the Deputy Inspector-General of Police in charge of the Force Criminal Investigation Department, the Commissioner of Police in charge of the X-Squad Unit and the Police Service Commission. The family claimed that the disputed New Oguntedo Village and Satellite Town settlement was created and allocated to the Olarinde Royal Family by the then Federal Military Government in 1978 following government acquisition and resettlement exercises. It further claimed that its ownership and possessory rights over the land were affirmed by a 1989 judgment of the High Court of Lagos State, which it said was subsequently enforced through warrants of possession issued in 1997 and 2010. The petitioners alleged that despite the court judgment and possession orders, the family had continued to face invasions, demolitions, intimidation and violence by persons it described as land-grabbing syndicates and criminal trespassers. They also accused some police officers of allegedly providing operational cover for armed thugs, preventing family members from accessing their ancestral properties and frustrating complaints made to the authorities. The petition partly read, “Rather than maintaining neutrality and enforcing lawful court judgments, the officers complained against provided operational cover and protection for armed thugs and land-grabbing syndicates; intimidated lawful occupants and family members. “They prevented members of the Olarinde family from accessing their ancestral homes and properties. While they supervised and facilitated unlawful invasions and demolitions, they also frustrated lawful complaints and petitions submitted by the petitioners; and used police authority as an instrument of oppression against victims while shielding alleged perpetrators.” The family further alleged that several violent incidents, including shootings, assaults, threats to life and destruction of property, had been reported to the police. Four arrested for killing Lagos man weeks before wedding Six-month refuse heap persists on Lagos road despite earlier evacuation LAWMA intensifies waste collection, faults old videos It specifically cited an alleged assassination attempt on Olanrewaju Olarinde on July 9, 2025, accusing armed thugs of attacking him and claiming that the police failed to arrest or prosecute those responsible despite repeated complaints. The petitioners also accused some officers of allegedly fabricating criminal allegations against members of the family, manipulating investigations and using criminal proceedings to harass and intimidate them. They cited the arraignment of Yussuf Olarinde, Sanni Olarinde and the Oba-elect, Latifu Sadiku Olarinde, over alleged murder and armed robbery charges, as well as a separate case involving another family member, Tope Olarinde. The family further alleged that properties belonging to its members were demolished, looted or unlawfully occupied during what it described as police-backed invasions. According to the petition, vehicles, building materials, cash, personal belongings and other valuables worth millions of naira were allegedly stolen or confiscated during the incidents. The family consequently urged the IG to order an independent investigation into the allegations and transfer the relevant case files from the Lagos State Command to the FCID or another investigative unit outside the state. It also requested the investigation of the officers named in the petition, recovery of allegedly stolen properties, prosecution of identified suspects and police protection for members of the Olarinde family and residents of Oguntedo Village and Satellite Town. The petitioners said, “We respectfully submit that the continued silence, compromise, neglect, and inaction of some officers of the Lagos State Police Command in the face of overwhelming complaints, subsisting court judgments, attempted killings, violent invasions, criminal intimidation, and organised unlawful activities constitute a dangerous perversion of justice and a grave threat to the rule of law. “We therefore passionately appeal to your distinguished office to urgently intervene in the interest of justice, public peace, protection of innocent citizens, preservation of lives and property, and safeguarding the integrity and credibility of the Nigeria Police Force.” The Force Public Relations Officer, Iniedu Okokon, could not be reached for reaction on Monday, as calls made to his telephone lines were unanswered. A text message sent to him had also yet to receive a response as of the time of filing this report. Dayo Oyewo Dayo is a journalist at Punch Newspapers with over nine years of experience covering politics, entertainment, crime, and environmental conservation. His reporting blends advocacy and accountability journalism, focusing on stories that inspire awareness and social change. Dayo’s work reflects practical newsroom experience and a strong commitment to public-interest reporting.\n\nNEWS UPDATE: Nigerians can now earn as much as $15,000- $25,000 with premium domains. You decide if you want payment in Naira or US Dollars. Be sure to ask for evidence and proof of people benefitting daily from this. CLICK HERE TO START.\n\nATTENTION NIGERIANS: You can now comfortably earn US Dollars when you acquire premium domains for as low as $3000 and profit $15,000-$35,000. Ideal for professionals like Entrepreneurs, Doctors, Civil/Public servants, IT Experts, Accountants ETC. CLICK HERE TO START.\n\nStay informed and ahead of the curve! Follow The Punch Newspaper on WhatsApp for real-time updates, breaking news, and exclusive content. Don't miss a headline – join now!\n\nStay in the know—fast. Get instant alerts, breaking headlines, and exclusive stories with the Punch News App . Download now and never miss a beat.","url":"https://punchng.com/lagos-family-petitions-ig-accuses-police-of-shielding-alleged-land-grabbers/?utm_source=rss.punchng.com&utm_medium=web","source":"Punch Nigeria","image":"https://cdn.punchng.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/08/18131509/punch-logo-500x179-1.png","published":"2026-09-08T07:19:19+00:00","category":"conflict","category_label":"Armed Conflict","region":"West Africa","country":"Nigeria","fatalities":0,"severity":"Moderate","severity_score":0},{"id":"816832750e96","title":"Amotekun nabs 21 illegal immigrants in Ondo","summary":"Ondo Amotekun Corps arrested 21 undocumented foreign nationals in Owo, among 53 suspects for various crimes including kidnapping and cultism. Read More: https://punchng.com/amotekun-nabs-21-illegal-immigrants-in-ondo/","body":"The Ondo State Security Network Agency, codenamed Amotekun Corps, has arrested 53 suspects, including 21 undocumented foreign nationals allegedly residing in two apartments in Owo, the headquarters of Owo Local Government Area of the state. The suspects were among those paraded by the corps at its headquarters in Akure on Monday for alleged involvement in various criminal activities, including kidnapping, self-kidnapping, murder, attempted murder, burglary, stealing and cultism. Parading the suspects, the state commander of the corps, Akogun Adetunji Adeleye, said the 21 foreigners were arrested following intelligence provided by members of the Fulani community in the state. Adeleye said the suspects claimed to have entered Nigeria from neighbouring West African countries to engage in menial jobs and phone repairs but could not produce documents authorising their stay in the country. He said the command had observed an influx of foreigners moving through the state’s forests and cities under different guises in recent months. The commander said, “I want to call the attention of the general public to the embarrassing way that foreigners traverse the state’s forests and cities under different guises. “Consistently in the last three months, we’ve been having foreigners pretending to be selling phones, pretending to be repairing phones, living in hotels that what they came into the country for is not commensurate with. “We’ve directed them to the relevant sister security agencies for scrutiny. “Again today, we have 21 of them that rented only two apartments in Owo under the guise of coming in from neighbouring West African countries to do menial jobs and repairs of phones, being coordinated and assisted by indigenes. “We found out that they don’t even have ECOWAS papers to guarantee their stay.” Adeleye said the landlords of the apartments could also face scrutiny, alleging that they were more concerned about collecting rent than knowing the identities and activities of their tenants. He warned that undocumented foreigners abandoned by their sponsors could become vulnerable to recruitment into criminal activities. “Apart from being likely to be suspected illegal immigrants, most times when their sponsors abandon them, they become ready tools as criminals and accomplices,” he said. Four arrested for killing Lagos man weeks before wedding NNPC disowns fuel truck conveying cannabis Benue NDC Assembly candidate unveils three-point agenda The commander, however, said the 21 foreigners were being handed over to the appropriate government agency for further investigation and necessary action. Meanwhile, Adeleye said the command had also uncovered a self-kidnapping syndicate involving four suspects who allegedly used social media to raise false alarms and demand money. He said the suspects included a man who reportedly alerted the Amotekun Corps that his siblings had been kidnapped but was later found to be an accomplice. According to him, investigators arrested two suspects and subsequently traced the purported kidnap victim to his home. “We have four people here today that give self-kidnapping and raise false alarms using social media. We moved in and were able to arrest the first two. “Upon detailed investigation, we found out that the man who rushed to the Amotekun Corps office to report that his siblings were kidnapped turned out to be an accomplice. And upon detailed scrutiny, he led us to his home where we found the man they claimed had been kidnapped,” he said. Adeleye also disclosed that seven other suspects were arrested for alleged kidnapping activities following collaboration with members of the Fulani community. He said a military uniform was recovered from one of the suspected kidnappers during the operation, while two kidnapped boys were rescued. According to him, one of the victims sustained machete injuries to his head and body during the attack but was rescued alive and taken for medical treatment. He said, “With the cooperation we received from the Fulani community, we were able to jointly carry out an operation where a military uniform was recovered from one of the kidnappers, and we successfully retrieved the two kidnapped boys, though wounded. “In an attempt to kill one of them, they macheted his head, his body and every part of him. We brought him back alive and he is now stable.” Adeleye commended members of the Fulani community for their cooperation in identifying and apprehending suspected criminal elements within their community. He also thanked the Ondo State Governor, Lucky Aiyedatiwa, for supporting the corps in its efforts to combat crime across the state. Peter Dada\n\nNEWS UPDATE: Nigerians can now earn as much as $15,000- $25,000 with premium domains. You decide if you want payment in Naira or US Dollars. Be sure to ask for evidence and proof of people benefitting daily from this. CLICK HERE TO START.\n\nATTENTION NIGERIANS: You can now comfortably earn US Dollars when you acquire premium domains for as low as $3000 and profit $15,000-$35,000. Ideal for professionals like Entrepreneurs, Doctors, Civil/Public servants, IT Experts, Accountants ETC. CLICK HERE TO START.\n\nStay informed and ahead of the curve! Follow The Punch Newspaper on WhatsApp for real-time updates, breaking news, and exclusive content. Don't miss a headline – join now!\n\nStay in the know—fast. Get instant alerts, breaking headlines, and exclusive stories with the Punch News App . Download now and never miss a beat.","url":"https://punchng.com/amotekun-nabs-21-illegal-immigrants-in-ondo/?utm_source=rss.punchng.com&utm_medium=web","source":"Punch Nigeria","image":"https://cdn.punchng.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/08/18131509/punch-logo-500x179-1.png","published":"2026-09-08T07:19:16+00:00","category":"kidnapping","category_label":"Kidnapping & Abduction","region":"West Africa","country":"Nigeria","fatalities":0,"severity":"Moderate","severity_score":0},{"id":"70459d97163c","title":"Shehu Buba’s aide resigns amid banditry allegations against Senator","summary":"Senior Legislative Aide to Senator Shehu Buba Umar, Hon. Abba Aliyu Tilde, has resigned from his position, citing a breakdown in mutual respect, communication and trust in his working relationship with the Bauchi South senator. Tilde announced his resignation in a letter dated September 6, 2026, saying it was effective immediately. The resignation comes amid […] Shehu Buba’s aide resigns amid band","body":"Senior Legislative Aide to Senator Shehu Buba Umar, Hon. Abba Aliyu Tilde, has resigned from his position, citing a breakdown in mutual respect, communication and trust in his working relationship with the Bauchi South senator. Tilde announced his resignation in a letter dated September 6, 2026, saying it was effective immediately. The resignation comes amid renewed allegations linking Senator Shehu Buba to banditry and terrorism, allegations the senator has strongly and repeatedly denied, describing them as fabricated, politically motivated and part of a campaign to damage his reputation. The latest allegations followed the circulation of videos and claims on social media alleging that suspected bandit kingpins had visited the senator’s residence. Buba has dismissed the materials as manipulated and said he has taken legal steps against individuals behind what he described as a smear campaign. In his resignation letter, however, Tilde did not accuse the senator of involvement in the allegations or link his resignation directly to the controversy. Instead, he cited what he described as repeated instances of being ignored and a lack of recognition for his role. He particularly referred to a recent visit by Senator Buba to his ward, where a meeting was held with residents without prior notification to him. “Given my position and my connection to the people of the ward, I found this deeply disrespectful and hurtful. I believe that, at the very least, I deserved to be informed and carried along,” Tilde wrote. He said the incident, together with other experiences, had made it impossible for him to continue working effectively under the circumstances. “I cannot continue in a position where I feel that there is little regard for my contributions or for the relationship between us,” he stated. Tilde said he believed any working relationship, particularly one involving political and public service, should be founded on “mutual respect, communication, trust, and recognition of one another’s roles.” “Unfortunately, I no longer feel that these principles exist in our working relationship,” he added. The former aide described his resignation as “the most honourable decision” available to him, saying he was leaving with a clear conscience after giving his best in the discharge of his responsibilities. Tilde expressed gratitude for the opportunity to serve as the senator’s Senior Legislative Aide and wished him success in his future endeavours. Shehu Buba’s aide resigns amid banditry allegations against Senator","url":"https://dailypost.ng/2026/09/08/shehu-bubas-aide-resigns-amid-banditry-allegations-against-senator/","source":"Daily Post","image":"","published":"2026-09-08T07:10:19+00:00","category":"kidnapping","category_label":"Kidnapping & Abduction","region":"West Africa","country":"Nigeria","fatalities":0,"severity":"Moderate","severity_score":0},{"id":"42dd4c978a9f","title":"Burundians rush to leave Kenya after crackdown on businesses","summary":"Ruto gave foreign-owned small businesses until Monday to shut down, saying the Kenya \"has not built investor confidence for hawkers\" The post Burundians rush to leave Kenya after crackdown on businesses appeared first on Vanguard News .","body":"Hundreds of Burundians living in Kenya flocked to their embassy in Nairobi on Monday seeking travel documents to leave the country, days after President William Ruto ordered the closure of small businesses run by foreigners. Read Also: Xenophobia: FG seeks compensation from South Africa over properties Nigerians leave behind Kenya has long attracted migrants from across the East African Community (EAC), whose members enjoy freedom of movement within the regional bloc. Last week, Ruto gave foreign-owned small businesses until Monday to shut down, saying the country “has not built investor confidence for hawkers”. The move sparked fear among many foreigners, particularly Burundians who make a living through street vending in Nairobi, prompting a rush to secure documents to return home. Clutching large brown envelopes, crowds queued for hours outside the Burundi embassy, while suitcases and plastic bags piled up at the compound as many prepared to leave. “They’ve expelled us like animals,” Pastor Jean-Baptiste Ndayizeye told AFP. Ndayizeye said two of his neighbours had been attacked and robbed, comparing the situation to the xenophobic violence in South Africa. AFP was not able to independently verify the claims. “What South Africans did to foreigners and what Kenyans are doing is the same thing,” he said, adding that it was unfair they had received no warning. Another Burundian, Eric Bizimana, who came to Kenya in 2022 after years of unemployment at home, said he had never applied for a work permit because of the high cost. “They’re so sad” to be leaving Kenya, said Philemon Jaika Adamba, a Kenyan motorcycle taxi driver who had driven two Burundians to the embassy. “It’s not fair.” ‘Misunderstood’ Burundi, a country of about 15 million people and one of the world’s poorest, is part of the EAC alongside Kenya, the region’s economic powerhouse. Burundi’s foreign minister Edouard Bizimana condemned the “violence against foreigners, particularly Burundians”. “Kenya government is responsible for the lives of Burundians living in Kenya,” he posted on X. “Letting Kenyan civilians do the work of the police gives the impression of a failed state because it gives free rein to violence, to looting, and to xenophobia,” he added. Kenya’s junior foreign minister Korir Sing’Oei visited the embassy later on Monday, apologised to Burundians and assured them police would protect areas where violence had been reported. Sing’Oei said the president’s remarks had been “misunderstood”. “Because of this lack of understanding, many have been attacked by their neighbours and their friends,” he said. “Our main aim is to know how you are living, where you are, that you have identification, and that your jobs are registered,” he said. Government spokesperson Charles Owino said authorities had opened a “formal documentation and registration window” and called on Burundians to present their paperwork at their embassy. “The government recognises that due to historical circumstances and regional dynamics, a number of east African nationals, including several Burundians, reside and operate in Kenya without full documentation,” Owino said. Vanguard News The post Burundians rush to leave Kenya after crackdown on businesses appeared first on Vanguard News .","url":"https://www.vanguardngr.com/2026/09/burundians-rush-to-leave-kenya-after-crackdown-on-businesses/","source":"Vanguard Nigeria","image":"","published":"2026-09-08T07:09:43+00:00","category":"conflict","category_label":"Armed Conflict","region":"Southern Africa","country":"South Africa","fatalities":0,"severity":"Moderate","severity_score":0},{"id":"17fc507ae2ad","title":"As Justin Davis Leaves Mogadishu, Washington Is Asking a Different Question About Somalia","summary":"By Omar Faruk Osman Omar Faruk Osman is a prominent Somali journalist, press-freedom advocate, and trade unionist. As Justin Davis concludes his tenure as Chargé d’Affaires at the United States Embassy in Mogadishu, he leaves at a time when the relationship between Somalia and the U.S.is changing, perhaps more significantly than official statements suggest. Over […] The post As Justin Davis Leaves","body":"By Omar Faruk Osman Omar Faruk Osman is a prominent Somali journalist, press-freedom advocate, and trade unionist. As Justin Davis concludes his tenure as Chargé d’Affaires at the United States Embassy in Mogadishu, he leaves at a time when the relationship between Somalia and the U.S.is changing, perhaps more significantly than official statements suggest. Over recent months, I have spoken with several American diplomats in Mogadishu, Nairobi, New York, Washington and elsewhere about where the U.S. now stands on Somalia. Some are serving officials, while others know the Somalia file closely. They did not agree on everything and some were more candid than others, but the conversations repeatedly came back to the same question: how much responsibility are Somalis themselves prepared to take for the future of their country? Davis has run the US Embassy through a particularly difficult period. Politics in Washington changed sharply as “America First” came to define the administration’s approach abroad. In Somalia, political divisions deepened, relations between the Federal Government and some Federal Member States deteriorated and Al-Shabaab and Daesh continued to pose serious security threats. At the same time, competition among foreign powers for influence in Somalia and the wider Horn of Africa intensified. One American diplomat described Davis’s challenge to me as finding a way to reconcile “America First” with “Somalia First”. When I asked what “Somalia First” meant, the answer was strikingly straightforward: Somalia for Somalis, strong Somali institutions and political leaders taking responsibility for their own country. Several diplomats worried that foreign interests had become too entangled with Somalia’s domestic political competition and that Somali politicians were themselves sometimes opening the door to that interference. “Somalia’s future is not for sale,” one diplomat told me. It was not an argument for Somalia to turn away from international partners. The country needs them and none of the Americans I spoke with disputed that. Their concern was that Somalia cannot insist on its sovereignty while allowing domestic political disputes to become entangled with competing foreign agendas. In their view, Somali politicians have to find ways of settling their differences without constantly looking towards foreign capitals. Another diplomat was equally direct. “Somalia is not a child. Somalia is a partner,” he said. America was prepared to cooperate with Somalia and support it, but the relationship had to be one between partners, with Somalia taking responsibility for its own choices and future. Under the current US policy, American interests in Somalia have become narrower and more clearly defined. Three priorities came up repeatedly in my conversations: counterterrorism, law enforcement cooperation and immigration coordination. These are increasingly the hard interests around which Washington is organising its relationship with Mogadishu. But the American assessment of Somalia was far from entirely negative. Diplomats acknowledged good progress in law enforcement cooperation and immigration coordination and spoke positively about the working relationship with Somali authorities in these areas. They also recognised that Mogadishu is considerably more peaceful than it was before, something several of them regarded as an important achievement that should not be overlooked. Their frustration was much greater when the conversation moved beyond Mogadishu. American diplomats were unhappy with the pace of counter-offensive operations against Al-Shabaab outside the capital. They had expected greater movement and more sustained gains on the ground. Their concern was not simply about launching military operations but whether those operations were producing lasting results and maintaining sufficient pressure on Al-Shabaab. Politics inevitably entered that discussion. Several diplomats believed the prolonged confrontation between the federal government, federal member states and opposition politicians was taking attention away from the security campaign. To them, political cohesion and the fight against Al-Shabaab are not separate issues. A country whose political leaders are consumed by disputes among themselves will inevitably find it more difficult to sustain a coherent national security effort. The reduction in American humanitarian and development assistance also reflects the wider change in Washington. One diplomat pushed back when I described this as a retreat from humanitarian engagement. He preferred to call it a “reorganisation of assistance”, with more emphasis on priorities, accountability and results. Others were more candid in acknowledging that America’s broader interest in Somalia had declined and that Washington was reconsidering how much money and political attention it was prepared to commit. Some also admitted that Washington itself had spent several years struggling to decide exactly what its overriding national security interests in Somalia were. Over time, American engagement had expanded into counterterrorism, security assistance, humanitarian relief, state-building, democratisation and political mediation. The current approach is much less expansive. The message now is simpler: America will help Somalia, but Somalia must also help itself. That view becomes particularly clear when American diplomats talk about Somali politics. They see fragmentation on almost every side. Within the federal government, the executive, parliament and other state institutions appear increasingly aligned. Yet relations between Mogadishu and important Federal Member States remain badly fractured, particularly with Puntland and Jubaland. The Americans are not putting all the blame on Villa Somalia. They are also critical of the opposition. Several diplomats, in separate conversations, used exactly the same word to describe it: “fragmented”. They questioned how much opposition leaders trust one another and whether their alliances are built around a common political programme or simply the convenience of opposing the current leadership. One diplomat posed a difficult question: if opposition to one administration is what brings everyone together, what happens when that administration is no longer there? What struck me during these conversations was that the Americans were rarely questioning the competence of Somalis themselves. They spoke about capable politicians, professionals, entrepreneurs, civil society leaders and a remarkably resilient population. Their frustration was that all this talent had still not produced enough political cohesion around a minimum national interest. One senior diplomat used the U.S. itself as an example. He spoke about “American exceptionalism”, not to suggest that American politicians agree with one another, but almost to make the opposite point. American politics can be bitterly divided, he said, yet there remains a sense of national identity and certain interests around which Americans can still rally. His question was: what is the Somali equivalent? Curiously, the Americans saw a glimpse of an answer in the controversy involving Somalia’s renowned referee Omar Artan. A senior diplomat told me that when Artan was returned from the U.S. and was not allowed into the country, calls to the US Embassy in Mogadishu were incessant. According to the diplomat, President Hassan Sheikh Mohamud was among those seeking an explanation. But what fascinated American officials even more was the public reaction that followed. Diplomats in Washington, Mogadishu, Nairobi and elsewhere were closely watching the scenes of large numbers of young Somalis gathering at Mogadishu Stadium. They saw Somalis who would normally find themselves on different sides of political divides rallying around the same issue. It prompted a question among some of those watching: if Somalis could rally together so powerfully around Omar Artan, why could that same sense of national solidarity not be mobilised around Somalia? For some of the diplomats, the episode demonstrated that the national feeling exists. The problem is turning it into a political culture that can survive elections, clan calculations, disputes between governments and the influence of foreign powers. There is an obvious contradiction in some of this American thinking and the diplomats themselves are not unaware of it. For years, the U.S. was one of the most influential external actors in Somali politics. When a serious political crisis erupted, Somali leaders and international partners looked towards Washington. The Americans convened, persuaded, applied pressure and helped push political rivals towards compromise. That America is less visible today. Some Somali politicians and international partners regard this as a weakness in the current American approach. They believe Washington has withdrawn too far from political engagement and that its reduced role has left a vacuum at pre","url":"https://radiodalsan.com/2026/09/08/as-justin-davis-leaves-mogadishu-washington-is-asking-a-different-question-about-somalia/?utm_source=rss&utm_medium=rss&utm_campaign=as-justin-davis-leaves-mogadishu-washington-is-asking-a-different-question-about-somalia","source":"Radio Dalsan","image":"","published":"2026-09-08T06:55:37+00:00","category":"terrorism","category_label":"Terrorism","region":"Horn of Africa","country":"Somalia","fatalities":0,"severity":"Moderate","severity_score":0},{"id":"dcb50ebfdb05","title":"South Sudan, Uganda sign agreement on cross-border health security","summary":"The governments of South Sudan and Uganda have signed a Memorandum of Understanding (MOU) to The post South Sudan, Uganda sign agreement on cross-border health security appeared first on Radio Tamazuj .","body":"The governments of South Sudan and Uganda have signed a Memorandum of Understanding (MOU) to strengthen cross-border cooperation in preventing and responding to disease outbreaks and other public health emergencies. The agreement, signed in Juba on Monday by the two countries’ health ministers, establishes a framework for joint surveillance, information sharing and coordinated preparedness and response to health emergencies along their shared border. South Sudan’s Health Minister Luke Thompson Thoan Teny said the agreement moves the two countries’ health cooperation from general goodwill and communication towards an institutionalised system for rapid joint action. “This agreement transitions our bilateral relationship from goodwill and communication to institutionalised rapid response action,” he said. Thoan said infectious diseases do not respect national borders and that an outbreak in one country can quickly become a regional emergency, making isolated national responses increasingly ineffective. Under the MOU, South Sudan and Uganda will strengthen real-time epidemiological information sharing, mutual notification of outbreaks, joint contact tracing and coordinated disease surveillance. The agreement also provides for cooperation in areas including biospecimen transfer, harmonised screening at points of entry and the deployment of coordinated mobile health teams during emergencies. Vice President Hussein Abdelbagi Akol, who oversees the Service Delivery Cluster, said the recent Ebola threat had demonstrated the need for countries sharing borders to strengthen joint preparedness. He said pathogens do not respect checkpoints or national boundaries, warning that the shared border should not become a corridor for public health emergencies. “Health security is national security,” Abdelbagi said, adding that the government would support measures to remove administrative barriers that could delay emergency health responses. Uganda’s Health Minister Chris Baryomunsi said the MOU represented more than a formal agreement, describing it as a new chapter in public health cooperation between the neighbouring countries. He said Uganda’s experience responding to an Ebola outbreak earlier this year had demonstrated the importance of early coordination and cross-border preparedness. Uganda recorded 20 confirmed Ebola cases during the outbreak, including 15 Congolese nationals, four health workers and a driver. Eighteen patients were successfully treated while two died, with the outbreak declared contained after 73 days, according to Baryomunsi. He said Uganda was prepared to share lessons from its experience with South Sudan and other countries facing similar risks. Baryomunsi, however, stressed that signing the MOU was only the beginning, calling for the development of a joint implementation plan with clear priorities, responsibilities, timelines and resources. He also proposed establishing a regional standby health workforce that could be rapidly deployed during public health emergencies, similar to existing regional security arrangements. Africa CDC South Sudan Country Director Tolbert Geewleh Nyenswah said the agreement came at a time when countries in the region continued to face the threat of Ebola and other infectious diseases. He said South Sudan had identified 15 high-risk counties and 22 priority points of entry as part of its preparedness measures, with surveillance and response activities organised through hubs in Western Equatoria, Central Equatoria and Eastern Equatoria. Nyenswah said South Sudan’s revised six-month preparedness plan, running from August to January, requires more than 30 million U.S. dollars to support laboratories, medical supplies, logistics, surveillance and protection of health workers. He urged authorities and partners to strengthen preparedness before the first case of an outbreak is detected, saying preventing the first generation of transmission remains the most effective strategy. The two countries said the new framework would also support cooperation with regional and continental institutions, including the Intergovernmental Authority on Development (IGAD), Africa CDC and the World Health Organization. The MOU comes as countries in East and Central Africa increasingly seek stronger cross-border mechanisms to address infectious disease threats, with regional health authorities emphasising joint surveillance, information sharing and coordinated outbreak response. The post South Sudan, Uganda sign agreement on cross-border health security appeared first on Radio Tamazuj .","url":"https://www.radiotamazuj.org/en/news/article/south-sudan-uganda-sign-agreement-on-cross-border-health-security","source":"Radio Tamazuj","image":"https://www.radiotamazuj.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Hussein-Abdel-Bagi.mp3","published":"2026-09-08T06:55:25+00:00","category":"other","category_label":"Other Security News","region":"Horn of Africa","country":"South Sudan","fatalities":0,"severity":"Moderate","severity_score":0},{"id":"4b06e9d4bad1","title":"Aweil mother kills 2 children, tries to commit suicide","summary":"A 21-year-old mother, Akon Lual Ayii, killed her two children aged one and three, and The post Aweil mother kills 2 children, tries to commit suicide appeared first on Radio Tamazuj .","body":"A 21-year-old mother, Akon Lual Ayii, killed her two children aged one and three, and tried to commit suicide in the Northern Bahr El Ghazal State capital, Aweil Town, last Friday, the police said. Speaking to Radio Tamazuj on Monday, Captain Madut Ngong Aleu, the South Sudan National Police Service’s Spokesperson in the state, said Akon Lual Ayii murdered her children by suffocation. “On 4 September, a woman identified as Akon Lual Ayii, 21, suffocated her two children to death. The children were identified as a daughter called Angong Ngong Akuoch, three, and a son, Akuoch Ngong Akuoch, one,” he said. “She tried to commit suicide, but she was rescued by the neighbors and taken to hospital on the same day.” According to the preliminary investigations, the accused claimed that she had been mistreated by her husband, Ngong Akuoch, a reason she murdered her kids to make it easy for her to leave the marriage completely. “She is now under investigation and is facing murder and suicide charges under the South Sudan Criminal Code 2008,” Capt. Ngong stated. “According to the investigations, the accused claimed that she has been mistreated by her husband and that is why she took the lives of her children to ease her separation from the man.” For his part, Malong Bak Malong, First Deputy Chairperson of the Northern Bahr El Ghazal State Civil Society Network, attributed the incident to the current economic hardships. “Such incidents are connected with current living conditions, and people who commit such crimes are the ones who have lost hope,” he said. “Our message to the communities is that people must be patient.” He warned against taking out problems on innocent children, saying the latter are the future. The post Aweil mother kills 2 children, tries to commit suicide appeared first on Radio Tamazuj .","url":"https://www.radiotamazuj.org/en/news/article/aweil-mother-kills-2-children-tries-to-commit-suicide","source":"Radio Tamazuj","image":"","published":"2026-09-08T06:43:21+00:00","category":"conflict","category_label":"Armed Conflict","region":"Horn of Africa","country":"South Sudan","fatalities":2,"severity":"High","severity_score":1},{"id":"33ad7e2b59cf","title":"Troops foil Boko Haram ambush in Borno, recover arms","summary":"By Ndahi Marama, Maiduguri MAIDUGURI — Troops of the 27 Task Force Brigade, under the Joint Task Force North East, Operation HADIN KAI, have foiled an ambush by suspected Boko Haram terrorists along the Buni Gari-Buratai road in Biu Local Government Area of Borno State. The terrorists reportedly ambushed the troops at about 10:45 a.m. […] The post Troops foil Boko Haram ambush in Borno, recover ar","body":"By Ndahi Marama, Maiduguri MAIDUGURI — Troops of the 27 Task Force Brigade, under the Joint Task Force North East, Operation HADIN KAI, have foiled an ambush by suspected Boko Haram terrorists along the Buni Gari-Buratai road in Biu Local Government Area of Borno State. The terrorists reportedly ambushed the troops at about 10:45 a.m. on Monday, September 7, 2026, while they were conducting operations along the route. The Acting Media Information Officer, Headquarters Joint Task Force North East, Operation HADIN KAI, Captain Mohammed Goni, disclosed this in a statement on Tuesday. According to him, one Mine-Resistant Ambush Protected, MRAP, vehicle belonging to the troops ran over an improvised explosive device, IED, during the encounter, damaging two of its tyres. He said the troops subsequently exploited the ambush site and recovered an AK-47 magazine, 30 rounds of 7.62x39mm special ammunition, two RPG bombs, one motorcycle and two bicycles. Goni said no casualty was recorded, adding that the troops had continued operations to dominate the area and deny terrorists freedom of movement. Four terrorist family members surrender In a related development, four family members of suspected terrorists, including an eight-month-old infant, have surrendered to troops of the 192 Battalion along the Gwoza-Limankara road in Borno State. The group reportedly surrendered at about 1:35 p.m. on Sunday, September 6. According to military sources, preliminary investigations showed that the four individuals escaped from the Hiduwa terrorist enclave in the Mandara Mountains, Gwoza Local Government Area. The sources said the surrendered persons told troops that a lack of basic necessities in the enclave forced them to flee and seek safety. The individuals and the infant are currently in the custody of the troops undergoing screening and further investigation. Troops detonate IED on Damboa-Maiduguri road Meanwhile, troops of the 19 Battalion, deployed under 25 Brigade, have safely detonated an IED planted by suspected terrorists along the Damboa-Bulabulin-Maiduguri road in Damboa Local Government Area. The explosive device was discovered at about 12:07 p.m. on Monday during a road-dominance patrol and escort operation for civilian vehicles. The troops reportedly found the IED around Asorok, shortly before Bulabulin, where it had been buried by suspected terrorists. The area was immediately secured before the explosive was safely detonated, averting possible casualties and damage to civilian and military vehicles using the route. Following the clearance of the threat, the troops continued their movement towards the checkpoint at Kumala. Troops arrest suspected Cameroonian Also, troops of 25 Brigade, working with members of the Civilian Joint Task Force, CJTF, have arrested a suspected foreign national along the Azir-Damboa road in Borno State. Military sources said the suspect was apprehended at about 5:55 p.m. on Saturday, September 5, following information from local residents about the movement of a suspected foreigner along the route. During preliminary questioning, the suspect reportedly identified himself as a Cameroonian national. He allegedly told the troops that he entered Nigeria through Taraba State and was travelling towards Niger State in search of artisanal work but lost his way. The suspect remains in military custody as authorities investigate his identity, travel history and circumstances surrounding his presence in the area. The post Troops foil Boko Haram ambush in Borno, recover arms appeared first on Vanguard News .","url":"https://www.vanguardngr.com/2026/09/troops-foil-boko-haram-ambush-in-borno-recover-arms/","source":"Vanguard Nigeria","image":"","published":"2026-09-08T06:41:43+00:00","category":"terrorism","category_label":"Terrorism","region":"West Africa","country":"Nigeria","fatalities":0,"severity":"Moderate","severity_score":0},{"id":"fdc9884b3489","title":"My email, social media flooded with ‘hogwash’ on Tinubu – Fani-Kayode attacks US firm working for Atiku","summary":"Former Minister of Aviation and ambassador-designate to South Africa, Femi Fani-Kayode, has attacked Karl Ikemefuna Von Batten and his United States-based lobbying and public relations firm over their activities relating to President Bola Tinubu. Fani-Kayode, in a statement posted on his X handle on Tuesday, accused Von Batten and his firm, Von Batten-Montague-York LC, of circulating […] My email,","body":"Former Minister of Aviation and ambassador-designate to South Africa, Femi Fani-Kayode, has attacked Karl Ikemefuna Von Batten and his United States-based lobbying and public relations firm over their activities relating to President Bola Tinubu. Fani-Kayode, in a statement posted on his X handle on Tuesday, accused Von Batten and his firm, Von Batten-Montague-York LC, of circulating what he described as false and misleading information about Tinubu. He said his email and direct messages across his social media platforms had been flooded with materials published by Von Batten about the Nigerian president. “My email and the DM on all my social media handles have been flooded with the hogwash that you publish about President Bola Ahmed Tinubu on a daily basis by people from all over the world,” Fani-Kayode said. The former minister accused Von Batten of attempting to portray Tinubu as a criminal despite the president not having been convicted of any crime. He also dismissed allegations that Tinubu allegedly attempted to bribe Von Batten with $3 million through an associate to secure his silence. Fani-Kayode described the allegation as false and challenged Von Batten to provide evidence to support the claim. He said those pursuing the disclosure case involving Tinubu in the United States should allow the court proceedings to run their course. According to him, the matter would ultimately yield nothing, adding that he agreed with the Minister of Aviation and Aerospace Development, Festus Keyamo, who had reportedly described the issue as “a storm in a teacup” that was “going nowhere”. Fani-Kayode expressed confidence that the American judiciary would uphold the rule of law and protect what he described as Tinubu’s legitimate privacy rights. He also accused Von Batten of having a personal obsession with the matter and questioned his identity, educational background and the circumstances surrounding his adoption of the surname Von Batten. The former minister claimed he had been informed that Von Batten adopted the surname through his marriage to Elizabeth Von Batten. He questioned why the US-based lobbyist, whose records allegedly carried the Nigerian name Ikemefuna Okeke, was publicly known as Karl Von Batten. Fani-Kayode further alleged that Von Batten had been hired by former Vice President Atiku Abubakar and paid $1.2 million to pursue legal and lobbying efforts against Tinubu. He accused Von Batten of misleading Atiku and pursuing what he described as a futile campaign. “If this were not the case how did you manage to fool His Excellency Abubakar Atiku, the great Wazirin Adamawa, into paying you 1.2 million USD for pursuing a futile cause and treading a fruitless path that will lead to nowhere and end in NOTHING!” he said. Fani-Kayode also questioned Von Batten’s claims about his academic qualifications, saying there appeared to be no publicly available confirmation of his alleged attendance at institutions including Oxford, Columbia, Harvard, Cornell and the New York Institute of Technology. He maintained that efforts by Von Batten, his firm and others allegedly working against Tinubu would fail. According to Fani-Kayode, neither legal proceedings in the United States nor lobbying activities would determine Tinubu’s political future. “President Tinubu’s destiny and fate is not in your hands but rather in the hands of God and the Nigerian people,” he said. Fani-Kayode added that the outcome of the ongoing legal and political controversies surrounding Tinubu would not be determined by social media campaigns or lobbying efforts, insisting that the president would remain focused on his administration and political future. My email, social media flooded with ‘hogwash’ on Tinubu – Fani-Kayode attacks US firm working for Atiku","url":"https://dailypost.ng/2026/09/08/my-email-social-media-flooded-with-hogwash-on-tinubu-fani-kayode-attacks-us-firm-working-for-atiku/","source":"Daily Post","image":"","published":"2026-09-08T06:30:53+00:00","category":"conflict","category_label":"Armed Conflict","region":"Southern Africa","country":"South Africa","fatalities":0,"severity":"Moderate","severity_score":0},{"id":"96cddf0d1341","title":"Police bust child trafficking syndicate in Oyo, rescue 17 children","summary":"By Kingsley Omonobi ABUJA — The Nigeria Police Force has uncovered an alleged child-stealing and human trafficking syndicate operating across Oyo, Ogun and Delta states, rescuing 17 children and arresting seven suspects. The Force Public Relations Officer, CSP Aniete Iniedu, disclosed this in a statement, saying the breakthrough followed an intelligence-led investigation into the disappearance […]","body":"By Kingsley Omonobi ABUJA — The Nigeria Police Force has uncovered an alleged child-stealing and human trafficking syndicate operating across Oyo, Ogun and Delta states, rescuing 17 children and arresting seven suspects. The Force Public Relations Officer, CSP Aniete Iniedu, disclosed this in a statement, saying the breakthrough followed an intelligence-led investigation into the disappearance of a three-year-old child, Ifakunle Ifagbenro, during a masquerade festival in Oyo town on June 29, 2026. According to Iniedu, police detectives arrested Adejoke Popoola, also known as Orente Alagbo, on August 4 following extensive investigations. He said Popoola allegedly confessed to involvement in the stealing, movement and sale of children from churches, parties and other event venues within and outside Oyo State. Her disclosures, the police spokesman said, led detectives to another suspect, Adekanbi Janet, also known as Hosanna, who was arrested in Mowe, Ogun State. “Four children and two pregnant young women were rescued from her custody,” Iniedu said. He added that information obtained during the investigation led detectives to the whereabouts of Ifakunle, who was rescued from the Gbagi area of Ibadan on August 20. The investigation subsequently revealed that the alleged criminal network extended beyond Oyo and Ogun states. According to the police, detectives on September 2 extended the operation to Agbor, Delta State, where four children allegedly stolen from Oyo State were rescued from persons suspected of having illegally acquired them. Christopher Joy and Rosemary Jesse were arrested in connection with the operation, the police said, adding that their arrests further confirmed the alleged interstate nature of the network. The investigation also uncovered allegations that deceased victims were transferred to persons suspected of involvement in ritual activities. The development led to the arrest of Muritala Fasasi and Yakubu Azeez, who are being investigated over the allegations, the police said. The Command also said it had received information about six children who allegedly died while in the custody of members of the network. It said investigations were ongoing to establish the circumstances surrounding the reported deaths. The Inspector-General of Police, IGP Olatunji Rilwan Disu, commended the Oyo State Command for the breakthrough and directed that efforts be intensified to dismantle the alleged network, locate more missing children and ensure that anyone found culpable is brought to justice. The Force reaffirmed its commitment to protecting children and other vulnerable persons, dismantling criminal networks and prosecuting perpetrators of child-related crimes in accordance with the law. The post Police bust child trafficking syndicate in Oyo, rescue 17 children appeared first on Vanguard News .","url":"https://www.vanguardngr.com/2026/09/police-bust-child-trafficking-syndicate-in-oyo-rescue-17-children/","source":"Vanguard Nigeria","image":"","published":"2026-09-08T06:17:24+00:00","category":"kidnapping","category_label":"Kidnapping & Abduction","region":"West Africa","country":"Nigeria","fatalities":0,"severity":"Moderate","severity_score":0},{"id":"3fe2311a8f5a","title":"Gov Ododo bans night arrests in Kogi","summary":"GOVERNOR Ahmed Usman Ododo of Kogi State yesterday banned night arrests across the state, warning security operatives to desist from invading homes under the cover of darkness. The governor gave the order while addressing first-class chiefs at the Banquet Hall of the Government House, Lokoja, in what stakeholders described as one of his strongest pro-people […] The post Gov Ododo bans night arrest","body":"GOVERNOR Ahmed Usman Ododo of Kogi State yesterday banned night arrests across the state, warning security operatives to desist from invading homes under the cover of darkness. The governor gave the order while addressing first-class chiefs at the Banquet Hall of the Government House, Lokoja, in what stakeholders described as one of his strongest pro-people security pronouncements. “No arrest should be made in the night. If you have any case against anybody, wait till daytime, follow due process, invite the person lawfully,” the Governor declared. “We will not allow our people to be harassed, intimidated or picked up like criminals in the middle of the night.” Ododo said the ban was necessary to protect the dignity and fundamental rights of citizens, stressing those security operations must be civil, lawful and humane. He charged the royal fathers to report any security agent who violates the directive, assuring them that his administration would not tolerate abuse of power under any guise. Beyond the landmark order, the Governor used the royal audience to heap praise on President Bola Ahmed Tinubu for his massive support to Kogi State and Nigeria at large, especially in infrastructure and security. “We will be doing ourselves a disservice if we don’t appreciate Mr. President,” Ododo told the monarchs. “President Tinubu has stood firmly behind us in infrastructure, in security funding, and in ensuring prompt release of allocations for payment of salaries.” He noted that the ongoing reconstruction of the Abuja-Lokoja-Okene-Benin highway, a critical economic corridor linking the North Central to the South-South, is a testament to the President’s commitment. The governor also lauded the president for strengthening the nation’s security architecture and for backing Kogi’s local security initiatives, which he said have restored peace to many communities. He further called for total support for “our brother, the Chief of Army Staff,” saying: “If somebody providing security for the whole nation, his own house is not safe and peaceful, then it means we are not supporting him and we are not appreciating him.” He thanked the monarchs for their sacrifices, saying Kogi’s peace was built on their prayers, guidance and even personal resources. “Some of you go out with us to confront these criminals. Some pray with the Quran and the Bible, others with tradition. Only God will reward you,” he said. He however warned that the new enemy is within: “Our problem now is not foreigners. It is our own brothers and sisters supplying food, logistics and information to criminals in the bush.” The Governor specifically indicted some wood-cutters and asked the royal fathers to summon their associations for caution. “If you are caught aiding criminals, we will not spare you, even if you are my brother,” he warned. He revealed that the strategy of blocking supply routes and shutting down compromised local markets is already “making them feel the heat.” With political activities gaining momentum, Ododo begged the traditional rulers to preach peace. “We cannot be killing ourselves to get to a position. Campaign should be issue-based, not about religion or ethnicity. We are all Kogites,” he said. With political activities gaining momentum ahead of the forthcoming general election, Governor Ododo and other government officials urged the traditional rulers to use their respected positions to promote peace, tolerance and a violence-free electoral process across the state. The governor called on the royal fathers to caution their subjects against acts capable of disrupting the peace of the state, stressing that political differences must never become a reason for violence, intimidation or destruction. The post Gov Ododo bans night arrests in Kogi appeared first on Vanguard News .","url":"https://www.vanguardngr.com/2026/09/gov-ododo-bans-night-arrests-in-kogi/","source":"Vanguard Nigeria","image":"","published":"2026-09-08T06:15:04+00:00","category":"other","category_label":"Other Security News","region":"West Africa","country":"Nigeria","fatalities":0,"severity":"Moderate","severity_score":0},{"id":"68f34c3623f2","title":"Many terrorists eliminated as military airstrikes hit hideout in Zamfara","summary":"Nigerian troops of Operation FANSAN YANMA have carried out air operations against suspected terrorists in Yankuzo community, Tsafe Local Government Area of Zamfara State, reportedly killing several fighters. This was made known in a post on X by a counter-insurgency and security expert in the Lake Chad region, Zagazola Makama, on Tuesday. Makama, citing local sources, […] Many terrorists eliminate","body":"Nigerian troops of Operation FANSAN YANMA have carried out air operations against suspected terrorists in Yankuzo community, Tsafe Local Government Area of Zamfara State, reportedly killing several fighters. This was made known in a post on X by a counter-insurgency and security expert in the Lake Chad region, Zagazola Makama, on Tuesday. Makama, citing local sources, said the operation took place on the evening of Sept. 7, 2026, when five military aircraft were said to have conducted a coordinated operation over the community. According to the report, sources said the aircraft engaged suspected terrorists in the area, with several of the fighters reportedly killed, although the exact number of casualties could not immediately be established. Among those reportedly killed was a terrorist identified as Isiya Gurgu, said to be a close associate of a notorious bandit leader known as Musa Commander, who is reportedly linked to the Ado Aleru network. The report claimed that Musa Commander is associated with several terrorist-controlled communities, including Tsamiya and other settlements in the area. According to the account, the security forces appeared to have exercised caution during the operation to minimise civilian casualties, with the engagement reportedly focused on individuals seen carrying weapons or dressed in military-style uniforms. Several other suspected terrorists were also reportedly wounded during the operation. Yankuzo has reportedly remained a stronghold for armed groups linked to the notorious Ado Aleru network, with residents allegedly facing persistent threats from fighters operating in and around the community. Further details on the casualties and the outcome of the operation are expected as security agencies continue to assess the situation. ​ Many terrorists eliminated as military airstrikes hit hideout in Zamfara","url":"https://dailypost.ng/2026/09/08/many-terrorists-eliminated-as-military-airstrikes-hit-hideout-in-zamfara/","source":"Daily Post","image":"","published":"2026-09-08T06:14:54+00:00","category":"terrorism","category_label":"Terrorism","region":"Sahel","country":"Chad","fatalities":0,"severity":"Moderate","severity_score":0},{"id":"3713fc522fad","title":"Borno: Troops intercept vehicle with 11 empty CNG cylinders, open-loop kit","summary":"Troops of the 233 Tank Battalion have intercepted a vehicle carrying 11 empty Compressed Natural Gas, CNG, cylinders and a Greco open-loop kit without the required security clearance in Borno State. According to security analyst Zagazola Makama, the vehicle was intercepted at about 1:00 pm on September 6, 2026, during a routine stop-and-search operation at […] Borno: Troops intercept vehicle with ","body":"Troops of the 233 Tank Battalion have intercepted a vehicle carrying 11 empty Compressed Natural Gas, CNG, cylinders and a Greco open-loop kit without the required security clearance in Borno State. According to security analyst Zagazola Makama, the vehicle was intercepted at about 1:00 pm on September 6, 2026, during a routine stop-and-search operation at the Mega Checkpoint. The troops arrested the driver and recovered the 11 CNG cylinders and one Greco open-loop kit from the vehicle. “The items were reportedly being transported without security clearance, prompting the troops to detain the driver for further investigation,” the report stated. The driver and the recovered items remain in military custody as the investigation continues. Borno: Troops intercept vehicle with 11 empty CNG cylinders, open-loop kit","url":"https://dailypost.ng/2026/09/08/borno-troops-intercept-vehicle-with-11-empty-cng-cylinders-open-loop-kit/","source":"Daily Post","image":"","published":"2026-09-08T06:08:08+00:00","category":"other","category_label":"Other Security News","region":"West Africa","country":"Nigeria","fatalities":0,"severity":"Moderate","severity_score":0},{"id":"567e306e3637","title":"Insecurity: Troops capture suspected foreign national in Borno","summary":"Troops of 25 Brigade, in collaboration with the Civilian Joint Task Force, CJTF, have arrested a suspected foreign national along the Azir–Damboa road in Borno State. This was disclosed in a statement posted on X by a counter-terrorism expert, Zagazola Makama, on Tuesday. Zagazola said that the suspect was apprehended at about 5:55 p.m. on September […] Insecurity: Troops capture suspected foreign","body":"Troops of 25 Brigade, in collaboration with the Civilian Joint Task Force, CJTF, have arrested a suspected foreign national along the Azir–Damboa road in Borno State. This was disclosed in a statement posted on X by a counter-terrorism expert, Zagazola Makama, on Tuesday. Zagazola said that the suspect was apprehended at about 5:55 p.m. on September 5 following a tip-off from local residents about the movement of a suspected foreigner along the route. According to him, during preliminary questioning, the suspect reportedly identified himself as a national of the Republic of Cameroon. He added that the suspect told the troops that he entered Nigeria through Taraba State and was travelling towards Niger State in search of artisanal work but lost his way. “The suspect remains in military custody for further investigation and necessary action, while authorities work to establish his identity, travel history and circumstances surrounding his presence in the area,” the security expert said. Insecurity: Troops capture suspected foreign national in Borno","url":"https://dailypost.ng/2026/09/08/insecurity-troops-capture-suspected-foreign-national-in-borno/","source":"Daily Post","image":"","published":"2026-09-08T06:05:49+00:00","category":"terrorism","category_label":"Terrorism","region":"Central Africa","country":"Cameroon","fatalities":0,"severity":"Moderate","severity_score":0},{"id":"7dd162c50be6","title":"Churches back state police to end insecurity","summary":"The Spiritual Advisory Council (SAC) of the Christ Church, an umbrella body of the Church of Nigeria, Anglican Communion, Methodist Church of Nigeria and the Presbyterian Church of Nigeria, has backed the establishment of state police and urged the federal government to ensure its full implementation to tackle security challenges in The church’s leadership, in […]","body":"The Spiritual Advisory Council (SAC) of the Christ Church, an umbrella body of the Church of Nigeria, Anglican Communion, Methodist Church of Nigeria and the Presbyterian Church of Nigeria, has backed the establishment of state police and urged the federal government to ensure its full implementation to tackle security challenges in The church’s leadership, in a communique signed by His Eminence, The Most Rev’d Ekpenyong Nyong Akpanika, Prelate and Moderator of the General Assembly of the Presbyterian Church of Nigeria, at the end of its Spiritual Advisory Council meeting held in Port Harcourt, Rivers State, said it took sufficient notice of the efforts of the federal government to constitutionally restructure the policing system in the country. Prelate Akpanika said the security challenges faced across the country will be addressed through the creation of a dual policing framework with the intent to establish state and federal police commands. Akpanika said the body welcomes what it described as a bold initiative of the federal government and expects that the end result of the policy would be a more localised and better effective policing system for Nigeria. The body, while commending the efforts of the federal government, armed forces and other security agencies on the recent gains in stemming the spate of insecurity in the country, however, expressed grave concern over the persistent recurrence of kidnappings across the country. It enjoined the government and the security services to rise to the occasion and quickly stem what it described as the seemingly intractable kidnapping situation and other incidents of violent crimes across the country.","url":"https://dailytrust.com/churches-back-state-police-to-end-insecurity/","source":"Daily Trust","image":"","published":"2026-09-08T05:27:56+00:00","category":"other","category_label":"Other Security News","region":"West Africa","country":"Nigeria","fatalities":0,"severity":"Moderate","severity_score":0},{"id":"adbc4ab0e5ae","title":"Gunmen kill 3 vigilante members in Kaduna","summary":"Gunmen have killed three members of the Kaduna State Vigilance Service (Kadvis) along the Zuntu-Zaria Road in Kubau Local Government Area of Kaduna State. The incident occurred around 12:20am on Monday when the victims were on official duty, conducting stop-and-search operations along the highway. It was gathered that the three officials had mounted a checkpoint […]","body":"Gunmen have killed three members of the Kaduna State Vigilance Service (Kadvis) along the Zuntu-Zaria Road in Kubau Local Government Area of Kaduna State. The incident occurred around 12:20am on Monday when the victims were on official duty, conducting stop-and-search operations along the highway. It was gathered that the three officials had mounted a checkpoint when a red Gulf vehicle, travelling at high speed, approached the area and refused to stop. The officials reportedly pursued the vehicle in their patrol car, during which the driver, identified as Mamiyo, who was also the deputy commander of Kadvis in Kubau LGA, was shot. A resident, Ibrahim Zuntu, said the bullet reportedly passed through Mamiyo’s ear. He said the occupants of the red Gulf vehicle were suspected to be armed robbers, adding that there was an exchange of gunfire between the gunmen and the vigilante officials during the chase. “The people inside the red Gulf, we suspected, were armed robbers. The incident happened not far away from our residence, and there was an exchange of gunshots with the Kadvis during the chase,” he said. Ibrahim added that after the driver was shot, the gunmen opened fire on another Kadvis official inside the vehicle before returning to the checkpoint. “They returned to the checkpoint and met the other official who was left behind at the checkpoint and shot him too,” he said. Another resident, Ali Zuntu, described the incident as painful, saying the community was shocked by the killings. Meanwhile, two residents of Maro village in Kajuru Local Government Area were also killed when bandits attacked the community in the early hours of Monday. The attackers also abducted three women and three children during the incident. A resident, who identified himself as Ruben Maro, said residents could not sleep throughout the night as they engaged the attackers. He identified those killed as Yonana Augustine and Ezekiel Dogara. Efforts to get the reaction of the Kaduna State Police Command were unsuccessful as its spokesperson, DSP Mansur Hassan, could not be reached on the phone at the time of filing this report.","url":"https://dailytrust.com/gunmen-kill-3-vigilante-members-in-kaduna/","source":"Daily Trust","image":"","published":"2026-09-08T05:27:11+00:00","category":"conflict","category_label":"Armed Conflict","region":"West Africa","country":"Nigeria","fatalities":3,"severity":"High","severity_score":1},{"id":"ad9955061f68","title":"Kidnapping: Kogi govt launches workplace security sensitisation for workers","summary":"The Kogi State Government has commenced a workplace security sensitisation campaign across the 21 local government areas to encourage workers to remain security-conscious and make informed decisions that protect their jobs and welfare. The programme, held under the theme, “Workplace Security: Protection, Sensitisation and Preparedness Against Banditry and Kidnapping,” began in Okene Local Governme","body":"The Kogi State Government has commenced a workplace security sensitisation campaign across the 21 local government areas to encourage workers to remain security-conscious and make informed decisions that protect their jobs and welfare. The programme, held under the theme, “Workplace Security: Protection, Sensitisation and Preparedness Against Banditry and Kidnapping,” began in Okene Local Government Area. Addressing workers in the Central Senatorial District yesterday, the Special Adviser to the Governor on Labour Matters, Comrade Onuh Edoka, said the initiative was designed to sensitise local government workers on vigilance, preparedness and workplace security. The former NLC chairman in the state also urged workers to remain alert and make informed political choices ahead of the 2027 general elections. He said the security measures introduced by the administration of Governor Ahmed Usman Ododo had helped create a conducive environment for workers and foster industrial harmony across the state. “Security is a collective responsibility, either in the workplace or beyond. Government is committed to providing leadership and resources, but cannot do it alone,” Edoka said. He added, “The political will is the foundation of all security progress. Governor Ododo is ready for all-round protection of the people and property. The workers’ job is not an exception.” Edoka urged workers to resist the “politics of mischief” and prioritise policies and programmes that directly affect their welfare and job security. In their remarks, the NULGE chairmen of Okene and Ogori-Magongo LGAs, Comrades Yakubu Ishaq and Alabi Shole, respectively, commended the campaign and pledged to ensure its objectives were achieved at the grassroots. Also speaking, the Vice Chairman of Ogori-Magongo LGA, Mr Williams, described organised labour as a dependable ally in democracy and called for continued engagement between government and workers. The Kogi State NLC chairman, Comrade Gabriel Amari, described the grassroots security sensitisation as timely. He urged workers to sustain the cordial relationship with the government and continue to support an administration that, in his words, “has not disappointed them.”","url":"https://dailytrust.com/kidnapping-kogi-govt-launches-workplace-security-sensitisation-for-workers/","source":"Daily Trust","image":"","published":"2026-09-08T05:23:30+00:00","category":"kidnapping","category_label":"Kidnapping & Abduction","region":"West Africa","country":"Nigeria","fatalities":0,"severity":"Moderate","severity_score":0},{"id":"2caa253e51ff","title":"Medical evidence sinks Hoorn’s murder defence","summary":"Duncan Hoorn has been convicted of murdering his wife, Chantel Pasqualle-Hoorn, but acquitted of kidnapping and defeating the ends of justice after the court rejected his account of her injuries.","body":"Duncan Hoorn was found guilty of the murder of Chantal Pasqualle-Hoorn. Ayanda Ndamane/ ANA Studio\n\nDuncan Hoorn’s account of how his wife suffered fatal injuries was rejected by the court after medical evidence showed that Chantel Pasqualle-Hoorn arrived at hospital unconscious and unable to breathe without assistance.\n\nHoorn was found guilty of murder on Monday but acquitted of kidnapping and defeating the ends of justice.\n\nDelivering judgment in the Western Cape High Court, sitting at the Wynberg Regional Court, Acting Judge Amanda van Leeve said Chantel’s condition was incompatible with Hoorn’s version of what happened after the couple had been alone at their Hanover Park home.\n\nThe court heard that Hoorn had been exceptionally jealous during the relationship and suspected Chantel of having an affair.\n\nEvidence showed that a tracking device had been attached to Chantel’s vehicle, which the State argued demonstrated Hoorn’s determination to monitor her movements.\n\nOn June 27, 2024, Hoorn located Chantel in Cape Town, where he said he found her with another man.\n\nThe court heard evidence about threatening voice notes sent that day, including one in which Hoorn said he was going to jail for what he intended to do.\n\nChantel’s sister testified that during calls that evening she could hear Chantel pleading for Hoorn to stop because he was hurting her.\n\nShe later went to police and accompanied officers to the couple’s home.\n\nAlthough what happened during the police visit was disputed, the sister testified that Chantel called for help, asked to be taken out of the house and was later seen on the balcony still calling for assistance.\n\nVan Leeve also criticised the police response, describing shortcomings in their conduct as regrettable, particularly in circumstances where a woman was allegedly being assaulted inside the house and officers remained there only briefly.\n\nCentral to the murder conviction, however, was the medical evidence.\n\nHoorn denied assaulting Chantel and maintained that she was conscious and able to walk to the vehicle that transported her to hospital the next morning.\n\nVan Leeve noted that Chantel arrived at hospital unconscious and that medical experts established she had suffered a traumatic brain injury requiring emergency intervention.\n\nThe doctors testified that she could not breathe independently, remained unconscious and later remained in a coma.\n\nThe judge found it particularly difficult to reconcile this medical evidence with Hoorn’s testimony that Chantel was able to walk to the car.\n\nHis version was therefore inconsistent with the objective medical evidence before the court.\n\nThe court also rejected aspects of Hoorn’s account of what happened in Cape Town.\n\nHe testified that he chased the man he believed Chantel had been with after seeing him outside the hotel.\n\nHowever, evidence before the court indicated that the man had not left the hotel while Chantel and Hoorn were outside, creating another difficulty with Hoorn’s version.\n\nVan Leeve found that Hoorn’s account contained a number of improbabilities when weighed against the broader body of evidence.\n\nThe judge also considered the tracking device, the threatening voice notes, Chantel’s cries for help, the evidence that the couple were alone at the residence and the severe injuries with which she arrived at hospital.\n\nHoorn had denied causing any of Chantel’s injuries and also denied cleaning the scene.\n\nBut the court found that the evidence, viewed as a whole, established that Hoorn was responsible for the injuries that ultimately led to Chantel’s death.\n\nThe proceedings were tense as judgment was delivered, with Hoorn sweating profusely in the dock.\n\nAt one point he raised his hand, prompting his defence to indicate this to the judge.\n\nVan Leeve, however, made it clear she would not be interrupted during judgment.\n\nHoorn was ultimately convicted of murder, but acquitted on the kidnapping and defeating-the-ends-of-justice charges.","url":"https://iol.co.za/capeargus/news/2026-09-08-medical-evidence-sinks-hoorns-murder-defence/","source":"IOL News","image":"https://iol-prod.appspot.com/image/8e065af1f4278aabaccd8f105c04f630d362ab2d","published":"2026-09-08T05:00:00+00:00","category":"kidnapping","category_label":"Kidnapping & Abduction","region":"Southern Africa","country":"South Africa","fatalities":0,"severity":"Moderate","severity_score":0},{"id":"21426c5aa8ef","title":"Protest in Sokoto over court order remanding Islamic cleric","summary":"Tension in Sokoto as youths protest the remand of Islamic cleric Dahiru Mai Barewa, clashing with security personnel. Learn more about the court order. Read More: https://punchng.com/protest-in-sokoto-over-court-order-remanding-islamic-cleric/","body":"Tension briefly gripped parts of Sokoto metropolis on Monday as youths protesting the continued remand of an Islamic cleric, Dahiru Mai Barewa, clashed with security personnel. The protesters, who demanded Mai Barewa’s release, reportedly blocked some roads and staged demonstrations in parts of the city before security operatives intervened to restore order. The protest followed a court order directing the re-arrest and remand of Mai Barewa, who is facing allegations of issuing death threats against Islamic scholars, Sheikh Musa Ayyuba Lukuwa and Malam Murtala Bello Assada. The development has drawn attention in Sokoto because of the religious dimension of the allegations and the support Mai Barewa enjoys among some youths and members of the community. The youths opposed the court decision, insisting that their concerns should be addressed through due process and calling for what they described as “justice and respect for the rule of law.” The situation, however, escalated when some of the protesters allegedly attacked security personnel in an attempt to forcefully secure Mai Barewa’s release from custody. “They attempted to take the law into their own hands and forcefully free him from custody,” a source familiar with the incident said. Security personnel were subsequently deployed to contain the situation and prevent further escalation, with the confrontation causing temporary disruption to movement in the affected areas. Bandits kill 10, abduct dozens in Sokoto raid UK anti-migrant protesters block roads after boat crossing Court stops demolition, construction on disputed Lagos property Mai Barewa’s case stems from a series of videos in which he was allegedly seen making threats against Lukuwa and Assada. The videos reportedly generated concern among residents and prompted calls for security agencies to investigate the matter. According to reports, Mai Barewa linked his alleged threat against Sheikh Lukuwa to comments attributed to the cleric concerning the parents of Prophet Muhammad (SAW). He also reportedly referred to disagreements with some authorities in another video. Assada had earlier said the alleged threats were reported to security agencies, claiming that Mai Barewa repeatedly threatened the clerics in several videos. The matter subsequently became a subject of legal proceedings, with the court ordering Mai Barewa’s re-arrest and remand. His supporters have, however, opposed his continued detention and demanded his release. The controversy has continued to attract public attention, particularly among religious communities in Sokoto, with supporters of Mai Barewa demanding his release, while others have urged authorities to treat the alleged threats seriously. The latest protest has also renewed calls for restraint, with stakeholders urging residents to allow the courts and security agencies to handle the matter rather than resorting to confrontation. “People should not take the law into their own hands. If there is a grievance, it should be addressed through the appropriate legal channels,” a stakeholder said. Authorities have since restored calm in the affected areas, while residents were urged to remain peaceful and avoid actions capable of disrupting public order. Animashaun Salman\n\nNEWS UPDATE: Nigerians can now earn as much as $15,000- $25,000 with premium domains. You decide if you want payment in Naira or US Dollars. Be sure to ask for evidence and proof of people benefitting daily from this. CLICK HERE TO START.\n\nATTENTION NIGERIANS: You can now comfortably earn US Dollars when you acquire premium domains for as low as $3000 and profit $15,000-$35,000. Ideal for professionals like Entrepreneurs, Doctors, Civil/Public servants, IT Experts, Accountants ETC. CLICK HERE TO START.\n\nStay informed and ahead of the curve! Follow The Punch Newspaper on WhatsApp for real-time updates, breaking news, and exclusive content. Don't miss a headline – join now!\n\nStay in the know—fast. Get instant alerts, breaking headlines, and exclusive stories with the Punch News App . Download now and never miss a beat.","url":"https://punchng.com/protest-in-sokoto-over-court-order-remanding-islamic-cleric/?utm_source=rss.punchng.com&utm_medium=web","source":"Punch Nigeria","image":"https://cdn.punchng.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/08/18131509/punch-logo-500x179-1.png","published":"2026-09-08T04:45:18+00:00","category":"conflict","category_label":"Armed Conflict","region":"West Africa","country":"Nigeria","fatalities":0,"severity":"Moderate","severity_score":0},{"id":"0e20c0848ca2","title":"El-Rufai family gives Defence Minister 7 days to prove killing allegation","summary":"The family of former Kaduna State governor, Nasir El-Rufai, has given Minister of Defence, Gen. Christopher Musa (retd.), seven days to substantiate his allegation that the former governor “deliberately planned” the killing of people in Southern Kaduna or publicly retract the claim and apologise. The post El-Rufai family gives Defence Minister 7 days to prove killing allegation appeared first on T","body":"• APC chieftain: Musa’s remarks not about religion, Southern Kaduna alone By Philip Nwosu and Paul Orude, Bauchi The family of former Kaduna State governor, Nasir El-Rufai, has given Minister of Defence, Gen. Christopher Musa (retd.), seven days to substantiate his allegation that the former governor “deliberately planned” the killing of people in Southern Kaduna or publicly retract the claim and apologise. But as the controversy over Musa’s remarks deepened, a chieftain of the All Progressives Congress (APC) in Bauchi State, Khamis Musa Darazo, has cautioned against giving the minister’s comments a religious or sectional interpretation, arguing that grievances against El-Rufai’s administration extended beyond Southern Kaduna. The ultimatum followed Musa’s remarks during an appearance on a live television show where he claimed that El-Rufai’s eight-year administration, deepened divisions in Kaduna. He linked his policies to the security crisis that ravaged parts of the state. In a statement issued on behalf of the former governor by his eldest son, Mohammed Bello El-Rufai, the family described the allegation as grave and unsubstantiated, insisting that an accusation of deliberate involvement in killings must be backed by evidence. He said “While General Musa (retd.) is entitled to hold and express his personal opinions, the El-Rufai family is equally entitled to demand that such serious allegations be substantiated with credible evidence.” The family demanded that the minister either produce evidence to support his allegation or withdraw it through the same medium on which it was made. “Accordingly, General Musa (retd) is hereby given seven (7) days from the date of this statement to either provide evidence in support of his allegation or issue a full public retraction and apology.” It warned that failure to comply would leave the family with “no option but to pursue all appropriate legal remedies available under the law.” Musa, who hails from Southern Kaduna, had described the atmosphere in Kaduna under El-Rufai, who governed from 2015 to 2023, as “toxic”, accusing the former governor of pursuing policies that widened divisions in the state. Asked whether people in Southern Kaduna remained unhappy with El-Rufai, Musa said: “If there is anything worse than unhappy, that’s it. We can never be happy with somebody who deliberately planned for the killing of our people.” Pressed on the gravity of the allegation, the minister reportedly referred to past statements and videos involving El-Rufai, including the controversy over payments made in attempts to end cycles of violence involving herders. However, Darazo, a supporter of President Bola Tinubu, said attempts to interpret Musa’s reference to “our people” as applying exclusively to Southern Kaduna or Christians distorted the wider context of the minister’s remarks. “The reality is that the grievances surrounding El-Rufai’s style of governance were felt across Kaduna State and not exclusively by the people of Southern Kaduna,” Darazo told journalists in Bauchi. He also rejected suggestions that Musa’s criticism was motivated by religion. “The Minister did not call out Nasiru El-Rufai because he is a Muslim. That interpretation is completely misplaced. “President Bola Ahmed Tinubu and Governor Uba Sani are both Muslims, yet both have continued to demonstrate efforts towards reducing division and promoting unity,” he said. Darazo said Musa’s reference to “our people” should be understood as encompassing Kaduna residents generally. “Our people can never mean only Southern Kaduna. It means the people of Kaduna State as a whole. “Different communities experienced different challenges, and it is unfair to erase the experiences of others by presenting the matter as though it concerned only one section of the state,” he said. The controversy has revived memories of one of the most turbulent periods of El-Rufai’s governorship, when repeated communal and armed attacks in Southern Kaduna triggered national outrage and fierce debate over the state government’s response. In late 2016 and early 2017, killings across communities in the area generated criticism of the administration’s handling of the crisis. The Catholic Diocese of Kafanchan said at the time that its records showed 808 people were killed between 2011 and 2016, a figure disputed by government and security authorities. El-Rufai’s approach attracted further controversy after his government acknowledged reaching out to some Fulani communities outside Nigeria in an attempt to halt what it described as reprisal attacks. Reports that compensation was paid to some herders became a major point of contention. Gen. Martin Luther Agwai (retd), who headed a committee that examined the crisis, later said the panel recommended dialogue and compensation for all aggrieved parties rather than any single group. The Southern Kaduna Peoples’ Union (SOKAPU) also repeatedly accused El-Rufai’s administration of failing to adequately protect communities. The former governor, however, consistently rejected allegations that his administration encouraged the violence. He maintained that the crisis predated his government and blamed the failure of successive administrations to implement recommendations of previous commissions of inquiry for allowing the cycle of violence to persist. His administration said it pursued dialogue and reconciliation, increased security deployments and sought the prosecution of suspected attackers. El-Rufai also said in 2017 that those behind the killings were being tracked and would face prosecution. Musa, in his television appearance, contrasted El-Rufai’s tenure with that of Governor Uba Sani, arguing that the incumbent had taken steps to heal divisions and give different sections of Kaduna a greater sense of belonging. Darazo similarly urged Nigerians not to turn the dispute into a religious or regional confrontation, saying the focus should remain on governance, peace and security. “Kaduna belongs to all its people, and no community should be made to feel that its experience does not matter,” he said. The exchange comes against the backdrop of El-Rufai’s estrangement from the APC and his emergence as a prominent figure in the opposition African Democratic Congress (ADC), adding a political dimension to a controversy now threatening to move from public debate to the courtroom. The post El-Rufai family gives Defence Minister 7 days to prove killing allegation appeared first on The Sun Nigeria .","url":"https://thesun.ng/el-rufai-family-gives-defence-minister-7-days-to-prove-killing-allegation/","source":"Sun News Online","image":"","published":"2026-09-08T04:22:31+00:00","category":"other","category_label":"Other Security News","region":"West Africa","country":"Nigeria","fatalities":0,"severity":"Moderate","severity_score":0},{"id":"2b2b0b7d9467","title":"UEFA Champions League: Lookman’s Atletico Madrid mate banned against Liverpool","summary":"Atletico Madrid could be short of options in attack for Wednesday’s trip to Liverpool, with one of Diego Simeone’s forwards serving a ban for an incident that happened more than two years ago and will thus put pressure on Ademola Lookman as they seek a win away from home. The post UEFA Champions League: Lookman’s Atletico Madrid mate banned against Liverpool appeared first on The Sun Nigeria .","body":"Atletico Madrid could be short of options in attack for Wednesday’s trip to Liverpool, with one of Diego Simeone’s forwards serving a ban for an incident that happened more than two years ago and will thus put pressure on Ademola Lookman as they seek a win away from home. Andoni Iraola takes charge of the Reds for the first time in the Champions League later this week, having picked up his first win as head coach with a 2-0 victory against Ipswich on Friday. The Spaniard is not expected to have any more players missing following that game, although Wataru Endo has not been included in Liverpool’s European squad after being named on the bench at Portman Road. His first opponent in the Champions League is a familiar foe for Liverpool, with the Reds having kicked off the league phase against the same opponents last season. Atletico hasn’t had the best build-up to Wednesday’s game, having lost 3-0 to Athletic Club at the weekend in a result that Simeone admitted was “deserved”. To make matters worse for the Argentine, he will have to travel to Anfield with a weakened attack. Winger Arnau Ortiz is among those who won’t feature, as he finally serves a ban for an incident back in 2024. Ortiz was sent off while playing for Polish club Slask Wroclaw in a Conference League qualifier, having received two yellow cards just a few minutes after making his way onto the pitch as a substitute, with his side trying to rescue their tie against Swiss side St Gallen. He picked up his first yellow for a dive before receiving his second for protesting the original decision, with Slask finishing that game with eight players after two of Ortiz’s teammates were sent off just minutes earlier. Ortiz has not played a European game since then, having joined Atletico last year and spent the campaign with the club’s B team. Therefore, according to Marca, he will have to serve his suspension this week and miss the game at Anfield. Ortiz has played in three of Atletico’s opening four games this season, starting the club’s opening game against Malaga, and would have been under consideration to play against Liverpool, given the lack of options at Simeone’s disposal. Atletico is also without striker Alexander Sorloth, while summer signing Jonathan David is yet to make an appearance after signing on loan on deadline day, and it’s not yet known whether he will be ready to face the Reds. Meanwhile, Julian Alvarez is expected to travel, although he hasn’t yet made a start for Atletico this season, having tried to force through a move to Barcelona during the transfer window – a ploy which has seen him come under fire from his own club’s supporters. The post UEFA Champions League: Lookman’s Atletico Madrid mate banned against Liverpool appeared first on The Sun Nigeria .","url":"https://thesun.ng/uefa-champions-league-lookmans-atletico-madrid-mate-banned-against-liverpool/","source":"Sun News Online","image":"","published":"2026-09-08T04:21:18+00:00","category":"conflict","category_label":"Armed Conflict","region":"West Africa","country":"Nigeria","fatalities":0,"severity":"Moderate","severity_score":0},{"id":"4fb1ae8622d5","title":"LA2028: Go for best technical crew for Falcons, Are-Odeli tells NSC","summary":"Former Super Falcons attacking pearl, Patience Avre-Odeli, has stressed the need for the National Sports Commission (NSC) to appoint the best technical crew and management team for the Super Falcons ahead of the 2028 Olympic Games in Los Angeles, United States of America. The post LA2028: Go for best technical crew for Falcons, Are-Odeli tells NSC appeared first on The Sun Nigeria .","body":"Former Super Falcons attacking pearl, Patience Avre-Odeli, has stressed the need for the National Sports Commission (NSC) to appoint the best technical crew and management team for the Super Falcons ahead of the 2028 Olympic Games in Los Angeles, United States of America. The former player of Ufuoma Babes FC of Warri and Princess Jegede Queens FC of Lagos, who is based in Chicago, United States, said poor coaching and administrative shortcomings contributed to the Super Falcons’ failure to successfully defend their title at the recently concluded Women’s Africa Cup of Nations (WAFCON) title in Morocco and, also, failed to qualify for the FIFA Women’s World Cup in Brazil next year. Avre-Odeli, who is the Chairperson of the United Ex-Super Falcons Association (Worldwide), said the former coaching crew, led by Justin Madugu, did their best, but their best was not enough to achieve the desired results. “Surely, we require a superior coaching and management team to take charge of the Super Falcons after Cameroon and South Africa brought an end to our proud record. Besides the shortcomings in coaching, there were also serious distractions involving some members of the backroom staff. “There were also concerns over delays in the payment of money promised by the government. A situation where key NSC officials reportedly had to intervene to prevent two possible strike actions during the WAFCON tournament in Morocco was unacceptable. Those who failed to carry out their responsibilities properly should not be involved in the new Super Falcons’ setup. “There must be no compromise in the appointment of the new coaches and backroom staff. We need people who are highly qualified, committed, and capable of restoring the Super Falcons to their rightful place at the top of African women’s football. “We are aware of plans to appoint a new coaching crew. The likes of Florence Omagbemi and Eucharia Uche have proven themselves, both as former Nigerian internationals and as coaches. They simply need the support of the authorities and all Nigerians to pilot the Super Falcons back to the zenith of African and world football,” Avre-Odeli stated. The post LA2028: Go for best technical crew for Falcons, Are-Odeli tells NSC appeared first on The Sun Nigeria .","url":"https://thesun.ng/la2028-go-for-best-technical-crew-for-falcons-are-odeli-tells-nsc/","source":"Sun News Online","image":"","published":"2026-09-08T04:21:12+00:00","category":"conflict","category_label":"Armed Conflict","region":"Southern Africa","country":"South Africa","fatalities":0,"severity":"Moderate","severity_score":0},{"id":"6b88ebbb694a","title":"Ogunsakin claims first Junior Grand Slam Main-Draw Victory At US Open","summary":"Nigeria’s rising junior tennis star Seun Ogunsakin has written a new chapter in his career by securing his first-ever win in the main draw of a Junior Grand Slam tournament, BSNSports.com.ng reports. The post Ogunsakin claims first Junior Grand Slam Main-Draw Victory At US Open appeared first on The Sun Nigeria .","body":"Nigeria’s rising junior tennis star Seun Ogunsakin has written a new chapter in his career by securing his first-ever win in the main draw of a Junior Grand Slam tournament, BSNSports.com.ng reports. The Aurum Tennis Academy player defeated 15th seed Kuan-Shou Chen of Chinese Taipei in a gripping three-set battle, 5-7, 6-3, 7-5, on the opening day of the 2026 US Open Junior Championships. Ogunsakin, who entered the 64-player main draw as a qualifier, showed remarkable resilience after dropping the opening set. He fought back to take the second and then edged a tense decider to seal the milestone victory. This triumph marks the first time the Nigerian teenager has won a match in the main draw of a junior Grand Slam event. It continues an impressive run that has seen him compete in three consecutive junior majors this year, following appearances at the French Open and Wimbledon, where he reached the boys’ singles main draw and the doubles semifinals. Ogunsakin had earned his place in the US Open main draw by navigating the qualifying rounds without dropping a set, including a notable upset of top seed Felix Roussel of Canada. The hard-court success in New York further cements Ogunsakin’s status as one of Africa’s most promising young talents and a key figure for Nigerian tennis on the international stage. He will look to build on this breakthrough in the next round. The post Ogunsakin claims first Junior Grand Slam Main-Draw Victory At US Open appeared first on The Sun Nigeria .","url":"https://thesun.ng/ogunsakin-claims-first-junior-grand-slam-main-draw-victory-at-us-open/","source":"Sun News Online","image":"","published":"2026-09-08T04:20:26+00:00","category":"conflict","category_label":"Armed Conflict","region":"West Africa","country":"Nigeria","fatalities":0,"severity":"Moderate","severity_score":0},{"id":"e463de9c8ce3","title":"NDDC Moot Court: 16 Law Faculties Battle for Honours, Ebie, Akpata Seek Stronger Legal Education","summary":"Blessing Ibunge in Port Harcourt Sixteen law faculties from universities across the Niger Delta are competing in the 2026 Niger Delta Development Commission (NDDC) Annual Moot Court and Mock Trial","body":"Blessing Ibunge in Port Harcourt Sixteen law faculties from universities across the Niger Delta are competing in the 2026 Niger Delta Development Commission (NDDC) Annual Moot Court and Mock Trial Competition, with stakeholders stressing the importance of practical legal training in preparing young lawyers for the challenges of the region. Declaring the competition open in Port Harcourt, Chairman of the NDDC 7th Governing Board, Chiedu Ebie, said the commission’s continued investment in the competition was driven by its strategic role in building the confidence and advocacy skills of young legal minds. Ebie said moot court competitions provided law students with opportunities to develop courtroom presentation and advocacy skills while gaining practical experience beyond the classroom. “It is our expectation that this competition lays the foundation for the advocacy skills these young legal minds will need to draw attention to the challenges of our region, even at this early stage of their careers,” he said. The NDDC Managing Director, Samuel Ogbuku, said the competition had been held for the third consecutive year under his stewardship, describing it as part of the commission’s commitment to educational and human-capacity development in the Niger Delta. Represented by the Executive Director, Finance and Administration, Boma Iyaye, Ogbuku said feedback from previous editions had demonstrated the importance of moot trials in equipping law students and aspiring lawyers with knowledge, advocacy skills and practical experience. He noted the competition was particularly relevant because participants were required to interrogate legal issues connected to the socio-economic and environmental challenges of the Niger Delta and propose legal solutions. Ogbuku further urged legal education stakeholders in the region to adapt curricula to emerging global realities, including energy transition and the rapid development of artificial intelligence. He said the NDDC would continue to support strategic educational interventions capable of positioning young people from the region to compete globally. Also speaking, the 30th President of the Nigerian Bar Association (NBA), Olumide Akpata, commended the NDDC for sustaining the initiative, describing investment in young people in the legal profession as one of the most meaningful investments in the future of the region. Akpata appealed to the NDDC Governing Board to revive the Memorandum of Understanding between the commission and the NBA, assuring that the association was ready to deepen its collaboration with the NDDC. He urged universities to establish and strengthen moot courts as a core component of legal education, arguing that practical advocacy skills remained indispensable despite advances in artificial intelligence. “Most arguments can now be done by machine. But the ability to stand in court and argue what you did not anticipate is what the machine cannot do for you,” he said. A retired Justice of the Supreme Court and Chairperson of the Rivers State University Governing Board, Justice Mary Odili, urged participants to treasure the experience gained from the competition, noting that the lessons would remain valuable throughout their professional careers. The Director, Legal Services of the NDDC, Victor Arenyeka, said the annual competition exposed students to topical issues in environmental law, oil and gas law, petroleum industry legislation and sustainable development. He added the programme promoted teamwork, networking, intellectual development and regional integration among participating universities.","url":"https://www.thisdaylive.com/2026/09/08/nddc-moot-court-16-law-faculties-battle-for-honours-ebie-akpata-seek-stronger-legal-education/","source":"ThisDay Live","image":"","published":"2026-09-08T02:25:47+00:00","category":"conflict","category_label":"Armed Conflict","region":"Sahel","country":"Niger","fatalities":0,"severity":"Moderate","severity_score":0},{"id":"33b0d2ee3985","title":"Alleged Defamatory Publication: El-Rufai Demands N10bn, Apology from Defence Minister","summary":"• Threatens legal action Alex Enumah in Abuja Embattled former governor of Kaduna State, Mallam Nasir El-Rufai, has demanded the sum of N10 billion as compensation, from the Minister of","body":"• Threatens legal action Alex Enumah in Abuja Embattled former governor of Kaduna State, Mallam Nasir El-Rufai, has demanded the sum of N10 billion as compensation, from the Minister of Defence, General Christopher Musa (Rtd.), over alleged defamatory publication made against his person. El-Rufai in a letter dated September 7, and signed by his lawyer, Ubong Akpan is also demanding an apology tendered in two national newspapers as well as a retraction of the said defamatory. Recall that the minister had while appearing as a guest on Channels Television’s Politics Today, on September 3, 2026, made certain statements considered as defamatory by the former governor. According to the letter, General Musa had among others alleged that el-Rufai “deliberately planned for the killing of our people”, and divided Kaduna State along ethnic and religious lines when he was governor of the state. The minister was also quoted as saying that the former governor paid ransom to bandits as well as orchestrated the demolition of properties belonging to critics; statements which his lawyer claimed has done serious harm to the reputation of the ex governor. Akpan, who described the allegations against his client as false submitted that they presented the former governor as “a criminal, a murderer, a sponsor of banditry, and a person of the most base and depraved character.” The letter while observing that el-Rufai had “never been involved in any offence relating to the deliberate planning of killings in Southern Kaduna,” maintained that “no evidence has ever been produced to establish that our Client ‘deliberately planned the killing of any person.” “Your statements carry particular weight and credibility in the eyes of the public. By making these allegations from such a position of authority, you have caused substantially greater damage to our Client’s reputation than would have been caused by a private individual”, Akpan said. “Our Client’s instruction is to demand the sum of #10,000,000,000.00 (Ten Billion Naira) as compensation for the injury to his reputation and the distress and inconvenience occasioned”, he added. Other demands they are making included a “prominent, unconditional and unambiguous retraction, correction and apology on Politics Today, with the same or greater prominence as the original statements”. The lawyers further demanded that “the apology be published in two nationally circulating newspapers, one principally circulating in Northern Nigeria and another principally circulating in Southern Nigeria”. Meanwhile, Musa was giving seven days to fully comply with the demands contained in the letter or face legal action.","url":"https://www.thisdaylive.com/2026/09/08/alleged-defamatory-publication-el-rufai-demands-n10bn-apology-from-defence-minister/","source":"ThisDay Live","image":"","published":"2026-09-08T02:24:19+00:00","category":"kidnapping","category_label":"Kidnapping & Abduction","region":"West Africa","country":"Nigeria","fatalities":0,"severity":"Moderate","severity_score":0},{"id":"1f4dbbe59b3f","title":"Anambra APC Protests Exclusion in Arrangements for Remi Tinubu’s Visit, Accuses APGA of Hijack","summary":"• As security agencies profile travel routes ahead of visit David-Chyddy Eleke in Awka The All Progressives Congress (APC) in Anambra State, has protested the exclusion of the party and","body":"• As security agencies profile travel routes ahead of visit David-Chyddy Eleke in Awka The All Progressives Congress (APC) in Anambra State, has protested the exclusion of the party and its women in the arrangements of the expected visit of the First Lady of Nigeria, Senator Oluremi Tinubu to the state on Thursday. Though the party leadership congratulated and welcomed the First Lady to the state, they added that the All Progressives Grand Alliance (APGA) is deceiving President Bola Tinubu on the expectation that progressives were working together, when in real sense they (APC) in Anambra were being alienated from national activities. This is coming as joint security agencies have commenced profiling of all routes to be travelled by the First Lady, including security mappings. In a statement issued on Monday in Awka, Anambra State and signed by the state Secretary of APC, Sir Obi Okpala and made available to journalists, APC said the party only knew about the visit and its arrangements through the announcement by the First Lady of Anambra State, Dr. Nonye Soludo. The statement read: “The Anambra State APC warmly welcomes Her Excellency, Senator Oluremi Tinubu CON, to our dear state and appreciates her unwavering commitment to the empowerment, welfare and economic advancement of Nigerian women, particularly her initiative to empower thousands of women across the Southeast. “However, the Anambra State APC is constrained to express its concern and disappointment that, as of the time of issuing this statement, the leadership of the All Progressives Congress in Anambra State, including its women leadership and recognised party structures, have neither been formally invited nor meaningfully carried along in the arrangements for this important visit. “As the leading political party at the national level and a major political stakeholder in Anambra State, we believe that an official visit by the First Lady of the Federal Republic of Nigeria, particularly one centred on the empowerment of women, should transcend partisan considerations and provide an opportunity for broad participation, consultation and inclusion. “It is therefore difficult for us to reconcile the apparent exclusion of the APC and its women structures with the repeated public narrative of “Progressives Are Working Together” “The Rhetorics of Progressives Working Together is only intended for the National Television shows as APGA as a party is not marketing President Bola Ahmed Tinubu and his achievements in the state but using it to hoodwink APC leadership and also to confuse the gullible public into voting APGA candidates in the national and state assembly elections. “We believe that this should not be interpreted as an attempt to politicise your humanitarian and empowerment programme, but rather as an appeal for greater inclusion and recognition of all relevant stakeholders in the spirit of national unity. “The APC has been structured and strengthened to deliver Mr. President in Anambra State, we totally reject the rhetoric of Progressives are working together because is not tenable anywhere rather they are confusing Mr. President with the 25 percent narrative which they know they are not capable of producing considering their background, because all their media communications are demarketing Mr. President and APC,” the party wrote. Meanwhile the Anambra State Police Command, in synergy with other security and safety agencies, have commenced profiling of routes to be traveled by wife of the President of Nigeria, Senator Oluremi Tinubu. A press release by the state police command’s spokesperson, SP Tochukwu Ikenga stated that: “This is the first phase of a comprehensive security and safety assessment across strategic locations in the state ahead of the planned visit of high-profile dignitaries to Anambra. “The exercise is part of the command’s proactive, intelligence-led security strategy to identify potential vulnerabilities, validate existing security arrangements and reinforce operational readiness around designated venues, major access routes, critical infrastructure and other strategic locations. “The assessment focuses on threat identification, intelligence coordination, access and movement control, strategic deployment, traffic management, emergency response capability and protection of lives and property. “It is being conducted through enhanced inter-agency coordination to ensure a seamless and unified security architecture before, during and after the visit,” the release read.","url":"https://www.thisdaylive.com/2026/09/08/anambra-apc-protests-exclusion-in-arrangements-for-remi-tinubus-visit-accuses-apga-of-hijack/","source":"ThisDay Live","image":"","published":"2026-09-08T02:22:31+00:00","category":"other","category_label":"Other Security News","region":"West Africa","country":"Nigeria","fatalities":0,"severity":"Moderate","severity_score":0},{"id":"a910b2751db7","title":"NAAPE, NDLEA Move to Strengthen Aviation Sector’s Role in Combating Drug Trafficking","summary":"Kasim Sumaina in Abuja National Association of Aircraft Pilots and Engineers (NAAPE) has pledged to support National Drug Law Enforcement Agency (NDLEA) in its effort to prevent illicit drug trafficking","body":"Kasim Sumaina in Abuja National Association of Aircraft Pilots and Engineers (NAAPE) has pledged to support National Drug Law Enforcement Agency (NDLEA) in its effort to prevent illicit drug trafficking and substance abuse within Nigeria’s aviation sector, NAAPE said the integrity of pilots and engineers was closely linked to flight safety and national security. The association made the commitment during a courtesy visit by its President, Captain Bunmi Gindeh, to Chairman and Chief Executive Officer of NDLEA, Brigadier-General Mohamed Marwa (Rtd), at the agency’s headquarters in Abuja. Both sides examined the role aviation professionals could play in strengthening the fight against illicit drugs. Speaking during the visit, Gindeh emphasised that the fight against narcotics and the preservation of aviation safety had become inextricably tied. He stated that licensed pilots and maintenance engineers operated at the most critical points of aviation operations, before departure, during flight, and inside hangars, where public trust must remain absolute. “This visit is not ceremonial,” Gindeh stated. He added, “Fighting illicit drugs and protecting aviation safety are now the same fight. They meet at our airports, our cockpits, our hangars, and in the lives of the young Nigerians we both serve. “Airports remain a top target for trafficking, and when a professional is compromised, it is not just a crime; it is a direct threat to flight safety and national security.” In a statement issued on Monday by Public Relations Officer of NAAPE, Engr. Blessing N. Ahmadu, Gindeh explained that aviation professionals were often the first line of defence in spotting unusual occurrences, including sudden changes in colleagues’ behaviour, unexplained wealth, or suspicious cargo-handling activities. He stressed that a proactive union was essential to closing vulnerabilities that drug cartels could exploit. Gindeh stated, “NAAPE does not see itself as a spectator in this effort. Our members work where aviation safety is protected every day. “We have a responsibility to promote integrity, fitness for duty, and a culture in which legitimate concerns can be raised before they become serious threats.” To operationalise the collaboration, NAAPE outlined four key areas of action: organising joint awareness and integrity sessions for flight crews, engineers, and aviation students, in collaboration with NDLEA airport commands; establishing clear, confidential reporting channels between NAAPE branches and NDLEA; developing joint fitness-for-duty and substance-abuse prevention programmes across airlines and maintenance facilities; and strengthening continuous professional education to ensure early identification of behavioural and security concerns. The NAAPE president also commended Marwa for the agency’s sterling work in tackling drug trafficking and abuse, as well as the renewed intensity of its campaign against drug trafficking, including its focus on disrupting trafficking networks, tracing cartel finances, and reducing drug demand among young Nigerians. During the visit, the NAAPE leadership also presented Marwa with an “Award for Excellence in Leadership” in recognition of his leadership and commitment to the fight against drug trafficking and abuse. Marwa commended NAAPE for its proactive leadership and willingness to align with the anti-narcotics crusade. He described aviation workers as crucial gatekeepers whose vigilance could significantly hamper the movement of illicit drugs across international borders. Marwa stated, “We welcome this timely partnership with NAAPE. The aviation sector is a vital gateway to our economy and international image, and we cannot afford to let drug cartels infiltrate it. “Pilots and engineers hold immense public trust, and your willingness to serve as eyes and ears for intelligence sharing will bolster our operational efficiency at all international airports.” The NDLEA boss said cooperation with professional bodies, such as NAAPE, could strengthen prevention efforts by extending the campaign beyond enforcement to workplaces, training institutions, and professional communities. He said, “Every responsible sector must contribute to prevention. We want to deepen partnerships that help us detect threats earlier, protect young Nigerians from substance abuse, and ensure that criminal networks do not exploit legitimate systems and professionals for their activities.” Both sides expressed interest in maintaining a regular working relationship, as NAAPE said it would continue to explore areas of cooperation that could strengthen professional integrity, prevent substance abuse, and support efforts to keep Nigeria’s aviation system safe.","url":"https://www.thisdaylive.com/2026/09/08/naape-ndlea-move-to-strengthen-aviation-sectors-role-in-combating-drug-trafficking/","source":"ThisDay Live","image":"","published":"2026-09-08T02:22:14+00:00","category":"conflict","category_label":"Armed Conflict","region":"West Africa","country":"Nigeria","fatalities":0,"severity":"Moderate","severity_score":0},{"id":"2d76dd2bd0b7","title":"IYC Rejects Call for Revocation of Pipeline Surveillance Contract","summary":"Olusegun Samuel in Yenagoa The leadership of the Ijaw Youth Council (IYC), Central Zone, has rejected the call for revocation of the pipeline surveillance contract involving Seven Oceans Oil and","body":"Olusegun Samuel in Yenagoa The leadership of the Ijaw Youth Council (IYC), Central Zone, has rejected the call for revocation of the pipeline surveillance contract involving Seven Oceans Oil and Gas Ltd., Kojo Sam Logistics Ltd., and Labrador Security Outfit. Reaction to a recent publication by Mr. Kelly Nengimote calling for the revocation of the contract, IYC said after carefully considering the issues raised and their potential implications for the security and economic interests of the Niger Delta, the Council considers it necessary to state its position. A statement signed by Boro Francis, Information Officer, IYC central zone, strongly condemned any attempt to use the name or collective interest of the Ijaw people to undermine fellow Ijaw persons or legitimate Ijaw-owned enterprises. “While individuals have the right to raise concerns about public or commercial activities, such concerns must be supported by credible evidence and addressed through appropriate institutional channels. “The Ijaw people must not become instruments in internal rivalries. We cannot continue to pull down our own people and later lament that opportunities meant for our communities are being taken over by others. “Constructive criticism should strengthen our society, not deepen division or weaken legitimate economic participation. “The Council therefore urges the federal government to maintain the existing pipeline surveillance arrangement involving Seven Oceans Oil and Gas Ltd., Kojo Sam Logistics Ltd., and Labrador Security Outfit, subject to established regulatory, operational, and security requirements. “Pipeline surveillance is critical to the protection of national infrastructure, prevention of oil theft and pipeline vandalism, safeguarding of government revenue, and maintenance of security across oil-producing communities. “Any decision capable of disrupting such operations must consequently be based on verified facts and a comprehensive assessment of its implications”, the statement reads. The statement also added that at this critical juncture, the Niger Delta requires cooperation among communities, government institutions, security agencies, contractors, and youth stakeholders. It added that actions capable of generating unnecessary tension around oil and gas infrastructure should therefore be avoided. “The Central Zone acknowledges the activities of Seven Oceans Oil and Gas Ltd., Kojo Sam Logistics Ltd., Labrador Security Outfit, and other operators working within the Tantita security framework. “From our assessment of developments in the areas covered by these operations, we recognise their contribution to infrastructure surveillance and the maintenance of order. “There is, therefore, no justification for terminating or disrupting the existing arrangement solely on the basis of an unverified publication. Where concerns exist, the appropriate response is independent investigation and objective evaluation rather than premature conclusions.” IYC called on the federal government and relevant authorities to allow the contractors to continue their responsibilities while ensuring strict compliance with performance standards, accountability measures, regulatory requirements, and national security obligations. The statement however, called on the government to also independently examine the claims contained in Mr. Kelly Nengimote’s publication before taking any action that could affect the current surveillance structure. “No allegation, regardless of its source, should automatically become the basis for a decision of significant national consequence. “If there are legitimate concerns about the conduct or performance of any contractor, they should be formally presented to the appropriate authorities and subjected to transparent investigation. “The IYC Central Zone will support accountability wherever wrongdoing is established, but accountability must not be used as a tool for personal rivalry, vendetta, or the destruction of legitimate Ijaw interests. “Our position is clear: justice must be guided by facts, decisions must follow due process, and the collective interest must prevail over individual ambition”, the statement added. It further stated that, “The Niger Delta has endured decades of insecurity, environmental degradation, economic hardship, and disruption of legitimate livelihoods. It would be counterproductive for internal disagreements to create additional instability in a region that requires sustained peace and economic development.” The body appealed to Ijaw leaders, youth organisations, community leaders, and other stakeholders to exercise restraint, responsibility, and maturity in addressing disagreements.","url":"https://www.thisdaylive.com/2026/09/08/iyc-rejects-call-for-revocation-of-pipeline-surveillance-contract/","source":"ThisDay Live","image":"","published":"2026-09-08T02:21:57+00:00","category":"other","category_label":"Other Security News","region":"Sahel","country":"Niger","fatalities":0,"severity":"Moderate","severity_score":0},{"id":"c2f74d0eefc7","title":"NAPTIP, NGO Target 6,000 Nigerian Youths Annually in Anti-trafficking Drive","summary":"Sylvester Idowu in Warri National Agency for the Prohibition of Trafficking in Persons (NAPTIP) and Next2None Support Initiative have launched an eight-year campaign to prevent human trafficking using fashion, sports,","body":"Sylvester Idowu in Warri National Agency for the Prohibition of Trafficking in Persons (NAPTIP) and Next2None Support Initiative have launched an eight-year campaign to prevent human trafficking using fashion, sports, entertainment, and vocational training to reach vulnerable young Nigerians. The initiative, tagged, “Threads of Resilience: Fashion for Freedom,” is expected to combine anti-trafficking education with skills development and survivor rehabilitation across the country. The organisations announced the partnership in a joint statement issued yesterday, following the signing of a Memorandum of Understanding on August 27. The programme, which would run from 2026 to 2034, targets an initial 6,000 young people annually and plans to train about 1,000 participants in each of Nigeria’s six geopolitical zones. The organisers said the campaign would culminate in an annual Global Fashion for Freedom event in Abuja, with its international launch scheduled for the second quarter of 2027. Director-General of Next2None Support Initiative, Mr. Sam Obu, said the nature of human trafficking had changed significantly, with traffickers increasingly exploiting social media, fake job offers, fraudulent migration schemes, and online relationships. Obu said the campaign was designed to tackle some of the conditions that made young people vulnerable to exploitation. He said, “Human trafficking is no longer just a law enforcement challenge; it is a crisis of poverty, unemployment, and lack of opportunity.” Obu said the initiative would go beyond rescuing trafficking victims by creating opportunities that could help vulnerable people avoid exploitation, in the first place. He said, “Threads of Resilience addresses this by moving beyond mere rescue. We are creating a sustainable, Nigerian-led international movement that uses creativity, sports, and education to establish pathways to absolute freedom and economic dignity.” Obu said the programme would focus on three areas – prevention, empowerment, and rebuilding – stressing that on prevention, the partners plan to take anti-trafficking messages to schools and communities through fashion campaigns, digital advocacy and other awareness programmes. The campaign would also educate young people on online safety and the tactics used by traffickers to lure victims. The empowerment component would provide practical training in fashion design, textile production, leather making, garment care, and other vocational areas. Participants would also be exposed to digital skills, e-commerce, financial literacy, and branding. The organisers said the aim was to help young people develop productive skills and reduce their vulnerability to trafficking and exploitation. For survivors, internally displaced persons, and returnees, the programme will provide rehabilitation, mentorship, mental health, and psychosocial support. The campaign would also use the popularity of fashion, Nollywood, and sports to reach wider audiences. According to the organisers, emerging designers and trafficking survivors would be encouraged to produce and showcase fashion collections carrying messages of healing, freedom, and resilience. They said discussions were being pursued with major international fashion brands, including Gucci, Balenciaga, Versace, and Hugo Boss. The project also plans to work with Nollywood and international filmmakers to produce anti-trafficking films, documentaries, and awareness skits. Young people would equally be trained in film production as part of the programme. Sports would form another major part of the campaign, with the organisers seeking partnerships with international sporting bodies. The statement said discussions were being pursued with FIFA for football initiatives, International Handball Federation for handball tournaments, and NBA for athlete ambassadors and sports clinics. The organisers said NAPTIP would provide government leadership and technical support, while Next2None would drive implementation. The programme would also have a monitoring and evaluation system to measure its impact, including employment outcomes, survivor rehabilitation, and funds mobilised. Director-General of NAPTIP, Binta Lami Adamu Bello, and the agency’s Director of Legal and Prosecution, Mrs Ijeoma Amugo, were among those who signed the MoU on behalf of NAPTIP. Obu and Mr. Frank Nwala signed for Next2None Support Initiative. The partners are now expected to begin wider stakeholder mobilisation and international engagement ahead of the planned 2027 global launch. Calling for support for the campaign, the organisers urged corporate organisations, international development agencies, foundations, philanthropists, volunteers, brand ambassadors, and foreign missions to join the initiative. “Together, we can use creativity to educate, skills to empower, and partnerships to rebuild lives,” the organisers said.","url":"https://www.thisdaylive.com/2026/09/08/naptip-ngo-target-6000-nigerian-youths-annually-in-anti-trafficking-drive/","source":"ThisDay Live","image":"","published":"2026-09-08T02:20:56+00:00","category":"kidnapping","category_label":"Kidnapping & Abduction","region":"West Africa","country":"Nigeria","fatalities":0,"severity":"Moderate","severity_score":0},{"id":"e636953c4828","title":"We’ll turn Benue cassava to Nigeria’s crude oil – Expert","summary":"An expert highlights Benue cassava’s potential to transform Nigeria’s economy, create jobs, and ensure food security, calling it the nation’s new ‘crude oi Read More: https://punchng.com/well-turn-benue-cassava-to-nigerias-crude-oil-expert/","body":"Benue State is set to ensure that it’s comparative advantage of agricultural produce is made an alternative to crude oil to boost the economy of the state. The Managing Director and Chief Executive Officer of Green Harvest Stuffs, Prince Joshua Apeh stated this at the weekend during the Farmers Awareness and Mobilisation Programme and the flag-off of the Green Harvest Cooperative Society in Benue South Senatorial District organised by Green Harvest Store located in Otukpo, Benue State. Prince Apeh observed that the Benue South senatorial district is blessed with cassava products which could serve as Nigeria’s new “crude oil” to transform the nation’s economy and guarantee food security. Apeh said huge investment in cassava production and processing in Benue South would accelerate job creation and economic transformation. He noted that Idoma land which produces the highest volume of cassava in the world is ready to lead a new agro-industrial chapter for Nigeria, stressing that farmers, government and other stakeholders must take advantage of the region’s agricultural potential. According to Apeh, “Benue South had enormous cassava resources capable of supporting large-scale industries, but lamented that the absence of adequate processing facilities had prevented farmers from maximising the economic value of their produce. “Our cassava is our crude oil. Just as crude oil transformed the fortunes of nations, cassava can transform the fortunes of our people if we are willing to invest in it, process it, add value to it and take it to the global market. “This is a new dawn for the people of Idoma and for Nigeria. With the capacity we have here, we can end hunger in Nigeria and across West Africa, just as crude oil once changed the fortunes of this nation. “Green Harvest Food Stuffs is strategically positioned to sustain millions of Nigerians and align with the Renewed Hope Agenda of President Bola Ahmed Tinubu’s administration. “Our region could become a major carbohydrate and renewable-energy hub if its agricultural resources were properly harnessed ,” Apeh remarked extensively. He maintained that cassava could be processed into food products and industrial materials, while also providing raw materials for bioethanol and biofuel production, thereby creating jobs and reducing Nigeria’s dependence on imported fuel. According to him, the cooperative, was established to create a reliable platform through which farmers could access inputs, financing, mechanisation and guaranteed markets for their produce. “We want to create a new environment for farmers, where they have an already off-taking market. They don’t need to struggle to sell their products after harvesting. We also want to secure food security and reduce harvest losses,” he said. He disclosed that about 3,000 farmers had so far obtained registration forms to participate in the cooperative, with more expected to join following the mobilisation programme. Apeh said members would benefit from loan facilities, improved cassava stems and seedlings, fertilisers, farm equipment and tractor services, among other interventions designed to increase production. He Benue South was selected as the base of the initiative because of the region’s huge cassava production potential. “We cannot have the highest deposit of cassava in Benue South and have no processing factory to transform it and add value to it. That is why we are here,” he said. Counterfeit crisis: NAFDAC blames Chinese dealers amid backlash RMAFC woos Chinese investors for Nigeria’s oil sector Workers forum demand subsidy return, arrears payment Apeh disclosed that Green Harvest currently had the capacity to produce about 250,000 kilogrammes of garri monthly and 300,000 kilogrammes of flour, while plans were underway to venture into livestock feed production and establish a major bioethanol plant. He said the planned bioethanol facility would utilise the abundant cassava resources in the region to produce fuel that could support Nigeria’s downstream oil and gas sector. Speaking on the insecurity ravaging the country, which has made some farmers to abandon their farms, Apeh said Green Harvest was developing measures to improve the safety of farmers and their farmlands, including the establishment of a situation room to monitor farms, detect security threats and alert security agencies promptly to protect farmers and farm workers. He disclosed that the company had provided motorcycles and vehicles for youth involved in farm surveillance and was also planning to deploy drones and cameras linked to a central control centre for monitoring agricultural areas. “We are already setting up a platform that could tackle the issue. We are strengthening our youths to stand up and protect the farmlands. We are also bringing in drones that can do surveillance and putting cameras around the farms so that we can monitor what happens during any hour of the day,” he said. Apeh acknowledged that the company faced challenges, particularly the need for more resources and stronger partnerships with government, but said its commitment was driven by the agricultural potential of Benue South. “This is my heritage. This is what our ancestors are known for. Since this is our heritage and what we are known for, we have to do it,” he said. He called on the government to support the Green Harvest model and replicate similar agricultural value-chain initiatives across Nigeria, saying such interventions could strengthen rural economies, empower farmers, create employment and reduce dependence on food imports. He described cassava as Benue’s “crude oil”, saying the crop had the potential to transform the fortunes of farmers if properly processed, packaged and exported to the global market. According to him, Benue’s fertile land and abundant cassava production could position the state as a major carbohydrate hub capable of contributing significantly to food security in Nigeria and West Africa. The Managing Director of Green harvest food stuffs however, acknowledged the chairman of the company, Letthemsay Braboke Inaibagha, for making it possible for the company to achieve the milestone. He enjoined those with the needs of doing business with the company to contact the relevant personnel. A guest speaker at the occasion, Mr. Gideon Oche Emmanuel, commended Green Harvest Stuffs for the initiative, calling on the government and other stakeholders to emulate the company and setup similar cassava factory in other regions of the country. Some of the community leaders who spoke, including Agara Uwoke, Adakole Onyilokwu, and Baba Acheme, all commended Green Harvest Foodstuffs for the support extended to them. They described the initiative as an opportunity for Idoma people to “rewrite their story” and gain global recognition through cassava. Other beneficiaries of the intervention, included the Okpachu Ode, Comfort Okubi, and James Adaka who all commended Green Harvest, saying the initiative would help farmers secure better returns from their agricultural investments. They said access to markets, farm inputs and other forms of support would encourage more people to return to farming and expand production. John Charles\n\nNEWS UPDATE: Nigerians can now earn as much as $15,000- $25,000 with premium domains. You decide if you want payment in Naira or US Dollars. Be sure to ask for evidence and proof of people benefitting daily from this. CLICK HERE TO START.\n\nATTENTION NIGERIANS: You can now comfortably earn US Dollars when you acquire premium domains for as low as $3000 and profit $15,000-$35,000. Ideal for professionals like Entrepreneurs, Doctors, Civil/Public servants, IT Experts, Accountants ETC. CLICK HERE TO START.\n\nStay informed and ahead of the curve! Follow The Punch Newspaper on WhatsApp for real-time updates, breaking news, and exclusive content. Don't miss a headline – join now!\n\nStay in the know—fast. Get instant alerts, breaking headlines, and exclusive stories with the Punch News App . Download now and never miss a beat.","url":"https://punchng.com/well-turn-benue-cassava-to-nigerias-crude-oil-expert/?utm_source=rss.punchng.com&utm_medium=web","source":"Punch Nigeria","image":"https://cdn.punchng.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/08/18131509/punch-logo-500x179-1.png","published":"2026-09-08T01:45:50+00:00","category":"other","category_label":"Other Security News","region":"West Africa","country":"Nigeria","fatalities":0,"severity":"Moderate","severity_score":0},{"id":"27092415ae0a","title":"Six-month refuse heap persists on Lagos road despite earlier evacuation","summary":"A six-month-old refuse heap on Lagos’ Idimu-Ejigbo Road median continues to cause offensive odours and health concerns for residents despite earlier evacua Read More: https://punchng.com/six-month-refuse-heap-persists-on-lagos-road-despite-earlier-evacuation/","body":"Residents and business owners along the Idimu-Ejigbo Road in Lagos State have raised the alarm over a six-month-old refuse heap on the road median, saying the decomposing waste has subjected them to offensive odours and environmental hazards. The refuse, located around the Dorcas and Silva Estate end of the road, has reportedly remained unattended despite the presence of waste management operators in the area and an earlier evacuation following a PUNCH Metro report. Our correspondent observed on Monday that the waste occupied about 150 metres of the road median, with different types of refuse piled along the stretch. Some of the waste had spilled onto portions of the road, while the prolonged accumulation had become a source of concern to residents, motorists, pedestrians and business owners. A shop owner at Dorcas Bus Stop, Fatai Adegbenro, said the situation had become unbearable, particularly during hot weather when the odour from the decomposing waste became more pronounced. He said, “This refuse has been here for about six months. It has become a serious problem for us because of the smell. Whenever you pass this place, especially in the afternoon, the odour is unbearable. “Both LAWMA and PSP operators collect refuse from residents, so we do not understand why this heap has been left here for this long. The road is being defaced, and nobody appears to be taking responsibility for clearing it.” Another resident, who identified himself only as Philip, described the situation as embarrassing, noting that the road was heavily used by motorists and pedestrians. “This is a major road, and people use it every day. Imagine seeing this kind of heap of refuse on the median for months. It is embarrassing, and the smell is terrible,” he said. The development is coming weeks after PUNCH Metro reported that residents of the area had complained about refuse heaps that had remained on road medians linking Ejigbo and Idimu for about four months. Following the report, the waste was evacuated from parts of the affected stretch. Lagos family petitions IG, accuses police of shielding alleged land grabbers Four arrested for killing Lagos man weeks before wedding LAWMA intensifies waste collection, faults old videos However, residents said the latest evacuation did not cover the entire area, leaving the refuse heap around the Dorcas and Silva Estate end of the road untouched. A trader in the community, who spoke on condition of anonymity, said, “They only cleared the ones at Idimu Bus Stop towards OPC Junction and never came to this part of the road. “So, it was assumed they cleared the whole refuse, but they never did.” Another shop owner, Anjarat Adewale, said the situation was affecting businesses around the area, particularly food vendors. She said, “Customers are reluctant to stay around, let alone buy food. This is due to the offensive odour. “The food vendor that was here before has gone elsewhere. She cannot continue to run at a loss because LAWMA failed to do their job. “We are appealing to them to come and clear it and ensure that it does not return. The PSP operators should also do what they are being paid to do.” Residents appealed to the Lagos Waste Management Authority to urgently evacuate the refuse and prevent further accumulation, warning that the prolonged presence of the waste could pose health and safety risks. The residents also called for closer monitoring of waste collection and evacuation activities along the road to prevent refuse cleared from one section from remaining in another. The persistence of refuse heaps in parts of Lagos has continued to generate complaints from residents, with waste sometimes accumulating on road medians, streets and major highways despite interventions by the state government and LAWMA. Efforts to obtain the reaction of the LAWMA Managing Director, Muyiwa Gbadegesin, were unsuccessful on Monday, as calls and WhatsApp messages sent to him were not responded to as of the time of filing this report. Dayo Oyewo Dayo is a journalist at Punch Newspapers with over nine years of experience covering politics, entertainment, crime, and environmental conservation. His reporting blends advocacy and accountability journalism, focusing on stories that inspire awareness and social change. Dayo’s work reflects practical newsroom experience and a strong commitment to public-interest reporting.\n\nNEWS UPDATE: Nigerians can now earn as much as $15,000- $25,000 with premium domains. You decide if you want payment in Naira or US Dollars. Be sure to ask for evidence and proof of people benefitting daily from this. CLICK HERE TO START.\n\nATTENTION NIGERIANS: You can now comfortably earn US Dollars when you acquire premium domains for as low as $3000 and profit $15,000-$35,000. Ideal for professionals like Entrepreneurs, Doctors, Civil/Public servants, IT Experts, Accountants ETC. CLICK HERE TO START.\n\nStay informed and ahead of the curve! Follow The Punch Newspaper on WhatsApp for real-time updates, breaking news, and exclusive content. Don't miss a headline – join now!\n\nStay in the know—fast. Get instant alerts, breaking headlines, and exclusive stories with the Punch News App . Download now and never miss a beat.","url":"https://punchng.com/six-month-refuse-heap-persists-on-lagos-road-despite-earlier-evacuation/?utm_source=rss.punchng.com&utm_medium=web","source":"Punch Nigeria","image":"https://cdn.punchng.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/08/18131509/punch-logo-500x179-1.png","published":"2026-09-08T01:32:15+00:00","category":"conflict","category_label":"Armed Conflict","region":"West Africa","country":"Nigeria","fatalities":0,"severity":"Moderate","severity_score":0},{"id":"7dcf50fd4b5b","title":"Tournée gouvernementale dans le Guiriko : Le Ministre d’État, le Général de Division Célestin SIMPORE, en contact des forces combattantes et des populations de Boromo","summary":"Dans le cadre de la tournée gouvernementale dans la région du Guiriko, le Camarade Ministre d’État, Ministre de la Guerre et de la Défense patriotique, le Général de Division Célestin SIMPORE, a effectué, ce lundi 7 septembre 2026, une premiere étape dans la ville garnison de Boromo. La délégation ministérielle a été accueillie au Haut-Commissariat … The post Tournée gouvernementale dans le Guirik","body":"Dans le cadre de la tournée gouvernementale dans la région du Guiriko, le Camarade Ministre d’État, Ministre de la Guerre et de la Défense patriotique, le Général de Division Célestin SIMPORE, a effectué, ce lundi 7 septembre 2026, une premiere étape dans la ville garnison de Boromo.\n\nLa délégation ministérielle a été accueillie au Haut-Commissariat par le Camarade BAKO Martin, Haut-Commissaire, en présence des autorités administratives, militaires et paramilitaires. Cette étape a également été marquée par des échanges avec les autorités coutumières et religieuses de la localité.\n\nSa Majesté Naaba Tegawendé, chef de canton de Boromo, a adressé ses remerciements au Camarade Ministre d’État et à sa délégation. Il a formulé des bénédictions pour la réussite de la mission des Forces combattantes et la victoire dans la lutte contre le terrorisme, rappelant son engagement aux côtés de la Nation en tant que premier Volontaire pour la Défense de la Patrie (VDP) de la zone.\n\nLe Général de Division Célestin SIMPORE a indiqué que cette halte à Boromo s’inscrit dans une démarche de proximité avec les populations, pour les saluer et leur exprimer la reconnaissance du Gouvernement pour leur accompagnement multiforme. Il a réitéré sa gratitude aux filles et fils de Boromo et des localités environnantes pour leur soutien et les a invités à poursuivre leurs prières et leur accompagnement en faveur de la reconquête du territoire.\n\nÉvoquant la Révolution Populaire et Progressiste (RPP), le Camarade Ministre d’État a souligné que celle-ci ne se limite pas au domaine sécuritaire. Elle vise également la restauration de l’intégrité et de la souveraineté nationale dans plusieurs secteurs, notamment l’agriculture, les infrastructures et la santé. Des invocations, prières et bénédictions ont ensuite été formulées pour la mission de la délégation ainsi que pour l’ensemble des Forces combattantes engagées dans la lutte contre le terrorisme.\n\nLa délégation s’est ensuite rendue au détachement militaire de Boromo, notamment auprès de la Deuxième Brigade d’intervention aéroportée. Le chef de corps, le Lieutenant Boli Abdoul Karim, a présenté un point de situation très acceptable sur l’évolution du contexte sécuritaire dans la zone.\n\nLe Général de Division Célestin SIMPORÉ a également rencontré une représentation des différentes forces engagées dans la sécurisation de Boromo et de ses environs : Gendarmerie nationale, Police nationale, Police municipale, Eaux et Forêts, Garde de sécurité pénitentiaire, Groupement de commandos parachutistes, Brigade nationale de sapeurs-pompiers et Volontaires pour la Défense de la Patrie.\n\nS’adressant à la troupe, le Général de Division Célestin SIMPORE a indiqué être porteur du message du Président du Faso, Chef de l’État et Chef suprême des Armées, le Camarade Capitaine Ibrahim TRAORÉ et de l’ensemble du Gouvernement. Il a adressé aux hommes et aux femmes engagés sur le théâtre des opérations et dans les missions de sécurisation du territoire les salutations, les félicitations et les encouragements du Gouvernement pour les efforts consentis.\n\nSelon lui, leurs efforts contribuent à la stabilisation progressive de la situation sécuritaire, à la restauration de la libre circulation entre les villes et à la reprise des activités socioéconomiques, notamment celles des agriculteurs.\n\nLe ministre d’État a exhorté les Forces combattantes à maintenir leur résilience et à redoubler de vigilance afin de consolider les acquis. Il les a également appelées à intégrer l’esprit de la RPP dans leurs comportements quotidiens, au service de la Nation, de leurs familles et de la communauté. « Cultiver les qualités de bons révolutionnaires », a-t-il recommandé, invitant chacun à apporter sa contribution à l’effort national, quelle que soit sa position.\n\n« Chacun joue son rôle et tout va fonctionner parfaitement », a-t-il martelé, insistant sur la complémentarité des différentes forces engagées sur le terrain. Si chaque corps dispose de missions spécifiques, leur efficacité repose, a-t-il rappelé, sur la solidarité, la cohésion et la complémentarité.\n\nLe ministre d’État a enfin adressé des félicitations particulières aux VDP pour les sacrifices consentis aux côtés des autres composantes des Forces combattantes.\n\nCette étape de Boromo aura ainsi permis au Camarade Ministre d’État de réaffirmer, au nom du Gouvernement, la reconnaissance de la Nation envers les Forces combattantes et les populations, tout en appelant à maintenir la mobilisation collective pour la restauration de l’intégrité du territoire et la consolidation de la souveraineté nationale.","url":"https://burkina24.com/2026/09/07/tournee-gouvernementale-dans-le-guiriko-le-ministre-detat-le-general-de-division-celestin-simpore-en-contact-des-forces-combattantes-et-des-populations-de-boromo/","source":"Burkina 24","image":"","published":"2026-09-07T22:47:45+00:00","category":"conflict","category_label":"Armed Conflict","region":"Sahel","country":"Burkina Faso","fatalities":0,"severity":"Moderate","severity_score":0},{"id":"724feab1079d","title":"Abuja Building Collapses Hours After FCTA Seal, Rescue Operation Underway","summary":"Men of the Federal Capital Territory Fire Service are at the scene conducting rescue operations, while three ambulances have also been stationed at the location. The post Abuja Building Collapses Hours After FCTA Seal, Rescue Operation Underway appeared first on Channels Television .","body":"A building has collapsed in Wuse Zone 4, Abuja, with rescue operations currently underway at the scene. Men of the FCT Fire Service are at the scene conducting rescue operations, while three ambulances have also been stationed at the location. The FCT Police Command said it received a distress call about the building collapse at approximately 7:45 p.m. on Monday, September 7, 2026. A police response team led by the Assistant Commissioner of Police, Department of Operations, ACP Edward Akaniyene, and the Area Commander, Metro, ACP Georgelyn Ufomadu, promptly responded to the scene. Personnel from the National Emergency Management Agency (NEMA), Federal Road Safety Corps (FRSC), Federal Roads Maintenance Agency (FERMA), Federal Fire Service (FFS), Security Service Department, Federal Capital Territory Administration (FCTA) and other relevant emergency response agencies also joined the rescue and search operations. The FCTA Development Control Department had earlier sealed the building and directed its occupants to vacate the premises. The police said preliminary investigations revealed that the building had earlier been marked and sealed by the Department of Development Control, Abuja. So far, no victims have been confirmed, according to the police, although search and rescue operations are still ongoing to identify and rescue any persons who may be trapped in the rubble. The police said investigation was also ongoing to determine the circumstances and possible cause of the building collapse. The Command urged residents to remain calm and security-conscious, avoid the affected area and cooperate with emergency responders to facilitate the ongoing rescue operations. Members of the public were also advised to promptly report any suspicious activity or emergency to the Command through the emergency lines: 08032003913 and 08061581938. See more photos from the building collapse below: The post Abuja Building Collapses Hours After FCTA Seal, Rescue Operation Underway appeared first on Channels Television .","url":"https://www.channelstv.com/2026/09/07/breaking-building-collapses-in-abuja-rescue-operation-underway/","source":"Channels TV","image":"https://www.channelstv.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/WhatsApp-Image-2026-09-07-at-21.43.10-1-150x150.jpeg","published":"2026-09-07T20:59:06+00:00","category":"other","category_label":"Other Security News","region":"West Africa","country":"Nigeria","fatalities":0,"severity":"Moderate","severity_score":0},{"id":"4bac39155289","title":"Foreign support for RSF major reason Sudan war persists: Sudan’s prosecutor general","summary":"Intisar Ahmed Abdelaal calls for ‘decisive measures’ to halt external support for paramilitary group","body":"External support for the paramilitary Rapid Support Forces (RSF) is a major reason for the prolonged war and continued violations in Sudan, the country’s prosecutor general said Monday, calling for decisive action to halt it.\n\nIntisar Ahmed Abdelaal made the remarks during a briefing to the UN Human Rights Council’s interactive dialogue on Sudan as part of its 63rd session in Geneva, according to the Sudanese News Agency.\n\nAbdelaal also chairs Sudan’s National Committee for Investigating Crimes and Violations of National and International Law.\n\n“External support for the militia represents a major reason for the prolongation of the war and the continuation of violations,” she said, referring to the RSF.\n\nShe urged the council to condemn the support and take “decisive measures” to stop it.\n\nAbdelaal renewed her call for the Human Rights Council and other UN mechanisms to support the national committee, uphold the principle of complementarity, and take immediate steps to end foreign support for the RSF.\n\nShe also reaffirmed Sudan’s rejection of external mechanisms that bypass national efforts and reiterated its opposition to the resolution establishing the Independent International Fact-Finding Mission for Sudan.\n\nOn Thursday, the UN fact-finding mission said external support networks are helping fuel the war in Sudan.\n\nThe Sudanese army and the RSF have been fighting since April 2023 in a conflict that has killed tens of thousands of people and displaced around 13 million, according to UN and international estimates.","url":"https://www.aa.com.tr/en/africa/foreign-support-for-rsf-major-reason-sudan-war-persists-sudan-s-prosecutor-general/4050152","source":"Anadolu · Africa","image":"","published":"2026-09-07T20:49:54+00:00","category":"conflict","category_label":"Armed Conflict","region":"Horn of Africa","country":"Sudan","fatalities":0,"severity":"Moderate","severity_score":0},{"id":"4fead2685109","title":"El-Rufai’s Lawyers Demand ₦10bn From Defence Minister Over Alleged Defamation","summary":"The lawyers maintained that none of the allegations had been tested, proved or determined by a court of competent jurisdiction and demanded strict proof of the claims. The post El-Rufai’s Lawyers Demand ₦10bn From Defence Minister Over Alleged Defamation appeared first on Channels Television .","body":"Lawyers to former Kaduna State Governor, Nasir El-Rufai, have demanded ₦10 billion in damages from the Minister of Defence, General Christopher Musa (retd.), over alleged defamatory remarks concerning their client. The demand was contained in a letter before action dated September 7, 2026, and addressed to the Defence Minister at the Federal Ministry of Defence in Abuja. The legal action followed Musa’s appearance on Channels Television’s Politics Today on September 3, 2026, where he spoke about insecurity in Kaduna State and made allegations concerning the former governor. According to the letter, the minister allegedly accused El-Rufai of deliberately planning the killing of people in Southern Kaduna and paying bandits. The lawyers also alleged that Musa claimed El-Rufai ordered the demolition of houses and used such actions against his opponents. El-Rufai’s legal team, Akpan Ubong Chambers, described the statements as false and defamatory, arguing that they portrayed the former governor as a criminal, murderer and sponsor of banditry. The lawyers demanded a public retraction and apology from the Defence Minister, alongside N10 billion in compensation for the alleged damage to their client’s reputation. They also denied the allegation that El-Rufai paid bandits, stating that the former governor had consistently maintained that he would neither negotiate with nor pay bandits. On the issue of demolitions, the lawyers said those carried out during El-Rufai’s tenure as governor were undertaken in accordance with the law and for public purposes. They further rejected claims that the former governor’s administration divided Kaduna State along ethnic and religious lines, saying the government implemented policies aimed at promoting unity and development across the state. The lawyers maintained that none of the allegations had been tested, proved or determined by a court of competent jurisdiction and demanded strict proof of the claims. Meanwhile, the family of the former governor has separately demanded the immediate retraction of the allegation that El-Rufai actively planned killings in Southern Kaduna. In a statement issued on Monday and signed by Honourable Mohammed Bello El-Rufai on behalf of the family, the allegation was described as grave and unsubstantiated. The family said the claim was made without any evidence being presented to support it. It said while General Musa was entitled to express his personal opinions, he should also be prepared to substantiate such a serious allegation with credible evidence. The family gave the minister seven days from the date of the statement to either provide evidence supporting the allegation or issue a full public retraction and apology through the same medium. It warned that failure to do so would leave it with no option but to pursue all appropriate legal remedies available under the law. The post El-Rufai’s Lawyers Demand ₦10bn From Defence Minister Over Alleged Defamation appeared first on Channels Television .","url":"https://www.channelstv.com/2026/09/07/el-rufais-lawyers-demand-%e2%82%a610bn-from-defence-minister-over-alleged-defamation/","source":"Channels TV","image":"","published":"2026-09-07T20:13:11+00:00","category":"kidnapping","category_label":"Kidnapping & Abduction","region":"West Africa","country":"Nigeria","fatalities":0,"severity":"Moderate","severity_score":0},{"id":"de421adff31a","title":"Six Abducted Children Die In Captivity, 17 Rescued As Police Parade Suspects In Oyo","summary":"This followed an intelligence-led investigation into a criminal network allegedly involved in the stealing, trafficking and concealment of children across state lines. The post Six Abducted Children Die In Captivity, 17 Rescued As Police Parade Suspects In Oyo appeared first on Channels Television .","body":"Six children abducted by a suspected child-stealing and human trafficking syndicate have been confirmed dead while in captivity, the Oyo State Police Command has disclosed. The command also announced the rescue of 17 children and the arrest of seven suspects following an intelligence-led investigation into a criminal network allegedly involved in the stealing, trafficking and concealment of children across state lines. The Oyo State Commissioner of Police, CP Abimbola Olugbenga, disclosed this at a press briefing held at the Police State Headquarters, Eleyele, Ibadan on Monday. According to him, the investigation began with a petition dated July 29, 2026, submitted by Adejoke Popoola, also known as Orente Alagbo, of Sogade Area, Ariyo, Ibadan, over allegations bordering on conspiracy, cyberbullying, threat to life and defamation of character. The petition was subsequently assigned to the Anti-Kidnapping Squad on August 3. Olugbenga said that while detectives were examining the circumstances surrounding the petition, their attention was drawn to the disappearance of a three-year-old boy, Ifakunle Ifagbenro, during an annual masquerade festival in Oyo Town on June 29. The commissioner said the child had accompanied his father to his father-in-law’s family compound for the festival, while Popoola had arrived two days earlier to participate in preparations for the event. He said Popoola offered to carry the child during the event, after which both of them disappeared from the venue. The matter was subsequently reported at the Atiba Police Divisional Headquarters, Oyo Town. According to the police chief, pictorial and video materials from the event showed Popoola carrying the missing child, while digital trails and statements obtained from persons connected to the event further established that she was with the child around the time of his disappearance. Popoola was arrested on August 4 and, according to the commissioner, admitted attending the masquerade festival and subsequently leaving with the missing child. Her statement, Olugbenga said, provided the first major breakthrough into what investigators discovered to be a wider criminal network. The suspect disclosed that she had been involved in the alleged stealing of more than 15 children, aged between two and seven years, from churches, parties and other event venues within and outside Oyo State. She also reportedly identified one Adekanbi Janet, also known as Hosanna, as her partner in the alleged criminal enterprise. Adekanbi was subsequently traced to Mowe, Ogun State, where police rescued four children aged between two and four years from custody and recovered two pregnant young women. The commissioner said Adekanbi admitted knowing Popoola for about 10 years in connection with the alleged stealing and sale of children, although she denied having custody of Ifakunle. According to him, Adekanbi further disclosed that some of the stolen children were kept in custody pending their sale, while the pregnant women were allegedly cared for until delivery. Information obtained from her subsequently led detectives to the location of Ifakunle, who was rescued around the Gbagi area of Ibadan on August 20. The investigation later extended beyond Oyo State. Olugbenga said detectives travelled to Agbor, Delta State, on September 2, where four children allegedly stolen from Oyo State were rescued from their suspected buyers. Two suspects, identified as 48-year-old Christopher Joy and 53-year-old Rosemary Jesse, were arrested and allegedly confessed to involvement in the business. The development, he said, confirmed the interstate dimension of the criminal network and showed that children were being moved outside Oyo State as part of the alleged criminal enterprise. The commissioner further disclosed that the investigation uncovered an alleged ritual dimension to the activities of the syndicate. According to him, one of the suspects confessed that whenever a child died while in their custody, the body would allegedly be sold to ritualists. The disclosure reportedly led to the arrest of Muritala Fasasi, an Alfa, and Yakubu Azeez, a herbalist, who the police said had confessed to their alleged involvement in using the bodies for ritual purposes. The development came against the backdrop of a chilling testimony attributed to one of the principal suspects, who allegedly told investigators that she had stolen 13 children before her arrest. In the testimony, the suspect, nicknamed Orente, reportedly said she would visit homes and, whenever she found nobody around, take a child. Asked how much the children were sold for, she mentioned amounts ranging from N500,000 to N600,000. She also claimed to have operated in the business since October 2024. The suspect allegedly described how children could be taken from places where traditional or spiritual activities were being held, including masquerade festivals, by gaining the confidence of parents. In one account, she reportedly said she would tell parents of children with disabilities that she could help heal them before taking the children away. The six deceased children were identified as Musharaf Lawal Tajudeen, two; Greatness Adeagbo, five; Sainat Amoo, two; Taiwo Usman, two; Daniel Solomon Olayinka, seven; and Miracle Olaniyi, four. The command said the 17 rescued children are Ifakunle Ifagbenro, three; Musibau Olasupo, five; Sulaimon Osamo, seven; Murina Fidaus, three; Aminat Yusuf, three; Taiwo Olamilekan, three; an infant male aged nine days; Rafiat Waheed Babalola, two; Quwiyat Alade, five; Rejoice Divine, three; Alhaji Divine, five; Eniolorunda, three; Victoria, three; Isoma Prince, three; Roqeeba, four; Boluwatife Odedeyi, three; and Ireoluwa Elizabeth Olapade, seven. The commissioner appealed to parents and guardians who had lost their children in recent times to visit the Police Command Headquarters, Eleyele, Ibadan, to identify the rescued children and facilitate their reunification with their families. He said the command was working with other security and government agencies to uncover the full structure of the alleged network, locate other missing children, establish the circumstances surrounding the reported deaths and ensure that anyone found culpable was brought to justice. Olugbenga urged members of the public to remain vigilant and report cases of missing children to the police without delay. He particularly cautioned parents and guardians to exercise care when dealing with individuals who present themselves as traditional or spiritual healers, especially where such persons seek to separate children from their families or take them to undisclosed locations. The commissioner also appealed to members of the public with credible information that could assist the investigation to contact the police, assuring that such information would be handled professionally and, where necessary, confidentially. He reaffirmed the command’s commitment to protecting children and other vulnerable persons, dismantling criminal networks and ensuring that justice was served in accordance with the law. The post Six Abducted Children Die In Captivity, 17 Rescued As Police Parade Suspects In Oyo appeared first on Channels Television .","url":"https://www.channelstv.com/2026/09/07/six-abducted-children-die-in-captivity-17-rescued-as-police-parade-suspects-in-oyo/","source":"Channels TV","image":"","published":"2026-09-07T18:37:06+00:00","category":"kidnapping","category_label":"Kidnapping & Abduction","region":"West Africa","country":"Nigeria","fatalities":0,"severity":"Moderate","severity_score":0},{"id":"35f68b1c6e76","title":"Sécurité : Un jeune entrepreneur fait don de lampes solaires au ministre de la sécurité","summary":"Le Ministre de la Sécurité, le Camarade Mahamadou SANA, a reçu en audience, ce lundi 7 septembre 2026, un jeune entrepreneur KINDO BUSINESS SERVICES (KBS). « Je suis venu exprimer ma gratitude au Ministre pour les multiples initiatives mises en œuvre en faveur de notre sécurité », a déclaré le jeune entrepreneur à l’entame de … The post Sécurité : Un jeune entrepreneur fait don de lampes solaires ","body":"Le Ministre de la Sécurité, le Camarade Mahamadou SANA, a reçu en audience, ce lundi 7 septembre 2026, un jeune entrepreneur KINDO BUSINESS SERVICES (KBS). « Je suis venu exprimer ma gratitude au Ministre pour les multiples initiatives mises en œuvre en faveur de notre sécurité » , a déclaré le jeune entrepreneur à l’entame de l’audience.\n\nAu cours de cette audience, le jeune entrepreneur a expliqué qu’à l’occasion de ses voyages d’affaires, il a découvert des lampes lumineuses solaires à haute performance, particulièrement adaptées aux besoins des forces de sécurité, notamment pour les missions de surveillance, de contrôle et d’intervention dans les zones insuffisamment éclairées.\n\n« Convaincu de leur utilité pour améliorer les conditions de travail des policiers, j’ai décidé d’en acquérir un lot au profit du ministère » , a-t-il expliqué.\n\nSon don illustre la conviction selon laquelle chaque initiative, aussi modeste soit-elle, constitue une pierre apportée à l’édification endogène de notre Nation. Ce geste citoyen s’inscrit pleinement dans la dynamique de la Révolution Progressiste Populaire (RPP), qui appelle chaque Burkinabè à apporter sa contribution à l’effort national de construction d’un Burkina Faso souverain, résilient et prospère.\n\nLe Camarade Ministre de la Sécurité a salué cette initiative patriotique et exprimé sa reconnaissance au donateur pour son engagement en faveur de la sécurité nationale. Il a souligné que la consolidation de la sécurité est une responsabilité partagée qui requiert l’implication de tous les fils et filles du Burkina Faso.","url":"https://burkina24.com/2026/09/07/securite-un-jeune-entrepreneur-fait-don-de-lampes-solaires-au-ministre-de-la-securite/","source":"Burkina 24","image":"","published":"2026-09-07T18:14:16+00:00","category":"other","category_label":"Other Security News","region":"Sahel","country":"Burkina Faso","fatalities":0,"severity":"Moderate","severity_score":0},{"id":"8b06482d9c92","title":"MRC : la diaspora Europe réaffirme son soutien sans faille à Maurice Kamto","summary":"© DR Dans une déclaration datée du 6 septembre 2026, les secrétaires départementaux MRC de la Région Europe apportent un soutien \"éclairé et lucide\" à leur président national élu, Maurice Kamto, au directoire et aux résolutions du Conseil national du 6 septembre 2025. A un peu plus d'un an après l'exclusion de Maurice Kamto de la course à la présidentielle du 12 octobre 2025, la fédération Europe ","body":"© DR Dans une déclaration datée du 6 septembre 2026, les secrétaires départementaux MRC de la Région Europe apportent un soutien \"éclairé et lucide\" à leur président national élu, Maurice Kamto, au directoire et aux résolutions du Conseil national du 6 septembre 2025. A un peu plus d'un an après l'exclusion de Maurice Kamto de la course à la présidentielle du 12 octobre 2025, la fédération Europe du Mouvement pour la Renaissance du Cameroun (MRC) sort du silence. Dans un texte de deux pages signé collectivement, les responsables départementaux dénoncent ce qu'ils qualifient d'acharnement politico-judiciaire contre leur leader et contre le parti. \"Notre parti dont vous tenez courageusement et avec brio le flambeau depuis la création, fait l'objet depuis toujours d'actes de déstabilisation de la part du pouvoir en place et de ses proxys\", écrivent-ils à l'adresse de Maurice Kamto. L'exclusion de 2025 au cœur de la déclaration Le communiqué revient longuement sur l'invalidation de la candidature de Maurice Kamto à la présidentielle de 2025, officialisée le 5 août 2025 par le Conseil constitutionnel après un rejet initial par ELECAM pour \"pluralité d'investitures\" au sein du MANIDEM. Pour les signataires, cette décision est le fruit \"d'un acte de cybercriminalité du MINAT\" et d'un \"complot d'Etat\" contre le MRC. Ils citent notamment deux correspondances du ministère de l'Administration territoriale adressées à M. Thierry Okala Ebode, présenté comme un ancien militant exclu pour trahison, et au secrétaire général du parti. Une contre-attaque contre le RDPC Le texte opère un renversement d'accusation. Les secrétaires Europe rappellent que depuis juillet 2025, un militant du RDPC a officiellement saisi le MINAT, Paul Atanga Nji, de l'illégalité de toutes les instances dirigeantes de son propre parti, dont le dernier Congrès remonte à septembre 2011. Une saisine portée ensuite devant le Tribunal de grande instance du Mfoundi pour faire juger \"nulles et de nul effet\" les résolutions du Bureau politique du 3 novembre 2016. \"Curieusement, ni le MINAT, militant du RDPC, ni le président du tribunal [...] n'a jugé utile de donner suite à sa saisine\", souligne la déclaration. Et d'interroger : \"Pourquoi donc tant d'agitation du RDPC et de ses prolongements étatiques contre le MRC et son Président National Elu, que vous êtes, alors que, sans avoir, enfin, organisé son Congrès [...] toutes les candidatures du RDPC vont logiquement être rejetées ?\" Cap sur les législatives et municipales Au-delà de la dénonciation, le message est un appel à la mobilisation. Les responsables Europe disent faire \"totalement confiance à l'intelligence des membres du directoire’’ et à leur président ‘’pour déjouer la nouvelle forfaiture du pouvoir en cours\". Ils invitent Maurice Kamto et le directoire à \"rester concentrés sur l'essentiel, la préparation victorieuse des élections législatives et municipales à venir\" et appellent \"tous les dynamiques et courageux militants à persister, avec vous et contre vents et marées\". Une déclaration qui se conclut sur une profession de foi en \"la vision humaniste, moderniste, développée, fondée sur le mérite, la solidarité, l'état de droit, la justice du MRC\", portée selon eux par un leader \"aimé par les Camerounais’’. Cet article MRC : la diaspora Europe réaffirme son soutien sans faille à Maurice Kamto est apparu en premier sur Journalducameroun.com .","url":"https://fr.journalducameroun.com/mrc-la-diaspora-europe-reaffirme-son-soutien-sans-faille-a-maurice-kamto/","source":"Journal du Cameroun","image":"","published":"2026-09-07T15:32:43+00:00","category":"conflict","category_label":"Armed Conflict","region":"Central Africa","country":"Cameroon","fatalities":0,"severity":"Moderate","severity_score":0},{"id":"26f413850a78","title":"Sierra Leone ex-president Koroma returns after treason case dropped","summary":"Former Sierra Leone president Ernest Bai Koroma returned to his home country Sunday, where authorities welcomed him after treason charges against him over an alleged coup attempt were dropped","body":"Former Sierra Leone president Ernest Bai Koroma returned to his home country Sunday, where authorities welcomed him after treason charges against him over an alleged coup attempt were dropped.\n\nKoroma had left for Nigeria in 2024, days after being charged with treason and other offences over a deadly 2023 army uprising.\n\nThe ex-president, who led Sierra Leone from 2007 to 2018, arrived at the international airport in the capital, Freetown, accompanied by Nigerian ex-president Olusegun Obasanjo and Nigerian Foreign Minister Bianca Odumegwu-Ojukwu, an AFP journalist said.\n\nHe was welcomed by Sierra Leone's Foreign Minister Mohamed Rahman Swarray and officials from Koroma's party, the opposition All People's Congress (APC).\n\n\"Today I have come back home and I'm happy,\" Koroma told journalists, smiling.\n\nKoroma had been cleared by Sierra Leonean authorities to travel to Nigeria for three months for medical treatment, but ended up staying more than two years.\n\nIn July, Sierra Leone's justice ministry said it had dropped the charges against him, without giving a reason.\n\nOn Friday, Vice President Mohamed Jukdeh Jalloh told a gathering of local leaders and residents in Koroma's stronghold, the central city of Makeni, that the ex-leader was returning home.\n\nHis homecoming comes ahead of presidential elections set for 2028.\n\nThe case against Koroma dated to November 2023, when armed attackers stormed a military armoury, two barracks, two prisons and two police stations, clashing with security forces.\n\nTwenty-one people were killed in what authorities deemed a coup attempt.\n\nTwo dozen soldiers and Koroma's former bodyguard were sentenced to prison terms ranging from 50 to 100 years by a military court for their alleged roles in the mutiny.\n\nKoroma had briefly visited Sierra Leone in July, after the charges were dropped, during a summit of the Economic Community of West African States (ECOWAS) at the invitation of current President Julius Maada Bio.","url":"http://www.africanews.com/2026/09/07/sierra-leone-ex-president-koroma-returns-after-treason-case-dropped/","source":"Africanews","image":"http://images.euronews.com/articles/stories/09/90/30/78/640x360_cmsv2_716ba81f-e5f6-545e-8c9e-5f31817099c8-9903078.jpg?1788693070","published":"2026-09-07T14:23:57+00:00","category":"conflict","category_label":"Armed Conflict","region":"West Africa","country":"Sierra Leone","fatalities":0,"severity":"Moderate","severity_score":0},{"id":"4bf2fde90676","title":"Human rights chief Türk renews call for end to Sudan war","summary":"United Nations human rights chief Volker Türk on Monday renewed calls for an immediate ceasefire The post Human rights chief Türk renews call for end to Sudan war appeared first on Radio Tamazuj .","body":"United Nations human rights chief Volker Türk on Monday renewed calls for an immediate ceasefire in Sudan, warning that civilians remained at imminent risk as fighting between the army and paramilitary forces intensified in several regions. The war between Sudan’s Armed Forces (SAF) and the Rapid Support Forces (RSF), which began in April 2023, has triggered one of the world’s largest humanitarian crises. More than 6 million people have been displaced within Sudan and about 4.5 million have fled to neighbouring countries, according to the United Nations. Addressing the U.N. Human Rights Council in Geneva, Türk called for an end to attacks on civilians and civilian infrastructure, including markets, hospitals and fuel stations. “There needs to be an immediate ceasefire, an end to foreign involvement, and a return to inclusive civilian rule,” Türk said. He warns that the situation is deteriorating in Kordofan, Blue Nile and North and West Darfur, where civilians face what he described as an “imminent” risk. Türk’s comments came as an independent U.N.-mandated fact-finding mission presented a report on human rights violations in the conflict. Protecting human rights means holding a mirror up to those in power, challenging the status quo, and pushing the boundaries. My update to the Human Rights Council: https://t.co/vWxeXKiDH8 pic.twitter.com/anirIkKhDM — Volker Türk (@volker_turk) September 7, 2026 Drone attacks The mission said both the SAF and RSF are increasingly using long-range drones, allowing attacks to reach areas far beyond traditional front lines. Mohamed Othman, chair of the U.N. Human Rights Council’s Fact-Finding Mission for Sudan, said drone strikes had killed and injured civilians in homes, schools, hospitals, markets and displacement sites, as well as during weddings, funerals and other gatherings. The mission also alleged significant support for the RSF from individuals and entities in the United Arab Emirates, Chad, southeastern Libya and Puntland in Somalia. The UAE has repeatedly denied allegations that it provides military support to the RSF. Othman said repeated RSF drone attacks on El Obeid, the capital of North Kordofan, had affected water supplies, energy and other essential civilian infrastructure. The city is under partial siege by the RSF. The report said the SAF and RSF had both carried out attacks that harmed civilians. It cited successive RSF drone strikes on a kindergarten and hospital in Kalogie, South Kordofan, that killed 114 people, including 60 children. It also said SAF attacks on Ad-Da’ein Teaching Hospital killed at least 70 people. “Based on the evidence gathered, we found reasonable grounds to believe that both the SAF and the RSF committed serious violations of human rights and international humanitarian law which may amount to war crimes,” Othman told the council. Hospitals devastated The World Health Organization said separately on Monday that three major hospitals in Sudan’s capital, Khartoum Teaching Hospital, the Ophthalmology Hospital and the Dental Hospital, had suffered extensive damage during the conflict. Before the war, the facilities served as major referral and teaching hospitals, the WHO said. “Operating theatres have been destroyed, imaging equipment lost and several wards have collapsed,” said Abdinasir Abubakar, the WHO representative in Sudan. “Entire blocks and departments are out of service. The buildings need urgent structural repair before patients can be admitted safely.” Despite the damage, medical staff continue to treat patients in parts of the hospitals that remain usable, Abubakar said, calling for assistance to repair the facilities. The conflict has devastated Sudan’s health system, with attacks on medical facilities, shortages of medicines and supplies, and the displacement of health workers further limiting access to care. The SAF and RSF have both been accused of widespread violations against civilians during the war, including attacks on civilian areas and critical infrastructure. Both sides deny targeting civilians. The post Human rights chief Türk renews call for end to Sudan war appeared first on Radio Tamazuj .","url":"https://www.radiotamazuj.org/en/news/article/human-rights-chief-turk-renews-call-for-end-to-sudan-war","source":"Radio Tamazuj","image":"","published":"2026-09-07T13:00:26+00:00","category":"conflict","category_label":"Armed Conflict","region":"Horn of Africa","country":"Sudan","fatalities":0,"severity":"Moderate","severity_score":0},{"id":"9616f5baaa4b","title":"Bentiu IDP camp court opens to strengthen access to justice","summary":"BENTIU — A new court has opened at the Bentiu internally displaced persons’ camp in South Sudan’s Unity State as authorities seek to improve access to justice for displaced communities, according to the United Nations. The Bentiu IDP Camp Court supported by the United Nations Mission in South Sudan (UNMISS), the Unity State government, the […] The post Bentiu IDP camp court opens to strengthen acc","body":"BENTIU — A new court has opened at the Bentiu internally displaced persons’ camp in South Sudan’s Unity State as authorities seek to improve access to justice for displaced communities, according to the United Nations.\n\nThe Bentiu IDP Camp Court supported by the United Nations Mission in South Sudan (UNMISS), the Unity State government, the Judiciary of South Sudan, and the Ministry of Justice and Constitutional Affairs will operate from Sept. 7 to 26.\n\nThe court is expected to hear cases involving sexual and gender-based violence, drug-related offenses, and gang violence.\n\nThe Bentiu IDP Camp Court is an initiative of the Unity State Government’s Task Force on Combating Drug Abuse and Gang Violence, bringing together security, justice, and health actors.\n\nThe Court is being deployed in cooperation with the Community High Committee (CHC) of the Bentiu IDP Camp in response to repeated concerns and requests from the community for stronger justice and rule of law mechanisms to address drug use, gang violence, and sexual and gender-based violence (SGBV), particularly affecting children and youth.\n\nIts deployment aims to bring the formal justice system directly to residents of the IDP camp and provide an opportunity for serious offences to be investigated and adjudicated.\n\nThis deployment builds on the re-establishment of the Judiciary in Unity State in May 2026 after 13 years of absence and the seven mobile courts deployed across Unity State since 2024 to combat impunity, reduce violence, deter crime and contribute to peace and security in the State.\n\nBentiu IDP camp is South Sudan’s largest camp for internally displaced persons, hosting more than 100,000 people.\n\nEstablished as a Protection of Civilians site following the outbreak of conflict in 2013, it was redesignated as an IDP camp in 2021 and remains a major focus of UNMISS’ civilian protection efforts in Bentiu.\n\nFollowing the outbreak of conflict in 2013, permanent judicial and prosecutorial capacity was restored in Bentiu only in May 2026.\n\nTo support access to justice, combat impunity, and promote accountability in support of peace and security, UNMISS has supported the deployment of seven mobile courts across Unity State since 2024, including in Southern Unity and Mayom.\n\nThese courts have resulted in the adjudication of 517 cases, following a period in which no cases were adjudicated through the formal justice system in Unity State in 2023.\n\nSudans Post is an independent, young, and grass roots news media organization aimed at providing readers with an alternate depiction of events that occur on Sudan, South Sudan and East Africa, and to establish an engaging social platform for readers to discover and discuss the various issues that impact the two countries and the region.","url":"https://www.sudanspost.com/bentiu-idp-camp-court-opens-to-strengthen-access-to-justice/?utm_source=rss&utm_medium=rss&utm_campaign=bentiu-idp-camp-court-opens-to-strengthen-access-to-justice","source":"Sudans Post","image":"","published":"2026-09-07T12:22:41+00:00","category":"conflict","category_label":"Armed Conflict","region":"Horn of Africa","country":"South Sudan","fatalities":0,"severity":"Moderate","severity_score":0},{"id":"55de1cbfe240","title":"Machar trial adjourned after judge falls ill","summary":"The trial of suspended South Sudan First Vice President Riek Machar and seven co-defendants was The post Machar trial adjourned after judge falls ill appeared first on Radio Tamazuj .","body":"The trial of suspended South Sudan First Vice President Riek Machar and seven co-defendants was adjourned on Monday after one of the three judges fell ill, leaving the panel without the quorum needed to continue proceedings. Presiding Judge James Alala Deng said the court could not proceed because Judge Stephen Simon was unwell and unable to attend. Justice Isaac Pur Majok was also present when the trial resumed on Monday. The court had been on a week-long adjournment to consider two applications, one from the defence and another from prosecutors. The defence has asked the court to clarify and apply what it says is the correct statutory procedure for the remaining defendants. Prosecutors have filed a counter-application seeking to have the defendants questioned on the prosecution’s evidence before they are allowed to submit written defence statements. The court adjourned the proceedings until Friday, Sept. 11 to allow the prosecution to respond to the defence submissions. Machar and the seven other defendants are being tried over violence in Nasir, Upper Nile state, in March 2025, when government forces suffered heavy casualties. They face charges including treason, murder, conspiracy, terrorism, destruction of public property and crimes against humanity. Prosecutors allege that forces aligned with Machar’s Sudan People’s Liberation Army in Opposition (SPLA-IO), together with the White Army militia, killed 257 soldiers of the South Sudan People’s Defence Forces (SSPDF), including military commander General David Majur Dak, during an attack on a military garrison in Nasir. The prosecution has also alleged that military equipment worth about $58 million was destroyed or seized during the fighting. Machar and the other defendants have denied the charges. The post Machar trial adjourned after judge falls ill appeared first on Radio Tamazuj .","url":"https://www.radiotamazuj.org/en/news/article/machar-trial-adjourned-after-judge-falls-ill-3","source":"Radio Tamazuj","image":"","published":"2026-09-07T11:45:55+00:00","category":"conflict","category_label":"Armed Conflict","region":"Horn of Africa","country":"South Sudan","fatalities":0,"severity":"Moderate","severity_score":0},{"id":"9a062401e706","title":"Sécurité des bâtiments : les maires et les ordres professionnels scellent un partenariat","summary":"© DR Face à la recrudescence des effondrements d'immeubles, les Communes et Villes Unies du Cameroun (CVUC), l'Ordre National des Architectes du Cameroun (ONAC) et l'Ordre National des Ingénieurs de Génie Civil (ONIGC) ont signé ce mercredi 03 septembre 2026 une convention-cadre tripartite historique. Objectif : verrouiller le suivi des chantiers privés après la délivrance du permis de construire.","body":"© DR Face à la recrudescence des effondrements d'immeubles, les Communes et Villes Unies du Cameroun (CVUC), l'Ordre National des Architectes du Cameroun (ONAC) et l'Ordre National des Ingénieurs de Génie Civil (ONIGC) ont signé ce mercredi 03 septembre 2026 une convention-cadre tripartite historique. Objectif : verrouiller le suivi des chantiers privés après la délivrance du permis de construire. C'est une réponse directe à une crise qui endeuille le Cameroun. Les CVUC, l'ONAC et l'ONIGC ont décidé de siffler la fin du désordre dans le secteur du BTP. Réunies le 03 septembre, les trois parties ont paraphé une convention qui change la donne. Fini le permis de construire délivré puis oublié. Désormais, le dispositif impose une maîtrise d'œuvre conjointe associant obligatoirement architecte et ingénieur sur les chantiers privés. Le texte prévoit des garde-fous concrets : instauration d'un carnet de suivi de chantier, obligation de points d'arrêt aux étapes critiques des travaux pour validation, et mécanisme de paiement progressif des honoraires des professionnels pour éviter les abandons. L'ambition politique est claire : prévenir les malfaçons, stopper les modifications anarchiques des plans approuvés et surtout, redonner aux mairies leur pouvoir de contrôle administratif, souvent contourné. Pour ne pas brusquer le système, une phase pilote sera lancée dans des communes volontaires, avec deux innovations majeures : la digitalisation du carnet de suivi et la formation des agents municipaux. À travers cet accord, les collectivités et les ordres professionnels envoient un message fort : la sécurité des populations prime sur les économies de bout de chandelle. Cet article Sécurité des bâtiments : les maires et les ordres professionnels scellent un partenariat est apparu en premier sur Journalducameroun.com .","url":"https://fr.journalducameroun.com/securite-des-batiments-les-maires-et-les-ordres-professionnels-scellent-un-partenariat/","source":"Journal du Cameroun","image":"","published":"2026-09-07T10:57:24+00:00","category":"other","category_label":"Other Security News","region":"Central Africa","country":"Cameroon","fatalities":0,"severity":"Moderate","severity_score":0},{"id":"9f996b9d59bb","title":"Libya arrests five over drone attacks on oil and electricity facilities","summary":"Libya’s internationally recognised Government of National Unity announced on Sunday that it had dismantled an organised sabotage cell allegedly responsible for suicide drone attacks on oil and electricity facilities in Tripoli and Zawiya. Five Libyan and foreign nationals were arrested during an operation conducted by the Ministry of Defense, according to a statement published on […]","body":"Libya’s internationally recognised Government of National Unity announced on Sunday that it had dismantled an organised sabotage cell allegedly responsible for suicide drone attacks on oil and electricity facilities in Tripoli and Zawiya.\n\nFive Libyan and foreign nationals were arrested during an operation conducted by the Ministry of Defense, according to a statement published on the government’s “Hokomitna” platform.\n\nAuthorities said the operation also foiled planned attacks on a power station south of Tripoli, other critical facilities and political, security and military leaders.\n\nInvestigations are continuing to identify other suspects and those accused of planning, financing or supporting the operations. The government said further details would be released after preliminary investigations were completed. The arrests followed weeks of attacks and sabotage against energy infrastructure in western Libya, which placed additional pressure on electricity supplies and public services during the peak summer period.\n\nGerman Minister of State for Migration Bernd Kroeser expressed Germany’s willingness to collaborate with Libya, both bilaterally and through the European Union Commission, to address illegal immigration. The remarks were made during a meeting with Libya’s acting Minister of Foreign Affairs, Taher Al-Baour, on Wednesday. Talks focused on illegal immigration and the efforts by Libyan […]\n\nOn the walls of Bab Al-Azizya, 34 murals have been unveiled by activists urging for women empowerment to attract more attention on their plight. The event which was part of ceremony called ‘Flame of Our Capital’ united local and international civil societies together with representatives of the British Government, which provided funding for the project. […]\n\nLibyan politicians are at a crucial crossroad as the country prepares for another major political move before the end of the month. The General National Congress, Libya’s parliament, came under gun attack to pressure it in holding a no-confidence vote. Prime Minister Zeidan has admitted that his government has failed and there will be a […]","url":"https://northafricapost.com/100084-libya-arrests-five-over-drone-attacks-on-oil-and-electricity-facilities.html","source":"North Africa Post","image":"","published":"2026-09-07T10:48:20+00:00","category":"conflict","category_label":"Armed Conflict","region":"North Africa","country":"Libya","fatalities":0,"severity":"Moderate","severity_score":0},{"id":"1ce89b84c0ac","title":"Heavy Gunfire Sparks Fear of Renewed Fighting Near CAR’s Capital","summary":"The renewed fighting reveals the fragile security situation in CAR, where peace agreements signed in recent years have repeatedly faltered, and where the government continues to struggle to assert authority beyond the capital. The post Heavy Gunfire Sparks Fear of Renewed Fighting Near CAR’s Capital appeared first on HumAngle .","body":"Intense fighting involving heavy and light weapons is taking place in the PK40-45 area along the Boali road, which leads to Bangui, the capital of the Central African Republic (CAR). Traffic in and out of the capital via this road has been halted, preventing any vehicles from entering or leaving the town. Local sources in the CAR capital say they have yet to determine the forces involved in the ongoing chaos and exchange of fire. “At this stage, the exact circumstances leading to this exchange of gunfire are not known. No human and material casualty figures are available right now,” a government source who asked for anonymity because he is not authorised to speak on behalf of the government told HumAngle in Bangui. Diplomatic sources suggested that the exchange of gunfire happened between the CAR national army and some of the rebel groups operating in the country. There are currently no physical indications that the authorities in Bangui are preparing to send reinforcements to Boali. The heavy exchange of gunfire near the town of Boali comes amid an ongoing conflict in the country. When a coalition of rebel groups known as the Séléka overthrew President François Bozizé in 2013, CAR was thrown into a cycle of violence between different armed groups, government forces, and international peacekeepers. Despite the deployment of United Nations peacekeepers and bilateral support from allies such as Russia and Rwanda, a huge part of the country is still being controlled by armed rebel groups. The deadly groups often exploit CAR’s weak state institutions, porous borders, and lucrative natural resources, including diamonds and gold, to sustain their operations. The town of Boali strategically lies on the main road linking Bangui to the country’s northern and western regions. Vital for trade, supply routes, and military logistics, this corridor is contested by armed groups and state forces. Past clashes in this area have disrupted humanitarian access and heightened insecurity for civilians, many of whom already face displacement and food shortages. The renewed fighting reveals the fragile security situation in CAR, where peace agreements signed in recent years have repeatedly faltered, and where the government continues to struggle to assert authority beyond the capital. The post Heavy Gunfire Sparks Fear of Renewed Fighting Near CAR’s Capital appeared first on HumAngle .","url":"https://humanglemedia.com/heavy-gunfire-sparks-fear-of-renewed-fighting-near-cars-capital/","source":"HumAngle","image":"","published":"2026-09-07T10:45:46+00:00","category":"conflict","category_label":"Armed Conflict","region":"Central Africa","country":"Central African Republic","fatalities":0,"severity":"Moderate","severity_score":0},{"id":"923b886f9b86","title":"RSF releases four journalists and lawyer after months in detention in North Darfur","summary":"The Rapid Support Forces (RSF) have released four female journalists and a lawyer in Nyala,... The post RSF releases four journalists and lawyer after months in detention in North Darfur appeared first on Dabanga Radio TV Online .","body":"The Rapid Support Forces (RSF) have released four female journalists and a lawyer in Nyala, the capital of South Darfur, after holding them for varying periods of time. Sources tell Radio Dabanga that the journalists, Dr Manahil Mustafa, Ishraqa Abdelrahman, Mawahib Ibrahim, and Zahra Mohamed El Hassan, were released alongside the lawyer, Magda Hassan Ali, after about six months in detention. The journalists and lawyer were arrested after taking part in a workshop held in February that discussed women’s issues and rights. Meanwhile, the family of El Hadi Abdelrahman, the former director general of the Ministry of Education, tells Radio Dabanga that the Rapid Support Forces have also released him after he spent more than a year and a half in Dagris prison in South Darfur. This report follows coverage by Radio Dabanga last week that the Sudanese Journalists’ Syndicate says five of its members remain victims of enforced disappearance, with their fate and whereabouts still unknown. It describes this as a serious violation of their basic rights, international law, and their right to life and personal safety. In a statement, the syndicate calls for the immediate disclosure of the journalists’ fate and whereabouts, and for a serious, independent investigation into the circumstances of their disappearance. Note: This report has been translated from Arabic with the assistance of artificial intelligence tools, and subsequently reviewed and adapted by a human editor before publication. Share article The post RSF releases four journalists and lawyer after months in detention in North Darfur appeared first on Dabanga Radio TV Online .","url":"https://www.dabangasudan.org/en/all-news/article/rsf-releases-four-journalists-and-lawyer-after-months-in-detention-in-north-darfur","source":"Dabanga Sudan","image":"","published":"2026-09-07T10:30:00+00:00","category":"conflict","category_label":"Armed Conflict","region":"Horn of Africa","country":"Sudan","fatalities":0,"severity":"Moderate","severity_score":0},{"id":"68012658a5dc","title":"Former speaker Kumba urges armed groups to lay down guns","summary":"Former parliament speaker Jemma Nunu Kumba has called on churches, communities and the government to The post Former speaker Kumba urges armed groups to lay down guns appeared first on Radio Tamazuj .","body":"Former parliament speaker Jemma Nunu Kumba has called on churches, communities and the government to work together to persuade armed opposition groups in Western Equatoria state to lay down their weapons and return home. Kumba made the appeal on Sunday during morning prayers at St Mary Church in Yambio, urging communities to take greater responsibility for restoring peace in the state. She said the Zande and Balanda communities had no fundamental differences and blamed continued insecurity largely on the involvement of some members of the communities in armed groups. “The Zande and Balanda have no problem. The problem is our sons and daughters who are still in the bush,” Kumba said. She called for continued dialogue with armed groups and said churches could play a key role in reaching out to opposition fighters and supporting reconciliation efforts. Kumba cited the Inter-Church Interface Group, which she said had previously helped persuade some former members of the Sudan People’s Liberation Movement-In Opposition (SPLM-IO) to leave the bush and return to their communities. “Anyone is welcome to put down the gun and come home to join his brothers and sisters to develop our state and our country,” she said. Kumba also appealed to former Western Equatoria governor Alfred Futuyo Karaba to return home with those still associated with him, describing him as a “son of the state.” She said President Salva Kiir Mayardit had granted an amnesty to people carrying guns and urged armed groups to take advantage of the offer by laying down their weapons and returning home. Those who have returned are being integrated into government institutions, including the national army, she said. Kumba also expressed concern about insecurity along the road linking Western Equatoria with the capital, Juba, saying civilians continue to face attacks and killings allegedly carried out by armed groups and criminal elements. “We are brothers and sisters. There is nobody coming from outside now to fight us here. It is us alone,” she said. She said lasting peace would enable children to attend school, farmers to cultivate their land, businesses to operate and people to travel without fear. Kumba said she would work with churches and the government to support efforts to restore lasting peace in Western Equatoria. “So, this is what I can tell you as your mother. We will join hands together with the church and the government to make sure that peace is restored in our state,” she said. Kumba’s appeal follows a similar call by Western Equatoria Governor Badagbu Daniel Rimbasa on Aug. 24 for armed groups operating around Duma, Mapuse, Bandala and Sue to end hostilities, return to their communities and participate in dialogue and peaceful integration. Rimbasa also called for an end to road ambushes and urged parents, relatives and community leaders to encourage young people involved in armed activities to return home. “We are tired of war. We cannot continue destabilizing our own area,” Rimbasa said. The post Former speaker Kumba urges armed groups to lay down guns appeared first on Radio Tamazuj .","url":"https://www.radiotamazuj.org/en/news/article/former-speaker-kumba-urges-armed-groups-to-lay-down-guns","source":"Radio Tamazuj","image":"","published":"2026-09-07T09:43:09+00:00","category":"conflict","category_label":"Armed Conflict","region":"Horn of Africa","country":"Sudan","fatalities":0,"severity":"Moderate","severity_score":0},{"id":"7d84e019bdb5","title":"Cameroun/ Rentrée scolaire : Maurice Kamto envoie un message à la communauté éducative","summary":"© (Archives) Le président national du Mouvement pour la renaissance du Cameroun s'adresse aux élèves, aux parents, aux enseignants au moment où l'année scolaire 2026/ 2027 commence. Encouragements, appel à la détermination, à la vigilance, à la protection et au meilleur encadrement des enfants sont des mots clés du message que Maurice Kamto adresse à la communauté éducative ce 07 septembre. Aux mi","body":"© (Archives) Le président national du Mouvement pour la renaissance du Cameroun s'adresse aux élèves, aux parents, aux enseignants au moment où l'année scolaire 2026/ 2027 commence. Encouragements, appel à la détermination, à la vigilance, à la protection et au meilleur encadrement des enfants sont des mots clés du message que Maurice Kamto adresse à la communauté éducative ce 07 septembre. Aux millions d'élèves attendus dans des établissements scolaires, l'homme politique souhaite santé, enthousiasme, détermination, et réussite. Il voit en ces derniers « l'espoir des transformations multiformes à venir » du Cameroun et de l'Afrique. Aux parents et au reste de la communauté éducative, l'opposant au régime de Yaoundé adresse ses encouragements. Tout en reconnaissant leur contribution dans l'éveil de la connaissance, leur formation aux savoirs-faire, à la citoyenneté, la fraternité et la conscience patriotique. En plus de ces reconnaissances et souhaits, le leader politique se penche sur des sujets préoccupants, en particulier l'enlèvement d'enfants, et insiste sur les précautions à prendre pour mettre les enfants à l'abri. Face au phénomène, la prudence et la vigilance sont les clés que l'auteur du message recommande d'utiliser. « Parents, accompagnons et protégeons davantage nos enfants », interpelle l'enseignante des universités, non sans oublier les enseignants à qui il demande d'avoir « une conscience aiguë » et de briller par « l'exemplarité » dans l'exercice de leur métier. Cet article Cameroun/ Rentrée scolaire : Maurice Kamto envoie un message à la communauté éducative est apparu en premier sur Journalducameroun.com .","url":"https://fr.journalducameroun.com/cameroun-rentree-scolaire-maurice-kamto-envoie-un-message-a-la-communaute-educative/","source":"Journal du Cameroun","image":"","published":"2026-09-07T09:02:12+00:00","category":"kidnapping","category_label":"Kidnapping & Abduction","region":"Central Africa","country":"Cameroon","fatalities":0,"severity":"Moderate","severity_score":0},{"id":"37b540dc27be","title":"Cameroun : les élèves reprennent le chemin de l’école","summary":"© CRTV Ce 07 septembre est jour de rentrée scolaire pour les élèves de la maternelle, du primaire et du secondaire. L'année scolaire débute avec des objectifs et des défis. C'est un nouveau départ pour la communauté éducative. Après trois mois de vacances, les campus scolaires reprennent vie. Les élèves de la maternelle, du primaire et du secondaire, principaux occupants de ces espaces de dispensa","body":"© CRTV Ce 07 septembre est jour de rentrée scolaire pour les élèves de la maternelle, du primaire et du secondaire. L'année scolaire débute avec des objectifs et des défis. C'est un nouveau départ pour la communauté éducative. Après trois mois de vacances, les campus scolaires reprennent vie. Les élèves de la maternelle, du primaire et du secondaire, principaux occupants de ces espaces de dispensation et d'acquisition des connaissances sont de retour. Ils sont environ neuf millions de d'enfants et d'adolescents qui sont attendus cette année dans les écoles, les lycées et collèges, dont environ 6 557 400 élèves dans l’éducation de base. Dès ce lundi, les programmes prévoient le début des cours par les la détermination du niveau des élèves et l'administration des correctifs pour ceux qui présentent des lacunes. Au niveau de l'éducation de base, le ministre Laurent Serges Etoundi Ngoa insiste sur l'atteinte des objectifs au premier rang desquels savoir lire, savoir écrire, savoir faire des calculs. Pour ce dernier élément, le membre du gouvernement entend faire former des enfants pour être aptes à effectuer des calculs sans recourir à la machine. Aux enseignements secondaires, l'intelligence artificielle ainsi que le numérique en général constituent des axes d'engagements pour cette nouvelle année. Au moment où les cours reprennent, les questions de sécurité se posent encore dans les régions du Nord-Ouest, du Sud-Ouest où du fait de la crise, les séparatistes imposent les villes mortes chaque lundi. Ainsi que dans la région de l'Extrême-Nord où des éléments de Boko Haram attaquent encore et enlèvent des personnes. Aussi, des inondations, la présence du choléra dans certaines parties du Septentrion, de même que l'insécurité dans les grandes villes sont autant de défis qui s'imposent cette année. Mais, malgré ces défis, les enseignants et responsables d'établissements sont appelés à respecter le calendrier de l'année scolaire fixé par le gouvernement. Cet article Cameroun : les élèves reprennent le chemin de l’école est apparu en premier sur Journalducameroun.com .","url":"https://fr.journalducameroun.com/cameroun-les-eleves-reprennent-le-chemin-de-lecole/","source":"Journal du Cameroun","image":"","published":"2026-09-07T08:20:51+00:00","category":"terrorism","category_label":"Terrorism","region":"Central Africa","country":"Cameroon","fatalities":0,"severity":"Moderate","severity_score":0},{"id":"7b022856fbae","title":"Amnesty International To Tinubu Govt: 37 Kaduna Worshippers Abducted During Easter Service Still In Captivity After Five Months","summary":"Amnesty International To Tinubu Govt: 37 Kaduna Worshippers Abducted During Easter Service Still In Captivity After Five Months Amnesty International said the incident was one of several attacks demonstrating what it described as the “utter failure of Nigerian authorities to protect the people.”","body":"Amnesty International said the incident was one of several attacks demonstrating what it described as the “utter failure of Nigerian authorities to protect the people.”\n\nAmnesty International has raised the alarm over the continued captivity of 37 worshippers abducted during raids on churches in Southern Kaduna, accusing the Nigerian government of failing to adequately protect citizens from recurring attacks, abductions and killings.\n\nThis comes despite assurances by President Bola Tinubu's administration that it is doing everything possible to protect citizens and secure the release of abductees.\n\nThe rights organisation on Monday, said the victims were abducted during Easter Sunday worship on April 5, 2026, in Ariko community, Kachia Local Government Area of Kaduna State, and have remained in captivity for five months.\n\nAmnesty International said the incident was one of several attacks demonstrating what it described as the “utter failure of Nigerian authorities to protect the people.”\n\nIt said the prolonged captivity of the worshippers had been compounded by worsening conditions, with three of the 11 people abducted from Awon community reportedly dying in captivity within the week.\n\nAccording to the organisation, a woman identified as Joy Samuel died on August 28 after giving birth in what it described as a deplorable condition. The baby she delivered also died.\n\nIt added that Mr. Yusuf Bawa, 34, and his five-year-old son also died in captivity as a result of severe illness and hunger.\n\n“Three of the 11 people abducted from Awon community have died in captivity this week. A woman Joy Samuel died on 28 August, after giving birth in a deplorable condition. The baby she delivered also died. Mr. Yusuf Bawa 34, and his 5-year-old son have also died as a result of severe illness and hunger,” Amnesty International said.\n\nThe organisation said the deaths and illnesses suffered by the abducted persons reflected the harsh conditions and abuses they were allegedly being subjected to while in captivity.\n\n“Deaths and severe illness these people are facing in captivity show the atrocities they are being subjected to,” it said.\n\nAmnesty International further alleged that the victims were being subjected to repeated threats, starvation, sexual violence, beatings and other forms of degrading treatment.\n\n“While in captivity, the abducted persons are always repeatedly threatened to be killed, starved, women and girls raped, beaten and dehumanized in so many ways,” the organisation said.\n\nThe rights group also expressed concern over the wider security situation in Southern Kaduna, saying recurring attacks, abductions for ransom and killings had continued to disrupt the lives of residents.\n\nIt said the insecurity had created a climate of fear that was affecting farming, education and other aspects of social life in communities across the region.\n\n“Recurring attacks, abductions for ransom, and killings are continuing to make life a hell in many parts of southern Kaduna. A climate of fear is affecting agricultural activities, education and other aspects of social lives of the people,” Amnesty International said.\n\nThe organisation said the continued captivity of the worshippers underscored the need for the Nigerian government to intensify efforts to protect civilians and secure the release of people held by armed groups.\n\nAmnesty International called on the government to fulfil its national and international human rights obligations by taking urgent steps to secure the safe release of the abducted worshippers.\n\n“The Nigerian government must deliver on its national and international human rights obligations by immediately redoubling efforts to ensure the safe release and return of not only the 37 worshippers abducted in Kachia LGA, but also all people held captive by gunmen across Nigeria,” it said.\n\nThe organisation's concerns come as communities in Southern Kaduna continue to face security challenges, with attacks and abductions threatening livelihoods and access to education while leaving families of victims in prolonged uncertainty over the fate of their loved ones.\n\nSaharaReporters reported in April that the Ariko community was plunged into mourning on Easter Sunday when suspected terrorists launched coordinated attacks on two churches, First ECWA Church and St. Augustine Catholic Church, killing at least seven worshippers and abducting several others.\n\nConfirming the attack, the Councillor representing Awon Ward, Mark Bawa, said heavily armed assailants had stormed the area during early morning services.","url":"https://saharareporters.com/2026/09/07/amnesty-international-tinubu-govt-37-kaduna-worshippers-abducted-during-easter-service","source":"Sahara Reporters","image":"","published":"2026-09-07T08:15:31+00:00","category":"kidnapping","category_label":"Kidnapping & Abduction","region":"West Africa","country":"Nigeria","fatalities":0,"severity":"Moderate","severity_score":0},{"id":"fb27c992b018","title":"Review: Historian Traces Boko Haram Back to Northern Nigeria’s Islamic History","summary":"In a new book, academic historian Prof. Moses Ebe Ochonu argues that Boko Haram didn't invent its ideology from scratch but instead borrowed Dan Fodio’s 19th-century legacy to legitimise a different war with foreign inspiration. The post Review: Historian Traces Boko Haram Back to Northern Nigeria’s Islamic History appeared first on HumAngle .","body":"Boko Haram has been explained in over a dozen different ways in the past 17 years since it turned violent. Several factors – such as poverty, political exclusion, corruption, climate change, state violence, foreign jihadist influence, and failed governance – have been blamed. Each of these explanations gets at something real, but none of them quite answers why the movement took root in northeastern Nigeria specifically, how it justified its violence in Islamic terms, or why some audiences bought the message while most rejected it. In his recently released book, “ Boko Haram: The Past of the Present Upheaval ”, Moses Ebe Ochonu , a Nigerian professor of African History, looks into the issue long before Boko Haram waged its first attack. Instead of treating the insurgency as a modern phenomenon, Prof. Moses traces it through nearly two centuries of arguments over Islamic authority, reform, and political legitimacy in northern Nigeria. The cover page of Prof. Moses Ochonu’s book, “Boko Haram: The Past of the Present Upheaval”. In his words, “Boko Haram did not emerge in a historical vacuum. For this reason, the complex religious, political, and socioeconomic processes that birthed the movement should be examined within the long trajectory of Islamic reform and counterreform in Northern Nigeria.” (pp. 5–6) His recent work is among the more historically grounded studies of the insurgency. In putting together the book, he used Arabic texts, colonial archives, jihadist propaganda, and decades of Sokoto Caliphate scholarship to argue that Boko Haram sits inside a much longer line of reformist movements that have fought over what Islamic governance should look like in Northern Nigeria. The book does not claim a straight intellectual line runs from the 19th Sokoto jihad to Boko Haram. What it tries to show is how the militants kept invoking Usman dan Fodio’s memory to bolster their own claim to religious legitimacy. The real contribution of the book lies in showing that the fight over Boko Haram was never just a fight over territory or doctrine, but a fight over history’s legacy — a struggle to claim that what the Sokoto jihad left behind is what Boko Haram is trying to restore. History as a weapon Prof. Moses’ argument rests on the premise that Dan Fodio occupies an important position in the region’s religious imagination, with almost every sect seeking to associate itself with him. Dan Fodio not only founded the Sokoto Caliphate; his descendants include the current Sultan of Sokoto, and his writings still carry weight in Islamic scholarship across West Africa. The book shows how both the founder of Boko Haram , Mohammed Yusuf, and its most violent leader, Abubakar Shekau , leaned on Dan Fodio’s name in their sermons and propaganda, framing colonialism and the modern Nigerian state as a break from an earlier Islamic order founded by Dan Fodio and which their movement claimed to be restoring. Their attempt to tie Boko Haram to the Sokoto jihad was deliberately designed to give them a way to fit a new insurgency into a story northern Nigerians already knew. A screenshot from one video in which Mohammed Yusuf was delivering a sermon and clips of other Boko Haram-related incidents. To expose that, the book quotes Mohammed Yusuf in one of his speeches, claiming that during Dan Fodio’s time “it was the Shari’a that was the practice in this country. Dan Fodio and other Islamic scholars carried out the jihad and ensured that Qur’anic law was implemented. Allah did not interfere with this situation until when our Muslim leaders accepted… the Europeans’ secular constitution.” (p. 3) However, Mohammed’s argument relies on a selective and fundamentally dishonest reading of history. Prof. Moses exposes the Boko Haram leader’s historical reference as a performance rather than a genuine doctrinal inheritance. Furthermore, the book distinguishes historical continuity from theological continuity because they are not the same thing. A clear line is shown between political symbolism and doctrinal influence. Boko Haram claimed Dan Fodio’s legacy, but it grew out of a different intellectual world, with roots in 20th-century Salafi reformism , global jihadist literature, and arguments that did not yet exist in Dan Fodio’s time. Theologically, Dan Fodio worked within the Sufi spiritualism – a tradition within Islam that emphasises inner devotion and spiritual purification, Ash’ari theology – a Sunni sect that uses rationalism alongside scriptures, and Maliki jurisprudence – one of the four Sunni schools of Islamic law – dominated West African Islam during his life. Boko Haram, by constrast, belongs to the Salafi-jihadi current – an extremist ideology that combines Salafi interpretation of Islam with an emphasis of Jihad as a primary purpose of the religion, which draws on ideas from well beyond northern Nigeria and is ideologically at odds with much of what Dan Fodio stood for. Prof. Moses doesn’t reduce the movement to foreign influence, though. His argument is that Boko Haram picked and chose while combining global jihadist ideas with regional symbols that would resonate locally. That keeps the book from falling into either trap of treating Boko Haram as either purely homegrown or purely imported. “The claim here is not that there is a direct instructional value in this preexisting jihad template for Boko Haram; rather, the point is to highlight the fact that Boko Haram… see[s] [itself] as continuing in the Dan Fodio jihad tradition… This history and tradition of reform, dissidence, and jihad have collectively become an all-purpose alibi to draw on.” (p. 23) From reform to radicalisation The book covers how Islamic reform movements changed in postcolonial northern Nigeria, tracing how a reformist current hardened into one that insurgents could later claim as their own. The author devotes considerable attention to the intellectual and spiritual founder of Jama’atu Izalatil Bidi’a Wa Iqamatus Sunnah (Izala), Sheikh Mahmud Gumi, and to the reformist currents that pushed back against established Sufi institutions in the latter half of the 20th century. Gumi’s movement reshaped religious debate across northern Nigeria and introduced new forms of scriptural literalism that influenced later generations. “Gumi was instrumental to the long process of Islamic dissidence that transformed the Northern Nigerian Muslim landscape from a predominantly Sufi one to one that came to be suffused in anti-Sufi [ideas].” (pp. 95-96) Even so, one notices that the author is careful not to draw a straight line from reform to violence. Reformist Salafism did not cause extremism, but he argues it changed the religious landscape enough that more radical figures later found room to claim authority within it. He notes that many scholars associated with reformist Salafism rejected armed rebellion outright, yet the intellectual climate they helped create still left an opening that more militant voices eventually filled. “The theological creeds of the Salafi clerics and the religious activists who embraced political Islam did not emerge from a historical void… While the analysis of this historical tapestry is not intended to be read as a linear progression, I argue that the ideational transformations resulting from successive reformist projects made the Boko Haram moment, if not Boko Haram itself, possible.” (p. 10) Abubakar Shekau. The book also examines how Boko Haram justified violence. Prof. Moses places the insurgency’s arguments inside long-running debates over governance, apostasy, religious reform, and what Muslim rulers owe their subjects. Readers may reject the movement’s conclusions, but the book insists its ideology can be still be understood rather than dismissed without examination. That framing, according to the author, also explains why Boko Haram kept reaching for local historical references even while aligning itself with global Salafi-jihadist networks. Groups tied to al-Qaeda or the Islamic State bring transnational frameworks with them, but they still need local stories to establish credibility in the places where they actually operate. In northern Nigeria, the memory of the Sokoto Caliphate became that story for Boko Haram. On ISWAP The closing chapters briefly turn to the Islamic State West Africa Province (ISWAP). Prof. Moses draws a line between ISWAP and Jama’atu Ahlis Sunna Lidda’awati Wal-Jihad (JAS) —Boko Haram’s formal name. Rather than leaning mainly on indiscriminate violence, ISWAP has put more effort into building administrative structures, collecting taxes, and cultivating ties with the civilians under its influence. “The former [ISWAP] predicated its decision to split from the latter [Shekau’s JAS] on Shekau’s flagrant flouting of jihad rules, which it interpreted as a debasement of the jihad ideal. The distinction between the tactics of Boko Haram’s two factions is significant and has become the major touchstone of the ongoing scorched-earth clashes betwe","url":"https://humanglemedia.com/review-historian-traces-boko-haram-back-to-northern-nigerias-islamic-history/","source":"HumAngle","image":"","published":"2026-09-07T08:00:00+00:00","category":"terrorism","category_label":"Terrorism","region":"West Africa","country":"Nigeria","fatalities":0,"severity":"Moderate","severity_score":0},{"id":"44329f7626d5","title":"UN envoy presses Sudan leader on truce and prisoner exchange","summary":"The UN special envoy for Sudan, Pekka Haavisto, has urged de-escalation of hostilities, and the... The post UN envoy presses Sudan leader on truce and prisoner exchange appeared first on Dabanga Radio TV Online .","body":"The UN special envoy for Sudan, Pekka Haavisto, has urged de-escalation of hostilities, and the protection of civilians in talks with Sudan’s army chief and Sovereignty Council chair, Gen Abdelfattah El Burhan, while raising the prospect of exchanging prisoners and civilian detainees. Haavisto held a series of meetings in Khartoum on Sunday with Burhan, who heads the Sovereign Council and the army, Prime Minister Kamil Idris, Finance Minister and Justice and Equality Movement (JEM) leader Jibril Ibrahim, focusing on developments in the country, reducing tensions, ongoing consultations on dialogue, and ways to end the war and achieve peace in Sudan. In a statement after meeting El Burhan, Haavisto says dialogue is the path to sustainable peace and a comprehensive ceasefire. He says he is ready to cooperate and engage closely with all Sudanese parties and stakeholders, and to work with partners in the Quintet to advance the political process, ensuring that it is inclusive, integrated, and responsive to the aspirations of the Sudanese people. Haavisto says he conveyed important messages to El Burhan concerning de-escalation and the protection of civilians. “I look forward to continuing our meetings towards an exchange of prisoners and civilian detainees, in close cooperation with the International Committee of the Red Cross,” he says. Kamil Idris during his meeting with Pekka Haavisto in Khartoum on Sunday, September 6, 2026 (Soverignty Council Media) Sovereignty first Prime Minister Kamil Idris told the UN envoy that Sudan’s leadership will not compromise on national sovereignty or sovereign decision-making, stressing the country’s complete rejection of all forms of foreign intervention. Jibril Ibrahim told the envoy that the Sudanese Armed Forces are the national institution constitutionally responsible for protecting the country and defending its sovereignty and territorial integrity. JEM rejects wider arms embargo Ibrahim says the Justice and Equality Movement (JEM) rejects proposals to expand the arms embargo currently imposed on Darfur to cover the whole of Sudan. Such a measure, he says, would conflict with national sovereignty and restrict the armed forces’ ability to carry out their constitutional duty of protecting the country. He calls on the United Nations to welcome and support the Sudanese dialogue initiative, and to work with Sudanese parties to develop it into an inclusive, Sudanese-owned political process capable of ending the war and laying the foundations for lasting peace and a stable state. Dr. Jibril Ibrahim, head of the Justice and Equality Movement and Minister of Finance, during his meeting with UN envoy Pekka Haavisto on Sunday, September 6, 2026 (Photo: Supplied) Note: This report has been translated from Arabic with the assistance of artificial intelligence tools, and subsequently reviewed and adapted by a human editor before publication. Share article The post UN envoy presses Sudan leader on truce and prisoner exchange appeared first on Dabanga Radio TV Online .","url":"https://www.dabangasudan.org/en/all-news/article/un-envoy-presses-sudan-leader-on-truce-and-prisoner-exchange","source":"Dabanga Sudan","image":"","published":"2026-09-07T07:52:00+00:00","category":"conflict","category_label":"Armed Conflict","region":"Horn of Africa","country":"Sudan","fatalities":0,"severity":"Moderate","severity_score":0},{"id":"6a3af4a68273","title":"Libya arrests five people in connection with drone strikes on refinery","summary":"Authorities in western Libya have arrested five people including foreigners who were involved in a recent string of drone attacks on a major energy complex, the Tripoli-based government said on Sunday.","body":"Authorities in western Libya have arrested five people in connection with a recent string of drone attacks on a major energy facility.\n\nThe government said the arrests exposed a “vast plot” and described the attacks as part of a coordinated campaign.\n\nAuthorities said the group were part of a \"sabotage cell\" who also planned to attack a power station south of Tripoli, as well as other vital facilities.\n\nThey said foreigners are among the suspects but didn’t give further details.\n\nIn August, a large petrol reservoir in Zawiya caught fire during a drone attack on a major oil facility near Tripoli, Libya's state-owned oil company NOC said.\n\nThe reservoir is part of the second-largest oil refinery in Libya, with a refining capacity of 120,000 barrels per day. Other drone attacks followed at the Zawiya complex, which is located some 45 kilometres west of Tripoli.\n\nOn Thursday, another attack on fuel tanks outside of Tripoli was thwarted, the NOC said.\n\nLibya has the largest oil reserves in Africa. But the country has been beset by violence and instability since the overthrow of Muammar Gaddafi in 2011.\n\nTwo governments are currently vying for power: the UN-recognised administration in Tripoli led by Prime Minister Abdulhamid Dbeibah, and a rival in the east backed by military commander Khalifa Haftar.","url":"http://www.africanews.com/2026/09/07/libya-arrests-five-people-in-connection-with-drone-strikes-on-refinery/","source":"Africanews","image":"http://images.euronews.com/articles/stories/09/90/35/59/640x360_cmsv2_89e9c878-659c-57fa-9cfa-65fe23a4c47d-9903559.jpg?1788759041","published":"2026-09-07T07:14:20+00:00","category":"conflict","category_label":"Armed Conflict","region":"North Africa","country":"Libya","fatalities":0,"severity":"Moderate","severity_score":0},{"id":"7940ba3a4b11","title":"Opinion| South Sudan cannot be governed through fear, division, and perpetual transition","summary":"South Sudan has now spent far too many years trapped in a cycle of political The post Opinion| South Sudan cannot be governed through fear, division, and perpetual transition appeared first on Radio Tamazuj .","body":"South Sudan has now spent far too many years trapped in a cycle of political crisis, insecurity, poverty, corruption allegations, and unfinished peace agreements. For more than a decade, the people of South Sudan have been promised peace, democracy, development, and accountable government, yet ordinary citizens continue to bear the consequences of political failure. Salva Kiir and those who continue to defend his government must answer a simple question: after all these years, where is the political, economic, and social transformation that South Sudanese were promised? Even in Warrap, Salva Kiir’s traditional political stronghold, public frustration cannot simply be dismissed. No community should be expected to support a government forever merely because its leader comes from that community. Loyalty based on ethnicity or regional identity cannot replace good governance, security, development, accountability, and respect for human dignity. The same principle applies everywhere in South Sudan. The people of Equatoria, Upper Nile, Bahr el Ghazal, and every other part of the country are citizens of one republic known as South Sudan. They are not political territories belonging to individual leaders or political parties. The 2026 elections must not become another political maneuver South Sudan is again approaching elections, currently scheduled for December 2026. But the central question is not simply whether elections will technically take place. The real question is whether South Sudan will create the political and security conditions necessary for credible, inclusive, peaceful and genuinely competitive elections . Even the United Nations has warned that the political environment remains fragile and that important conditions for credible elections have yet to be secured. UN officials have specifically raised concerns about the absence of key opposition actors from important political processes, the lack of consensus, stalled implementation of the peace agreement, and the shrinking political space. An election without political freedom is not democracy. An election in which opposition leaders cannot freely organize, travel, campaign, and communicate with citizens is not a genuine democratic contest. An election conducted while political opponents are detained or excluded from meaningful participation cannot easily command public confidence. An election conducted without sufficient security, institutional independence, and political consensus risks becoming another source of conflict rather than a peaceful mechanism for transferring political power. The lesson of 2013 and 2016 must not be forgotten. South Sudanese people remember what happened after the political crisis within the SPLM in 2013 and the return to fighting in 2016. The 2016 violence was not an abstract political disagreement. It produced deaths, displacement, destruction, forced disappearance, and immense human suffering. The Revitalized Peace Agreement of 2018 was supposed to prevent South Sudan from returning to that cycle. Yet the implementation of the agreement remains incomplete. UNMISS reports that the transitional period has been extended repeatedly and that the implementation of the peace agreement remains unfinished. That is why South Sudan cannot afford another political confrontation disguised as an electoral process. The country needs political competition—not political elimination. It needs debate—not intimidation. It needs an independent judiciary—not a judiciary perceived as serving political interests. It needs professional security institutions—not security forces used as instruments of political competition. It needs elections in which every legitimate political actor can compete according to the law. Dr. Riek Machar’s political rights cannot simply be wished away Whether someone supports Dr. Riek Machar or opposes him is a political choice. But whether he and other South Sudanese citizens are entitled to political rights is a matter of law. The detention and subsequent trial of Dr. Machar have already become a major concern in the peace process. UNMISS called for restraint and respect for the Revitalized Peace Agreement following his detention in March 2025. More recent UN reporting has continued to identify his detention and trial as major factors contributing to the political crisis. No political leader should be forced to surrender his political rights simply because his participation may be inconvenient to those currently exercising power. If Dr. Riek Machar has committed a criminal offense, the answer must be lawful, independent, and fair judicial proceedings , not political conditions requiring him to abandon political life. If he has not committed such an offense, then continued political detention cannot be justified merely because his political participation is considered a threat. The same standard must apply to every South Sudanese politician—whether they belong to the SPLM, SPLM-IO, a smaller opposition party, civil society, or any other political movement. What international law says The political rights of South Sudanese citizens are not gifts from Salva Kiir, Dr. Riek Machar, the SPLM, SPLM-IO, or any political organization. International human rights law protects them. Article 21 of the Universal Declaration of Human Rights provides that everyone has the right to participate in the government of his or her country, directly or through freely chosen representatives, and that the will of the people shall be expressed through periodic and genuine elections based on universal and equal suffrage. Article 19 of the UDHR protects freedom of opinion and expression, while Article 20 protects peaceful assembly and association. The International Covenant on Civil and Political Rights (ICCPR) similarly protects political participation, peaceful assembly, and freedom of association. The UN Human Rights Committee has emphasized that political participation and peaceful assembly are closely connected. Most importantly for South Sudan as an African state, Article 13 of the African Charter on Human and Peoples’ Rights provides: “Every citizen shall have the right to participate freely in the government of his country, either directly or through freely chosen representatives…” The African Charter also protects freedom of expression under Article 9, freedom of association under Article 10, and peaceful assembly under Article 11. The African Charter on Democracy, Elections and Governance further emphasizes regular, transparent, free and fair elections, political pluralism, participation of citizens, transparency, accountability, respect for human rights, and the role of opposition political parties. These principles matter because democracy is not simply about putting a ballot box somewhere and telling citizens to vote. Democracy requires a political environment in which citizens are genuinely free to choose. South Sudan does not belong to one man or one tribe South Sudan belongs to its people. It does not belong to Kiir. It does not belong to Machar. It does not belong to SPLM. It does not belong to SPLM-IO. It does not belong to the Jieng Council of Elders. It does not belong to any tribe, military faction, political party, or political family. It belongs to the South Sudanese people. Therefore, nobody should fear a genuinely competitive election. If a political leader believes that the people support him, the most legitimate way to demonstrate that support is through a free, fair, transparent, and credible election . The people—not the army, not political appointments, not detention, not ethnic mobilization, and not government propaganda—should decide who governs South Sudan. The people deserve more than 21 years of political survival South Sudanese citizens deserve functioning hospitals, schools, roads, clean water, electricity, employment, security, justice and a government capable of paying its workers. They deserve a state in which public resources are used for public purposes. They deserve institutions that work. They deserve an independent judiciary. They deserve security forces that protect citizens rather than frighten them. They deserve political leaders who can be challenged without turning political disagreement into ethnic conflict. And, above all, they deserve the opportunity to choose their leaders peacefully. Egypt has demonstrated how quickly a state can transform parts of its infrastructure and build major national projects when political will and resources are mobilized. South Sudan, despite enormous oil wealth and international assistance, continues to struggle with basic services and infrastructure. The question therefore remains: How much longer can our people be asked to wait? NO MORE POLITICAL EXCLUSION Dr. Riek Machar’s political future should not be determined by President Kiir, the security apparatus, political elders, or any other individual or group. It should be determined under South Sudan’s constitutional and legal frame","url":"https://www.radiotamazuj.org/en/news/article/opinion-south-sudan-cannot-be-governed-through-fear-division-and-perpetual-transition","source":"Radio Tamazuj","image":"","published":"2026-09-07T07:05:50+00:00","category":"other","category_label":"Other Security News","region":"Horn of Africa","country":"South Sudan","fatalities":0,"severity":"Moderate","severity_score":0},{"id":"1232ff96ff24","title":"Displaced people living under dire conditions in Sudan’s Blue Nile State","summary":"The humanitarian situation has worsened for dozens of families displaced from the town of Qaysan The post Displaced people living under dire conditions in Sudan’s Blue Nile State appeared first on Radio Tamazuj .","body":"The humanitarian situation has worsened for dozens of families displaced from the town of Qaysan in Blue Nile State in Sudan. They fled fighting and are now living under trees in the Khor al-Qana area, east of Wad al-Mahi locality. According to local estimates, approximately 150 families are living under trees in Khor al-Qana, in extremely difficult conditions, lacking shelter, food, and necessities. Several displaced people told Radio Tamazuj that their humanitarian situation is rapidly deteriorating due to the lack of necessities. They appealed to humanitarian agencies and organizations to intervene urgently and provide food and shelter. One displaced person pointed out that their suffering is exacerbated by the lack of shelter, and urgently requested blankets and tarpaulins to protect themselves and their children from the harsh weather. Displaced person Farouk Adam said that displaced families have not received any aid or assistance so far, calling for the urgent provision of relief supplies and necessities. For her part, displaced person Hafsa Ismail said the humanitarian situation is “extremely dire,” noting that they fled the fighting and left everything behind, only to find themselves now without food or shelter. The conditions of the displaced in Khor al-Qana reflect the growing humanitarian needs of civilians fleeing the fighting in the Blue Nile region, as the exodus continues in search of safer areas. The post Displaced people living under dire conditions in Sudan’s Blue Nile State appeared first on Radio Tamazuj .","url":"https://www.radiotamazuj.org/en/news/article/displaced-people-living-under-dire-conditions-in-sudans-blue-nile-state","source":"Radio Tamazuj","image":"","published":"2026-09-07T06:59:22+00:00","category":"conflict","category_label":"Armed Conflict","region":"Horn of Africa","country":"Sudan","fatalities":0,"severity":"Severe","severity_score":2},{"id":"5c83475dc57f","title":"Somali cleric Ali Wajis endorses killing of government critics","summary":"MOGADISHU (Somaliguardian) – A prominent pro-government religious scholar told a gathering of senior Somali officials, including the defense minister, on Sunday that people who oppose the administration of President Hassan Sheikh Mohamud should be treated as terrorists and killed – a statement that has alarmed opposition figures already on edge after a drone strike wounded […]","body":"MOGADISHU (Somaliguardian) – A prominent pro-government religious scholar told a gathering of senior Somali officials, including the defense minister, on Sunday that people who oppose the administration of President Hassan Sheikh Mohamud should be treated as terrorists and killed – a statement that has alarmed opposition figures already on edge after a drone strike wounded a former security chief turned government critic on Thursday. Sheikh Ali Wajis, a cleric known for his consistent public backing of the Federal Government, made the remarks at a meeting attended by government officials, including the Minister of Defense. “Anyone who fights Somalia’s current government should face the same punishment as terrorists and should be killed,” Wajis told the gathering. Neither Wajis nor the Ministry of Defense could be reached for comment. Villa Somalia had not issued a statement responding to the remarks as of publication, and no official who attended Sunday’s meeting has publicly distanced the government from them. Coming on the heels of an attack on an opposition figure Wajis’s remarks land just days after a strike on the residence of Gen. Mahad Abdirahman Aden , widely known as “Taliye Shub,” in the border town of Doolow, in the Gedo region. Gen. Mahad, Somalia’s former Custodial Corps commander, was wounded in the attack along with his wife and several others; at least one person, reported to be an Ethiopian domestic worker, was killed. He was evacuated for treatment across the border in Ethiopia. Gen. Mahad, who was removed from his post earlier this year and briefly placed under house arrest, has since emerged as a vocal critic of the Federal Government and is aligned with the political camp of former Southwest State President Abdiaziz Hassan Mohamed “Laftagareen.” Authorities in Jubaland have accused Turkish military aircraft of carrying out the strike, calling it a deliberate, politically motivated attack tied to Gen. Mahad’s opposition to Mogadishu. In a statement, Jubaland said two missiles were fired, with one also striking the residence of Doolow’s district commissioner. The Federal Government has denied any involvement in an airstrike, offering a different account of what caused the damage to the house. Turkey, which has become one of Somalia’s most significant military partners, providing training, equipment and air support, has not publicly responded to the accusation. The disputed strike triggered protests in Doolow, with residents demanding an independent investigation into who was responsible and why Gen. Mahad’s residence was targeted. A cleric with a history of hardline rhetoric This is not the first time Wajis, who has served as an adviser to successive Somali administrations, has taken a hardline public stance in defense of the government. In April, as Somali National Army desertions mounted during Al-Shabab’s offensive in the Shabelle regions, Wajis publicly branded soldiers who fled their posts as apostates and called for their arrest – remarks that were echoed at the time by a senior Custodial Corps commander, who warned that deserters and their families could face arrest or execution. His latest comments arrive at a moment of unusually sharp strain between Mogadishu and Somalia’s federal member states. Jubaland, Puntland and ousted Southwest administration have all rejected constitutional amendments extending federal institutions’ mandates, and have formally suspended ties with the Federal Government, accusing it of interference in their political and security affairs. Why it matters Coming so soon after an unresolved and contested attack on a prominent opposition-aligned general, a call from a cleric closely associated with the presidency – delivered in front of the defense minister – for opponents of the government to be killed is likely to deepen fears among opposition figures and federal member states that political dissent in Somalia is being conflated with terrorism. Independent verification of the strike’s origin, and any government response to Wajis’s remarks, remained pending as of publication. Contact us: info@somaliguardian.com","url":"https://www.somaliguardian.com/news/somalia-news/somali-cleric-ali-wajis-endorses-killing-government-critics/","source":"Somalia Guardian","image":"","published":"2026-09-06T18:00:11+00:00","category":"terrorism","category_label":"Terrorism","region":"Horn of Africa","country":"Somalia","fatalities":0,"severity":"Moderate","severity_score":0},{"id":"ae05bf99fc90","title":"Foreign Policy Column Urges Trump Administration to Recognize Somaliland","summary":"https://www.horndiplomat.com/feed/ By: Mohamed Duale WASHINGTON, Sept. 6, 2026 (Horndiplomat) — A prominent U.S. foreign-policy analyst has urged President Donald Trump’s administration to recognise Somaliland, arguing that closer relations with Hargeisa would advance American security and economic interests in the strategically important Red Sea region. The commentary, published by Foreign Policy","body":"","url":"https://www.horndiplomat.com/foreign-policy-column-urges-trump-administration-to-recognize-somaliland/","source":"Horn Diplomat","image":"","published":"2026-09-06T14:20:13+00:00","category":"other","category_label":"Other Security News","region":"Horn of Africa","country":"Somaliland","fatalities":0,"severity":"Moderate","severity_score":0},{"id":"43ebaa2194c4","title":"Ethiopia, U.S. Discuss Trade and Horn of Africa Security","summary":"https://www.horndiplomat.com/feed/ By: Mohamed Duale ADDIS ABABA, Sept 6 (Horndiplomat) — Ethiopian Foreign Minister Gedion Timothewos met U.S. Assistant Secretary of State for African Affairs Frank Garcia on Friday for talks aimed at strengthening economic relations and addressing security tensions across the Horn of Africa, officials from both countries said. Ethiopia’s Ministry of Foreign Affai","body":"","url":"https://www.horndiplomat.com/ethiopia-u-s-discuss-trade-and-horn-of-africa-security/","source":"Horn Diplomat","image":"","published":"2026-09-06T14:14:31+00:00","category":"other","category_label":"Other Security News","region":"Horn of Africa","country":"Ethiopia","fatalities":0,"severity":"Moderate","severity_score":0},{"id":"bc90522e2dd0","title":"22 killed in wedding venue fire in DR Congo: Mayor","summary":"Investigation launched to determine cause of deadly blaze, mayor says","body":"At least 22 people were killed and several others injured when a fire broke out at a wedding venue in the Democratic Republic of Congo's capital Kinshasa on Saturday, a local official said.\n\nThe fire erupted during a wedding reception at a building in Kinshasa's Lingwala commune, according to Lingwala Mayor Norbert Mushinga.\n\n\"After inspecting the place, I can confirm that the provisional death toll stands at 22,\" Mushinga told reporters in Kinshasa.\n\n\"People faced difficulties reaching the emergency exit; everyone was in a state of panic,\" he said.\n\nMushinga said property inside the building was also destroyed and several injured people were taken to health facilities in Kinshasa for treatment.\n\nHe said an investigation has been launched to ascertain the cause of the fire, with an electrical fault suspected.\n\nIn a statement, Interior Minister Jacquemain Shabani directed all provincial governors to conduct immediate inspections of venues hosting public events.\n\nShabani ordered authorities to close any establishments that do not meet safety standards.","url":"https://www.aa.com.tr/en/africa/22-killed-in-wedding-venue-fire-in-dr-congo-mayor/4048843","source":"Anadolu · Africa","image":"","published":"2026-09-06T12:16:34+00:00","category":"conflict","category_label":"Armed Conflict","region":"Central Africa","country":"DR Congo","fatalities":22,"severity":"Severe","severity_score":2},{"id":"4ef12160b78b","title":"Int’l coalition condemns attack on WFP aid convoy in Kordofan, Sudan","summary":"The missions of the United Kingdom, Netherlands, Sweden, Ireland, Germany, France, Denmark, Canada, Norway and... The post Int’l coalition condemns attack on WFP aid convoy in Kordofan, Sudan appeared first on Dabanga Radio TV Online .","body":"The missions of the United Kingdom, Netherlands, Sweden, Ireland, Germany, France, Denmark, Canada, Norway and the European Union, as members of the Coalition for Atrocity Prevention and Justice in Sudan, have issued a joint statement to condemn an attack on a World Food Programme (WFP) humanitarian convoy carrying life-saving assistance in Dilling, South Kordofan on 30 August, which resulted in injuries to civilian drivers and damage to humanitarian supplies. As previously reported by Radio Dabanga, the attack on 30 August injured civilian drivers and damaged life-saving humanitarian supplies. The aerial attack on two trucks carrying food aid occurred outside Dilling on 30 August, while carrying WFP assistance to Kadugli. WFP: “More than 50 tonnes of food were destroyed, including cereals, yellow split peas, vegetable oil, and salt. The supplies were enough to provide life-saving assistance to 5,800 people for a month.” The agency says it has recorded eight aerial attacks since the beginning of the year that have damaged its premises and disrupted operations. The statement by the coalition says attacks targeting humanitarian personnel and activities violate international humanitarian law. The convoy’s trucks were clearly marked with World Food Programme emblems, and all required notifications of its movement had been made to the parties to the conflict, it adds. ‘Yet another example of the growing devastation caused by drone warfare on civilians and civilian objects…’ The attack is “yet another example of the growing devastation caused by drone warfare on civilians and civilian objects”, the statement says, pointing to the destruction of markets, health facilities, displacement camps, and other locations critical to civilian survival. The coalition renews its call on all parties to the conflict to protect civilians and humanitarian workers, and to ensure the safe, rapid, and unimpeded passage of humanitarian relief. It also calls on those enabling such violations through arms transfers, including drones, to recognise the consequences of their actions for Sudanese civilians. A credible investigation into the attack, including clear accountability measures where appropriate, must be carried out to prevent a recurrence, the statement says. Repeated strikes on humanitarian convoys and the continued obstruction of humanitarian operations will only deepen the suffering of millions of Sudanese civilians, the coalition warns. It says such actions prevent civilians from accessing life-saving aid, directly contribute to civilian harm, and violate international humanitarian law and the legitimate expectations of the Sudanese people. Share article The post Int’l coalition condemns attack on WFP aid convoy in Kordofan, Sudan appeared first on Dabanga Radio TV Online .","url":"https://www.dabangasudan.org/en/all-news/article/intl-coalition-condemns-attack-on-wfp-aid-convoy-in-south-kordofan","source":"Dabanga Sudan","image":"","published":"2026-09-06T09:38:00+00:00","category":"conflict","category_label":"Armed Conflict","region":"Horn of Africa","country":"Sudan","fatalities":0,"severity":"Moderate","severity_score":0},{"id":"8367b35963d5","title":"Communist Party: ‘Sudan’s war economy deepens the economic abyss’","summary":"Sudan’s Communist Party asserts the war has pushed the country into the gravest economic and... The post Communist Party: ‘Sudan’s war economy deepens the economic abyss’ appeared first on Dabanga Radio TV Online .","body":"Sudan’s Communist Party asserts the war has pushed the country into the gravest economic and social crisis in its modern history, creating a parallel economy in which military and commercial interests intertwine with smuggling networks. A detailed statement by the party’s Economic Committee*, seen by Radio Dabanga, suggests that the war has compounded a structural economic crisis built up over decades of failed policies, uneven development, a weakened productive sector, corruption, and reliance on rents and levies rather than productive activity. Since the war began in April 2023, it says, resources have been drained into armed conflict rather than production, development, and public services. State institutions have collapsed, infrastructure has been destroyed, agriculture, industry, and trade have been disrupted, and unemployment, poverty, hunger, and food insecurity have risen. The party says the signs of economic collapse include the absence of an auditor-general’s report on what it describes as opaque annual budgets whose details are not published. The war, it says, has halted most economic activity, including industry and agriculture, put much of the banking system out of operation, weakened exports, reduced imports, and driven up inflation and unemployment. From crisis to the abyss The Communist Party statement asserts Sudan lost a cumulative 43% of its real output in 2023 and 2024 as economic activity stopped across large parts of the country. It cites estimates by the Central Bureau of Statistics indicating that 82.8% of the population lives below the poverty line, while food insecurity has spread. Inflation, it says, has remained among the highest in the world, exceeding 200% at times, with prices continuing to rise far faster than incomes. Gold trading in Sudan (File photo: RD) The Sudanese pound has lost most of its value, the party says. The US dollar rose in the parallel market from about SDG 1,100–1,300 during 2024 to nearly SDG 2,900–3,200 by mid-2025, an increase of more than 150% in a year, pushing up the cost of imports and basic goods. By August and September 2026, it says, the dollar had surpassed SDG 6,000. The customs exchange rate has also risen several times since the start of 2024, the party says, increasing the prices of imported goods and the cost of living. It says the rate exceeded SDG 3,900 in August 2026. Public revenues have fallen sharply, with the authorities estimating an 80% decline, while the state has become increasingly dependent on fees and levies borne by citizens and producers. Mill used to grind gtold-bearing ore (Archive photo: Supplied) Purchasing power collapses The most dangerous aspect of the crisis, the party says, is the near-total collapse in the purchasing power of wages. Salaries for civil servants, teachers, health workers, engineers, labourers, and pensioners now cover only a small fraction of a family’s basic needs, it says. “Estimates of living costs indicate that average income may cover only about 8–10% of a family’s needs, meaning the living-cost gap exceeds 90%,” the statement says. Millions of families have consequently reduced spending on food, medicine, and education, and have become dependent on debt or aid, it says. The party argues that the crisis is not the product of war alone, but also of economic policies based on adherence to the recommendations and policies of the International Monetary Fund and the World Bank. It says Sudan’s debt burden has exceeded $66bn, while neither donors nor the government have provided transparency over how the debts were spent or who benefited from them. The party calls for a review of those debts, greater transparency from donors, and the cancellation of debts from which the Sudanese people did not benefit, rather than continued adherence to the recommendations of international institutions and further austerity policies that it says are hostile to economic and social development. Sudan’s Ministy of Finance (Archive photo: Sudan Govt.) Resources, privatisation, and security companies The party identifies the plundering of Sudan’s resources, including gold, gum arabic, and copper, as another factor in the crisis, along with the dismantling of electricity infrastructure. It also points to what it describes as the repeated and organised targeting of mining sites, the seizure of miners’ production and equipment, and the killing of miners. Another factor, it says, is the sale of public-sector institutions under the banner of privatisation, the failure to support productive sectors, the loss of major sources of public revenue, and the growing reliance on taxes and fees. It says corruption has also expanded, acting as a form of indirect taxation whose burden falls disproportionately on poor and low-income people. Public institutions, the statement argues, have been replaced by monopolistic security companies that do not disclose their activities, their borrowing from public funds, their contribution to state revenues, or how they spend their money. The disruption of agricultural production and mass displacement have also expanded the informal sector, it says, while people in conflict areas and displacement centres face daily struggles to earn a living, particularly women who are responsible for their households. The party also cites weak humanitarian access and what it describes as signs of systematic corruption in the distribution of aid. Among other factors, it points to higher prices justified by the removal of subsidies, expanding levies and fees on producers and citizens, neglect of agriculture, industry, and national projects, weak oversight of the gold and foreign-exchange markets, and a lack of transparency and accountability in the management of public resources. “The war has become a means of draining resources, while people alone pay the price of collapse,” the party statement says. Gum Arabic — a natural ingredient widely used in soft drinks, cosmetics, and pharmaceutical products — remains one of the country’s most internationally significant exports. Before the war, Sudan accounted for an estimated 70 per cent to 80 per cent of global crude gum arabic exports (File photo: Radio Dabanga / Andrew Bergman) An existential crisis The party says Sudan’s economic crisis is no longer merely a financial or monetary one, but a comprehensive humanitarian and cost-of-living crisis threatening the survival of millions. It says the economy contracted by about 29.4% in 2023, followed by a further 14% in 2024, making it one of the sharpest economic contractions experienced by any economy in recent years. State revenues, it says, fell from about 10% of GDP in 2022 to less than 5% in 2024 and 2025, sharply reducing the state’s ability to finance basic services, including health, education, water, and electricity. Prices rose cumulatively by about 848% between 2023 and 2025, the party statement says, adding that a basket of goods costing SDG 100 before the war would now cost about SDG 948. It says the rising dollar and the collapse in the value of the Sudanese pound are the result of the interaction between war, collapsing production and exports, budget deficits, monetary expansion, weak reserves, and high demand for foreign currency. Food insecurity has meanwhile reached what the party describes as one of the world’s worst humanitarian crises. About 19.5 million people face acute food insecurity, it says, while hundreds of thousands are living in catastrophic conditions approaching famine in some areas. The party statement says more than 40% of the population faces acute food insecurity as hunger continues to spread because of war, disrupted agricultural production, displacement, and difficulties in delivering humanitarian aid. (Graphic RD) Gold and the war economy Since April 2023, the party statement says, the war has created a parallel economy in which military and commercial interests intersect with smuggling networks, while transparency and public oversight have deteriorated sharply. Gold has become the clearest example of a national resource being turned into a means of financing the war rather than a source of public revenue, it says. According to Bank of Sudan data cited by the party, gold production reached about 70.15 tonnes in 2025, while officially recorded exports amounted to only 14 tonnes. More than 56 tonnes therefore did not pass through official channels, according to that comparison. The party statement says gold has become one of the most important sources of funding for both sides in the war, with its revenues used to buy weapons, fuel, ammunition, drones, and other military supplies. The problem extends beyond smuggling, it argues, to the absence of public information and institutional oversight of revenues, spending, contracts, and resources. During wartime, it says, spending outside the budget expands, while financial resources and public assets can be systematically plundered without accountability. The party also ","url":"https://www.dabangasudan.org/en/all-news/article/communist-party-sudans-war-economy-deepens-the-economic-abyss","source":"Dabanga Sudan","image":"","published":"2026-09-06T08:50:00+00:00","category":"conflict","category_label":"Armed Conflict","region":"Horn of Africa","country":"Sudan","fatalities":0,"severity":"Moderate","severity_score":0},{"id":"d8aa34a6c9fd","title":"Spanish opposition indulges in warmongering against Morocco turning Ceuta crisis into proxy battle","summary":"The mass irregular crossings into Ceuta at the end of July 2026 have been transformed into a domestic political football in Spain. Opposition parties are competing in increasingly confrontational language that critics describe as warmongering, while indulging anti-Moroccan sentiment and reopening centuries-old territorial and historical wounds, often without presenting whatsoever evidence of high-","body":"The mass irregular crossings into Ceuta at the end of July 2026 have been transformed into a domestic political football in Spain.\n\nOpposition parties are competing in increasingly confrontational language that critics describe as warmongering, while indulging anti-Moroccan sentiment and reopening centuries-old territorial and historical wounds, often without presenting whatsoever evidence of high-level Moroccan state responsibility.\n\nDuring Pedro Sánchez’s parliamentary appearance on 3 September, PP leader Alberto Núñez Feijóo and Vox leader Santiago Abascal led the charge.\n\nFeijóo asserted that the operation “came from Morocco, was consented by Morocco, and there are indications that Morocco coordinated it,” describing it as a possible act of hybrid warfare and an ongoing “invasion.”\n\nAbascal went further, insisting Ceuta had been “taken by force,” calling the events an outright “invasion” and part of a “hybrid war” perpetrated by Morocco, while labelling Sánchez the “ambassador of Morocco in Spain” and Morocco his “master.”\n\nBoth framed the crisis as a deliberate assault on Spanish sovereignty rather than a complex migratory episode driven by multiple factors, including social-media amplification and local capacity strain and legal pull factors created by Madrid.\n\nSimilar accusations of an “attack” by Morocco came from some government partners, including voices from ERC and Sumar.\n\nYet Sánchez maintained that no solid evidence had been delivered to the government proving Moroccan authorities planned or executed the crossings.\n\nMeanwhile in Spanish streets on 2 September, demonstrations in support of Ceuta took place. Outside the Congress in Madrid, members of the neonazi group Núcleo Nacional joined the gathering.\n\nParticipants chanted slogans including “musulmán el que no bote” (a variant of the football-style chant meaning “the one who doesn’t jump is a Muslim”), “Spain is Christiana and not Muslim” and other anti-migrant chants.\n\nPolice later dispersed parts of the protest after objects were thrown and racist chants intensified. Similar far-right elements appeared in other cities, using the crisis to push broader anti-Muslim and anti-Moroccan messaging.\n\nOn the military side, retired and serving senior officers have privately discussed the risks of future scenarios with Spanish media highlighting the state of fear in Spain. Some urged clearer deterrent messaging and strengthened presence in the occupied enclave of Ceuta.\n\nAs anti-Moroccan circles in Spain instrumentalize the kingdom in their domestic proxy battles, they take for granted the essential role Morocco continues to play in curbing irregular flows.\n\nThrough sustained interceptions, dismantling of smuggling networks and close operational cooperation with Spain, Moroccan efforts have helped keep pressure on the Western Mediterranean route comparatively contained relative to other corridors, a dynamic regularly reflected in IOM tracking of arrivals and route trends.\n\nThis escalatory rhetoric does not go unnoticed in Rabat, which has so far treated the exchanges as internal proxy battles.\n\nSpanish Prime Minister Pedro Sanchez firmly rejected any suggestion of Moroccan involvement in the mass migrant crossings into Ceuta (Sebta) at the end of July, instead praising Rabat’s “instantaneous” and “frankly positive” cooperation. Speaking on Cadena SER’s Hoy por Hoy program on August 31, Sánchez addressed the events of July 30 and 31, when tens […]\n\nTwo figures of the Spanish opposition expressed their opposition to Spain’s plans to co-host the FIFA 2030 World Cup with Portugal and Morocco as they question the advantage of a joint bid. The objections came from the People’s party figure, Vicente Azpitarte and Roberto Nunez of Citizens-Party, who were both of them attending a meeting […]","url":"https://northafricapost.com/100057-spanish-opposition-indulges-in-warmongering-against-morocco-turning-ceuta-crisis-into-proxy-battle.html","source":"North Africa Post","image":"","published":"2026-09-05T17:45:38+00:00","category":"conflict","category_label":"Armed Conflict","region":"North Africa","country":"Morocco","fatalities":0,"severity":"Moderate","severity_score":0},{"id":"8f0b8ac8981d","title":"Somalia: Fahad Yasin links Jama to Gen. Shub drone strike","summary":"MOGADISHU (Somaliguardian) – Somalia’s former intelligence chief Fahad Yasin has accused the Speaker of the House of the People, Abdulkadir Mohamed Nur – widely known by the nicknames “Jama” and “Minister TikTok” – of directing the drone strike that hit the Doolow residence of former Custodial Corps commander Gen. Mahad Abdirahman Aden late Thursday. Gen. […]","body":"MOGADISHU (Somaliguardian) – Somalia’s former intelligence chief Fahad Yasin has accused the Speaker of the House of the People, Abdulkadir Mohamed Nur – widely known by the nicknames “Jama” and “Minister TikTok” – of directing the drone strike that hit the Doolow residence of former Custodial Corps commander Gen. Mahad Abdirahman Aden late Thursday. Gen. Mahad, popularly known as “General Shub,” survived the overnight attack in the Gedo region town near the Ethiopian border. Jubaland’s Ministry of Information, Culture and Tourism said five people in total were hurt: Gen. Mahad, who sustained minor injuries, and four civilians, including his wife. The allegation In a Facebook statement, Yasin warned that the strike would carry “dangerous repercussions capable of spreading beyond Somalia to neighboring countries,” a remark he made in the context of accusing President Hassan Sheikh Mohamud’s administration of crossing a dangerous line. Yasin said Nur first demonstrated what he described as command responsibility for lethal operations in December 2017, when he served as acting head of the National Intelligence and Security Agency (NISA) and, according to Yasin, led the raid on the Mogadishu home of opposition politician Abdirahman Abdishakur Warsame. Yasin alleged that Nur – who is now serving as acting president while President Hassan Sheikh Mohamud is on an overseas trip – has again used that authority, this time to direct the strike on Gen. Mahad. Competing accounts of the attack Jubaland’s Ministry of Information, Culture and Tourism said Turkish military aircraft carried out the overnight operation, adding that two missiles were fired – one of which struck the home of Doolow’s district chairman. Other Jubaland officials and prominent political figures, including former presidents Sharif Sheikh Ahmed and Mohamed Abdullahi Farmaajo and the opposition Somali Future Council, have echoed the drone-strike account and demanded an independent investigation. The Federal Government has denied involvement and disputed that a drone strike took place at all. Its preliminary investigation, officials said, indicates the blast at Gen. Mahad’s residence originated from explosives being handled inside the house rather than from an external strike. Turkey, which operates a military base in Mogadishu and trains and equips federal government forces, has not responded to the allegations that its aircraft were involved. Contact us: info@somaliguardian.com","url":"https://www.somaliguardian.com/news/somalia-news/somalia-fahad-yasin-links-speaker-jama-drone-strike-gen-shub/","source":"Somalia Guardian","image":"","published":"2026-09-05T16:59:59+00:00","category":"conflict","category_label":"Armed Conflict","region":"Horn of Africa","country":"Somalia","fatalities":0,"severity":"Moderate","severity_score":0},{"id":"a6598fb928f3","title":"Sahara: Denmark Joins Countries Supporting Morocco-Proposed Autonomy Plan","summary":"Morocco has gained the support of another European country for its territorial integrity and sovereignty over its Sahara ahead of the upcoming UN Security Council meeting planned on Sahara by end of October. During his trip paid Friday to Copenhagen, Moroccan foreign minister Nasser Bourita has secured the backing of his Danish peer Lars Løkke […]","body":"Morocco has gained the support of another European country for its territorial integrity and sovereignty over its Sahara ahead of the upcoming UN Security Council meeting planned on Sahara by end of October.\n\nDuring his trip paid Friday to Copenhagen, Moroccan foreign minister Nasser Bourita has secured the backing of his Danish peer Lars Løkke Rasmussen to the Autonomy Plan offered by Morocco under its sovereignty for a lasting resolution of the Sahara regional conflict.\n\nIn a press statement following his talks with Moroccan top diplomat, Mr. Lars Løkke has described the Moroccan Autonomy plan as a “credible” proposal that it “brings in a serious and essential contribution to the ongoing UN process,” saying the Moroccan plan is a “good basis for reaching a mutually agreed solution by all parties.”\n\nHe also said his country stands firmly behind the European Union’s position which welcomes the adoption by UNSC of Resolution 2797, endorsing the Autonomy plan under Moroccan sovereignty.\n\nDenmark is currently serving as a non-permanent member of the UNSC, which is expected to take in October pivotal decisions on the Sahara and MINURSO.\n\nIn a statement to the danish media, Lars Løkke Rasmussen said that Europe and Denmark need a reliable friend like Morocco, and that his meeting with Bourita produced positive results, which will be crowned by a strategic partnership agreement covering the political, security and economic dimensions, particularly issues related to climate and investment.\n\nThis agreement, he stressed, is expected to be concluded next December in Rabat, noting that the two countries are taking the necessary time to reach a deal that will benefit both Morocco and Denmark. The deal will also provide for the establishment of a chamber of commerce.\n\nRasmussen further said that he discussed with his Moroccan counterpart bilateral security cooperation, particularly the extradition of criminals, an issue that will be further addressed as part of the strategic agreement currently being prepared.\n\nKing Mohammed VI stressed the need for closing ranks in order to counter the scourge of extremism and terrorism that targets innocents and hinders efforts towards coexistence, social progress and prosperity. “As ignorance spreads in the name of religion, Muslims, Christians and Jews have to close ranks in order to tackle all forms of extremism, […]\n\nMorocco has expressed concern about the United Nations Organization’s biased stands when it comes to the issue of human rights in Western Sahara and in the Algeria-based Tindouf camps that are run by the separatist Polisario Front. The worries were reignited by the report that the UN Secretary General Ban Ki -Moon is to submit […]\n\nMorocco’s King inaugurated on Friday in Tangier the Mohammed VI University Hospital Center, a new medical facility offering to the inhabitants of the Northern region comprehensive healthcare services and advanced training to nurses, doctors and phamacists. The new medical complex has a 797-bed capacity. It is built on a 23-hectare site located near the regional […]","url":"https://northafricapost.com/100054-sahara-denmark-joins-countries-supporting-morocco-proposed-autonomy-plan.html","source":"North Africa Post","image":"","published":"2026-09-05T14:40:34+00:00","category":"other","category_label":"Other Security News","region":"North Africa","country":"Morocco","fatalities":0,"severity":"Moderate","severity_score":0},{"id":"5ffde45c726d","title":"Niger blames France for inciting failed mutiny, Paris denies involvement","summary":"Niger has blamed France for inciting last week’s failed military mutiny, according ‌to a government statement read on television on Friday. Paris has denied any involvement in the attack.","body":"Niger has blamed France for inciting last week’s thwarted military mutiny , according ‌to a government statement read on television on Friday and cited by Reuters and AFP.\n\nOn August 29, disgruntled soldiers attacked a strategic army base in Niamey and fired shots near the presidential palace. But the army and its Russian paramilitary allies eventually neutralised the mutiny .\n\nThe unrest killed three members of the presidential guard, according to Niger's Foreign Affairs Ministry. AFP reported a total number of five deaths, including civilians.\n\nOn Friday, Niger’s Council of Ministers said it had concluded that the attack had been incited by ‌a ⁠vast network of accomplices led by France, Niger's former colonial power.\n\n\"A substantial body of evidence has been compiled regarding this attack, and Niger ​reserves the ​right to ⁠bring the matter before international courts,\" the government said in the ​statement quoted by Reuters\n\nIn response, French Foreign Minister Jean-Noël Barrot denied any involvement of France in the attack.\n\n\"These are nothing but pure fantasies,\" Barrot told French radio station RFI on Saturday. \"All of this is, as usual, a way of wrongly blaming France for something it is simply not responsible for.\"\n\nAccording to RFI, on Tuesday already, a Nigerian military official had appeared on television to accuse several states, including France, Ivory Coast and Benin, of supporting the mutiny.\n\nLike its fellow junta-run Sahel neighbours in Burkina Faso and Mali, Niger has turned its back on Paris since the 2023 coup that brought General Abdourahamane Tiani to power.\n\nAll three have sought closer ties with Russia in tackling the region's deadly jihadist conflict, which has continued to rage unabated.","url":"http://www.africanews.com/2026/09/05/niger-blames-france-for-inciting-failed-mutiny-paris-denies-involvement/","source":"Africanews","image":"http://images.euronews.com/articles/stories/09/90/25/76/640x360_cmsv2_2cb0eb50-c60e-5842-b97d-a33ef2de4780-9902576.jpg?1788606834","published":"2026-09-05T12:35:20+00:00","category":"conflict","category_label":"Armed Conflict","region":"Sahel","country":"Niger","fatalities":0,"severity":"Moderate","severity_score":0},{"id":"b43eb0d3cfcb","title":"Ethiopia questions Somali soldiers who crossed border to visit families in Somalia","summary":"MOGADISHU (Somaliguardian) – Ethiopia’s security agencies have begun interrogating dozens of Somali National Army (SNA) soldiers who crossed into Ethiopian territory after completing training in Somalia’s Galmudug region, according to reports by regional media. Around 80 soldiers crossed into Ethiopia’s Somali region following the training, which was funded by Saudi Arabia and saw roughly 3,000 […","body":"MOGADISHU (Somaliguardian) – Ethiopia’s security agencies have begun interrogating dozens of Somali National Army (SNA) soldiers who crossed into Ethiopian territory after completing training in Somalia’s Galmudug region, according to reports by regional media. Around 80 soldiers crossed into Ethiopia’s Somali region following the training, which was funded by Saudi Arabia and saw roughly 3,000 SNA members graduate at once. The soldiers have since been taken to Jigjiga, the capital of Ethiopia’s Somali region. Most of the soldiers are reported to have come from Somalia’s Bakol region and were on their way to their original homes at the time. The soldiers were reportedly on authorized leave, as confirmed by Somalia’s National Security Advisor Aways Hajji Yusuf, and had chosen to take a long detour through Ethiopian territory for security reasons, in order to avoid possible attacks by Al-Shabaab. The militant group controls major roads connecting Somalia’s Hiran and Bakol regions along the border with Ethiopia. The soldiers were recruited from El Barde in Bakol region and nearby villages, and mostly belong to the Ogaden clan. Latest reports indicate that both Ethiopia’s Somali region and the federal government in Addis Ababa were fully aware of the arrangement. It is not clear, however, why the soldiers have been taken to Jigjiga for interrogation. There has been no announcement from Ethiopia, the Somali region, or the Somali federal government about the current location of the troops or the wider circumstances surrounding the case. Contact us: info@somaliguardian.com","url":"https://www.somaliguardian.com/news/somalia-news/ethiopia-questions-somali-soldiers-border-crossing-families-somalia/","source":"Somalia Guardian","image":"","published":"2026-09-05T10:16:25+00:00","category":"terrorism","category_label":"Terrorism","region":"Horn of Africa","country":"Ethiopia","fatalities":0,"severity":"Severe","severity_score":2},{"id":"73e003a19a11","title":"Guinea buries 36 victims of Conakry landfill collapse","summary":"Thousands of people attended a mass funeral held in Guinea on Friday for the 36 people killed in a landfill collapse in a suburb of the capital last month.","body":"Guinea held a mass funeral attended by thousands on Friday for the 36 people killed in a landfill collapse in a suburb of the capital last month following heavy rains.\n\nMany of the victims were sleeping when part of the massive garbage dump, located near homes in the Dar-es-Salam neighbourhood, gave way overnight between August 22 and 23.\n\nDuring the ceremony Friday, authorities announced an updated toll of 36, including that there were 17 Sierra Leonean nationals among the dead.\n\nThe three dozen bodies were taken to a mosque in Bambeto, a Conakry suburb, for Muslim funeral prayers.\n\nMany mourners struggled to make sense of the tragedy . \"We are completely disillusioned. We are deeply troubled by this tragedy. We don’t know what to do,\" said Aboubacar Camara, who survived the collapse.\n\nAmong the crowd of thousands, several women overcome with grief fell into a trance-like state, an AFP journalist witnessed.\n\n\"Guinea is in mourning, and we share the suffering of the victims' loved ones,\" Conakry Governor General Mahawa Cisse said during the ceremony.\n\nAfterwards, the bodies were taken to the Bambeto cemetery for their burial, attended by family, religious leaders and government officials.\n\nAround twenty people remain hospitalised, and more than 1,600 people have received assistance, according to the country's emergency management agency.\n\nSince the collapse, authorities have been demolishing houses in the zone considered at risk from the rubbish dump, citing the need to protect the population from another tragedy.\n\nThe residents have criticised the difficult conditions under which the operation was carried out and are calling for relocation assistance.","url":"http://www.africanews.com/2026/09/05/guinea-buries-36-victims-of-conakry-landfill-collapse/","source":"Africanews","image":"http://images.euronews.com/articles/stories/09/90/23/18/640x360_cmsv2_8b60d521-0e10-539d-95c4-bb5a097e42e8-9902318.jpg?1788588376","published":"2026-09-05T07:38:38+00:00","category":"conflict","category_label":"Armed Conflict","region":"West Africa","country":"Guinea","fatalities":36,"severity":"Severe","severity_score":2},{"id":"b74afdfc67f9","title":"EXPLAINER - From Ethiopia border to Darfur: Where are Sudan’s war fronts heating up?","summary":"Blue Nile, Kordofan, Darfur emerge as key theaters as army, RSF, allied forces battle for strategic towns, supply routes, border areas","body":"Sudan’s most active battlefronts stretch across a vast arc from the Blue Nile state near the Ethiopian border in the east, through three Kordofan states, to Darfur in the west.\n\nAl-Baraka, Bakori, Geissan and Kurmuk in the Blue Nile; Bara, Al-Abbasiya and Kauda in Kordofan; and Um Baru, Kernoi and Abu Suruj in Darfur have emerged as some of the most significant centers of fighting and military movement.\n\nControl of the areas is divided among the army, the paramilitary Rapid Support Forces (RSF) and armed groups allied with each side. Drone attacks have increasingly carried the war into towns and villages far from traditional front lines.\n\nAnadolu reviewed the main hotspots, their military significance and the current balance of control.\n\nAl-Baraka, in the Kurmuk locality in the southeastern Blue Nile state, has become one of the newest fronts between the army on one side and the RSF and its ally, the Sudan People’s Liberation Movement-North (SPLM-N), on the other.\n\nThe area lies on the outskirts of Kurmuk near the Ethiopian border, giving it strategic importance for movements between border zones and the interior of Blue Nile.\n\nOn Friday, the army said it repelled an attack by the RSF and SPLM-N on Al-Baraka.\n\nThe army’s 4th Infantry Division said forces inflicted personnel and equipment losses on the attackers and forced them to retreat toward the Sudan-Ethiopia border.\n\nBakori, in the Geissan locality, is another key hotspot in Blue Nile.\n\nThe area hosts a military base and the headquarters of the army’s 14th Brigade, making it a contested position for both sides.\n\nBakori lies 170 kilometers (106 miles) southeast of Damazin, the state capital.\n\nIn recent weeks, the RSF and SPLM-N have taken control of Geissan and Kurmuk, the administrative centers of the two localities bearing the same names.\n\nThe army, however, continues to hold other parts of both localities, as well as Damazin, Er Roseires and the localities of Bau, Wad al-Mahi and Tadamon.\n\nBeyond Kurmuk and Geissan, the SPLM-N, led by Abdelaziz al-Hilu, has controlled Yabus since 2011.\n\nThe area, one of the movement’s main strongholds in Blue Nile, lies near the borders with Ethiopia and South Sudan, around 80 kilometers from Kurmuk.\n\nThe SPLM-N has fought the Sudanese government since 2011, demanding greater autonomy in Blue Nile and South Kordofan before joining forces with the RSF during the current war.\n\nIn North Kordofan, Bara remains one of the most strategically important towns because of its location on the national highway linking it to Omdurman, west of the national capital, Khartoum.\n\nIn late July, the army retook Bara, Jabra al-Sheikh and Um Sayala, allowing it to secure the highway and reshape the military balance in the state bordering Darfur and West Kordofan.\n\nFighting around Bara has since shifted largely from direct ground clashes to drone attacks on military positions and villages surrounding the town.\n\nVillages west of Bara that contain no military deployments have also come under RSF attacks, according to the Emergency Lawyers and the Sudan Doctors Network.\n\nOther military movements are concentrated in northwestern North Kordofan, where the RSF controls Hamrat al-Sheikh, Al-Mazroub and Sodari.\n\nTheir importance stems from their proximity to Darfur and West Kordofan, and their location along movement and supply routes connecting the two regions.\n\nNorth Kordofan has also faced repeated drone attacks, alongside similar strikes in South and West Kordofan.\n\nIn South Kordofan, Kadugli and Dilling remain among the most important army-held positions.\n\nThe army broke the siege on both cities months ago, and strengthened its control in large parts of the state, except for areas still held by the SPLM-N under al-Hilu.\n\nAlthough major ground fighting around the two cities has subsided, both continue to face intermittent drone attacks that have killed and wounded civilians.\n\nThe Al-Abbasiya locality has become another hotspot in South Kordofan amid repeated attacks and deteriorating security conditions.\n\nOn Thursday, the International Organization for Migration (IOM) said 6,420 people had been displaced from nine villages in Al-Abbasiya because of worsening insecurity from Sept. 1 to 2.\n\nKauda, in the far south of South Kordofan, remains under SPLM-N control and is one of the movement’s most important strongholds.\n\nAlongside its role in the broader conflict between the SPLM-N and the government, Kauda has also witnessed local fighting between SPLM-N forces and members of the Otoro tribe because of a land dispute.\n\nThe clashes began in late June after a dispute about border demarcation between the Shawaya and Otoro tribes escalated into armed conflict, killing dozens and displacing thousands.\n\nNorth Darfur remains one of the most active fronts in the Darfur region, with the army and the Joint Force of Armed Movements conducting operations against the RSF.\n\nThe Joint Force includes armed groups such as the Sudan Liberation Movement, led by Minni Arko Minnawi, and the Justice and Equality Movement, led by Jibril Ibrahim.\n\nUm Baru and Karnoi have emerged as key hotspots after the army and Joint Force gained control of large parts of both localities.\n\nTheir proximity to the Chadian border makes them important bases for advancing into other parts of North Darfur and securing supply routes in the state.\n\nIn West Darfur, army and Joint Force movements have been concentrated north of El Geneina, the state capital.\n\nIn June, the forces captured Kulbus, about 160 kilometers north of El Geneina, before withdrawing more than two weeks later and repositioning around the town.\n\nThe army and the Joint Force later took Bir Saliba and Jebel Moon north of El Geneina as part of efforts to expand their foothold in the state.\n\nOn Aug. 29, West Darfur Gov. Bahr al-Din Adam Karama said the army and the Joint Force had taken control of Abu Suruj in the Sirba locality following clashes with the RSF.\n\nAbu Suruj is among the latest areas where territorial control has shifted in West Darfur as military movements continue north of El Geneina.\n\nThe RSF and allied forces still control most of Darfur, while the army and the Joint Force maintain positions in parts of the region’s north and west.\n\nDespite the different strategic value of each front, displacement remains the most consistent consequence of the widening conflict.\n\nIn Blue Nile, the IOM said 3,000 people had been displaced from three villages in the Kurmuk locality, while the UN estimated that 22,000 people fled fighting in Geissan in one week.\n\nIn South Kordofan, 6,420 people were displaced from villages in Al-Abbasiya, in addition to thousands forced from their homes by the conflict in Kauda.\n\nThe growing use of drones has also expanded the threat well beyond frontline areas. The UN said 1,100 civilians were killed in drone attacks between January and June.\n\nThe army and RSF have been at war since April 2023 because of disputes surrounding the political transition and plans to integrate the paramilitary force into the regular military.\n\nAs front lines continue to shift between Blue Nile, Kordofan and Darfur, Sudan’s map of military hotspots remains fluid, with no clear prospect of an end to the war.","url":"https://www.aa.com.tr/en/africa/explainer-from-ethiopia-border-to-darfur-where-are-sudan-s-war-fronts-heating-up/4047920","source":"Anadolu · Africa","image":"","published":"2026-09-05T06:19:05+00:00","category":"conflict","category_label":"Armed Conflict","region":"Horn of Africa","country":"Ethiopia","fatalities":0,"severity":"Moderate","severity_score":0},{"id":"44a296a27a0f","title":"SPLA-IO spokesman rules out armed infighting despite leadership crisis","summary":"JUBA – South Sudan’s main armed opposition group will not descend into armed infighting despite a deepening leadership crisis that has split senior officials over who should run the movement in the absence of detained leader Riek Machar, its military spokesman said on Friday. Speaking to Sudans Post in an interview on Friday, SPLA-IO spokesman […] The post SPLA-IO spokesman rules out armed infight","body":"SPLA-IO deputy spokesman Col. Lam Paul Gabriel speaks to media about the progress and challenges facing the transitional security arrangement in Juba on October 2, 2019. [Photo by Getty Images] JUBA – South Sudan’s main armed opposition group will not descend into armed infighting despite a deepening leadership crisis that has split senior officials over who should run the movement in the absence of detained leader Riek Machar, its military spokesman said on Friday.\n\nSpeaking to Sudans Post in an interview on Friday, SPLA-IO spokesman Col. Lam Paul Gabriel described the dispute as primarily an administrative and constitutional disagreement, ruling out the possibility that it would lead to armed clashes between forces aligned with the rival leadership camps.\n\n“What is currently happening is administrative issues, understanding the constitution of the movement and how it’s supposed to function,” Lam said.\n\n“It is not really something that will trigger violence, as people might think, that maybe there will be clashes between the SPLA-IO forces. That is not going to happen because the SPLA-IO is bigger than an individual or a few individuals.”\n\nThe comments follow days of turmoil within the SPLM/A-IO after Oyet Nathaniel Pierino, Machar’s deputy who had been serving as acting chairman and commander-in-chief, suspended interim Secretary-General Regina Joseph Kaba and seven other senior officials on August 31.\n\nOyet accused the officials of trying to undermine his authority, establish parallel structures and separate the movement’s political and military wings. He described their actions as a “conspiracy and coup attempt” and said his measures were intended to prevent the SPLM-IO from collapsing and disintegrating.\n\nKaba and other Political Bureau members rejected Oyet’s move and issued a counter-order suspending him , accusing him of defying collective leadership and taking unilateral decisions that had contributed to deaths and divisions within the movement.\n\nLam said the SPLA-IO military command had endorsed the Political Bureau’s decision and no longer regarded Oyet as the movement’s acting leader.\n\n“Honorable Oyet Nathaniel is not currently the acting chair of the SPLM-IO, but he is just a member,” Lam said. “He will wait for that to be revoked or for him to be reinstated. I don’t know when, but that is something that can only happen when there is another sitting probably done and when there is an investigation as to why he got suspended.”\n\nHe said Kaba was now in charge of the political wing while Lt. Gen. Peter Thok headed the military as acting chief of general staff, with communication between the political leadership and forces expected to pass through the military chain of command.\n\nSenior SPLA-IO commanders meeting this week adopted the Political Bureau resolutions, recognized Kaba as Acting Interim Deputy Chair and reaffirmed Thok as the only Acting Chief of General Staff, according to a statement issued by Lam.\n\nLam also said Machar had previously instructed Oyet to halt military deployments and political assignments after some of the changes allegedly contributed to deaths in Nasir and Panyijiar.\n\n“Due to that, we were able to receive a message from our chair and commander-in-chief, which was sent also to Honorable Nathaniel to stop all these deployments for now to avoid the death of these innocent civilians or some soldiers who might have taken matters into their hands,” Lam said.\n\nHe said the communication also made clear that responsibility for the army rested with Thok and that instructions to SPLA-IO forces should pass through the acting chief of general staff.\n\nLam, however, stopped short of saying that Machar had personally approved the Political Bureau’s latest decision to suspend Oyet.\n\n“One thing I’ll be very sincere with you is that when it comes to the status of Dr. Riek currently, I can’t tell whether he is communicating or is not communicating,” he said.\n\nLam said he nevertheless believed Machar was aware of the developments, arguing that information could reach him despite his detention.\n\n“I can’t rule out completely that he did not get this information,” Lam said. “I know he got and I believe he got this information that Honorable Regina is now the acting interim deputy chair of the SPLM/SPLA.”\n\nLam provided no evidence that Machar had received the Political Bureau resolution, and his remarks reflected his belief rather than confirmation of direct communication with the detained opposition leader.\n\nMachar has been detained in Juba since March 2025 following a political and security crisis triggered by fighting in Nasir. His prolonged absence has left the movement grappling with questions over how far officials acting on his behalf can exercise his political and military powers.\n\nThe SPLM-IO’s official website continued to list Machar as chairman and commander-in-chief and Oyet as deputy chairman this week, though the site may not yet reflect the latest internal decisions.\n\nAsked about statements from some commanders and structures continuing to recognize Oyet, Lam said individual officers could support him personally but insisted that such declarations did not represent the SPLA-IO as an institution.\n\n“The army, the SPLM/SPLA or the SPLA, does not belong to an individual. Let’s just put this clear and everybody should understand this,” he said. “It belongs to the system. It belongs to this movement.”\n\nLam acknowledged that Oyet retained support among some soldiers, which he linked partly to Oyet’s continued public campaign for Machar’s release.\n\n“If Honorable Oyet moves aside just a little bit and says, ‘I don’t understand the document,’ you will see how many people will say no to him,” Lam said.\n\n“But it is because he still says that, ‘I am still fighting for the release of Dr. Machar.’ Some soldiers still have got that respect for him for that. And that’s why they are standing with him.”\n\nHe said declarations of support for Oyet by individual military figures should not be confused with the official position of the army.\n\n“If there are individuals within the army that want to say that they stand with Nathaniel, these are individuals, but not the SPLM-IO,” he said.\n\nLam rejected suggestions that the current turmoil demonstrated that Machar had failed to prepare the movement to survive his absence.\n\nHe said SPLA-IO soldiers remained loyal to Machar and argued that the movement had not prepared for such a situation because Machar had instead sought to unify opposition and government forces under the 2018 peace agreement.\n\n“These soldiers are loyal to Dr. Machar. That’s why up to today, they are dying. They are fighting, they are defending themselves, they are defending the people under their control because of Dr. Machar,” Lam said.\n\nHe said Machar’s intention upon returning to Juba was not to preserve the SPLA-IO indefinitely as a separate force, but to have the rival armies unified into a national military under the peace deal.\n\n“That was why we never prepared for this situation,” Lam said. “So it’s not the fault of Dr. Machar. It is just because he loved peace and he wanted change in this country.”\n\nLam acknowledged, however, that there was bitterness among SPLA-IO soldiers over the decision to return to Juba and place their faith in the peace process.\n\n“People, in fact, including myself, I’m bitter. I’m bitter just like almost every soldier who is bitter in the bush, questioning why did we go to Juba and prepare because people didn’t trust the regime. They know the regime was going to cause problems,” he said.\n\nDespite the bitterness within the ranks and the competing claims to leadership, Lam insisted the dispute would remain political and administrative and would not lead to armed infighting within the SPLA-IO.\n\nSudans Post is an independent, young, and grass roots news media organization aimed at providing readers with an alternate depiction of events that occur on Sudan, South Sudan and East Africa, and to establish an engaging social platform for readers to discover and discuss the various issues that impact the two countries and the region.","url":"https://www.sudanspost.com/spla-io-spokesman-rules-out-armed-infighting-despite-leadership-crisis/?utm_source=rss&utm_medium=rss&utm_campaign=spla-io-spokesman-rules-out-armed-infighting-despite-leadership-crisis","source":"Sudans Post","image":"","published":"2026-09-05T05:10:59+00:00","category":"other","category_label":"Other Security News","region":"Horn of Africa","country":"South Sudan","fatalities":0,"severity":"Moderate","severity_score":0},{"id":"4e7e818a499d","title":"Sudan doctors group says 21 killed in RSF-allied SPLM-N attack in South Kordofan","summary":"Sudan Doctors Network says women, children among victims, accuses group of targeting civilians on an ‘ethnic basis’","body":"At least 21 people, including women and children, were killed in an attack by the Sudan People’s Liberation Movement-North (SPLM-N), allied with the Rapid Support Forces (RSF), in South Kordofan state, the Sudan Doctors Network said on Friday.\n\nThe independent medical network said in a statement that the attack targeted the Debi and Luweiri areas, south and west of Kauda, which are inhabited by the Atoro tribe.\n\nThe SPLM-N forces attacked areas in South Kordofan, killing the victims including women and children, and injuring others, the network said, adding the attack attack also forced a large number of civilians to flee the area.\n\nIt condemned the attack and accused SPLM-N forces of targeting civilians “on an ethnic basis” and displacing large numbers of them from their areas.\n\nThe Sudan Doctors Network described the attack as “a blatant violation of international humanitarian law and the rules protecting civilians.”\n\nOn Aug. 25, the Sudan Doctors Network said 27 people, including four children, were killed in attacks it accused the SPLM-N of carrying out on two areas in South Kordofan “on an ethnic basis.”\n\nOn Aug. 20, SPLM-N forces took control of Kauda, the group’s stronghold, as part of a wider military operation to suppress an Atoro tribe rebellion in areas of Heiban locality in South Kordofan.\n\nOn July 2, the Sudanese government condemned the “bloody events and grave violations” in Kauda, saying they left civilians dead and injured.\n\nThe clashes followed fighting between the SPLM-N and a group from the Atoro tribe after disputes about border demarcation between the Shawaya and Atoro tribes turned into armed confrontations.\n\nOn May 13, the Sudan Doctors Network said more than 61 people were killed, including nine children and five women, in clashes between the SPLM-N and a group from the Atoro tribe in Kauda.\n\nThe Al-Hilu-led SPLM-N has fought the Sudanese government since 2011, demanding autonomy in South Kordofan and Blue Nile regions.\n\nSince April 2023, the Sudanese army and the RSF have been locked in a war that erupted because of disputes regarding the integration of the paramilitary force into the military.\n\nThe war has killed tens of thousands and displaced around 13 million, while creating one of the world’s worst humanitarian and hunger crises.","url":"https://www.aa.com.tr/en/africa/sudan-doctors-group-says-21-killed-in-rsf-allied-splm-n-attack-in-south-kordofan/4047882","source":"Anadolu · Africa","image":"","published":"2026-09-04T21:29:05+00:00","category":"conflict","category_label":"Armed Conflict","region":"Horn of Africa","country":"Sudan","fatalities":21,"severity":"Severe","severity_score":2},{"id":"7145c1654e09","title":"Climate pressures and displacement deepen Sudan’s rural challenges","summary":"Sudan’s pastoralists and smallholder farmers are facing mounting pressure from climate change, while displaced families... The post Climate pressures and displacement deepen Sudan’s rural challenges appeared first on Dabanga Radio TV Online .","body":"Sudan’s pastoralists and smallholder farmers are facing mounting pressure from climate change, while displaced families in South Kordofan continue to struggle with access to water and healthcare, according to initiatives highlighted by the UN Development Programme (UNDP) and Médecins Sans Frontières (MSF). Pastoralists have traditionally followed north-south migration routes, tracking seasonal rainfall between pastures that can be up to 500km apart. In a recent report published by the UNDP , they state that livestock traditionally avoid farmland during the growing season before moving into fields after harvests to feed on crop residue while fertilising and turning over the soil for the next planting season. But “increasingly intermittent rainfall” is shortening grazing periods and forcing herders into earlier, unplanned migrations into farming areas, the UNDP says. At the same time, agricultural activity has expanded into pastoral areas through mechanisation and land privatisation, increasing resource conflicts between farmers and pastoralists, particularly during climate shocks. Sudan is developing climate-risk management tools, including weather-index insurance for farmers and pastoralists. According to the UNDP, satellite rainfall data and local weather stations can trigger automatic payouts when predetermined dry-spell thresholds are crossed. For pastoralists, the UNDP says, these payments can help cover “feed, water trucking and veterinary care”. Insurance products can also be linked to microfinance, while early warning and climate information services help herders decide where, when and for how long to graze. ‘Climate finance could support peace’ The UNDP describes targeting farmers and pastoralists equally as a pioneering approach with “substantial peace dividends”. “To succeed, a comprehensive enabling environment for climate risk management has to be built,” the UNDP says, including effective climate observation infrastructure and a robust regulatory framework for climate-risk financial services. The approach draws on lessons from the GEF-LDCF-funded Climate Risk Finance for Sustainable and Climate Resilient Rainfed Farming and Pastoral Systems project, implemented by Sudan’s Higher Council for Environment and Natural Resources from 2014 to 2023 with a $5.7 million budget and UNDP support. ‘Displacement strains South Kordofan services’ In South Kordofan’s Abu Jubeiha locality , 57,880 displaced people are living across 62 locations, according to the International Organization for Migration (IOM), making it the second-largest hosting locality in South Kordofan after Kadugli. Local authorities estimate the actual number is higher. Many displaced people live in the El Safa camps, where MSF says families face persistent needs for water, sanitation and healthcare. “When you go to the camps, you see the conditions people are living in,” says Ayşe Bilge Öztürk, MSF project coordinator in Abu Jubeiha. MSF says families live in temporary and semi-permanent shelters, including fabric tents and straw structures covered with plastic. Some tents are as small as 10 square metres, while many residents are women and children. “The support available has not kept pace with the number of people who have arrived or the length of time they have been displaced,” Öztürk says. ‘Water brought closer’ MSF began its response in Abu Jubeiha in June 2026, making water provision in the three El Safa camps one of its first priorities. Teams initially trucked water to the camps before drilling a borehole, extending a water network and installing a solar-powered pump. MSF also repaired latrines and provided cleaning materials. The organisation says it has supplied more than one million litres of water since the response began. Previously, residents had to walk at least three kilometres to collect water. Aisha Ahmed, 37, first stayed in a school after being displaced before moving to El Safa camp. She says water was one of their most urgent needs. “Sometimes we would go a whole week without water,” Ahmed says. “Now, after MSF drilled the borehole and extended the network, water is available 24 hours a day.” “I am happy. Now the water is there whenever we need it,” she adds. ‘Healthcare needs grow’ MSF says its teams have expanded healthcare services after identifying further needs in the camps. During a visit to El Safa, an MSF team encountered a mother whose daughter was suffering from severe diarrhoea and referred her to Abu Jubeiha hospital. MSF also provided logistical support for a Ministry of Health vaccination campaign covering measles, rubella, polio and yellow fever, alongside routine vaccinations. At Abu Jubeiha General Hospital, MSF says it is strengthening the emergency department through rehabilitation, equipment, medicines and other essential supplies. The rainy season brings further difficulties, with MSF reporting that unpaved roads make movement and the delivery of aid and supplies harder. Share article The post Climate pressures and displacement deepen Sudan’s rural challenges appeared first on Dabanga Radio TV Online .","url":"https://www.dabangasudan.org/en/all-news/article/climate-pressures-and-displacement-deepen-sudans-rural-challenges","source":"Dabanga Sudan","image":"","published":"2026-09-04T17:54:46+00:00","category":"conflict","category_label":"Armed Conflict","region":"Horn of Africa","country":"Sudan","fatalities":0,"severity":"Moderate","severity_score":0},{"id":"7a914c2f094d","title":"Turkey accused of war crime after drone strike on former Somali commander","summary":"MOGADISHU (Somaliguardian) – A drone strike tore through the residence of General Mahad Abdirahman, Somalia’s former custodial corps chief, in the border town of Dolow on Thursday, and he survived it – but the attack has ignited a political firestorm, with Jubaland authorities pointing at Turkey and warning that a dangerous new front has opened […]","body":"MOGADISHU (Somaliguardian) – A drone strike tore through the residence of General Mahad Abdirahman, Somalia’s former custodial corps chief, in the border town of Dolow on Thursday, and he survived it – but the attack has ignited a political firestorm, with Jubaland authorities pointing at Turkey and warning that a dangerous new front has opened in Somalia’s fracturing security landscape. A video circulating online showed the general receiving treatment at a hospital in the Ethiopian side of Dolow, the twin town separated from its Somali counterpart by the border. A retaliatory strike, hours after Baidoa attack The strike came only hours after Darwish forces loyal to ousted Southwest State President Abdiasis Laftagaren stormed the city of Baidoa on Thursday morning, overrunning army bases and briefly seizing parts of the city’s neighborhoods before fierce clashes. General Mahad is reported to be the backbone of Laftagaren’s resistance against the federal government, which seized Baidoa in a late-March offensive backed from the air by the Turkish air force. He is also Laftagaren’s father-in-law and a prominent figure commanding wide support among the population of Baidoa. The drone strike on his home is reported to be a retaliatory response to Thursday’s assault on Baidoa – a city that has become a battleground since Laftagaren’s ouster, with local forces under new Southwest leader Adan Madobe and federal troops unable to stop the guerrilla-style attacks that keep hitting the town. “Crossing into a very dangerous territory” Adam Aw Hirsi, Somalia’s former minister of state for environment and climate change, condemned the strike in sharp terms. “This is crossing into a very dangerous territory. I do not think the decision to initiate this attack was wise,” he said. He went further, warning that the episode could set off a cycle of retaliation across Somalia’s fractured political map: “If it is confirmed that foreign drones (Somalia has none) at the behest of @TheVillaSomalia attacked a general’s residence in Dollow, give it less than a year before you hear a drone belonging to an FMS or a clan in hinterland Somalia has struck a foreign embassy in Mogadishu.” “Foreigners involved in this need to reassess,” he added. “Somalis are a tough nut to scare. And you are not without competition in this game. Calculate the benefit of one man’s one-day thrill against the alienation of an entire people and squandering their goodwill.” Jubaland points to a Turkish drone, alleges war crime Jubaland State, which administers Dolow, said the strike was carried out by a Turkish drone. The regional administration described the act as a war crime and said it illustrates the dangerous direction in which President Hassan Sheikh Mohamud and Turkey are jointly steering Somalia. Jubaland – which is at odds with Mogadishu and has repeatedly clashed with federal forces – said Turkey cannot position itself as part of international efforts to resolve Somalia’s political crisis while conducting such strikes, and it called on international stakeholders to intervene urgently before the fallout unravels the broader political process. Former presidents accuse Hassan Sheikh Mohamud of reckless force Former Somali Presidents Sharif Sheikh Ahmed and Mohamed Abdullahi Farmajo both condemned the attack. They accused President Hassan Sheikh Mohamud of using reckless force through foreign powers to pressure his opponents, characterizing the strike as part of a pattern of assassination attempts against political rivals. Mogadishu distances itself, cites explosives inside the house The federal government distanced itself from the air raid, saying an initial investigation indicates the blast was triggered by explosives planted inside the house. It did not say whether Turkey carried out the attack. Turkey itself has not commented on the allegations. What residents heard and saw Despite Mogadishu’s denial of a drone strike, residents in Dolow told local media they heard the sound of a drone and saw a flash of light in the sky before a missile, apparently fired from above, struck the ground with a loud bang. They said the drone’s sound was heard again after the strike. The strike reportedly targeted with precision the room where General Mahad usually sleeps. He was not inside the room when it was hit. Part of a wider pattern of accusations against Ankara Turkey has faced mounting accusations in recent months of siding unreservedly with President Hassan Sheikh Mohamud in his political disputes, a departure from what had long been an international norm under which partners supported Mogadishu on counterterrorism but stayed out of its politicians’ internal disagreements. Ankara has been accused of carrying out deadly drone strikes against Southwest Darwish forces near Baidoa. It has also been linked to suicide drone strikes on buildings housing former President Sharif Sheikh Ahmed and former Prime Minister Hassan Ali Khaire, which came under attack from federal government forces during two days of fighting in Mogadishu in early June. Turkey was further accused of killing 14 civilians in Puntland State under the stated justification of an anti-piracy operation. Ankara has not commented on any of these accusations. Analysts warn Somalia risks becoming a foreign battleground Somali analysts and politicians say the country could soon become a proxy battleground for foreign powers, and that it would not come as a surprise if international partners aligned with various regional states began launching their own drone strikes on government offices in Mogadishu – now that Turkey, in their view, has set the precedent. Former Somali intelligence chief Fahad Yasin drew a historical parallel, calling the strike as dangerous a turning point as the 2006 car bomb attack on President Abdullahi Yusuf Ahmed in Baidoa – the first such explosive attack on a Somali official. He said Friday’s drone strike marks the first drone attack on a former Somali security official who had served the state for a long time. Contact us: info@somaliguardian.com","url":"https://www.somaliguardian.com/news/somalia-news/turkey-accused-war-crime-drone-strike-former-somali-commander/","source":"Somalia Guardian","image":"","published":"2026-09-04T17:49:11+00:00","category":"conflict","category_label":"Armed Conflict","region":"Horn of Africa","country":"Somalia","fatalities":0,"severity":"Moderate","severity_score":0},{"id":"87803c5ca3ef","title":"Les universitaires d’Afrique centrale lancent un réseau stratégique à Yaoundé pour lutter contre le crime organisé","summary":"© DR Des professeurs et chercheurs représentant onze unités de recherche stratégique issues d'universités du Cameroun, du Gabon, du Tchad et de la République démocratique du Congo se sont réunis à l'hôtel Star Land de Yaoundé le 4 septembre 2026 pour la réunion inaugurale d'un nouveau réseau régional de recherche. Ce rassemblement de deux jours visait à établir une alliance collaborative entre les","body":"© DR Des professeurs et chercheurs représentant onze unités de recherche stratégique issues d'universités du Cameroun, du Gabon, du Tchad et de la République démocratique du Congo se sont réunis à l'hôtel Star Land de Yaoundé le 4 septembre 2026 pour la réunion inaugurale d'un nouveau réseau régional de recherche. Ce rassemblement de deux jours visait à établir une alliance collaborative entre les institutions académiques des quatre nations afin de renforcer leur capacité collective à analyser, signaler et mener des actions de plaidoyer contre le crime organisé et la violence armée en Afrique centrale. Au cœur de cette initiative se trouve le projet d'Observatoire de l'Afrique centrale sur le crime organisé et la violence, lancé en 2024 par l'Initiative mondiale contre la criminalité transnationale organisée (GI-TOC) en partenariat avec l'Institut d'études de sécurité (ISS). S'étendant jusqu'en 2027, ce projet vise à créer un observatoire régional de la société civile axé sur l'analyse du lien entre criminalité et conflits, la cartographie des écosystèmes criminels et le renforcement des capacités de plaidoyer à travers l'Afrique centrale. Ses objectifs plus larges s'articulent autour de la production de données fiables, du renforcement de la recherche au sein de la société civile et de l'incitation des autorités judiciaires et politiques régionales à adopter des réponses fondées sur des preuves. Le directeur de l'Observatoire, le professeur Saïbou Issa, a souligné que cette initiative permet de faire le pont entre la recherche académique et l'action publique. Il a noté que bien que les universités disposent d'une expertise scientifique approfondie sur des enjeux complexes tels que la criminalité financière, les enlèvements avec demande de rançon, la traite des êtres humains et la cybercriminalité, ces menaces forment un écosystème criminel interconnecté. Il a précisé qu'à l'issue de la conférence, le réseau donnera la priorité à des formations spécialisées pour aider les chercheurs à transformer leurs travaux théoriques en outils décisionnels exploitables par les décideurs politiques pour lutter efficacement contre la criminalité transfrontalière. Axant son propos sur la nécessité d'une coopération régionale, le professeur René Joly Assako Assako, doyen de la Faculté des Arts, Lettres et Sciences Humaines de l'Université de Douala et représentant du Centre d'Études et de Recherche en Géoéconomie, Géopolitique et Géostratégie des Espaces Littoraux et Maritimes (3GELM), a exprimé l'espoir que ce réseau transforme la réponse sécuritaire de l'Afrique centrale. Il a insisté sur le fait que l'expertise locale ne manque pas au sein des universités et des chercheurs de la région ; le maillon manquant réside plutôt dans la coordination, le partage des ressources et la mutualisation des compétences nécessaires pour répondre aux menaces globales par des mesures unifiées. D'autres universitaires de la région ont également réitéré l'urgence d'une approche coordonnée et transfrontalière pour démanteler les réseaux criminels. La professeure Régine Nambuwa de l'Université de Kinshasa, représentant le Centre de criminologie et de pathologie sociale, a souligné que les actions isolées sont insuffisantes face à des problèmes comme le trafic illégal de minerais quittant la RDC via des pays voisins comme le Cameroun. De même, le professeur Serge Loungou de l'Université Omar Bongo au Gabon a souligné que cette initiative permet de sortir du « désengagement géopolitique », offrant aux chercheurs l'opportunité d'analyser conjointement des crimes transfrontaliers tels que le trafic d'enfants et le braconnage dans les parcs nationaux partagés. Pour la seconde journée de cette réunion inaugurale, le samedi 5 septembre 2026, les participants se concentreront sur la fixation des bases juridiques et opérationnelles du réseau. L'ordre du jour prévoit l'examen du protocole d'accord (MoU), l'identification des thématiques de recherche collaborative prioritaires et la constitution de groupes thématiques spécialisés. De plus, les délégués élaboreront un plan de travail initial couvrant les 12 à 24 prochains mois, effectueront des restitutions de travaux, établiront formellement le réseau et adopteront une feuille de route consolidée avant la clôture officielle de l'événement. À la suite de cette rencontre de deux jours, les centres de recherche stratégique se concerteront avec leurs universités respectives pour mettre en œuvre le plan de travail convenu. La première étape prioritaire portera sur le déploiement de programmes de formation méthodologique et de rédaction de rapports pour les membres du réseau, posant ainsi les jalons d'une prospective stratégique, d'une recherche collaborative durable et d'un plaidoyer politique fondé sur des données probantes en Afrique centrale. Cet article Les universitaires d’Afrique centrale lancent un réseau stratégique à Yaoundé pour lutter contre le crime organisé est apparu en premier sur Journalducameroun.com .","url":"https://fr.journalducameroun.com/les-universitaires-dafrique-centrale-lancent-un-reseau-strategique-a-yaounde-pour-lutter-contre-le-crime-organise/","source":"Journal du Cameroun","image":"","published":"2026-09-04T16:16:33+00:00","category":"kidnapping","category_label":"Kidnapping & Abduction","region":"Central Africa","country":"Cameroon","fatalities":0,"severity":"Moderate","severity_score":0},{"id":"c421f7ba3eba","title":"Israeli Actors Should Face a Choice: Building in E1 or Business with Europe","summary":"Israeli Actors Should Face a Choice: Building in E1 or Business with Europe eschelhaas Fri, 09/04/2026 - 17:38 Latest Updates Africa Asia-Pacific Europe Latin America & Caribbean Middle East & North Africa United States Global Issues & Institutions My Reading List Israeli far-right Finance Minister Bezalel Smotrich holds a map of an area near the settlement of Maale Adumim, a land corridor known a","body":"","url":"https://www.crisisgroup.org/stm/middle-east-north-africa/israelpalestine-europe-united-states/israeli-actors-should-face-choice-building-e1-or-business-europe","source":"International Crisis Group","image":"","published":"2026-09-04T15:38:54+00:00","category":"terrorism","category_label":"Terrorism","region":"North Africa","country":"","fatalities":0,"severity":"Moderate","severity_score":0},{"id":"e57a901ec612","title":"Somalia: Jubaland Accuses Türkiye of Carrying Out Doolow Airstrike","summary":"https://www.horndiplomat.com/feed/ By: Hanah Olson, Horndiplomat Global Reporter Nairobi, Kenya , Sept. 4, 2026 — Somalia’s Jubaland administration accused Türkiye on Friday of carrying out an airstrike on a residential compound in Doolow, in the Gedo region, sharply escalating tensions over Ankara’s expanding military role in the country. In a statement, Jubaland said Turkish military aircraft fi","body":"","url":"https://www.horndiplomat.com/somalia-jubaland-accuses-turkiye-of-carrying-out-doolow-airstrike/","source":"Horn Diplomat","image":"","published":"2026-09-04T13:47:53+00:00","category":"conflict","category_label":"Armed Conflict","region":"Horn of Africa","country":"Somalia","fatalities":0,"severity":"Moderate","severity_score":0},{"id":"6a1cce66b3b0","title":"Suweida’s Exam Standoff Triggers Fresh Clashes in Southern Syria","summary":"Suweida’s Exam Standoff Triggers Fresh Clashes in Southern Syria pzeiler Fri, 09/04/2026 - 14:57 Latest Updates Africa Asia-Pacific Europe Latin America & Caribbean Middle East & North Africa United States Global Issues & Institutions My Reading List Analyst's Notebook / Middle East & North Africa 04 September 2026 2 minutes Suweida’s Exam Standoff Triggers Fresh Clashes in Southern Syria Crisis G","body":"","url":"https://www.crisisgroup.org/anb/middle-east-north-africa/syria/suweidas-exam-standoff-triggers-fresh-clashes-southern-syria","source":"International Crisis Group","image":"","published":"2026-09-04T12:57:35+00:00","category":"conflict","category_label":"Armed Conflict","region":"North Africa","country":"","fatalities":2025,"severity":"Severe","severity_score":2},{"id":"628a33ea0fd2","title":"Transnational network fuelling war in Sudan, UN report claims","summary":"Sudan’s brutal three-year war is being fueled by a transnational network of foreign companies, the United Nations said in a report on Thursday.","body":"The United Nations said in a report Thursday that foreign networks are helping fuel the brutal war in Sudan that has raged between the army and the paramilitary group Rapid Support Forces since 2023, killing at least 59,000 people, displacing some 13 million and pushing many parts of the African nation into famine.\n\nIt said a network of companies based in the United Arab Emirates, Colombia and Chad, among other nations, had supplied military contractors, weapons and training to the RSF.\n\n\"The mission finds reasonable grounds to believe that a transnational network supplied the Rapid Support Forces with weapons, logistics, training and foreign personnel, including Colombian contractors involved in advanced weapons and drone systems,\" according to a summary of the investigation by the Independent International Fact-Finding Mission for the Sudan, an independent body mandated by the UN Human Rights Council.\n\nIt found that \"individuals and entities operating from, registered in, or linked to the United Arab Emirates played a significant role in networks involved in the recruitment, financing, transportation and deployment of foreign military contractors and the provision of military-related support benefiting the Rapid Support Forces.\"\n\nThe report also said that foreign-supplied drones \"expanded\" the Sudanese army's drone capabilities, though it added that the findings about external support to the army \"remain preliminary.\"\n\nThe investigators called for the UN Security Council to extend its arms embargo on Sudan.\n\n\"The weapons, financing, personnel and technology that sustain this war must be constrained,\" it said.\n\nInvestigators said they had documented drone attacks on civilians and civilian infrastructure by both sides that may amount to war crimes.\n\nThey also linked foreign-trained fighters to two massacres in 2025 in Zamzam camp and El Fasher.\n\nSurvivors among the more than 100,000 people who fled el-Fasher reported RSF fighters gunning down civilians in homes and in the streets, leaving the city littered with bodies. Satellite photos have since appeared to show RSF disposing of bodies in mass graves or by burning them.\n\nChad, Colombia and the United Arab Emirates cooperated with the investigators but \"no substantive response\" came from Ethiopia, Libya, South Sudan and Somalia. Panama, whose finance industry has faced accusations of industrial-scale money laundering, requested more time to reply. So far, the UN said neither the Sudanese military nor the RSF have cooperated with the investigation.\n\nAn AP investigation in July showed that Colombian mercenaries had trained children to fight for the RSF. Rights groups say the Colombians were recruited by a state-linked firm in the UAE, which denies providing arms or military support to any warring party in Sudan.\n\nThe United States has urged the UN to impose new sanctions to bring the warring parties to the negotiating table.\n\nA draft of the US resolution, seen by The Associated Press in August, deplores \"the continuous influx of external military assistance, weapons, and ammunition to the warring parties, which is prolonging the conflict and undermining mediation efforts,\" including launching drones and unmanned aerial systems from third countries.\n\nThe proposed resolution demands that all countries \"cease any direct or indirect support to the warring parties, be it logistical, financial or military, which enables conflict to continue.\"","url":"http://www.africanews.com/2026/09/04/transnational-network-fuelling-war-in-sudan-un-report-claims/","source":"Africanews","image":"http://images.euronews.com/articles/stories/09/90/16/15/640x360_cmsv2_f68de986-922b-56df-aa99-669692502f67-9901615.jpg?1788519472","published":"2026-09-04T11:58:57+00:00","category":"conflict","category_label":"Armed Conflict","region":"Horn of Africa","country":"Sudan","fatalities":0,"severity":"Moderate","severity_score":0},{"id":"11a1e8811a5f","title":"SPLM-IO governor says Oyet suspension came ‘directly’ from Machar","summary":"JUBA — A senior official of South Sudan’s main armed opposition group said on Friday that the suspension of acting chairman Oyet Nathaniel was ordered directly by detained leader Riek Machar, weighing into a leadership dispute that has fractured the movement’s political and military ranks. Dhoal Koryom Lingling, an opposition-appointed governor for Unity State, said […] The post SPLM-IO governor s","body":"SPLM-IO Governor of Unity State Dhoal Koryom Lingling. [Photo Courtesy] JUBA — A senior official of South Sudan’s main armed opposition group said on Friday that the suspension of acting chairman Oyet Nathaniel was ordered directly by detained leader Riek Machar, weighing into a leadership dispute that has fractured the movement’s political and military ranks.\n\nDhoal Koryom Lingling, an opposition-appointed governor for Unity State, said Oyet remains a member of the Sudan People’s Liberation Movement-in-Opposition (SPLM-IO) despite the suspension and could resume his duties if he accepts the decision.\n\n“The reason why I am speaking to the media is because I know where this order is coming from. Oyet appointed me as governor, by the way,” Koryom said in recorded statements on Friday morning.\n\n“If I thought the way you do, I could speak the way you do. But the truth is that Oyet was suspended directly by the chairman (Riek Machar). If you are not aware of that, you should seek that information,” Koryom said.\n\nKoryom did not explain how Machar, who has been held in detention since March 2025, communicated the decision or specify when it was issued.\n\nUnder the movement’s official leadership structure, Machar remains chairman and commander-in-chief, while Oyet serves as his deputy. Oyet has been exercising acting leadership responsibilities during Machar’s detention.\n\n“Oyet has not been expelled from the movement. He is still a member,” Koryom said. “If he respects the chairman’s decision, he will return to his duties as deputy chairman and acting chairman.”\n\nThe comments add new complexity to a leadership crisis that erupted publicly this week after Oyet suspended interim Secretary-General Regina Joseph Kaba and seven other senior officials, accusing them of misconduct and attempting to undermine his authority.\n\nOyet said the suspensions were disciplinary measures authorized under the group’s constitution and internal regulations. He accused several members of the Political Bureau of attempting to interfere with the movement’s constitutional order and destabilize its leadership.\n\nKaba rejected Oyet’s authority to suspend her and issued a counter-directive suspending him as deputy chairman and acting chairman. She also instructed the movement’s political, military, financial, and administrative structures to disregard directives or appointments issued by Oyet.\n\nThe dispute has since spread to the SPLA-IO military command.\n\nSenior military commanders meeting this week endorsed the Political Bureau’s resolutions and recognized Kaba as Acting Interim Deputy Chair, while reaffirming Lt. Gen. Peter Thok as the sole Acting Chief of General Staff, according to a statement by the SPLA-IO spokesman.\n\nThe command said that directives from “the commander-in-chief” must be respected and implemented. While the statement did not mention Machar by name in that context, its reference to the commander-in-chief appeared to suggest that the decisions had Machar’s backing.\n\nThe conflicting decisions have raised questions over who holds political and military authority within the movement while Machar remains unable to lead directly.\n\nKoryom’s assertion that Machar personally authorized Oyet’s suspension could prove significant, as rival factions continue to declare loyalty to Machar while contesting who holds the authority to run the movement in his absence.\n\nSudans Post could not independently verify whether Machar had personally communicated or approved Oyet’s suspension.\n\nSudans Post is an independent, young, and grass roots news media organization aimed at providing readers with an alternate depiction of events that occur on Sudan, South Sudan and East Africa, and to establish an engaging social platform for readers to discover and discuss the various issues that impact the two countries and the region.","url":"https://www.sudanspost.com/splm-io-governor-says-oyet-suspension-came-directly-from-machar/?utm_source=rss&utm_medium=rss&utm_campaign=splm-io-governor-says-oyet-suspension-came-directly-from-machar","source":"Sudans Post","image":"","published":"2026-09-04T11:56:44+00:00","category":"other","category_label":"Other Security News","region":"Horn of Africa","country":"South Sudan","fatalities":0,"severity":"Moderate","severity_score":0},{"id":"4ac3fb3e35bc","title":"M23 Rebels Killed As Congolese Army Recaptures South Kivu Towns","summary":"The clashes are still ongoing in many towns in the eastern Democratic Republic of Congo. The post M23 Rebels Killed As Congolese Army Recaptures South Kivu Towns appeared first on HumAngle .","body":"At least 23 armed fighters have been killed in intense fighting between the Armed Forces of the Democratic Republic of Congo and the M23/AFC rebel group. The clashes are still ongoing in the town of Masango and surrounding areas in South Kivu province. Congolese forces announced on Thursday, Sep. 3, that the national army recaptured Masango, Ruhua, and Rubarati after defeating M23/AFC rebels and their Twirwaneho allies in the Hauts Plateaux of Mwenga in South Kivu. Reagan Mbuyi Kalonji, the army spokesperson for Operation Sukola 2 in South Kivu, said these major advances resulted from serious military operations in the zones. “Following our major response against the terrorist coalition of the Rwandan Defence Forces (RDF) and their allies of the M23, Twirwaneho and Red Tabara in the Hauts Plateaux of Uvira and Fizi, the FARDC have neutralised 23 rebels, 11 others surrendered, and several weapons were recovered,” Reagan said. During the course of operations carried out in Malimba, Masango, Ruhua and Rubarat, the army declared having killed 15 combatants of the M23-Twirwaneho coalition, while six others surrendered before being taken to Uvira. Violent clashes have intensified between the M23 and the Congolese forces in Uvira. The country’s army has said that many rebels have since withdrawn toward Kashongo and Magunda, two strategic localities, allowing the national armed forces to reinforce their positions in the Minembwe region. The army has also claimed it has recaptured several towns and villages from the M23/AFC rebels, including Kamimbi, Nagishashu and Irumba in the Basimunyaka-Sud area. During fighting in the area, eight rebel combatants were killed, and five others surrendered. However, a mass grave was discovered in Masango, where civilians killed by the rebels were buried during the occupation of the town by a rebel coalition, led by the M23 armed group and allegedly supported by Rwanda. The post M23 Rebels Killed As Congolese Army Recaptures South Kivu Towns appeared first on HumAngle .","url":"https://humanglemedia.com/m23-rebels-killed-as-congolese-army-recaptures-south-kivu-towns/","source":"HumAngle","image":"","published":"2026-09-04T11:19:28+00:00","category":"terrorism","category_label":"Terrorism","region":"Central Africa","country":"DR Congo","fatalities":15,"severity":"Severe","severity_score":2},{"id":"373213bc0771","title":"How Drones are Changing Colombia’s Conflict","summary":"How Drones are Changing Colombia’s Conflict eschelhaas Fri, 09/04/2026 - 11:41 Latest Updates Africa Asia-Pacific Europe Latin America & Caribbean Middle East & North Africa United States Global Issues & Institutions My Reading List Video / Latin America & Caribbean 04 September 2026 1 minute How Drones are Changing Colombia’s Conflict In this video, Crisis Group expert Bram Ebus explains how dron","body":"","url":"https://www.crisisgroup.org/vid/latin-america-caribbean/colombia/how-drones-are-changing-colombias-conflict","source":"International Crisis Group","image":"","published":"2026-09-04T09:41:59+00:00","category":"conflict","category_label":"Armed Conflict","region":"North Africa","country":"","fatalities":0,"severity":"Moderate","severity_score":0},{"id":"48de48b84d74","title":"The Children Forced into Street Labour in Chad","summary":"The precarious situations that many families and their children face in urban centres of Chad are pushing school-age children, who remain at home due to poverty, into street labour and hawking as a means of survival. The post The Children Forced into Street Labour in Chad appeared first on HumAngle .","body":"Idrissa Mahamat navigates the harsh realities of life as a child vendor, selling sachet water under the scorching sun of the bustling Taradona market in the Republic of Chad . Like him, many children are forced into the streets due to family crises, unplanned pregnancies, and abandonment by their parents. Many of these children lack any familial support, compelling them to fend for themselves in a struggle for survival. The harsh conditions that lead to their circumstances often leave them vulnerable and alone, forcing them to confront the challenges of adulthood far too early, locals say. Amid economic hardship and insecurity, a disturbing cycle of vulnerability is taking root, as children are forced into street labour in N’Djamena, the capital of Chad. Driven by poverty and neglect, school-age children face threats of violence and exploitation. In the neighbourhoods and markets of the Chadian capital, it is common to see minors carrying buckets full of sachet water on their heads, searching for customers for their goods. They navigate the streets, motor parks, and bus stations. “The price of the water sachets I sell varies between 25 and 50 FCFA, and on good days, I can sell between one and three buckets full of sachet water and make a profit of about 1,000 FCFA (about US$2), which I hand over to my parents to add to what they have to use in taking care of our family,” nine-year-old Idrissa told HumAngle. As child labour becomes a common phenomenon in Chad, parents and locals in the country’s capital told HumAngle that the largest cause of this scourge is “irresponsible parenting” and carelessness of some guardians and child carers. “Difficult times, divorces, unwanted pregnancies, forced marriages or sometimes the death of parents explain why some of these children find themselves in the situations in which they are,” says a local social worker in Chad, who simply gave their name as Sussane. Tchinduebe Joseph, a father of four, says that after giving birth, many young mothers find themselves alone, struggling to care for both themselves and their newborns. With their partners unwilling to shoulder their share of the burden, these women are often left with no choice but to fend for themselves, sometimes even being pushed out into the streets to survive. At least one in every two school-age children is not enrolled in school, totalling approximately three million children outside the education system in Chad, according to a report by the United Nations International Children’s Emergency Fund (UNICEF). The situation seems to have established societal imbalances driving some children into armed groups, while unparented girls face increased risk of rape and sexual exploitation due to their living conditions. The UN agency reports that the abandonment of these minors reflects poorly on Chadian society and families. However, the precarious situations that many families and their children face in urban centres of Chad are pushing school-age children, who remain at home due to poverty, into street labour and hawking as a means of survival. A top official of the N’Djamena City Council in Chad, who asked not to be named in this report, stressed the importance of parental responsibility. Disturbed by the growing number of unparented children in the country, the official quoted a poignant Ivorian adage, saying, “The child has to be fed until he/she grows teeth, and in turn, that child will eventually care for you when you lose your own teeth.” He noted that nurturing and supporting children lays a foundation for mutual care and reciprocity in later life. “In this 21st century, when money is indispensable for survival, poverty within most families and most communities in Chad has been wreaking havoc on normal family life. Even children are not exempted from this unfortunate reality as most of them are being forced into the streets in search of money to supplement the low incomes of their parents,” the official added. UNICEF, on its part, says that children have a right to health, potable water, sufficient food, as well as education as they grow. The child has to be protected against violence, maltreatment, exploitation and armed conflicts while benefiting from a safe home and leisure. The post The Children Forced into Street Labour in Chad appeared first on HumAngle .","url":"https://humanglemedia.com/the-children-forced-into-street-labour-in-chad/","source":"HumAngle","image":"","published":"2026-09-04T09:00:00+00:00","category":"other","category_label":"Other Security News","region":"Sahel","country":"Chad","fatalities":0,"severity":"Moderate","severity_score":0},{"id":"1a9b24f8d019","title":"Somaliland’s Irro departs for US to pitch region’s strategic value as Somalia sends rival team","summary":"MOGADISHU (Somaliguardian) – Somaliland President Abdirahman Mohamed Abdullahi Irro departed Hargeisa on Thursday for a working visit to the United States, part of an effort to present the self-declared republic as an emerging power of geoeconomic and geosecurity value to Washington and other Western capitals, with particular emphasis on the Bab el-Mandeb chokepoint. Officials said […]","body":"MOGADISHU (Somaliguardian) – Somaliland President Abdirahman Mohamed Abdullahi Irro departed Hargeisa on Thursday for a working visit to the United States, part of an effort to present the self-declared republic as an emerging power of geoeconomic and geosecurity value to Washington and other Western capitals, with particular emphasis on the Bab el-Mandeb chokepoint. Officials said Irro will make a stopover in the United Arab Emirates en route to the US. The Somaliland leader is expected to pitch his territory’s importance to Washington on two fronts: its rare earth mineral deposits and its proximity to Yemen and the Bab el-Mandeb chokepoint, a position officials believe could support a US military footing there and help counter threats from Houthi rebels. Somalia’s federal government has moved in parallel, dispatching a delegation led by Deputy Prime Minister Salah Jama on a counter-diplomacy push aimed at undercutting Irro’s Washington trip. The rival missions have drawn attention to the family ties binding the two camps. “For those who don’t know – Jama is actually from Somaliland and Irro’s kin,” said Rashid Abdi, a Horn of Africa analyst at Sahan Research. “Fascinating diplomacy,” Abdi added. Contact us: info@somaliguardian.com","url":"https://www.somaliguardian.com/news/somalia-news/somaliland-irro-us-visit-strategic-value-somalia-rival-delegation/","source":"Somalia Guardian","image":"","published":"2026-09-04T08:45:57+00:00","category":"conflict","category_label":"Armed Conflict","region":"Horn of Africa","country":"Somaliland","fatalities":0,"severity":"Moderate","severity_score":0},{"id":"ae55256f1b90","title":"Exiled opposition politician Khalid Omar attacks campaign promoting Burhan for president","summary":"KHARTOUM — Exiled Sudanese opposition politician Khalid Omar Youssef on Friday attacked a campaign promoting army chief Abdelfattah al-Burhan for president, saying it showed that the country’s devastating war was ultimately a struggle for power. Omar, a former cabinet affairs minister and deputy president of the Sudanese Congress Party, made the remarks after a poster […] The post Exiled oppositio","body":"Former Sudanese cabinet minister and exiled opposition politician Khalid Omer Yousif, speaks during an interview with Reuters in Khartoum, Sudan January 31, 2022. [Photo by REUTERS] KHARTOUM — Exiled Sudanese opposition politician Khalid Omar Youssef on Friday attacked a campaign promoting army chief Abdelfattah al-Burhan for president, saying it showed that the country’s devastating war was ultimately a struggle for power.\n\nOmar, a former cabinet affairs minister and deputy president of the Sudanese Congress Party, made the remarks after a poster circulated on social media bearing a photograph of Burhan and the slogan, “We want Burhan as president,” describing the call as “a popular demand.”\n\nThere was no indication that Burhan himself produced, launched or endorsed the campaign.\n\n“With the launch of a campaign to nominate Burhan for president of the republic, we have reached the final scene of the film very quickly,” Omar wrote on his social media Facebook page. “Here is a clear answer to the question: Whose war is this, and why?”\n\nOmar accused Burhan of pursuing political power at enormous cost to Sudan and pointed to the army chief’s role in the October 2021 military takeover that ended the civilian-led transition established after the overthrow of longtime ruler Omar al-Bashir.\n\n“The general is hastening his steps toward achieving his dream, which has cost the country and its people dearly,” Omar said.\n\nHe accused Burhan of betraying the 2019 revolution that brought millions of Sudanese onto the streets and ultimately ended Bashir’s nearly three decades in power.\n\nBurhan led the October 2021 takeover alongside Rapid Support Forces militia commander Mohamed Hamdan Dagalo, widely known as Hemedti. The two later fell out amid disputes over the political transition and the integration of the RSF militia into the regular army, culminating in war between their forces in April 2023.\n\nThe conflict has devastated large parts of Sudan, killed tens of thousands of people and displaced millions inside and outside the country.\n\nOmar said Burhan’s years at the head of Sudan’s military-led authorities had coincided with war, economic collapse, mass displacement and threats to the country’s unity and sovereignty.\n\n“He should ask his predecessor, Bashir: Where did this path ultimately lead him?” Omar said.\n\nOmar has lived in exile since leaving Sudan amid the war. He is deputy president of the Sudanese Congress Party and one of the prominent civilian politicians opposed to the conflict and to continued military rule.\n\nHe and several other civilian opposition figures have faced criminal accusations from authorities aligned with the army, which have accused anti-war politicians of supporting or providing political cover for the RSF.\n\nThe politicians have denied backing the paramilitary group and say the accusations are intended to silence opponents of the war.\n\nBurhan has also publicly attacked some of the exiled civilian politicians and said they would not be allowed to return to Sudan.\n\nOmar served as cabinet affairs minister in the civilian-military transitional government established after Bashir’s overthrow and remained a prominent figure in the civilian political movement following the 2021 coup.\n\nIn his statement on Friday, Omar rejected the army and its supporters’ description of the conflict as a “war of dignity,” saying the appearance of a campaign promoting Burhan for president reinforced his view that the conflict was about political power.\n\n“This is another wake-up call for everyone who naively believed that this was a war of dignity or sovereignty,” he said. “It is a disgraceful war for power.”\n\nOmar described the conflict as a “counter-revolution against the Sudanese people’s dream of freedom, peace and justice” and called for its rejection.\n\nBurhan has not publicly declared that he intends to run for president, and the appearance of posters promoting him does not amount to an official candidacy. Sudan has not held a presidential election since Bashir was removed from power in April 2019.\n\nSudans Post is an independent, young, and grass roots news media organization aimed at providing readers with an alternate depiction of events that occur on Sudan, South Sudan and East Africa, and to establish an engaging social platform for readers to discover and discuss the various issues that impact the two countries and the region.","url":"https://www.sudanspost.com/exiled-opposition-politician-khalid-omar-attacks-campaign-promoting-burhan-for-president/?utm_source=rss&utm_medium=rss&utm_campaign=exiled-opposition-politician-khalid-omar-attacks-campaign-promoting-burhan-for-president","source":"Sudans Post","image":"","published":"2026-09-04T08:25:38+00:00","category":"conflict","category_label":"Armed Conflict","region":"Horn of Africa","country":"Sudan","fatalities":0,"severity":"Moderate","severity_score":0},{"id":"aaa2895c2aeb","title":"UN report says foreign suppliers boosted drone capabilities of Sudan’s belligerents","summary":"GENEVA — Foreign suppliers have significantly expanded the drone capabilities of both the Sudanese army and the paramilitary Rapid Support Forces (RSF), allowing the warring sides to conduct increasingly sophisticated operations far beyond traditional front lines, United Nations investigators said in a report released on Thursday. The UN Independent International Fact-Finding Mission for the Sudan","body":"Drone attack on Port Sudan targeting an oil depot on May 5, 2025. [Photo courtesy] GENEVA — Foreign suppliers have significantly expanded the drone capabilities of both the Sudanese army and the paramilitary Rapid Support Forces (RSF), allowing the warring sides to conduct increasingly sophisticated operations far beyond traditional front lines, United Nations investigators said in a report released on Thursday.\n\nThe UN Independent International Fact-Finding Mission for the Sudan said foreign recruitment, weapons transfers and logistical networks operating across several countries were strengthening the military capabilities of parties to the conflict, with civilians bearing much of the cost.\n\nThe investigators documented what they described as transnational networks involved in recruiting, training and deploying foreign personnel to support the RSF, as well as facilitating transfers of weapons, ammunition and military technology.\n\nThe report also found reasonable grounds to believe that the Sudanese Armed Forces (SAF) had used foreign-supplied drones in military operations that harmed civilians and civilian objects.\n\n“The consequences of this external support are being felt most acutely by civilians,” Mohamad Chande Othman, chair of the Fact-Finding Mission, said.\n\n“This external support has not brought greater protection for civilians. Instead, civilians are paying the price through attacks on homes, hospitals, schools, markets and places of refuge.”\n\nSudan has been engulfed in war since April 2023, when fighting erupted between the SAF, headed by army chief Abdel Fattah al-Burhan, and the RSF, commanded by Mohamed Hamdan Dagalo, widely known as Hemedti.\n\nThe UN investigators said foreign assistance had helped expand the use of drones by both sides, increasing their ability to conduct strikes at considerable distances from the main battlefields.\n\nThe report documented the deployment of as many as 2,000 former Colombian military contractors who it said were directly involved alongside RSF units in Darfur and Kordofan, including in the operation of combat drones, artillery and other advanced weapons.\n\nInvestigators said there were reasonable grounds to believe that private entities, recruiters and logistical facilitators operating from countries including Colombia and the United Arab Emirates used transit points in Chad, southeastern Libya and the Somali city of Bosaso to move personnel, training, weapons and logistical supplies to the RSF.\n\nMona Rishmawi, a member of the mission, said investigators had identified “a transnational chain of networks spanning several countries” supplying the RSF with foreign personnel, training, weapons and logistical assistance.\n\n“This support fed into RSF operations in Zamzam camp and later during the takeover of El-Fasher, where the Mission documented grave human rights and international humanitarian law violations and international crimes, including conduct displaying hallmarks of genocide,” she said.\n\nThe findings come as drones have assumed an increasingly prominent role in Sudan’s war, allowing the belligerents to attack targets well beyond areas of direct ground combat. The mission said foreign suppliers had substantially enhanced the drone capabilities of both the SAF and RSF.\n\nInvestigators said states connected to the supply chains had obligations under international law to investigate and disrupt illicit mercenary and weapons networks operating from or through their territories and to prevent transfers that could contribute to serious violations.\n\nThe mission called for enforcement of the existing UN arms embargo on Darfur and urged the UN Security Council to extend the restrictions to the whole of Sudan. It also called for investigations into individuals and entities involved in illicit support networks.\n\nOthman said repeated international warnings over abuses in Sudan had failed to produce sufficient action.\n\n“Protecting civilians in Sudan requires taking action not only against those waging this war, but also against the individuals, entities, and networks that enable its continuation.”\n\nSudans Post is an independent, young, and grass roots news media organization aimed at providing readers with an alternate depiction of events that occur on Sudan, South Sudan and East Africa, and to establish an engaging social platform for readers to discover and discuss the various issues that impact the two countries and the region.","url":"https://www.sudanspost.com/un-report-says-foreign-suppliers-boosted-drone-capabilities-of-sudans-belligerents/?utm_source=rss&utm_medium=rss&utm_campaign=un-report-says-foreign-suppliers-boosted-drone-capabilities-of-sudans-belligerents","source":"Sudans Post","image":"","published":"2026-09-04T08:25:37+00:00","category":"conflict","category_label":"Armed Conflict","region":"Horn of Africa","country":"Sudan","fatalities":0,"severity":"Moderate","severity_score":0},{"id":"bccbb805456d","title":"Injuries as violence erupts at South Africa anti-migrant protest","summary":"A demonstration against undocumented migrants in South Africa descended into violence Thursday with one foreign national arrested and a community leader saying five Congolese were hurt","body":"A demonstration against undocumented migrants in South Africa descended into violence Thursday with one foreign national arrested and a community leader saying five Congolese were hurt.\n\nThe protest was in the port city of Durban which has been at the epicentre of weeks of action against irregular migration in which at least four foreign nationals have been killed and tens of thousands have fled the country.\n\nPolice said about 100 marchers from an anti-illegal migrant group went on what they called \"a clean-up campaign\" against an encampment of several hundred foreign nationals outside a government office.\n\nForeigners, many of them from the Democratic Republic of Congo (DRC), moved to the site 13 weeks ago to seek protection against mobs of South Africans campaigning against undocumented migrants.\n\n\"During the commotion, stones were thrown around, and one which was thrown by a foreign national landed on a police officer,\" police said.\n\nThe person was arrested, a statement said, without giving details.\n\nA leader of the group of foreigners told AFP that five Congolese nationals including two children were hurt in the clash.\n\n\"The march today was very violent,\" said Bishop Raphael, who is from the DRC.\n\n\"They found us here and immediately attacked our people. We are not committing any crime by being here. This is uncalled for,\" he said.\n\nMany of the people in the group claim to be refugees from the DRC.\n\nRaphael said the protesters were armed with sticks and attempted to attack the encampment while police tried to separate them.\n\nPolice used stun grenades to disperse the crowds, resulting in the injuries, he said.\n\nTensions soared across South Africa after fringe groups campaigning against undocumented migrants set a June 30 deadline for them to leave.\n\nMore than 200,000 people fled, according to an AFP tally based on figures released by African governments that have repatriated their nationals.\n\nAt least four migrants were killed in that violence, according to the South African police, though some foreign governments say the toll is higher.","url":"http://www.africanews.com/2026/09/04/injuries-as-violence-erupts-at-south-africa-anti-migrant-protest/","source":"Africanews","image":"http://images.euronews.com/articles/stories/09/90/08/77/640x360_cmsv2_979f7edf-80dc-543b-a89e-c497e29fd5a2-9900877.jpg?1788497017","published":"2026-09-04T06:35:56+00:00","category":"other","category_label":"Other Security News","region":"Southern Africa","country":"South Africa","fatalities":0,"severity":"Moderate","severity_score":0},{"id":"ffc7fe1c2d31","title":"On Our Radar","summary":"On Our Radar eschelhaas Fri, 09/04/2026 - 00:00 Latest Updates Africa Asia-Pacific Europe Latin America & Caribbean Middle East & North Africa United States Global Issues & Institutions My Reading List Special Coverage / Global 04 September 2026 20+ minutes On Our Radar On Our Radar scans conflicts and crises around the globe every week and features some of the hotspots Crisis Group’s analysts are","body":"","url":"https://www.crisisgroup.org/sco/global/our-radar","source":"International Crisis Group","image":"","published":"2026-09-03T22:00:00+00:00","category":"kidnapping","category_label":"Kidnapping & Abduction","region":"Central Africa","country":"DR Congo","fatalities":700,"severity":"Severe","severity_score":2},{"id":"d441243806d1","title":"Drought deepen food crisis in northern Ethiopia as livestock die and crops fail","summary":"Taemo Hadish is a farmer in Haweleti, in Raya Zebo, Tigray region. She says her cattle have become too weak to move because of a lack of fodder, while the farmland she relies on to feed her family have failed to grow. “We have not seen rain for the whole year,” she says. “Our animals are dying because there is nothing for them to eat.” The UN Food and Agriculture Organization (FAO) has warned that","body":"Taemo Hadish is a farmer in Haweleti, in Raya Zebo, Tigray region. She says her cattle have become too weak to move because of a lack of fodder, while the farmland she relies on to feed her family have failed to grow. “We have not seen rain for the whole year,” she says. “Our animals are dying because there is nothing for them to eat.” The UN Food and Agriculture Organization (FAO) has warned that parts of Ethiopia could experience below-average rainfall during the June–September rainy season as El Niño conditions develop. In northern Ethiopia, particularly Tigray, conflict-related disruptions to farming seasons have left 21 woredas at high risk of severe food insecurity, with 2.45 million people in need of emergency food assistance. Similar conditions are being reported in neighbouring Afar, where livestock are also dying as the drought worsens. According to a July FAO report, more than 18,000 livestock have died in Yaqer locality, Afar region, due to shortages of water and fodder. Farmers and woreda officials interviewed by the BBC say farmers in three woredas in Tigray region are struggling because of delayed rains linked to El Niño. The weather patterns have caused drought in the southern, south-eastern and central zones, while some woredas that received rainfall have experienced heavy hailstorms and flooding. In its May seasonal forecast, the Ethiopian Meteorology Institute warned that parts of southern and eastern Tigray, North and South Wollo, Afar and eastern Ethiopia were likely to receive below-average rainfall. It also forecast dry conditions in northern and eastern parts of the country in August. “We have never experienced a drought like this. There has not been enough rain, and we are going through a very difficult year,” says Alem Misgana, who lives in Alitena, in the Eastern Zone. Article in Amharic. The post Drought deepen food crisis in northern Ethiopia as livestock die and crops fail appeared first on Ethiopia Observer .","url":"https://www.ethiopiaobserver.com/2026/09/03/drought-deepen-food-crisis-in-northern-ethiopia-as-livestock-die-and-crops-fail/","source":"Ethiopia Observer","image":"","published":"2026-09-03T21:39:41+00:00","category":"conflict","category_label":"Armed Conflict","region":"Horn of Africa","country":"Ethiopia","fatalities":0,"severity":"Moderate","severity_score":0},{"id":"2e8c35986e1e","title":"Sudanese anti-war forces reject AU backing for El Burhan dialogue","summary":"Sudanese anti-war political and civil forces have rejected the African Union’s endorsement of a “Sudanese-Sudanese... The post Sudanese anti-war forces reject AU backing for El Burhan dialogue appeared first on Dabanga Radio TV Online .","body":"Sudanese anti-war political and civil forces have rejected the African Union’s endorsement of a “Sudanese-Sudanese Dialogue Initiative” proposed by Sovereignty Council President and Commander-in-Chief of the Sudanese Armed Forces (SAF) Lt Gen Abdelfattah El Burhan, warning that the process could legitimise military rule and deepen Sudan’s division. In a memorandum submitted on September 2 to the African Peace and Security Council and the AU Political Affairs, Peace and Security Commission, the groups rejected the AU Commission’s August 31 statement welcoming the initiative . They said the endorsement contradicted the Peace and Security Council’s position that a humanitarian truce should lead to an immediate and comprehensive ceasefire before any political process. They also criticised the AU for backing an initiative proposed by El Burhan, whose October 2021 military coup resulted in Sudan’s suspension from AU activities. The groups stressed that dialogue itself was not the problem but argued that any credible political process must be genuinely Sudanese-owned, with participants, agenda and timing agreed by Sudanese stakeholders. They called for the humanitarian crisis and cessation of hostilities to remain at the centre of the process. A group photo of members of the Sudanese-Sudanese Dialogue Committee after the committee’s press conference in Khartoum on Sunday, August 30, 2026 (Photo: Social media) They also demanded the exclusion of the National Congress Party, the Islamic Movement and affiliated forces, which they accused of playing a documented role in starting and prolonging the war. The memorandum warned that holding dialogue in territory controlled by one side of the conflict, without a prior ceasefire or broad participation, could entrench the political and territorial fragmentation created by the war. The groups called on the AU to explain its endorsement, reaffirm its ceasefire-first position, maintain neutrality and coordinate international mediation efforts, including the Quartet and Quintet mechanism. The AU Commission said its support was based on the need for an inclusive process involving Sudanese civil, political and societal actors. It said the dialogue should ultimately lead to a united, sovereign Sudan governed by legitimate civilian institutions, with a single national security structure and peaceful mechanisms for resolving political differences. Othman Mirghani, spokesman for the Sudanese-Sudanese Dialogue Committee, welcomed the AU statement, saying it came at a crucial stage as the committee began its work. Speaking to Radio Dabanga, Mirghani said regional backing could help secure wider international support while preserving the committee’s independence. He said the initiative sought direct dialogue among Sudanese inside the country and that the AU could help facilitate contacts with the UN, United States, UK and European Union. The paramilitary Rapid Support Forces aligned-government, Sudan Founding Alliance (Tasees) , also rejected the AU endorsement, describing it as support for the army’s position and bias towards one side of the conflict. Tasees spokesperson Ahmed Tagad Lisan said El Burhan had called for the dialogue, formed its preparatory committee and determined its agenda and venue, giving the SAF’s leadership undue influence over the process. The anti-war forces reiterated that they remain open to dialogue but insisted that a credible process must first stop the fighting, protect civilians and prevent the war from being used to legitimise competing centres of power. Share article The post Sudanese anti-war forces reject AU backing for El Burhan dialogue appeared first on Dabanga Radio TV Online .","url":"https://www.dabangasudan.org/en/all-news/article/sudanese-anti-war-forces-reject-au-backing-for-el-burhan-dialogue","source":"Dabanga Sudan","image":"","published":"2026-09-03T20:39:50+00:00","category":"conflict","category_label":"Armed Conflict","region":"Horn of Africa","country":"Sudan","fatalities":0,"severity":"Moderate","severity_score":0},{"id":"892ebc8d79cb","title":"Laftagaren forces attack Somali army positions in Baidoa again","summary":"MOGADISHU (Somaliguardian) – Heavy fighting erupted in Baidoa on Thursday after forces loyal to ousted Southwest State President Abdiasis Laftagaren attacked the city from multiple directions, seizing two federal army bases on its outskirts before entering the town and engaging government troops in fierce gun battles that lasted for hours. Laftagaren’s Darwish forces entered Baidoa […]","body":"MOGADISHU (Somaliguardian) – Heavy fighting erupted in Baidoa on Thursday after forces loyal to ousted Southwest State President Abdiasis Laftagaren attacked the city from multiple directions, seizing two federal army bases on its outskirts before entering the town and engaging government troops in fierce gun battles that lasted for hours. Laftagaren’s Darwish forces entered Baidoa early in the morning, according to a spokesman for the forces, who said they would continue fighting until the last federal soldier leaves the region and control of the region is fully restored to its people. The forces later withdrew from the city, bringing the fighting to an end. Southwest State Information Minister Nur Sidow told reporters in Baidoa after the fighting that those who had attacked the city were Al-Shabaab , and said the assault had begun with suicide car bombs. The Somali federal government made a similar claim last month after Laftagaren’s forces launched another attack on Baidoa. Regional authorities and the federal government have yet to provide any tangible evidence for the assertion that the attackers were Al-Shabaab. The latest fighting left multiple people dead and caused other casualties, although there are no official figures. Among those killed was the commander of the personal guards of current Southwest leader Adan Madobe. Isaq Abdulle, commander of the Somali army’s 8th Brigade of the 60th Division, sustained serious injuries. Baidoa was once a relatively peaceful town under Laftagaren, but it has become a battleground since he was toppled. The repeated attacks by his forces have exposed the inability of the federal government and its Southwest allies to defend the city, even after thousands of troops were deployed there since March, analysts say. The latest assault highlights the continuing security crisis in Baidoa, where recurrent fighting has challenged the federal government’s control of the Southwest State capital. Contact us: info@somaliguardian.com","url":"https://www.somaliguardian.com/news/somalia-news/laftagaren-forces-attack-somali-army-positions-baidoa-again/","source":"Somalia Guardian","image":"","published":"2026-09-03T17:50:48+00:00","category":"terrorism","category_label":"Terrorism","region":"Horn of Africa","country":"Somalia","fatalities":0,"severity":"Moderate","severity_score":0},{"id":"e43221aa6f68","title":"Somalia’s End-of-year Cliffhanger","summary":"Somalia’s End-of-year Cliffhanger pzeiler Thu, 09/03/2026 - 16:47 Latest Updates Africa Asia-Pacific Europe Latin America & Caribbean Middle East & North Africa United States Global Issues & Institutions My Reading List Podcast / Africa 03 September 2026 1 minutes Somalia’s End-of-year Cliffhanger This week on The Horn , Alan talks with Samira Gaid, founder and senior analyst at the Mogadishu-base","body":"","url":"https://www.crisisgroup.org/pod/africa/somalia/somalias-end-year-cliffhanger","source":"International Crisis Group","image":"","published":"2026-09-03T14:47:04+00:00","category":"terrorism","category_label":"Terrorism","region":"Horn of Africa","country":"Somalia","fatalities":0,"severity":"Moderate","severity_score":0},{"id":"cf84074c1a3f","title":"Red Cross helps 38,500 families reconnect as Sudan’s missing-person crisis deepens","summary":"By Suleiman Siri for Radio Dabanga More than 11,000 people are now registered as missing... The post Red Cross helps 38,500 families reconnect as Sudan’s missing-person crisis deepens appeared first on Dabanga Radio TV Online .","body":"By Suleiman Siri for Radio Dabanga More than 11,000 people are now registered as missing in Sudan, a 40% increase in the past year, as the war fuels displacement, arrests, and disappearances, according to the International Committee of the Red Cross (ICRC). The ICRC has helped 38,512 families reconnect or maintain contact with relatives, while clarifying the fate and whereabouts of 613 missing people in the first half of 2026, its spokesperson in Sudan, Farid Al Humaid, told Radio Dabanga. Farid El Humaid, spokesperson for the ICRC delegation in Sudan (Photo: Supplied / ICRC) The issue of people who have disappeared or gone missing is no longer simply a matter of figures in human rights and media reports. It has become a grave concern for families, communities, and international and local organisations as disappearances become increasingly common as the war spreads. The crisis has worsened amid waves of displacement, as civilians flee fighting between the army and the Rapid Support Forces (RSF). After battles end, campaigns of arrests often follow, with people accused of political or ethnic affiliation. In many cases, relatives are denied information about detainees, leaving families in a cycle of anguish, uncertainty, and psychological distress. Organisations searching for missing people face major obstacles, including a lack of coordination and the absence of a central database recording people who have disappeared or gone missing. The absence of legislation criminalising enforced disappearance has also contributed to the lack of specialised prosecution and courts. For international organisations such as the ICRC, access to detention facilities remains a major challenge. Disrupted communications networks in some areas, combined with the direct impact of the conflict, have further complicated field coordination. The International Committee of the Red Cross (ICRC) facilitated the release of 64 Sudanese army detainees who had been held captive by the Rapid Support Forces (RSF) (File photo: ICRC) Field coordination Al Humaid says field coordination depends heavily on the ICRC’s partnership with the Sudanese Red Crescent Society, whose volunteers are present on the frontlines and are well placed to reach vulnerable communities and act as a link between the ICRC and the people it serves. “In the first half of 2026 alone, the ICRC and the Sudanese Red Crescent Society provided assistance to 1,973 people through telephone calls and field visits, including through the ICRC’s Restoring Family Links hotline,” he says. “In addition, we helped 38,512 families reconnect or maintain contact through our free telephone service.” Families searching for missing relatives can contact the ICRC, the Red Cross, or Red Crescent societies where they are based, or call the ICRC’s free Restoring Family Links hotline from inside Sudan on 60222, Al Humaid tells Radio Dabanga. In 2025, the ICRC clarified the fate and whereabouts of 846 missing people registered with it, providing their families with some reassurance and an end to the uncertainty they had endured. A child who fled with his family from El Fasher to Tawila shows signs of beatings and injuries (Photo: ICRC) During the first half of 2026, the ICRC also provided wireless internet services to 7,630 people and mobile phone charging services to 7,226, helping people maintain contact and avoid losing communication during emergencies. Preventing disappearances The only real guarantee against disappearances being repeated is strict adherence to international humanitarian law by all parties involved in hostilities, Al Humaid says. “We continue to urge all these parties to take every possible measure to prevent people from disappearing,” he says. This includes registering the identities of everyone who is detained or displaced, allowing detainees to communicate with their families, clarifying the fate of people who disappear, and ensuring that the remains of the dead are managed safely and with dignity. The ICRC says it maintains confidential and ongoing dialogue with authorities and armed actors to incorporate these obligations under international humanitarian law into their operational procedures. Al Humaid told Radio Dabanga that the ICRC’s biggest obstacles concern access to detention facilities, which is currently severely restricted. Disrupted communications networks in some areas and the direct impact of the conflict have made field coordination even more difficult. Despite the challenges, the ICRC continues to engage in bilateral and confidential dialogue with all parties to the conflict to seek safe, unimpeded, and sustained humanitarian access, including to detention facilities. Displaced people queue for aid in Darfur (File photo: Mohamed Jamal/ICRC) “In these discussions, we remind them of their obligations under international humanitarian law to respect minimum standards, ensure detainees can be traced, treat them with dignity, and meet their basic needs,” Al Humaid says. Missing or forcibly disappeared? There may be some overlap between detainees and victims of enforced disappearance, Al Humaid says. A family may consider a relative missing if they receive no information about their fate or whereabouts after the person has been arrested or detained. However, not every case of detention without notification necessarily constitutes enforced disappearance. The term has a specific legal meaning, and determining whether it applies requires an assessment of the individual circumstances of each case. Families regularly contact the ICRC about relatives they believe may have been arrested or detained. The organisation records these requests and, through confidential bilateral dialogue with the relevant authorities, seeks to establish the fate and whereabouts of the people concerned and, where possible, help restore contact with their families. As a neutral and independent humanitarian organisation, the ICRC does not publish detailed statistics on individual cases in which missing people are found in detention or dead during search efforts. “This confidential approach is essential,” Al Humaid says. “It enables us to secure access, search for missing people on behalf of their families, and ultimately obtain answers for them, without politicising the process or putting our access to vulnerable people at risk.” Three-part response The ICRC is carrying out a comprehensive response in close coordination with the Sudanese Red Crescent Society, centred on three areas: restoring contact, searching for missing people, and managing human remains. The organisation operates a Restoring Family Links hotline and provides direct services, including free telephone calls and Wi-Fi, to gather initial information and help families maintain or restore contact, particularly during emergencies such as displacement. ICRC teams and Sudanese Red Crescent volunteers collect requests to search for missing people and conduct searches on behalf of their families. The ICRC also supports relevant authorities and the Sudanese Red Crescent Society in managing human remains safely and with dignity. This includes distributing body bags, rehabilitating mortuaries, and training first responders and forensic workers in safe burial practices. Such measures are crucial for ensuring that people can be identified in the future, Al Humaid concludes. Relief agency with Sudanese refugees (File photo” Radio Dabanga) Note: This report has been translated from Arabic with the assistance of artificial intelligence tools, and subsequently reviewed and adapted by a human editor before publication. Share article The post Red Cross helps 38,500 families reconnect as Sudan’s missing-person crisis deepens appeared first on Dabanga Radio TV Online .","url":"https://www.dabangasudan.org/en/all-news/article/red-cross-helps-38512-families-reconnect-as-sudans-missing-person-crisis-deepens","source":"Dabanga Sudan","image":"","published":"2026-09-03T11:16:00+00:00","category":"conflict","category_label":"Armed Conflict","region":"Horn of Africa","country":"Sudan","fatalities":0,"severity":"Moderate","severity_score":0},{"id":"bdd5ec8774aa","title":"Air strike destroys WFP food aid trucks in Sudan’s Kordofan","summary":"The UN World Food Programme (WFP) has condemned an aerial attack on two trucks carrying... The post Air strike destroys WFP food aid trucks in Sudan’s Kordofan appeared first on Dabanga Radio TV Online .","body":"The UN World Food Programme (WFP) has condemned an aerial attack on two trucks carrying food aid to families in South Kordofan. A WFP statement on Wednesday confirms that the trucks were hit outside Dilling on 30 August while carrying WFP assistance to Kadugli. They were clearly marked with WFP banners and flags, WFP says. “More than 50 tonnes of food were destroyed, including cereals, yellow split peas, vegetable oil, and salt. The supplies were enough to provide life-saving assistance to 5,800 people for a month,” the WFP statement says. In Kadugli, the organisation is supporting nearly 58,000 people facing emergency levels of food insecurity or worse with food aid. The agency laments that the attack is the latest to disrupt the work of the WFP and other humanitarian agencies in Sudan. The agency says it has recorded eight aerial attacks since the beginning of the year that have damaged its premises and disrupted operations. The WFP has urged all parties involved in the fighting to respect international humanitarian law and protect civilians, humanitarian workers, and aid supplies. “Attacks on aid workers and humanitarian operations are unacceptable and must stop,” it says. Share article The post Air strike destroys WFP food aid trucks in Sudan’s Kordofan appeared first on Dabanga Radio TV Online .","url":"https://www.dabangasudan.org/en/all-news/article/air-strike-destroys-wfp-food-aid-trucks-in-sudans-kordofan","source":"Dabanga Sudan","image":"","published":"2026-09-03T08:46:00+00:00","category":"conflict","category_label":"Armed Conflict","region":"Horn of Africa","country":"Sudan","fatalities":0,"severity":"Moderate","severity_score":0},{"id":"20c2557a9ebc","title":"Sudan’s Sovereignty Council chief inspects army's readiness in Blue Nile State amid escalating clashes with RSF","summary":"Abdel Fattah al-Burhan's visit comes amid escalating clashes between army, Raid Support Forces in Blue Nile State","body":"The chairman of Sudan’s Sovereign Council, Abdel Fattah al-Burhan, inspected the readiness of the army on Wednesday in Blue Nile state in southeastern Sudan.\n\nThe visit comes amid escalating clashes in the state between the army and the Rapid Support Forces (RSF) and its ally, the Sudan People’s Liberation Movement-North (SPLM-N).\n\nIn a statement, the army’s 4th infantry division said that Burhan, who also commands the Sudanese army, visited the city of Damazin, the state capital and the headquarters of the division.\n\nIt added that Burhan inspected the division’s forces and assessed their combat readiness and preparedness to carry out their assigned missions.\n\nHe was also briefed on the security and operational situation in the region, according to the statement.\n\nBurhan stressed “the need to raise levels of preparedness and vigilance and strengthen discipline and combat readiness,\" according to the statement.\n\nHe also called for “continuing to carry out missions in accordance with military plans and directives.\"\n\nBurhan also inspected the military hospital in Damazin, where he was briefed on the conditions of wounded soldiers and patients, and the hospital’s operations.\n\nHe affirmed the army command’s commitment to its personnel and to providing them with the necessary care.\n\nThe statement quoted 4th Infantry Division Commander Maj. Gen. Ismail al-Tayeb, who said Burhan’s visit “enhanced their readiness to carry out their missions.”\n\n“The division’s forces are fully prepared to perform their military duties, secure the region and protect civilians,” he said.\n\nHe added that the division was ready to “continue implementing the plans and directives issued by the command.”\n\nBurhan’s visit comes amid rapidly developing military developments in Blue Nile state.\n\nOn Tuesday, the first contingent of joint forces from armed movements allied with the army arrived in Blue Nile state.\n\nOn Saturday, the army announced that it deployed an elite brigade affiliated with the General Intelligence Service to the battlefield in the Bakori axis in the state.\n\nThe RSF announced Friday that it seized the Bakori and Sarkam military bases southeast of Damazin.\n\nThe army also announced last Thursday that it had shot down a “hostile” drone in Blue Nile state after it crossed the border from Ethiopia.\n\nIt said it was the third drone it had announced shooting down within a week.\n\nIn addition to Blue Nile and the Darfur region in western Sudan, clashes have been ongoing in the three Kordofan states -- North, West and South Kordofan -- between the army and the RSF since Oct. 25.\n\nSudan has been witnessing a war between the army and the RSF since April 2023, which has killed tens of thousands and displaced around 13 million, according to UN and international estimates.","url":"https://www.aa.com.tr/en/africa/sudan-s-sovereignty-council-chief-inspects-armys-readiness-in-blue-nile-state-amid-escalating-clashes-with-rsf/4045861","source":"Anadolu · Africa","image":"","published":"2026-09-02T23:53:18+00:00","category":"conflict","category_label":"Armed Conflict","region":"Horn of Africa","country":"Sudan","fatalities":0,"severity":"Moderate","severity_score":0},{"id":"9c29b7db4ac5","title":"Policing the Bangsamoro: Peace in the Southern Philippines","summary":"Policing the Bangsamoro: Peace in the Southern Philippines cthuratong Thu, 09/03/2026 - 00:00 Latest Updates Africa Asia-Pacific Europe Latin America & Caribbean Middle East & North Africa United States Global Issues & Institutions My Reading List policing-bangsamoro-peace-southern-philippines_latest.pdf (4.51 MB) policing-bangsamoro-peace-southern-philippines_latest.pdf (4.51 MB) Soldiers and pol","body":"","url":"https://www.crisisgroup.org/rpt/asia-pacific/philippines/358-policing-bangsamoro-peace-southern-philippines","source":"International Crisis Group","image":"","published":"2026-09-02T22:00:00+00:00","category":"kidnapping","category_label":"Kidnapping & Abduction","region":"North Africa","country":"","fatalities":0,"severity":"Moderate","severity_score":0},{"id":"d066a24574f5","title":"The Copyright Trap: How fake claims are silencing independent media and journalists in Somalia","summary":"https://www.horndiplomat.com/feed/ By: Somali Journalists Syndicate – SJS MOGADISHU, Somalia 30 August 2026 – Independent media houses and journalists in Somalia are facing an escalating wave of online attacks and censorship, including coordinated fake copyright claims, content takedowns and demands for payment, often from individuals apparently operating inside and outside Somalia, according to a","body":"","url":"https://www.horndiplomat.com/the-copyright-trap-how-fake-claims-are-silencing-independent-media-and-journalists-in-somalia/","source":"Horn Diplomat","image":"","published":"2026-09-02T14:54:08+00:00","category":"conflict","category_label":"Armed Conflict","region":"Horn of Africa","country":"Somalia","fatalities":0,"severity":"Moderate","severity_score":0},{"id":"24629b3eecb0","title":"Somalia: Armed militias seize election centers in Beledweyne","summary":"MOGADISHU (Somaliguardian) – Armed militias seized centers designated for voter registration in Beledweyne on Tuesday, as Somalia’s electoral commission set September 30 for Hirshabelle municipal and parliamentary elections, intensifying opposition to the planned vote, Caasimada Online news website reported on Tuesday. Some of the militias, speaking to local media, said they oppose the elections b","body":"MOGADISHU (Somaliguardian) – Armed militias seized centers designated for voter registration in Beledweyne on Tuesday, as Somalia’s electoral commission set September 30 for Hirshabelle municipal and parliamentary elections, intensifying opposition to the planned vote, Caasimada Online news website reported on Tuesday. Some of the militias, speaking to local media, said they oppose the elections being conducted by the government-appointed electoral commission. They said they would not accept a vote in which only one favored candidate is allowed to contest while all other candidates are banned, arguing that the process mirrors such an arrangement. The Somali federal government has not yet taken any action against the militias. The seizure comes as Hawadle clan politicians strongly oppose the planned Hirshabelle election, saying the process is not fair. They are also demanding their turn at the presidency of the Hirshabelle regional administration. The competing objections have placed the planned elections under heightened political tension in Beledweyne, where the seizure of the registration centers has directly disrupted locations designated for the electoral process. The militias’ opposition centers on what they describe as a process that favors one candidate while excluding others, while Hawadle politicians are challenging the fairness of the Hirshabelle election and pressing for their turn at the regional presidency. With the electoral commission having scheduled the Hirshabelle municipal and parliamentary elections for September 30, the dispute over the process remains unresolved, while the Somali federal government has yet to take action against the militias. Contact us: info@somaliguardian.com","url":"https://www.somaliguardian.com/news/somalia-news/somalia-armed-militias-seize-election-centers-beledweyne/","source":"Somalia Guardian","image":"","published":"2026-09-02T14:03:56+00:00","category":"conflict","category_label":"Armed Conflict","region":"Horn of Africa","country":"Somalia","fatalities":0,"severity":"Moderate","severity_score":0},{"id":"7d29b0d0f349","title":"Violent Clashes Stall School Reopenings in DRC","summary":"Since at least 2025, hundreds of schoolchildren have been denied access to schools and educational facilities due to the violent clashes between the rebel groups and state forces. The resurgence of the Ebola virus has worsened the situation, as schools tried to reopen amid the disease outbreak. The post Violent Clashes Stall School Reopenings in DRC appeared first on HumAngle .","body":"Violent clashes between the Congolese armed forces and the M23 rebels have disrupted school reopenings in several localities in North Kivu in the Democratic Republic of Congo (DRC). As the 2026-2027 academic year begins, schools have not yet reopened in several rural areas of the region, particularly in the Bankindi, Banamulema, and Balindu communities of the Kisimba tribal group. Just before the school year was set to begin, many families were forced to flee their homes to escape the intense fighting between the armed groups, particularly the Wazalendo militia, and the M23/AFC rebels in the region. Parents, students, and teachers have sought refuge to avoid the crossfire. Some families have taken shelter in the bushes, while others have relocated to towns like Pinga and Kalembe. The situation is severely compromising the normal reopening of school activities. For the displaced families, the immediate priority is survival, especially since they lack the means to pay school fees for their children. “We escaped with our children without carrying along our belongings. Today, we do not even know where we will sleep or what we will eat,” a parent taking refuge with his family in Pinga told HumAngle. “Under these conditions, it is difficult for us to think about school fees, uniforms or other school needs for our children. We very much wish for peace to return so that our children can return to school.” Some schools in Pinga have started admitting children of displaced families from zones affected by the fighting. A senior official from Kabeza Secondary School in Pinga, whose name is withheld for safety reasons, stated that the influx of students creates space issues, as the school must accommodate additional students from war-affected areas. “We are forced to welcome children from displaced families in order to enable them to continue their education. However, admitting them is not easy because some come without school requirements and their parents do not have the means to meet their needs. We demand assistance so that these children are not abandoned because of the war,” the official said. Faced with this situation, authorities in the education sector of the Walikale 3 sub-division are encouraging displaced parents to enrol their children in schools in their refuge zones, notably in Pinga and Kalembe, so they can continue schooling. Families hiding in bushes with their children, lacking access to schools, subsistence means, and essential services, are compromising their children’s educational futures. Since at least 2025, hundreds of schoolchildren have been denied access to schools and educational facilities due to the violent clashes between the rebel groups and state forces. The resurgence of the Ebola virus has worsened the situation, as schools tried to reopen amid the disease outbreak. The post Violent Clashes Stall School Reopenings in DRC appeared first on HumAngle .","url":"https://humanglemedia.com/violent-clashes-stall-school-reopenings-in-drc/","source":"HumAngle","image":"","published":"2026-09-02T13:27:17+00:00","category":"kidnapping","category_label":"Kidnapping & Abduction","region":"Central Africa","country":"DR Congo","fatalities":0,"severity":"Moderate","severity_score":0},{"id":"1def27184ae2","title":"Kidnapping on the Rise Near Cameroon’s Border with Nigeria","summary":"The surge in kidnapping near Cameroon’s border with Nigeria coincides with several ongoing crises in the region. The post Kidnapping on the Rise Near Cameroon’s Border with Nigeria appeared first on HumAngle .","body":"A report from the United Nations Office for the Coordination of Humanitarian Affairs (UNOCHA) has revealed that over 300 persons were kidnapped by terrorists in the Far North region of Cameroon during April, May, and June of 2026. In the same timeframe in 2025, 141 abductions were reported, which is a staggering 118 per cent increase in kidnappings. The recent report shows a serious decline in the region’s security landscape in the second quarter of 2026. During this period, 72 people were killed, and 48 were injured, which actually reflects a 6.5 per cent decrease in fatalities compared to the previous year, when 77 civilians lost their lives. The UNOCHA report stresses that understanding the worsening security situation in Far North Cameroon requires more than just looking at casualty numbers. Compared to the latter half of 2025, there were 167 more kidnappings, suggesting that abductions have become the major form of violence affecting communities near the Nigerian border. Beyond the loss of lives, terrorist operations in the region seem to have established a pervasive climate of fear, limiting movement and undermining the livelihood of local communities. The report states that such violence can lead to new displacements as people flee to areas deemed safer. For humanitarian organisations, the ongoing insecurity complicates efforts to deliver assistance to those in need. The report also noted a resurgence of improvised explosive devices (IEDs) in the Logone-et-Chari division, with two such incidents occurring between April and June this year. While the limited occurrences of IEDs do not definitively indicate a sustained reduction in violence, they add to the potential risks faced by residents and humanitarian workers, especially during periods of displacement in violence-affected areas. This surge in kidnappings coincides with several ongoing crises in the region. The UNOCHA report mentions new population displacements, a worsening measles outbreak, the emergence of cholera cases, and ongoing food insecurity. The post Kidnapping on the Rise Near Cameroon’s Border with Nigeria appeared first on HumAngle .","url":"https://humanglemedia.com/kidnapping-on-the-rise-near-cameroons-border-with-nigeria/","source":"HumAngle","image":"","published":"2026-09-02T10:13:36+00:00","category":"kidnapping","category_label":"Kidnapping & Abduction","region":"Central Africa","country":"Cameroon","fatalities":72,"severity":"Severe","severity_score":2},{"id":"0f754964c7ce","title":"Displaced people in Sudan’s Darfur and Kordofan face humanitarian and health catastrophe","summary":"About 6,200 children are suffering from malnutrition in Malha in North Darfur, while a cholera... The post Displaced people in Sudan’s Darfur and Kordofan face humanitarian and health catastrophe appeared first on Dabanga Radio TV Online .","body":"About 6,200 children are suffering from malnutrition in Malha in North Darfur, while a cholera outbreak is causing a health crisis in Um Jak administrative area and the Hamir Um Taqola village in West Kordofan, the General Coordination of Displaced Persons and Refugee Camps and the Dar Hamar Emergency Room say. Adam Rijal, the official spokesperson for the coordination, says in a statement on Tuesday that about 6,200 children in Malha are malnourished, including around 1,500 suffering from severe acute malnutrition. About 1,350 pregnant and breastfeeding women are also malnourished, he says. Several diseases, including tuberculosis, anaemia, and diarrhoea, are widespread in the locality, Rijal says, while medicines are available only in very limited quantities and humanitarian organisations are completely absent from the area. He describes the humanitarian and health situation in Malha as catastrophic and forgotten by the world, and calls on the United Nations and international organisations to break the isolation imposed on Malha, send emergency teams, and open an independent international investigation into the violations that have led to the humanitarian collapse. In West Kordofan, the Dar Hamar Emergency Room expresses concern about the catastrophic health situation in Um Jak administrative area and Hamir Um Taqola village, in the rural El Odaya Zakaria area and neighbouring villages, following a severe cholera outbreak. The emergency room says in a statement on Tuesday that eight people have died and 40 cases have been recorded over the past 48 hours. A spokesperson for the emergency room told Radio Dabanga that the area has no medical staff and no life-saving medicines or intravenous fluids. There are also no health isolation centres capable of containing the outbreak, the spokesperson says. Humanitarians step up cholera response amid conflict, rising needs OCHA reports that despite active conflict and funding shortfalls, humanitarian organizations continue to deliver life-saving assistance across Sudan. From January to July, the UN and humanitarian partners reached 12.3 million people across Sudan with at least one form of assistance. However, just 2.2 million people – or 18 per cent of those reached – received assistance across multiple sectors, underscoring the impact of underfunding and other challenges on efforts to reach people with the full range of support they need. Meanwhile, the UN and partners are stepping up efforts to tackle cholera as the rainy season continues. According to the World Health Organization (WHO), over 2,600 cases had been reported by early August across multiple states, with the Kordofan region at the epicentre. Health workers continue to provide treatment, conduct vaccination campaigns and distribute essential medical supplies. Tragically,WHO reports that a vaccinator was killed in an attack on a market in West Kordofan State on Saturday, while searching for her son among casualties from an earlier attack. Meanwhile, humanitarians are scaling up efforts to provide clean water. In Kordofan State, a water system rehabilitated with UNICEF support in Abu Jubayhah is now providing safe water to 25,000 people every day, including 5,000 displaced people. At the same time, insecurity continues to drive displacement, including in the Kordofan region. IOM reports that some 730 people fled several villages in Abassiya locality, South Kordofan, on Sunday. They sought safety elsewhere in Abassiya and in Um Rawaba locality in neighbouring North Kordofan. OCHA once again calls on all parties to protect civilians, including health and humanitarian workers, and to facilitate rapid, safe, unhindered and sustained humanitarian access. Note: This article has been translated from Arabic with the assistance of artificial intelligence tools, and subsequently reviewed and adapted by a human editor before publication. Share article The post Displaced people in Sudan’s Darfur and Kordofan face humanitarian and health catastrophe appeared first on Dabanga Radio TV Online .","url":"https://www.dabangasudan.org/en/all-news/article/displaced-people-in-sudans-darfur-and-kordofan-face-humanitarian-and-health-catastrophe","source":"Dabanga Sudan","image":"","published":"2026-09-02T09:50:00+00:00","category":"conflict","category_label":"Armed Conflict","region":"Horn of Africa","country":"Sudan","fatalities":0,"severity":"Moderate","severity_score":0},{"id":"59a3806c1871","title":"Somalia NSA confirms troops crossed into Ethiopia region","summary":"MOGADISHU (Somaliguardian) – Somalia’s National Security Adviser, Aways Haji Yusuf, on Tuesday confirmed for the first time that an undisclosed number of Somali National Army (SNA) troops who recently completed a Saudi Arabia-funded training program in Hanaanbure, Galmudug, have crossed into Ethiopia’s Somali region. Yusuf said the soldiers, who had undergone more than a year […]","body":"MOGADISHU (Somaliguardian) – Somalia’s National Security Adviser, Aways Haji Yusuf, on Tuesday confirmed for the first time that an undisclosed number of Somali National Army (SNA) troops who recently completed a Saudi Arabia-funded training program in Hanaanbure, Galmudug, have crossed into Ethiopia’s Somali region. Yusuf said the soldiers, who had undergone more than a year of training, were granted leave to visit their families and that the federal government is aware of their presence in the region for that purpose. “If there are other issues, we will let you (media) know,” Yusuf told reporters. Defection Reports Preceded the Admission The confirmation comes days after multiple Somali media outlets, including outlets based in Ethiopia’s Somali region, reported that dozens of soldiers – part of a cohort of 3,000 trained at Hananbure – had defected and were subsequently captured by regional forces before being taken to Jigjiga. Reported figures for the number involved have varied widely across outlets, with some citing 300, others 180, and others as high as 900. None of these figures has been independently confirmed, and it remains unclear whether the soldiers defected or were simply on approved leave to visit their families, as the government maintains. Analyst: Admission “Likely to Generate More Questions” A Nairobi-based Horn of Africa analyst with Sahan Research described the National Security Adviser’s admission as “refreshing and commendable, but likely to generate more questions.” “Did Somalia recruit forces from the Somali Regional State of Ethiopia with permission from regional and federal authorities? If not, how will Addis Ababa respond?” the analyst asked. A Backdrop of Strained Ties The episode unfolds against a backdrop of strained relations between Mogadishu and Addis Ababa in recent years, with the two governments aligned to separate geopolitical camps. Ethiopia has increasingly sided with the United Arab Emirates, including in its support for the Rapid Support Forces in Sudan, while Somalia has increasingly aligned itself with Saudi Arabia and Turkey. Contact us: info@somaliguardian.com","url":"https://www.somaliguardian.com/news/somalia-news/somalia-nsa-confirms-troops-crossed-ethiopia-somali-region/","source":"Somalia Guardian","image":"","published":"2026-09-02T08:46:42+00:00","category":"other","category_label":"Other Security News","region":"Horn of Africa","country":"Ethiopia","fatalities":0,"severity":"Severe","severity_score":2},{"id":"d12d2272fd2d","title":"22,000 displaced by fighting in Sudan’s Blue Nile state in last week: UN","summary":"Agency says civilians fleeing violence in Geissan district have sought safety in several areas, including state capital Ed Damazine","body":"About 22,000 people have been displaced from Sudan’s Blue Nile state in the last week as fighting intensified in the Geissan district, the UN Office for the Coordination of Humanitarian Affairs (OCHA) said Tuesday.\n\nOCHA said in a statement that continued violence is putting civilians at risk and forcing thousands to flee their homes.\n\n“In Blue Nile State, local authorities report that nearly 22,000 people may have been displaced in the past seven days due to escalating fighting in the locality of Geissan,” it said.\n\nThe statement said that “many are sheltering in schools or have fled to other areas, including towards the state capital, Ed Damazine.”\n\nThe Geissan Emergency Room, a local aid body, said Sunday that nearly 20,000 people had fled the area of Medina 12 in the Geissan district because of the fighting and worsening security conditions.\n\nBlue Nile has seen a new escalation in the last two days after the paramilitary Rapid Support Forces (RSF) and allied Sudan People’s Liberation Movement-North launched attacks in several areas of the state.\n\nOCHA also said attacks on Saturday on villages north and northwest of El Obeid, the capital of North Kordofan state, caused casualties and displaced civilians, amid reports that civilian property and livestock were looted.\n\n“In West Darfur State, civilians were reportedly killed and injured when a drone strike hit a fuel market in Adekong, near the border with Chad, last Thursday. The attack also damaged civilian property,” it said.\n\nOCHA renewed its demand for all parties to the conflict “to protect civilians and civilian infrastructure, respect international humanitarian law, and allow humanitarian organizations to operate safely and independently.”\n\nThe International Organization for Migration (IOM) said Monday that 450 people were displaced from the village of al-Qardud Abu Zeid in the al-Rahad district of North Kordofan because of deteriorating security in a single day.\n\nAlongside Blue Nile and Darfur, fighting has continued since Oct. 25 in Sudan’s three Kordofan states -- North Kordofan, West Kordofan and South Kordofan -- between the army and the RSF.\n\nSudan has been at war between the army and the RSF since April 2023. The conflict has killed tens of thousands of people and displaced about 13 million, according to UN and international estimates.","url":"https://www.aa.com.tr/en/africa/22-000-displaced-by-fighting-in-sudan-s-blue-nile-state-in-last-week-un/4044644","source":"Anadolu · Africa","image":"","published":"2026-09-02T00:14:05+00:00","category":"conflict","category_label":"Armed Conflict","region":"Horn of Africa","country":"Sudan","fatalities":0,"severity":"Moderate","severity_score":0},{"id":"0dd426f1eff4","title":"Terrorism forces 57 primary schools to shut in Nigeria’s Borno, 80 students remain captive: Official","summary":"Soldiers have been deployed in Mussa and Lassa alongside local vigilantes.","body":"At least 57 primary schools are currently not functioning in Nigeria’s northeastern Borno State due to insecurity, while about 80 students abducted during attacks on schools remain in captivity, according to local education authorities.\n\nThe situation was disclosed by Fasuma Askira, education secretary of Askira-Uba Local Government Area, where only 56 of the 113 primary schools are currently operating.\n\nOn May 15, terrorists attacked Mussa Central Primary School and abducted dozens of children. Although the exact number remains disputed, the Borno State Government has more recently put the number of missing pupils at about 44.\n\nOn June 29, insurgents attacked Government Day Secondary School in Lassa while students were sitting for a National Examinations Council examination. Some students were subsequently rescued, but 36 students and a teacher remained in captivity.\n\nCombining the latest government estimate from Mussa with the verified Lassa figure puts the number of students still in captivity at about 80.\n\nAs of Aug. 31, the children abducted from Mussa had spent 108 days in captivity, while the Lassa students had been held for 63 days.\n\nSimon Waindu, whose daughter is among the Lassa students, said none of the children had returned and that families had received no information about their rescue.\n\nMeanwhile, authorities have closed schools in communities affected by insecurity. Authorities have also proposed the deployment of community vigilantes around schools when classes resume, while soldiers have been deployed in Mussa and Lassa alongside local vigilantes.\n\nThe latest developments come after Anadolu reported in June that terrorists abducted six women and two babies near the scene of a school attack in Borno state.\n\nThe incident occurred shortly after suspected insurgents attacked Government Day Secondary School in Lassa while students were taking examinations, further highlighting the vulnerability of civilians around schools in the conflict-affected region.\n\nNigeria's northeastern states have faced more than a decade of insurgency, with Boko Haram and ISWAP among the armed groups responsible for attacks, abductions and displacement.\n\nThe continued captivity of students and closure of schools raise concerns over the long-term educational consequences of the conflict, particularly for children in communities already affected by displacement and poverty.","url":"https://www.aa.com.tr/en/africa/terrorism-forces-57-primary-schools-to-shut-in-nigeria-s-borno-80-students-remain-captive-official/4044454","source":"Anadolu · Africa","image":"","published":"2026-09-01T18:52:15+00:00","category":"kidnapping","category_label":"Kidnapping & Abduction","region":"West Africa","country":"Nigeria","fatalities":0,"severity":"Moderate","severity_score":0},{"id":"dcb533fef839","title":"Displacement and the Right to Vote: Addressing the Exclusion of Nigeria’s IDPs in the 2027 GeneralElections","summary":"Introduction Between April 2025 and June 2026, over 2.3 million internally displaced people (IDPs) were identified by the International Organisation for Migration’s 51st DTM Site Assessment. The data highlights a 2% increase from Round 50 of the assessment. The number represents a population of Nigerians covered by the constitution and, specifically, the National Policy on […] The post Displacemen","body":"Introduction Between April 2025 and June 2026, over 2.3 million internally displaced people (IDPs) were identified by the International Organisation for Migration’s 51st DTM Site Assessment. The data highlights a 2% increase from Round 50 of the assessment. The number represents a population of Nigerians covered by the constitution and, specifically, the National Policy on IDPs to restore their rights, agency and dignity. Mostly uprooted by long-running conflicts, HumAngle found that IDPs across various communities are being pushed outside the map of the 2027 electoral processes, denying them the opportunity to exercise their rights and exposing systemic failure and neglect. What’s New? HumAngle travelled to rural communities and town centres where IDPs are hosted. Across communities in Adamawa, Borno and Katsina states, there is a pattern of recurring barriers to IDPs’ electoral participation . These include incomplete voter registration exercises, difficulty replacing or transferring Permanent Voter Cards (PVCs), limited voter education, lack of information about registration procedures, the cost and insecurity associated with travelling to original polling units, and declining confidence in the electoral process. Key findings HumAngle identifies a policy gap in the management of IDP challenges. The National Policy on IDPs says all rights contained in the Nigerian constitution and humanitarian instruments are to be enjoyed by IDPs. Yet, this has not been the case across many IDP camps. There are practical barriers to voter registration. Often, due to logistical failure to reach IDP camps and the poor implementation of the Independent National Electoral Commission’s IDPs Voting Regulations . IDPs have demonstrated their desire for inclusion and participation in electoral processes, with many first-time voters hoping to be enrolled in the voter register. Voter education and information gaps in IDP communities persist. IDPs with lost or damaged voter cards do not know how to get a replacement, while INEC’s online self-service option is ineffective due to the digital gaps in IDP and conflict-affected communities. No polling infrastructure within IDP camps. Where field visits were conducted, IDPs who have their voter cards have to go back to communities they fled to vote, further exposing them to the risks of harm. Why does it matter? Inclusion is crucial for a constitutional democracy and a nation attempting to heal from radicalisation. In this context, it restores confidence in the state and gives agency to citizens who are in their most vulnerable state. Invariably, this enhances recovery, and restores a sense of belonging for a population that seems to exist in limbo. Social recovery does not work in isolation. It has to be a whole approach, and INEC’s voter registration and participation in elections by IDPs is part of this larger process of recovery and agency-giving for IDPs. The integrity of electoral processes is only intact when no one feels excluded. What can be done INEC should create a special window urgently to regularise IDPs, register those of voting age with a special target to first-time voters across identified communities. This will enable the completion of registration, enrolment of new voters, and replacement of lost or damaged cards ahead of the 2027 elections. INEC should revamp its voter education campaign through collaboration with the National Orientation Agency and Local Government Community Development Departments to reach IDPs with clear and needed voter education and information. Civil society and the media should continue independent monitoring and reporting on voter registration and election-related exercises to keep issues visible to policymakers and the public. Humanitarian and camp management actors should integrate voter education activities within camp programmes to bridge awareness gaps. They should also leverage existing camp leadership structures to coordinate with INEC, schedule visits and follow up. The National Commission for Refugees, Migrants and Internally Displaced Persons (NCFRMI) should review the National IDP Policy and develop a complementary national framework that elaborately operationalises how the participation of IDPs in elections should henceforth be governed. Conclusion The 2027 general election is near, and IDPs, especially first-time voters, are enthusiastic and hopeful to participate in a civic exercise. The constitution granted them the right; the laws and policies empower them with the opportunity, but the system is locking them out. This clash between policy and practice has consequences. It disenfranchises and breeds national grievances where healing, recovery and agency should take centre. INEC and all relevant stakeholders have a role to reverse the dying hope for inclusion, stronger democracy and electoral integrity. Further reading Nigeria’s Displaced Communities Risk Being Shut Out of Elections, https://www.humanglemedia.com/nigerias-displaced-communities-risk-being-shut-out-of-elections Regulations for Voting by Internally Displaced Persons (IDPs), https://www.inecnigeria.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/04/REGULATIONS.pdf Acknowledgment This policy brief was made possible through the dedication of a talented team of professionals, led by Abdussamad Ahmad Yusuf, for the research, analysis, and drafting of this document. The post Displacement and the Right to Vote: Addressing the Exclusion of Nigeria’s IDPs in the 2027 GeneralElections appeared first on HumAngle .","url":"https://humanglemedia.com/displacement-and-the-right-to-vote-addressing-the-exclusion-of-nigerias-idps-in-the-2027-generalelections/","source":"HumAngle","image":"","published":"2026-09-01T14:07:10+00:00","category":"conflict","category_label":"Armed Conflict","region":"West Africa","country":"Nigeria","fatalities":0,"severity":"Moderate","severity_score":0},{"id":"4e03fc988c16","title":"Italy Expands Police Training Support for Somalia","summary":"Talyaaniga ayaa Muqdisho ku shaaciyay qorshe lagu xoojinayo iskaashiga amni ee Soomaaliya, iyadoo diiradda la saarayo tababarka booliiska, dhismaha hay’adaha amniga iyo ka hortagga xagjirnimada. The post Italy Expands Police Training Support for Somalia first appeared on Radio Dalsan .","body":"Italy expands police training and security support for Somalia Italian Ambassador Pier Mario Daccò Coppi says Rome will deepen its cooperation with Somalia on police training, institutional development and efforts to prevent violent extremism. MOGADISHU, September 1, 2026 — Italy has pledged to strengthen its support for Somalia’s security institutions, with an emphasis on police training, stabilisation and government-led projects in communities affected by conflict.Speaking at Somalia’s National Internal Security Conference in Mogadishu, Italian Ambassador Pier Mario Daccò Coppi described the gathering as an important opportunity to bring together the political and institutional leadership of the internal security sector The two-day conference brings together representatives of the federal government and federal member states to discuss security governance, institutional coordination and the supervisory role of the Ministry of Internal Security.Daccò Coppi said Somalia was making important progress in institution-building and stabilisation, although the process remained gradual. Longstanding police and security cooperation The ambassador described relations between Italy and Somalia as a historic partnership that had endured the instability and conflict of Somalia’s recent past. He said Italy’s policy was guided by support for Somalia’s sovereignty, territorial integrity, security and stabilisation. The two countries have maintained a defence cooperation framework since 2013. The Italian Carabinieri began training members of the Somali Police Force’s Darwish units in Djibouti in 2014. According to the Italian Ministry of Defence, the MIADIT programme trained 3,100 Somali police personnel over its first 10 years. The courses cover law enforcement, stability policing and responses to high-risk security incidents. Italian Ministry of Defence Italy also participates in three European Union operations linked to Somalia: EUTM Somalia, EUCAP Somalia and Operation Atalanta.EUTM supports military training, EUCAP helps strengthen civilian law-enforcement capacity, and Atalanta focuses on maritime security and counter-piracy operations. Five-year police training framework Daccò Coppi highlighted a memorandum signed in Mogadishu on July 3, 2026, by Somali Internal Security Minister Abdullahi Sheikh Ismail Fartag and Italian Interior Minister Matteo Piantedosi.The memorandum creates a government-to-government framework for vocational and academic police training, train-the-trainer programmes, study visits, exchanges and the sharing of professional expertise.It also establishes a joint working group responsible for coordinating implementation, reviewing progress and planning future activities.The framework will remain in force for an initial five years and may be renewed through the written consent of both governments. Somalia’s Ministry of Internal Security The ambassador said a further agreement on police training was expected to follow. He added that broader cooperation between the two countries’ police institutions would support efforts against transnational organised crime, irregular migration and terrorism. No date or detailed terms for the expected follow-up agreement were announced. Preventing violent extremism Daccò Coppi argued that force and legislation alone were not sufficient to counter violent extremism.He said security measures should be accompanied by efforts to address social and economic conditions that can contribute to radicalisation.The ambassador connected this approach to the Towards Peace and Stability in Somalia , or TPSS, programme launched by Italy and the Federal Government of Somalia.Under the initiative, Somali authorities identify priority interventions, while Italy provides funding for projects intended to strengthen services and stability in vulnerable areas. Health projects in three districts Somalia’s Office of the Prime Minister selected health projects in three districts: Afmadow in Jubaland Harardhere in Galmudug Adale in Hirshabeelle In Adale, the programme will rehabilitate and bring Sheikh Ahmed Gabyow Hospital into operation. The district has a population of approximately 100,000 people, including internally displaced communities.The TPSS programme has an initial budget of €3.82 million. It is financed by Italy and implemented by the Somali Prime Minister’s Office. Italian Agency for Development Cooperation Daccò Coppi said he hoped the conference would strengthen institutional coordination and improve the capabilities of Somalia’s security forces.He said the long-term objective was for Somali institutions to assume full responsibility for national security through the lawful, legitimate and accountable use of force. The post Italy Expands Police Training Support for Somalia first appeared on Radio Dalsan .","url":"https://radiodalsan.com/2026/09/01/italy-somalia-police-training-support/?utm_source=rss&utm_medium=rss&utm_campaign=italy-somalia-police-training-support","source":"Radio Dalsan","image":"","published":"2026-09-01T09:40:11+00:00","category":"conflict","category_label":"Armed Conflict","region":"Horn of Africa","country":"Somalia","fatalities":0,"severity":"Moderate","severity_score":0},{"id":"bc759251226c","title":"Türkiye pledges continued security and economic support for Somalia","summary":"Danjiraha Turkiga Alper Aktaş oo ka hadlaya Shirweynaha Qaran ee Amniga Gudaha ee Muqdisho The post Türkiye pledges continued security and economic support for Somalia first appeared on Radio Dalsan .","body":"Turkish Ambassador Alper Aktaş says Ankara will maintain its support for Somali security forces while expanding cooperation in energy, fisheries and other strategic sectors. MOGADISHU, September 1, 2026 — Türkiye has pledged to continue supporting Somalia’s security institutions, counterterrorism operations and long-term economic development as the country works towards greater self-sufficiency.Addressing Somalia’s National Internal Security Conference in Mogadishu, Turkish Ambassador Alper Aktaş said the country had made visible security progress, although significant challenges and uncertainties remained.Aktaş said his more than two and a half years in Somalia had allowed him to witness changes in Mogadishu firsthand. “Just a few years ago, Mogadishu was associated with major explosions, instability and persistent conflict,” he said. “Today, we speak of rising skyscrapers, ambitious development projects and genuine hope.” Training for Gorgor and Haramcad units The ambassador said Türkiye-trained Gorgor special forces continued to take part in operations against terrorist groups.He added that Türkiye was also training specialised Haramcad units within the Somali Police Force.According to Aktaş, more than 1,000 personnel have been trained and are helping respond to security threats in Somali cities. He did not provide a breakdown of the personnel, the period covered by the figure or their current deployments.Türkiye opened its largest overseas military base in Mogadishu in 2017 and provides training to both the Somali military and police. Air support for security operations Aktaş said Türkiye had increased its air assets in Somalia at the request of Somali authorities to support operations on the ground.He said the additional support was already contributing to counterterrorism efforts. However, he did not identify the aircraft involved, disclose their number or provide independently verifiable information about their operational impact.Further information from the Somali and Turkish authorities would therefore be needed to establish the scope and results of the deployment. Connecting security with economic development The ambassador argued that security gains would not be sustainable without economic progress. He linked Türkiye’s security engagement to its growing involvement in Somalia’s energy, fisheries and infrastructure sectors.Aktaş referred to the continuing work of the Çağrı Bey deep-sea drilling vessel, planned fisheries initiatives and a proposed spaceport project.The Çağrı Bey arrived off Mogadishu in April 2026 to begin Türkiye’s first overseas deep-sea oil exploration project. The planned well is expected to reach a depth of 7,500 metres, while the drilling operation was projected to last about 10 months.The operation follows an intergovernmental energy agreement signed in March 2024 covering the exploration, evaluation, development and production of oil and gas in Somalia’s offshore and onshore areas. Exploratory drilling does not, however, guarantee the discovery of commercially recoverable oil. The results of the well, the possible size of any reserves and the eventual financial benefits for Somalia have not yet been announced. Commitment to continued partnership Aktaş said international partners should maintain their assistance as Somalia moves towards greater self-reliance. “Türkiye remains fully committed to doing its part,” he said. He concluded by thanking Somalia’s security institutions and described the country as a second home for himself and his family.His remarks presented Türkiye’s engagement as a broad partnership spanning security, defence, energy and economic development. Some proposed initiatives, particularly the spaceport, still require further official details concerning financing, implementation agreements and timelines. The post Türkiye pledges continued security and economic support for Somalia first appeared on Radio Dalsan .","url":"https://radiodalsan.com/2026/09/01/turkiye-somalia-security-support/?utm_source=rss&utm_medium=rss&utm_campaign=turkiye-somalia-security-support","source":"Radio Dalsan","image":"","published":"2026-09-01T08:31:44+00:00","category":"terrorism","category_label":"Terrorism","region":"Horn of Africa","country":"Somalia","fatalities":0,"severity":"Moderate","severity_score":0},{"id":"6b203c930107","title":"Somalia Sets September 30 for Galmudug, Hirshabelle Elections","summary":"The elections hold broader significance for Somalia's political stability. Federal member state elections are crucial for establishing legitimate regional administrations that can partner with the federal government on security, development, and constitutional reform. Any delays or disputes could undermine progress toward the national elections scheduled for 2027. Both elections will proceed simul","body":"Electoral commission announces fresh polls for two federal member states following court ruling that annulled Galmudug’s previous results. Mogadishu – Somalia’s Independent National Electoral and Boundaries Commission has scheduled September 30, 2026, as polling day for local council and parliamentary elections in Galmudug and Hirshabelle. The commission released separate electoral timelines for both federal member states on Tuesday, marking a significant step toward completing the country’s ongoing regional election process. The decision comes after Somalia’s Supreme Court annulled Galmudug’s previous election results on August 23, citing irregularities in the electoral process. This ruling forced the federal member state to conduct fresh polls, adding pressure on the electoral commission to deliver credible and transparent elections within a compressed timeframe. Hirshabelle, meanwhile, will proceed with its scheduled elections as originally planned. Galmudug’s electoral calendar begins on September 10, when the commission will designate polling centers and voting stations. Political associations must submit their candidate lists by September 20, followed by the accreditation of party agents and observers on September 26. The shortened timeline reflects the urgency of completing the process after the court’s intervention. Campaign activities will conclude on September 28, two days before polling day. Hirshabelle’s electoral process started earlier, with the publication of a preliminary voter register on September 2. The commission will subsequently establish polling stations, register voters, distribute voter cards, and verify candidate lists. Political associations will announce their candidates on September 22, followed by the accreditation of party agents and observers. Campaigning will end on September 28. “These elections represent an important opportunity for citizens in Galmudug and Hirshabelle to exercise their democratic rights,” a senior electoral official told this reporter on condition of anonymity. The official emphasized the commission’s commitment to transparency and fairness. The elections hold broader significance for Somalia’s political stability. Federal member state elections are crucial for establishing legitimate regional administrations that can partner with the federal government on security, development, and constitutional reform. Any delays or disputes could undermine progress toward the national elections scheduled for 2027. Both elections will proceed simultaneously on September 30, with results expected shortly thereafter. The post Somalia Sets September 30 for Galmudug, Hirshabelle Elections first appeared on Radio Dalsan .","url":"https://radiodalsan.com/2026/09/01/somalia-sets-september-30-for-galmudug-hirshabelle-elections/?utm_source=rss&utm_medium=rss&utm_campaign=somalia-sets-september-30-for-galmudug-hirshabelle-elections","source":"Radio Dalsan","image":"","published":"2026-09-01T07:53:35+00:00","category":"other","category_label":"Other Security News","region":"Horn of Africa","country":"Somalia","fatalities":0,"severity":"Moderate","severity_score":0},{"id":"8776fa8a4e60","title":"Al-Shabaab-Houthi alliance poses a new threat to the U.S. and its allies","summary":"MOGADISHU (Somaliguardian) – On a balmy evening, Jibril “One-Armed” Yahya Ali and his new bride set out for a honeymoon drive along the beach outside al-Ghaydah, Yemen. Jibril, a Somali, and his Yemeni wife had met through a mutual acquaintance, an arms dealer who helped Jibril buy military-grade weapons from Yemen’s Houthi rebels. Jibril would then […]","body":"MOGADISHU (Somaliguardian) – On a balmy evening, Jibril “One-Armed” Yahya Ali and his new bride set out for a honeymoon drive along the beach outside al-Ghaydah, Yemen . Jibril, a Somali, and his Yemeni wife had met through a mutual acquaintance, an arms dealer who helped Jibril buy military-grade weapons from Yemen ’s Houthi rebels. Jibril would then smuggle the arms to al-Shabaab, an Islamist insurgent group fighting to overthrow the government in Somalia. The budding alliance benefited both sides. Al-Shabaab, the world’s richest al Qaeda wing, had cash but needed weapons and training. The Houthis had sophisticated arms and tech know-how, but coveted money and a new perch for attacks against the West. The exchange also made Jibril a target . On Feb. 12, four months after Jibril married Budoor Murshid, the couple took the Toyota Corolla on their sunset drive. Budoor’s phone rang. They parked, and Budoor stepped outside to take the call. Moments later, a U.S. missile slammed through the car roof. Rather than explode, it sliced up the Corolla like a weaponized can opener. Jibril died instantly. A friend spirited Budoor away. The airstrike was an opening shot in the American effort to disrupt the unlikely alliance between two implacable U.S. foes: al-Shabaab militants who have been fighting in Somalia for two decades, and the Houthi rebels, longtime beneficiaries of weapons and training from Iran’s Islamic Revolutionary Guard Corps. For the U.S., Jibril’s death came too late to stop the chain of events he helped set in motion. The Houthis and al-Shabaab had been sectarian adversaries until Jibril and others brokered a partnership that now threatens to further destabilize the Middle East and energy markets worldwide. Their home countries, Yemen and Somalia, sit on opposite shores of the Gulf of Aden , through which 12% of the world’s seaborne oil passed before the Iran war. With the Strait of Hormuz all but closed by Iranian forces, the gulf could serve as an escape route for Middle East oil. Except the Houthis have come to the aid of their allies in Tehran, attacking Saudi-linked oil tankers with missiles and drones in the Red Sea. Since at least 2023, Jibril had served as a liaison between the two militant groups, united in their hatred of America and Israel, according to U.S., Somali and Yemeni officials. Jibril was killed two weeks before the U.S. began the war with Iran. He never saw his legacy—how two U.S.-designated terrorist groups, locked in a marriage of convenience, inherited a strategic maritime pinch-point that empowers them to threaten the global oil trade in a time of crisis. “You have al-Shabaab, al Qaeda’s largest and most lucrative affiliate, gaining access to more sophisticated weapons and training,” U.S. Gen. Dagvin Anderson, commander of American forces in Africa, told The Wall Street Journal. “On the other side, the Houthis are extending their geographic reach, gaining a foothold that could further destabilize one of the world’s most critical economic chokepoints,” he said. “This isn’t just a regional problem. It has global implications.” Two American sources familiar with the operation confirmed it was a U.S. airstrike that killed Jibril. U.S. Central Command, in charge of American forces in the Middle East, said it wasn’t a military operation. A Central Intelligence Agency spokesperson declined to say whether the spy service was behind the killing. Yemen’s ambassador to the U.S. declined an interview request. Houthi officials didn’t respond to written questions about the group’s relationship with al-Shabaab. Budoor couldn’t be reached for comment. Enemy of my enemy The Houthis, predominantly Shia Muslims, and al-Shabaab, which adheres to Sunni Islam, are among the hundreds of millions divided by a seventh-century schism over who was the rightful successor to the Prophet Muhammad. The sectarian split still flares into violence in spots from Afghanistan to Iraq to Syria. Common enemies, however, now unite the Houthis and al-Shabaab. “They hate the West, they hate Israel,” said Abdulaahi Mohamed Ali, a former Somali intelligence chief. Yemen’s Counter-Terrorism Service described Jibril as “one of the key figures in strengthening ties between the Houthis and al-Shabaab through the exchange of expertise and coordination, particularly in facilitating the transfer of weapons and military equipment,” according to an internal assessment reviewed by the Journal. Jibril moved clandestinely between Somalia and Yemen, operating as al-Shabaab’s liaison with Houthi command, according to Matt Bryden, a former United Nations official who monitored arms flows to Somalia. Bryden outlined Jibril’s life and death in a closed-door intelligence briefing in June attended by U.S. special-operations personnel and East African government officials. Jibril, 38 years old when he died, had studied veterinary medicine in Yemen and ran a charity in Mogadishu, Somalia’s capital. He owned a foreign-exchange outlet and an import-export firm. He bore scars from gunshot wounds. His injuries earned him the nickname Gacaamey—roughly translated as one-armed—despite his having both limbs. The Houthis provided Jibril with a furnished apartment in San’a, the rebel-controlled Yemeni capital, according to the briefing, and he attended official functions. The rebels issued him a phony but convincing Yemeni passport under an assumed name, according to a document viewed by the Journal. In 2024, al-Shabaab dispatched a more senior envoy, Sheikh Ahmed Elmi Samatar, to work alongside Jibril. Samatar presented the Houthis with a lengthy weapons wish list, viewed by the Journal, which included shoulder-fired antiaircraft missiles, 107 mm Katyusha rockets, Dragunov sniper rifles, explosive attack drones, antitank missiles and U.S. night-vision gear. A U.S. military intelligence official confirmed that al-Shabaab sought advanced weaponry from the Houthis, in addition to rifles, machine guns and ammunition. Yemeni government officials arrested Samatar in January 2025 and later released him in a prisoner exchange with the Houthis, according to the briefing. Samatar returned to Somalia, leaving Jibril to press al-Shabaab’s interests in Yemen. Jibril continued to broker arms shipments, and al-Shabaab dispatched two weapons experts to Yemen to assist him. After Jibril’s killing, al-Shabaab picked a replacement in Yemen to continue the work, Bryden said. Jibril’s wife is on the run, he said. Front companies in Yemen disguise the shipments as civilian products and move them to Mukalla and other ports, the Yemeni intelligence report said. The weapons cross the Gulf of Aden in speedboats or fishing vessels and are transferred ashore on poorly monitored sections of coastline in northern Somalia, according to the U.S. military-intelligence official. They are transported to al-Shabaab combat units in southern Somalia, where the insurgents’ activities are concentrated. In April 2025, the U.S. destroyed two unflagged boats entering Somali waters from Yemen, carrying what U.S. Africa Command described as advanced conventional weapons. It isn’t clear what items on the lengthy al-Shabaab wish list have reached the militants in Somalia. But the idea that al-Shabaab might acquire attack drones or other arms capable of striking U.S. and Somali bases from the air makes both militaries nervous. “I don’t know what their plans are, but there’s definitely something cooking,” said Brig. Gen. Ahmed Abdullahi Sheikh, a former commander of Somali special forces. Complicating security concerns was Israel’s decision in December to recognize the independence of Somaliland, a region within the northwest of Somalia’s acknowledged borders that declared itself a separate country in 1991. Somaliland could provide Israel access to Berbera, a Red Sea port city with a runway so long that NASA once rented it as an emergency landing strip for the space shuttle. Berbera could give the Israelis another position where they could monitor and attack their Houthi enemies. Michael Milshtein, a former senior Israeli intelligence officer, said the Houthis were front of mind when Israel recognized Somaliland as an independent nation. “Military deployment in Somaliland gives you a very strong position to confront the Houthis,” he said. The Israeli government didn’t respond to requests for comment. In a televised speech this month, Houthi leader Abdul Malik al-Houthi said the group was closely monitoring Israeli actions in Somaliland, which he alleged were aimed at controlling the Gulf of Aden, the Bab al-Mandeb strait and the Red Sea. “We will move at any time to target any activity or presence of the Israeli enemy in Somaliland,” al-Houthi said. Traffic stop In June, a four-person Houthi delegation set sail from Yemen, crossed the Gulf of Aden and came ashore in southern Somalia. The men trekked through the bush to meet with their counterparts in Jilib, the de facto capital of al-Shabaab-held territory, according","url":"https://www.somaliguardian.com/news/somalia-news/al-shabaab-houthi-alliance-us-allies-threat/","source":"Somalia Guardian","image":"","published":"2026-09-01T07:46:06+00:00","category":"terrorism","category_label":"Terrorism","region":"Horn of Africa","country":"Somalia","fatalities":0,"severity":"Moderate","severity_score":0},{"id":"414673bf13c1","title":"SNA brobes video of soldiers suffocating bound Al-Shabaab suspect","summary":"Somalia’s military opened a preliminary investigation on Tuesday after a video circulated online appeared to show members of the Somali National Army (SNA) abusing a man in custody during an operation against Al-Shabaab, the SNAF Command said in a statement, adding that the footage contravenes its Code of Conduct and the laws of armed conflict. […] The post SNA brobes video of soldiers suffocating","body":"Somalia’s military opened a preliminary investigation on Tuesday after a video circulated online appeared to show members of the Somali National Army (SNA) abusing a man in custody during an operation against Al-Shabaab, the SNAF Command said in a statement, adding that the footage contravenes its Code of Conduct and the laws of armed conflict. The probe lands at a sensitive moment for Somalia’s armed forces, which are locked in an intensifying offensive against Al-Shabaab across multiple regions. Any confirmed breach of prisoner-of-war protections could complicate ties with international partners who fund and train SNAF units, and would test the military’s willingness to discipline its own ranks publicly. The footage, widely shared on social media, shows uniformed men believed to be Somali soldiers surrounding a young man who appears to be in custody. The men are seen placing a plastic bag over the prisoner’s head, apparently suffocating him, triggering widespread condemnation online. The SNAF Command said the investigation would determine the circumstances of the incident and identify those responsible. “The SNAF Command strongly warns all personnel against engaging in such conduct,” the military said in its statement. The statement did not name any soldiers shown in the video, nor did it disclose where or when the incident occurred. It also did not clarify the identity or current condition of the man seen being held. Officials said personnel found to have violated prisoner-of-war protections or broader human rights standards would face both legal and military disciplinary action. “Firm legal and military disciplinary action will be taken against anyone found to have violated the laws of armed conflict, the rights of prisoners of war or human rights,” the statement said. Rights monitors and international law experts have indirectly noted that mistreatment of detainees during counter-insurgency operations can undermine both battlefield legitimacy and cooperation with foreign backers, according to [source/report, if available]. The SNAF has not said whether any soldiers have been arrested or suspended pending the outcome of the investigation. The case comes as Somali government troops and allied fighters continue offensives against Al-Shabaab in multiple regions, part of a broader campaign to reclaim territory from the militant group. The post SNA brobes video of soldiers suffocating bound Al-Shabaab suspect first appeared on Radio Dalsan .","url":"https://radiodalsan.com/2026/09/01/sna-brobes-video-of-soldiers-suffocating-bound-al-shabaab-suspect/?utm_source=rss&utm_medium=rss&utm_campaign=sna-brobes-video-of-soldiers-suffocating-bound-al-shabaab-suspect","source":"Radio Dalsan","image":"","published":"2026-09-01T07:10:11+00:00","category":"terrorism","category_label":"Terrorism","region":"Horn of Africa","country":"Somalia","fatalities":0,"severity":"Moderate","severity_score":0},{"id":"f443fd4e813a","title":"DR Congo Ebola cases jump above 6,000","summary":"Since outbreak declaration in May, virus has killed more than 2,900 people in Congo, according to health authorities","body":"Confirmed Ebola cases in the Democratic Republic of Congo surpassed 6,000 on Monday as authorities intensified efforts to contain the outbreak, official data showed.\n\nCongo has reported 6,041 cases, including 2,911 deaths, since the outbreak was declared in mid-May, according to the Health Ministry’s latest situation update.\n\nAt least 1,366 people have recovered, while 619 patients remain in isolation or hospital.\n\nThe latest Ebola outbreak in Congo is the world’s second-largest by recorded infections, according to the World Health Organization (WHO).\n\nThe outbreak, caused by the Bundibugyo virus, has affected 60 health zones across six provinces: Haut-Uele, Ituri, North Kivu, South Kivu, Bas-Uele and Tshopo.\n\nThe ministry said response efforts remain concentrated in areas with active transmission, with particular attention to Ituri, which accounts for the majority of cases and affected health zones.\n\nThe rise in cases and wider geographic spread over the past three months represent a “substantial increase” in the scale of the outbreak, according to the WHO.\n\nThe government has rolled out an Ebola vaccination campaign targeting health and frontline workers.\n\nThe Coalition for Epidemic Preparedness Innovations (CEPI) announced last week a new $16.5 million partnership with African biopharmaceutical company Minapharm Group to develop its Bundibugyo vaccine candidate.","url":"https://www.aa.com.tr/en/africa/dr-congo-ebola-cases-jump-above-6-000/4042728","source":"Anadolu · Africa","image":"","published":"2026-08-31T12:37:43+00:00","category":"conflict","category_label":"Armed Conflict","region":"Central Africa","country":"DR Congo","fatalities":0,"severity":"Moderate","severity_score":0},{"id":"086598fb8053","title":"200 fighters defect from Sudan's paramilitary RSF, join army: State media","summary":"Latest defection follows departure of hundreds of fighters, several commanders from paramilitary group in recent weeks","body":"A force of 200 fighters has defected from Sudan's paramilitary Rapid Support Forces (RSF) and joined the Sudanese army, state media reported Sunday.\n\nThe group, which included prominent field commanders, arrived in Omdurman, west of the capital Khartoum, according to the state-run Sudan News Agency.\n\nThe force was led by Abu Bakr Hamouda Dahloop, commander of the special missions intelligence division in North Kordofan state, the agency said.\n\nThe group announced that it was joining the Sudanese army with all its equipment and was ready to take part in combat operations alongside the military and allied forces.\n\nDahloop said the fighters had realized \"their mistake in following the project being implemented by the RSF militia.\"\n\nHe said they had joined the \"national camp\" and would fight \"for the demands of the Sudanese people to eliminate the rebel RSF militia.\"\n\nThe latest move follows several other reported defections from the RSF in recent weeks.\n\nOn Aug. 19, Sudanese authorities announced that four military commanders, 318 fighters and 50 combat vehicles had defected from the RSF.\n\nThat group, led by Lt. Col. Musa Ali Ahmed, known as \"Khamsin,\" joined the army, according to authorities.\n\nOn Aug. 13, authorities said another RSF force comprising 21 commanders and 270 fighters had defected and joined the army.\n\nThe RSF has also seen defections among senior field and political figures, including Fares Al-Nour, political adviser to the RSF commander; Maj. Gen. Al-Nour Ahmed Adam, known as \"Al-Nour Al-Qubba\"; and field commander Ali Rizqallah, known as \"Savanna.\"\n\nThe three Kordofan states -- North, West and South -- have witnessed clashes between the army and the RSF since October 2025.\n\nSudan has been embroiled in war between the army and the RSF since April 2023. The conflict has killed tens of thousands of people and displaced around 13 million, according to UN and international estimates.","url":"https://www.aa.com.tr/en/africa/200-fighters-defect-from-sudans-paramilitary-rsf-join-army-state-media/4042356","source":"Anadolu · Africa","image":"","published":"2026-08-31T08:35:30+00:00","category":"conflict","category_label":"Armed Conflict","region":"Horn of Africa","country":"Sudan","fatalities":0,"severity":"Moderate","severity_score":0},{"id":"d2d7ebc94716","title":"Imported From Egypt: The Extremist Ideology That Inspired Boko Haram","summary":"This is the story of how modern Islamist extremism emerged from the edge of an African prison and moved into Nigeria, escalating into an unending fire of insurgency. The post Imported From Egypt: The Extremist Ideology That Inspired Boko Haram appeared first on HumAngle .","body":"In August 2016, Mamman Nur, one of Boko Haram’s foremost commanders , dramatically sat before a camera on the shore of the Sambisa Forest, a vast terrorist hideout in North East Nigeria. He publicly accused the group’s supreme leader, Abubakar Shekau, of doctrinal deviance and transgressions. He said Abubakar had become power-drunk, killing his own commanders over minor disagreements and spilling the blood of fellow Muslims. He named several terrorist figures Shekau had killed, including Mustapha Chad, Abu Amr Falluja, Kaka Allai and Mallam Ba’ana Banki. He titled the recording Exposé , deliberately weaponising the same word Shekau had used to attack his critics. He also invoked the words of the former Islamic State spokesman Abu Muhammad al-Adnani , saying: “Whoever changes, we will also change him.” His message was not only a threat; it was also a campaign to depose Shekau as the leader of the terror group. Within months, Mamman’s faction formally broke away to become the Islamic State West Africa Province (ISWAP), as Shekau led the Jama’atu Ahlussunnah Lidda’awati wal Jihad (JAS). This movement had existed before Boko Haram pledged allegiance to the Islamic State. Behind the sudden division lay an unfolding theological debate within the global Salafi-jihadi movements for decades. It revolved around one question that has repeatedly divided jihadist organisations from the Middle East to the Lake Chad Basin: How far can the practice of takfir – declaring other Muslims as non-believers – go before this doctrine, which is meant to validate jihad, starts to undermine the very concept of jihad itself? Drawing on Boko Haram’s internal correspondence, Arabic treatises, multimedia recordings, and local sermons, HumAngle traces how an ideology born in Egypt travelled through Jordan, Iraq, and Syria before taking root in northeastern Nigeria. What later appeared as a personal power struggle between Shekau and Mamman Nur was, in reality, the culmination of a decades-long ideological dispute over faith, authority, governance, and the limits of violence. The journey that turned the former terrorist allies into bitter enemies began several decades back, in an Egyptian prison cell. Sayyid Qutb: Reinventing the concept of jahiliyya A popular photo of Sayyid Qutb while in detention in Egypt. Source: ABC News The story begins with Sayyid Qutb, an Egyptian author and one of the foremost figures in modern Sunni Islamic revivalism. He’s the author of a popular Islamist book, Ma’alim fi al-Tariq, mostly known as Milestones , which he wrote while in prison under the Egyptian President Gamal Abdel Nasser, and published shortly before his execution in 1966. The book was said to have transformed a classical Islamic concept into a revolutionary political doctrine. The term “jahiliyya” literally referred to the pre-Islamic Arabian era before the Prophet Muhammad’s message, but Sayyid gave it a radically different meaning in his book. He argued that contemporary Muslim societies had fallen back into a new jahiliyya because they accepted political systems that placed human authority above divine law. Sayyid firmly believes that sovereignty is solely God’s domain. He argues that any government that creates laws outside of Sharia, even if its leaders identify as Muslims, is essentially practising a modern form of paganism. The solution, he claimed, was the emergence of a committed Islamic leadership capable of separating itself from corrupt society before working to transform it. That radical framework would become one of the most influential ideas in contemporary jihadist literature. Variations of it would later appear in the writings and speeches of militant organisations from Egypt and Jordan to Iraq, Syria, and Nigeria. The book’s influence on Boko Haram was neither indirect nor accidental. Mohammed Yusuf, Boko Haram’s founder, openly defended Sayyid against critics within Nigeria’s Salafi movement. In one of the sermons reproduced in The Boko Haram Reader, a book edited by Abdulbasit Kassim and Michael Nwankpa, Yusuf warned his followers against reading books that dismissed Sayyid and Hasan al-Banna , the founder of the Muslim Brotherhood, a transnational Sunni Islamist organisation founded in Egypt in 1928. He praised Sayyid as a scholar whom Allah had granted a unique understanding of communism and what he described as polytheistic political systems. Citing the Albanian-born Syrian Hadith scholar Muhammad Nasir al-Din al-Albani , he argued that no contemporary scholar had explained the meaning of la ilaha illa Allah (there is no god but Allah) as comprehensively as Sayyid had in his book, Milestones. He also claimed that Sayyid’s writings played a role in inspiring Osama bin Laden. Throughout much of his public preaching, the Boko Haram founder portrayed himself as someone restoring the original teachings of Islam rather than introducing new doctrines. However, his sermons revealed that one of the intellectual foundations of his terrorist organisation had been laid decades earlier by an Egyptian author, who had written from prison. Although Sayyid didn’t create Boko Haram, he introduced the conceptual language that jihadist ideologues expanded, refined, and eventually transported into northeastern Nigeria. From diagnosis to doctrine Abu Muhammad Al-Maqdisi, a Salafi-Jihadi ideologue who is the key intellectual of modern jihadism. Source: Wikipedia While Sayyid seemed to have established the groundwork for radical jihadist ideologies, Abu Muhammad al-Maqdisi, a Palestinian writer and Salafi Islamist , developed the legal framework. Born in 1959, the Jordanian ideologue emerged during the 1980s as one of the most influential theorists of modern Salafi-jihadism. His book Millat Ibrahim (The Religion of Abraham), completed in 1984, built on Sayyid’s concepts of sovereignty, transforming them into a practical doctrine. Abu Muhammad argued that leaders who chose to govern by conventional, secular laws, instead of divine revelation, have deviated from the teachings of Islam, and therefore have abandoned the religion. From this, he developed the concept of takfir al-hukkam (declaring such rulers as apostates) and the doctrine of al-wala’ wal-bara’ (complete loyalty to believers and total disavowal of unbelievers, their institutions, and political systems). In his publication with the Combating Terrorism Centre , Joas Wagemakers, an associate professor of Islamic and Arabic Studies at Utrecht University, stated that Abu Muhammad was the first prominent Islamist to publicly declare the Saudi ruling family apostate for embracing the idea of democracy. His influence spread far beyond his own extremist writings. In the mid-1990s, he met a young Jordanian militant named Ahmad Fadil al-Khalayleh, better known as Abu Mus’ab al-Zarqawi , in prison. The two spent years together in detention, with Abu Muhammad serving as Abu Musab’s principal ideological mentor. When Abu Musab later established al-Qaeda in Iraq after the U.S. invasion of 2003, he carried much of that intellectual framework with him. The relationship eventually became the first major case study of a pattern that would later recur within Boko Haram. Abu Mus’ab Al-Zarqawi. Source: The Guardian By 2004, Abu Mus’ab had taken the excommunication doctrine far beyond what his teacher considered acceptable. His campaign against Iraqi Shiites relied on mass excommunication and indiscriminate violence, treating entire communities as legitimate military targets. Abu Muhammad publicly objected to his approach. After his release from prison in July 2005, he told Al Jazeera that it was “forbidden to equate the ordinary Shiite with the Americans in warfare”. His objection was not over the legitimacy of jihad itself but over the widening circle of Muslims being declared unbelievers. Abu Mus’ab rejected the criticism, claiming his mentor’s intervention harmed the jihad in Iraq instead of resolving it. The dispute would soon draw the attention of al-Qaeda’s top leaders. In July 2005, Ayman al-Zawahiri, then Osama bin Laden’s deputy, sent Abu Mus’ab a letter that has since become one of the movement’s most important internal documents. Ayman warned that the indiscriminate killing of Shiite civilians was alienating ordinary Muslims and undermining al-Qaeda’s broader objectives. He questioned why Iraqi Shiites should be targeted wholesale when many could be excused because of ignorance. This theological qualification would later reappear almost verbatim in Boko Haram’s own internal correspondence. The disagreement established a pattern that resurfaced repeatedly over the next decades. First, an ideologue developed the doctrine. Then a militant commander applied the doctrine more aggressively than its authors. Finally, an internal rebellion argued that the doctrine itself had been pushed beyond its limits. Years later, Boko Haram replayed almost the same sequence. How the ideology moved to Maiduguri Muhammad Yusuf during a pre","url":"https://humanglemedia.com/imported-from-egypt-the-extremist-ideology-that-inspired-boko-haram/","source":"HumAngle","image":"","published":"2026-08-31T08:00:00+00:00","category":"terrorism","category_label":"Terrorism","region":"West Africa","country":"Nigeria","fatalities":0,"severity":"Moderate","severity_score":0},{"id":"a6b9d7172fe5","title":"Algeria sends 4 fighter jets to Niger after unrest in Niamey","summary":"2 patrols comprising 4 Sukhoi Su-30 aircraft land at Niamey airport, accompanied by aerial refueling plane, says Defense Ministry","body":"Algeria sent four fighter jets to Niger on Sunday following recent unrest in the capital Niamey, the Defense Ministry said.\n\nThe ministry said two patrols comprising four Sukhoi Su-30 aircraft landed at Niamey airport, accompanied by an aerial refueling plane.\n\nThe aircraft were deployed at the request of Nigerien authorities on “a mission to support the Nigerien army in confronting an attempt to destabilize the brotherly state of Niger,” it added.\n\nThe deployment was ordered by Algerian Army Chief of Staff Said Chengriha under instructions from President Abdelmadjid Tebboune to strengthen strategic cooperation between Algeria and Niger, “particularly in the military field.”\n\nThe ministry said the operation “reaffirms Algeria’s firm commitment and determination to continue its efforts to strengthen security and stability in the region.”\n\nIt also renewed Algeria’s “constant support for the government and people of Niger in overcoming this sensitive period.”\n\nNigerien Prime Minister Ali Mahamane Lamine Zeine received the Algerian mission and expressed “his thanks and gratitude” to Tebboune and the Algerian people for the support, according to the statement.\n\nThe recent unrest included attacks around Diori Hamani International Airport and Air Base 101, a strategic military facility in Niamey, as well as other sites in the capital.\n\nNiger's defense minister said military activity in the capital Niamey had been brought under control and security forces had regained control of critical sites that were targeted.","url":"https://www.aa.com.tr/en/africa/algeria-sends-4-fighter-jets-to-niger-after-unrest-in-niamey/4041966","source":"Anadolu · Africa","image":"","published":"2026-08-30T19:21:27+00:00","category":"other","category_label":"Other Security News","region":"North Africa","country":"Algeria","fatalities":0,"severity":"Moderate","severity_score":0},{"id":"bcadd79e565d","title":"7 civilians killed, several missing in rebel attack in DR Congo","summary":"Suspected ADF rebels also set fire to residential houses, says local official","body":"At least seven civilians were killed in a weekend attack by suspected Allied Democratic Forces (ADF) rebels in a village in eastern Democratic Republic of Congo, a local official said on Sunday.\n\nThe attack took place in the Mambasa territory of Ituri province on Saturday, according to Alberic Kahundirwa, a local official.\n\n“The death toll remains provisional because several people are missing. But the attackers also set fire to about 20 houses,” Kahundirwa told reporters.\n\nThe latest attack came a week after separate other attacks in various villages in the same territory left 16 people were killed and several others abducted.\n\nThe ADF is affiliated with the ISIS (Daesh) and has been active in eastern Congo for several years.\n\nThe rebel group is accused of killing more than 1,000 civilians in Congo in 2025, and remains among the most dangerous armed groups in the eastern part of the country, according to a report of UN experts in July.\n\nNoting that the ADF maintained ideological ties and communications with Daesh, the UN experts said systematic and large-scale attacks against the civilian population took the form of killings, abductions and sexual violence, particularly affecting women and children.","url":"https://www.aa.com.tr/en/africa/7-civilians-killed-several-missing-in-rebel-attack-in-dr-congo/4041693","source":"Anadolu · Africa","image":"","published":"2026-08-30T14:06:31+00:00","category":"conflict","category_label":"Armed Conflict","region":"Central Africa","country":"DR Congo","fatalities":0,"severity":"Moderate","severity_score":0},{"id":"ceb0c29af175","title":"The Scholarly Rebuttal of Boko Haram’s Logic of Terror","summary":"Different insurgent groups in Nigeria take Islamic texts out of their historical theological contexts to justify their acts of violence and bloodshed. The post The Scholarly Rebuttal of Boko Haram’s Logic of Terror appeared first on HumAngle .","body":"In his small study at the entrance of his home in Kano, North West Nigeria , Tijjani Ismaila, the deputy imam of the Kwankwasiyya City Mosque, lays out a theological problem that has occupied generations of Islamic scholarship even before Boko Haram’s emergence in northeastern Nigeria. The question is simple yet intriguing: how should verses revealed in one historical era be used to judge situations in a different era that bears little resemblance to the one in which they were revealed? The Islamic cleric emphasises the importance of this question, given the thousands who have died and the escalating armed violence in Nigeria, as extremists impose their interpretations of verses to justify violence whenever and wherever they desire. “They take Qur’anic verses out of context,” he says. “They turn a verse revealed for an active battlefield in seventh-century Arabia into a permanent command even in times of peace. They also misapply a verse about combatant non-Muslims onto a fellow Muslim, because they redefine who is Muslim and who is not according to their own criteria.” For more than a decade, violent extremist groups, most prominently Boko Haram, have manipulated Islamic texts to justify their terror campaigns, stripping theological principles of their historical or logical contexts. Boko Haram and other jihadist organisations in Nigeria rely on three theological theories . One is hakimiyya , which brands any government not run on its version of Sharia as illegitimate; another is jihad – interpreted as permanent war on whom they regard as nonbelievers – and takfir , a concept allowing the group to declare fellow Muslims disbelievers and legitimate targets. Insurgent groups rely on these fundamental concepts to legitimise civilian massacres, abductions, and forced labour. The Kano-based Islamic scholar presents a counter-narrative that addresses the three concepts and critiques the classical scholarship that extremists rely on. Sheikh Tijjani Ismaila Khalifa, the deputy imam at Kwankwasiyya City mosque. Credit: Aliyu/HumAngle. Hakimiyya: “Rulership belongs to none but Allah” The jihadists’ foundational claim is that any government not run strictly on its version of Sharia is illegitimate, and that Muslims who serve it, vote in it, or simply live peacefully under it have committed kufr, an act of disbelief. There are major disagreements among the jihadists themselves. The Islamic State West Africa Province (ISWAP) and Jama’atu Ahlussunnah Liddaawati Wal Jihad (JAS) split over whether someone living under this secular government without knowing should be excused on grounds of ignorance. But they both agree on the core point that living under the government, or serving in it intentionally, is an act of kufr . The hakimiyya governance system was popularised in the twentieth century by the Indian Islamist ideologue Abul A’ala Al-Maududi. It was later sharpened by the Egyptian Islamist ideologue Sayyid Qutb , who argued that any society not governed purely by Sharia was living in the pre-Islamic period. Sayyid relied heavily on the verdicts of 13th-century jurist and theologian Ibn Taymiyyah, especially on divine sovereignty. However, he applied these ideas in a political and revolutionary context that the medieval scholar never intended. The jihadists’ argument for the radical hakimiyya system rests on Quran 5:44: “And whoever does not judge by what Allah has revealed; then it is those who are the disbelievers.” Speaking on the Islamic exegesis of the Quranic verse above, Tijjani noted how many people have wrongly interpreted “what Allah has revealed” to mean Sharia law. “When people hear Sharia, the first thing that comes to their mind is criminal law and Hudud (prescribed punishments) such as penalties for theft or alcohol consumption. But Sharia encompasses far more than just penalties. It covers leadership, governance, justice, and ethics,” he said. Comparing modern democracy, the scholar explains that “when you look at modern democracy, there is nothing in its core mechanisms that inherently contradicts Islamic principles. The only point of debate is the source of legislation: whether laws are derived solely from Islamic text or through legislative bodies.” “But even in Islamic tradition, scholars perform Ijtihad (independent reasoning) to formulate rulings where texts are not explicit. Qur’anic texts provide a constitutional framework rather than a minute code for every single societal rule. Therefore, when legislators pass laws that align with public interest and do not contradict explicit Qur’anic prohibitions, this functions similarly to ijtihad,” he says, asserting that the Second Caliph, Umar ibn Khattab, was willing to adapt rulings to new circumstances through ijtihad, demonstrating that Islamic law is designed for flexibility rather than the rigid literalism demanded by jihadist preachers. “There is nothing in [democracy’s] core mechanisms that inherently contradicts Islamic principles.” He adds that Islamic governance has never required a rigid theocracy in the way jihadist groups imagine. The constitution of Medina – the Prophet’s own charter for governing a mixed community of Muslims, Jews and pagan tribes – was, in his description, a civic document built for coexistence, not a template for excommunicating everyone outside one interpretation. The cleric explains that the verse the jihadists quote has a far narrower classical reading. Ibn Abbas, one of the earliest interpreters of the Quran, was asked directly what it meant, and explained it means “disbelief less than disbelief”. He clarifies that mainstream commentary from early scholars asserts that the verse’s most severe judgment is directed at the rejection of the law’s divine origin, rather than at every administrative failure to implement it. “Their problem stems from their fundamental way of thinking. They follow only the surface level of the text – what is called literalism. They look at a verse literally without considering its actual meaning or context of revelation. They drew these ideas from certain perspectives, such as their reading of Ibn Taymiyyah, who attempted to dismiss metaphor (majaz) and insisted that words in the Qur’an or Hadith must be taken strictly as they appear,” the Islamic scholar adds. Jihad and the sword verse The second pillar is Quran 9:5, often called “the verse of the sword,” which instructs Muslim believers to fight polytheists after a grace period expires, “wherever you find them”. The jihadists also quote a hadith in which the Prophet says he has been “commanded to fight people until they testify there is no god but Allah.” Extremists in the Sahel have deployed these texts to recruit naive individuals into what they term open-ended war against unbelievers. Responding to this, Tijjani stresses that the extremists have only taken a single text in isolation to justify their radical beliefs, ignoring complementary texts and the overall objectives (Maqasid) of Islamic law. “Historically, the Prophet, peace be upon him, lived peacefully alongside non-Muslim neighbours and communities,” he said. “Any Hadith or verse regarding fighting must be contextualised within the broader framework of the Qur’an. Allah says: Fight in the way of Allah those who fight you, but do not transgress. The Hadith [that says] I have been commanded to fight the people… cannot mean fighting every human being on earth simply for not being Muslim, because Islam explicitly forbids forcing people into religion. Faith requires genuine conviction.” But there is another problem. Jihadists claim that every verse of the Quran that promotes peace was abrogated after the religion became powerful and Muslims constituted the majority of people in Medina. However, Tijjani disputed that claim, calling it a popular but weak argument. “The claim that peace verses were completely abrogated is largely an issue of scholarly interpretation (Ijtihad), not an explicit textual declaration from the Qur’an itself. Verses regarding peace, diplomacy, and coexistence remain valid and applicable depending on the circumstances and context of the Muslim community. The idea that all peaceful interactions were permanently cancelled is a misunderstanding used by extremists to justify perpetual conflict,” he says, noting that some scholars within the Hanafi School of Jurisprudence rejected abrogation altogether and tied verses strictly to their historical occasion. Scholars who accepted abrogation never read the 9:5 verse as a license to kill any non-Muslim anywhere; the dispute inside the tradition was about which boundary applied, not whether the verse had one. The cleric pointed to the Prophet’s record of honouring peace treaties like the Treaty of Hudaybiyyah, which was kept even when his companions thought its terms were unfavourable. “Islam’s default state in international relations and human interaction is peace,” he says, reiterating that war in the classical framework is a defensive, e","url":"https://humanglemedia.com/the-scholarly-rebuttal-of-boko-harams-logic-of-terror/","source":"HumAngle","image":"","published":"2026-08-28T08:00:00+00:00","category":"terrorism","category_label":"Terrorism","region":"West Africa","country":"Nigeria","fatalities":0,"severity":"Moderate","severity_score":0},{"id":"c0dedaa6ab5f","title":"US welcomes Obasanjo’s Tigray visit as fears of renewed conflict grow","summary":"The US embassy in Ethiopia says it has been encouraged by former Nigerian President Olusegun Obasanjo’s visit to Mekelle yesterday. In a statement, US Ambassador Ervin Massinga said Mr Obasanjo’s work under his African Union mandate was critical at a time of mounting concern over renewed fighting. Tensions between the Tigrayan authorities and Ethiopia’s federal government have remained high for mo","body":"The US embassy in Ethiopia says it has been encouraged by former Nigerian President Olusegun Obasanjo’s visit to Mekelle yesterday. In a statement, US Ambassador Ervin Massinga said Mr Obasanjo’s work under his African Union mandate was critical at a time of mounting concern over renewed fighting. Tensions between the Tigrayan authorities and Ethiopia’s federal government have remained high for months, fuelling fears that the conflict could once again erupt into war. Mr Massinga said all sides should instead focus on addressing the drought conditions affecting communities across the country, as millions of people across the region face acute hunger. The Greater Horn of Africa is enduring one of its worst droughts in decades, compounded by years of conflict and instability and the effects of a strengthening El Niño event. “Getting people home and back to their productive lives, safely and with dignity, should be the objective of all leaders, and that will take compromise,” he said. “We look forward to engaging former President Obasanjo on this matter of critical importance to the future of the people of Ethiopia,” Mr Massing added. However, TPLF leaders say Mr Obasanjo’s visit to Mekelle “produced no new proposals or tangible progress towards dialogue with the Ethiopian federal government.” In a statement issued today, the TPLF said Mr Obasanjo’s visit was made without a prior appointment, but that Tigray’s president rearranged his schedule to meet him. During the meeting, the TPLF “expressed disappointment over what it described as a lack of meaningful follow-up to discussions held in June, when several issues were identified as potential entry points for dialogue” with the federal government. The post US welcomes Obasanjo’s Tigray visit as fears of renewed conflict grow appeared first on Ethiopia Observer .","url":"https://www.ethiopiaobserver.com/2026/08/25/us-welcomes-obasanjos-ethiopia-visit-as-fears-of-renewed-conflict-grow/","source":"Ethiopia Observer","image":"","published":"2026-08-25T21:02:03+00:00","category":"conflict","category_label":"Armed Conflict","region":"Horn of Africa","country":"Ethiopia","fatalities":0,"severity":"Moderate","severity_score":0},{"id":"728152c9caad","title":"At least Six Dead in Addis Traffic Accident","summary":"ADDIS ABABA – A traffic accident involving a minbus and an excavator claimed the lives of at least six people in Addis Ababa on Monday, according to a","body":"ADDIS ABABA – A traffic accident involving a minbus and an excavator claimed the lives of at least six people in Addis Ababa on Monday, according to a police report. The deadly accident occurred in the morning of August 24 when a bucket of the excavator fell on a moving minibus taxi in Bole Subcity of Ethiopia’s capital. Six passengers were killed in the accident, the Addis Ababa Police said in a statement issued Monday. The driver and several other passengers aboard the minibus also sustained serious or minor injuries. They were taken to nearby health facilities for medical treatment, said the Police, who have launched an investigation to determine the exact cause of the accident. The incident is among a series of deadly traffic accidents in the East African nation, which are often linked to reckless driving, weak law enforcement, poor vehicle conditions and other factors. With vehicle numbers rising alongside the country’s growing economy and expanding middle class, experts call for stricter safety enforcement, thorough vehicle inspections, and a more rigorous licensing system to prevent future tragedies. — EM, news agencies","url":"https://ethiopianmonitor.com/2026/08/25/at-least-six-dead-in-addis-traffic-accident/","source":"Ethiopian Monitor","image":"","published":"2026-08-25T20:33:37+00:00","category":"conflict","category_label":"Armed Conflict","region":"Horn of Africa","country":"Ethiopia","fatalities":0,"severity":"Moderate","severity_score":0},{"id":"33398e2961f5","title":"American missionary abducted in Niger freed after nearly 10 months of captivity","summary":"Kevin Rideout, who was abducted by gunmen from his home in Niger’s capital of Niamey last October and held by the Islamic State, was freed this week in Libya. The exact nature of his release remains murky, but Rideout has thanked Libyan officials for their efforts. The post American missionary abducted in Niger freed after nearly 10 months of captivity appeared first on FDD's Long War Journal .","body":"","url":"https://www.longwarjournal.org/archives/2026/08/american-missionary-abducted-in-niger-freed-after-nearly-10-months-of-captivity.php","source":"FDD Long War Journal","image":"","published":"2026-08-17T16:11:47+00:00","category":"kidnapping","category_label":"Kidnapping & Abduction","region":"Sahel","country":"Niger","fatalities":0,"severity":"Moderate","severity_score":0},{"id":"fa982df97506","title":"From Guantanamo Bay to ISCAP: The case of Jamal Kiyemba","summary":"In 2002, Jamal Kiyemba, a Ugandan national living in the United Kingdom, was arrested in northwest Pakistan after allegedly attempting to join Al Qaeda in Afghanistan and was sent to the US prison facility at Guantanamo Bay, Cuba. Released in 2006 and sent to Uganda, Kiyemba has since been in and out of prison, most recently over his support of the Islamic State’s Central Africa Province (ISCAP). ","body":"","url":"https://www.longwarjournal.org/archives/2026/08/from-guantanamo-bay-to-iscap-the-case-of-jamal-kiyemba.php","source":"FDD Long War Journal","image":"","published":"2026-08-10T18:20:35+00:00","category":"terrorism","category_label":"Terrorism","region":"Horn of Africa","country":"Uganda","fatalities":0,"severity":"Moderate","severity_score":0},{"id":"c8c1f6c05055","title":"Generation Jihad | Losing ground: Somalia and Mali’s jihad problem","summary":"The US is losing leverage in Somalia as frustration with Mogadishu threatens critical African Union support and risks strengthening Al Shabaab’s hold. In Mali, renewed US airstrikes against JNIM raise a parallel concern—whether air power alone, without a ground strategy or political reform, can contain the group’s advance. Bill Roggio and Caleb Weiss examine these dynamics across the Sahel and the","body":"","url":"https://www.longwarjournal.org/archives/2026/08/generation-jihad-losing-ground-somalia-and-malis-jihad-problem.php","source":"FDD Long War Journal","image":"","published":"2026-08-10T17:08:11+00:00","category":"terrorism","category_label":"Terrorism","region":"Horn of Africa","country":"Somalia","fatalities":0,"severity":"Moderate","severity_score":0},{"id":"6f39c26f5030","title":"The overlooked conflict in the Amhara region","summary":"The last time Aster saw her husband was when he left for work after lunch. As he usually did, he said goodbye to his wife and their two-year-old child before leaving. It would be the last time Aster saw him alive. Later that day, she called her husband several times. He told her that he and his colleagues were safe but warned her not to leave the house because no one knew what might happen. Aster’","body":"The last time Aster saw her husband was when he left for work after lunch. As he usually did, he said goodbye to his wife and their two-year-old child before leaving. It would be the last time Aster saw him alive. Later that day, she called her husband several times. He told her that he and his colleagues were safe but warned her not to leave the house because no one knew what might happen. Aster’s fear stemmed from reports that two federal “army commanders” had been killed on Saturday, 21 October 2023. The area was tense. The two commanders were killed outside a small business run by Aster’s husband. Soldiers accused him of informing Fano fighters about the commanders’ movements, leading to their deaths. The 36-year-old, the owner of the business, and a security guard were taken to a temporary military camp for questioning. Only one of the three returned alive. “I waited until about six in the evening. Then I heard gunshots. I was terrified, but I never thought they would kill him. No one deserves to die like that. It’s not just because he was my husband. He was a kind and generous man who treated everyone the same, whether they were family or strangers. He always put others before himself,” Aster said, her voice breaking with emotion. A few hours after he was taken away, Aster’s husband was found dead on the road. Their child was only two years old at the time. Another man who was killed was a security guard whose body was found near the camp; he had previously served in the defence forces and was a father of three:his wife was eight months pregnant. As the conflict in the Amhara region enters its fourth year, civilians continue to bear the cost of the violence. People like Aster’s husband and the security guard, who were not directly involved in the fighting, have paid the ultimate price. ACLED, which monitors political violence and conflict around the world, said that since the start of the Fano insurgency in April 2023, millions of people in the Amhara region have been affected by violence. Between February and August 2026 alone, more than 8 million people were exposed to conflict. According to ACLED, the violence has affected all parts of the Amhara region, with the heaviest fighting reported in West Gojjam, North Wollo and North Shewa. Both government forces and Fano militias have been accused of committing abuses, although government forces have been blamed for the majority of the reported violations. Observers say the government has not only failed to fulfil its legal and moral responsibility to protect the rights and safety of its citizens, but has also become a perpetrator of abuses. The region has been under a state of emergency since August 2023, with restrictions on civil rights still in place. Reports indicate that many attacks targeting civilians take place after clashes between the two sides, while a significant number of residents have also been killed by heavy weapons that missed their targets and struck civilian areas. Beyond the fighting on the front lines, there have also been targeted killings of government officials. Government forces have also targeted families whose members have joined Fano, with reports of relatives being arrested or killed because of their alleged links to the armed group. The killing of local officials . On 6 June 2024, Milishu Bekele, the administrator of Kewet woreda in North Shewa, was killed on the road. She was six months pregnant at the time and was among the government officials killed by insurgents in the Amhara region. Since the outbreak of the conflict, many local officials have been forced to leave their homes and live inside administrative buildings under tight security, according to their family members. Milishu, who had worked in the health sector for 15 years, was killed while on her way to spend the night at the administrative office. Her husband said she was ambushed by two individuals he identified as Fano members. Officials in Oromia and Tigray have also been killed in attacks linked to their association with the government. ACLED’s 2026 report surveys 66 incidents of violence targeting local officials around the country. The Fano armed group more than doubled its attacks on local officials over the past year, targeting both individuals and public institutions and weakening local administrations. Seen by armed groups as representing opposing interests, local officials have borne a heavy toll from the conflict. The group has targeted woreda administrators, cabinet members and Prosperity Party officials. South Gondar Zone appears to have been particularly affected, with several woreda administrators and government officials killed. One source said local officials who speak out strongly against Fano during meetings are particularly likely to be targeted. The source said the group gathers information on local officials, selects those it wants to target, and sends them threatening messages warning that they will be killed. The source said more than 10 local officials he personally knew had been killed in the zone.The threats against government officials have forced many of them to work remotely or from safer locations.Officials can only travel under strict security arrangements and are often forced to spend the night in military camps for their safety. South Gondar police chief Commander Niggus Tamene said assassinations of local officials increased in 2025 and 2026. Human rights reports indicate that government security forces have subjected the families of Fano members and civilians accused of supporting the group to harassment, threats, arrest, property seizures, killings and other abuses. In August 2024, in Dejen town, six-month-pregnant Nitsuh Mekonen, whose husband had been accused of being a Fano member, was taken from a police station along with her mother-in-law. Both women were later killed, according to relatives. Family members said Nitsuh and five other relatives, including children, had been arrested in an attempt to pressure her husband to return. ACLED data show that fighting between the two sides accounted for about 80% of conflict events recorded in the Amhara region over the past three years, highlighting the scale and spread of the violence. More than eight million people have been exposed to conflict. The conflict in the region tends to intensify during military operations and then subside. ACLED senior analyst Jalale Getachew Birru said these operations can trigger a sharp increase in clashes, often lasting for two weeks or even a month, before the fighting declines. He said the clashes are not confined to battlefields but often take place in villages where people live, making the human toll particularly heavy. An excerpt from a longer report published by BBC Amharic. The post The overlooked conflict in the Amhara region appeared first on Ethiopia Observer .","url":"https://www.ethiopiaobserver.com/2026/08/07/the-overlooked-conflict-in-the-amhara-region/","source":"Ethiopia Observer","image":"","published":"2026-08-07T07:29:42+00:00","category":"conflict","category_label":"Armed Conflict","region":"Horn of Africa","country":"Ethiopia","fatalities":0,"severity":"Moderate","severity_score":0},{"id":"a25e98fdb1d7","title":"Fighting ends between federal forces and TPLF, but calm remains fragile","summary":"Fighting between federal forces and rebels from the Tigray People’s Liberation Front (TPLF), which lasted for several hours, ended on Saturday, local and security sources told AFP on Sunday. A TPLF member, speaking on condition of anonymity, said armed clashes in Shererina, near the Sudan border, involving heavy artillery and drone strikes, began at about 6:00 am on Saturday and ended around 5:00 ","body":"Fighting between federal forces and rebels from the Tigray People’s Liberation Front (TPLF), which lasted for several hours, ended on Saturday, local and security sources told AFP on Sunday. A TPLF member, speaking on condition of anonymity, said armed clashes in Shererina, near the Sudan border, involving heavy artillery and drone strikes, began at about 6:00 am on Saturday and ended around 5:00 pm. The source said the fighting was slowing down but did not provide any information about casualties. On Saturday, the TPLF accused the federal authorities of escalating their military actions and launching what it described as a “large-scale infantry offensive.” Abraham Belay, head of the Ministry of Irrigation and Lowlands, who is originally from Tigray, confirmed that clashes had erupted between federal forces and TPLF fighters. In a Tigrinya-language statement posted on his official social media page, he accused the TPLF of initiating hostilities in the Shererina area and warned that continued fighting could lead to further harm. The clashes came after tensions between the two sides had intensified in recent months, following that TPLF leaders had dismantled the regional interim administration established by the federal government and taken control. The post Fighting ends between federal forces and TPLF, but calm remains fragile appeared first on Ethiopia Observer .","url":"https://www.ethiopiaobserver.com/2026/08/02/fighting-ends-between-federal-forces-and-tplf-but-calm-remains-fragile/","source":"Ethiopia Observer","image":"","published":"2026-08-02T17:52:39+00:00","category":"conflict","category_label":"Armed Conflict","region":"Horn of Africa","country":"Ethiopia","fatalities":0,"severity":"Moderate","severity_score":0},{"id":"883ccbe98c9e","title":"Calls for greater international attention to Amhara conflict","summary":"A call was made for the European Union, the United Nations and the wider international community to give greater attention to the ongoing conflict in the Amhara region, the Reporter newspaper reported. According to the paper, the call was made during a panel discussion organised by the Institute for Peace and Security Studies (IPSS) on 31 July 2026. Amanuel Tesfaye, a doctoral researcher at the Un","body":"A call was made for the European Union, the United Nations and the wider international community to give greater attention to the ongoing conflict in the Amhara region, the Reporter newspaper reported. According to the paper, the call was made during a panel discussion organised by the Institute for Peace and Security Studies (IPSS) on 31 July 2026. Amanuel Tesfaye, a doctoral researcher at the University of Helsinki, said the conflict in the Amhara region had received limited international attention, as global crises—including the wars in Ukraine, the Middle East and neighbouring Sudan—had dominated the agendas of international organisations. The discussion focused on the conflict in the Amhara region, political uncertainty, limited economic prospects, and the possibilities for peaceful dialogue. Experts called on the federal government to engage in genuine peace talks with representatives of Fano, an armed group fighting government forces in the region, with the support of third-party mediation to help resolve the ongoing conflict. They also urged the European Union, the United Nations and other international organisations to use their influence to encourage dialogue and support efforts towards a peaceful resolution, the Amharic Reporter wrote on Sunday. Experts argued that the roots of the conflict should be viewed beyond a purely security perspective, highlighting broader political grievances, perceptions of economic discrimination, administrative bottlenecks, and limited employment opportunities as underlying factors contributing to instability in the region. Presenting his study, “Beyond Grievance: War Economy, State Entanglement, and the Dynamics of Protracted Conflict in Ethiopia’s Amhara Region,” Yared Debebe, a researcher at the African Research Universities Alliance, said that the ongoing fighting between government forces and Fano militants has continued to create serious economic, social, and humanitarian challenges, with no clear end to the conflict in sight. Yared stressed the need for long-term peace efforts that address the root causes of the conflict, improve people’s livelihoods, and go beyond short-term agreements. He referred to the government’s previous peace talks with the TPLF in South Africa and the Oromo Liberation Army (OLA) in Tanzania as examples. Although efforts supported by the African Union and IGAD have resulted in agreements with armed groups operating in the Amhara region, the conflict has continued, underscoring the need for a long-term political solution that addresses its root causes. Experts also stressed the need for greater attention to the conflict’s impact on education and development, noting that an estimated 4 million school-age children have been unable to attend school because of the crisis. Researchers concluded that ending the conflict is essential for Ethiopia’s recovery, saying that stopping the violence could help the country return to its path as a model of economic growth in Africa. (Image: Yosef Debasu, 19, receives rehabilitation treatment in Bahir Dar on 8 December 2025 after being injured by a landmine explosion while driving his tuk-tuk in Merawi in 2024. AFP) The post Calls for greater international attention to Amhara conflict appeared first on Ethiopia Observer .","url":"https://www.ethiopiaobserver.com/2026/08/02/calls-for-greater-international-attention-to-amhara-conflict/","source":"Ethiopia Observer","image":"","published":"2026-08-02T15:59:17+00:00","category":"conflict","category_label":"Armed Conflict","region":"Horn of Africa","country":"Ethiopia","fatalities":0,"severity":"Moderate","severity_score":0},{"id":"e0109822b480","title":"El JNIM ya no habla como una insurgencia: la estrategia de Al Qaeda para gobernar el Sahel","summary":"Introducción Durante años, el éxito de las organizaciones yihadistas se ha medido por el número de atentados, las bajas infligidas […]","body":"Introducción Durante años, el éxito de las organizaciones yihadistas se ha medido por el número de atentados, las bajas infligidas a las fuerzas de seguridad o la extensión de los territorios bajo su control. Sin embargo, esos indicadores solo muestran una parte del problema. En conflictos prolongados como el del Sahel, la verdadera batalla no consiste únicamente en conquistar territorio, sino en convencer a la población de quién debe ejercer la autoridad cuando el Estado deja de ser capaz de hacerlo. La entrevista publicada el 1 de agosto de 2026 por la fundación mediática az-Zallaqa , brazo propagandístico del Jama’at Nusrat al-Islam wal-Muslimin (JNIM) , ofrece una oportunidad excepcional para comprender esa evolución. A lo largo de nueve páginas, el jeque Abou Mahmoud , emir del JNIM en Burkina Faso, expone la visión de la organización sobre el conflicto, su relación con la población, el papel de la religión, la administración del territorio y sus objetivos estratégicos. A primera vista, podría parecer una entrevista propagandística más. Sin embargo, una lectura detallada revela algo diferente. El documento apenas dedica espacio a reivindicar operaciones militares o a exaltar la violencia. En su lugar, concentra buena parte de su discurso en cuestiones como la gobernanza, la legitimidad, la justicia, la gestión del territorio y la construcción de una autoridad alternativa al Estado. El combate aparece como un instrumento para alcanzar esos objetivos, no como un fin en sí mismo. Este cambio de enfoque resulta especialmente significativo. El JNIM no intenta dirigirse a una audiencia internacional ni movilizar a simpatizantes en Occidente. Su mensaje está pensado, ante todo, para quienes viven en las zonas donde la organización compite diariamente con el Estado por el control efectivo del territorio y de la población. La entrevista constituye, por tanto, mucho más que un ejercicio de propaganda: es una declaración sobre cómo el grupo entiende el poder y sobre la forma en que aspira a consolidarlo. Este análisis examina los principales mensajes del documento, identifica las audiencias a las que se dirige y evalúa qué revela esta entrevista sobre la evolución de la estrategia política y comunicativa del JNIM en Burkina Faso. Más que describir lo que dice Abou Mahmoud, el objetivo es comprender por qué lo dice y qué pretende conseguir con ello . ¿Quién es Abou Mahmoud y por qué importa esta entrevista? Antes de analizar el contenido del documento conviene responder a una pregunta básica: ¿quién es Abou Mahmoud y por qué merece atención una entrevista de este tipo? Abou Mahmoud es el emir del Jama’at Nusrat al-Islam wal-Muslimin (JNIM) en Burkina Faso, la rama nacional de una organización que, desde su creación en 2017 bajo el liderazgo de Iyad Ag Ghaly, se ha consolidado como la principal franquicia de Al Qaeda en el Sahel. El JNIM reúne a varias organizaciones yihadistas que operan de forma coordinada en Mali, Burkina Faso y Níger, combinando acciones militares, redes de apoyo local y una intensa actividad propagandística. En los últimos años, Burkina Faso se ha convertido en uno de los principales escenarios de expansión del JNIM. La organización ha incrementado su presencia en amplias zonas rurales, donde el debilitamiento de las instituciones estatales, la inseguridad y las tensiones entre comunidades han favorecido el desarrollo de estructuras paralelas de autoridad. En muchas de esas áreas, el conflicto ya no se limita a una disputa militar, sino que enfrenta dos modelos de poder que compiten por la lealtad de la población. En este contexto, la entrevista publicada por az-Zallaqa , el aparato mediático del JNIM, adquiere una relevancia especial. No se trata de un comunicado reivindicando un atentado ni de un vídeo propagandístico destinado a exaltar la violencia. Es una conversación extensa y cuidadosamente estructurada en la que el emir expone cómo interpreta el conflicto, qué papel atribuye a la organización y cuál es su visión sobre el futuro de Burkina Faso. Precisamente por eso, este documento ofrece una oportunidad poco habitual para analizar no solo la narrativa del JNIM, sino también sus prioridades estratégicas. A lo largo de la entrevista se aprecia que el grupo dedica más esfuerzo a justificar su capacidad para gobernar que a describir sus operaciones militares, un detalle que ayuda a comprender la evolución de Al Qaeda en el Sahel. Una entrevista dirigida a mucho más que sus combatientes Uno de los errores más habituales al analizar la propaganda yihadista consiste en asumir que todos los mensajes están dirigidos exclusivamente a sus propios seguidores. Sin embargo, una lectura detallada de esta entrevista demuestra que el JNIM ha diseñado un discurso mucho más complejo, adaptado a diferentes audiencias y con objetivos específicos para cada una de ellas. A lo largo de las nueve páginas del documento, Abou Mahmoud apenas dedica tiempo a glorificar el combate o a reivindicar operaciones militares concretas. En su lugar, construye un relato que busca influir simultáneamente sobre la población civil, los simpatizantes potenciales, los combatientes de la organización, las fuerzas de seguridad e incluso las organizaciones humanitarias que operan en Burkina Faso. Esta diversidad de destinatarios explica la estructura del documento. Cada bloque responde a una finalidad distinta dentro de una estrategia de influencia más amplia. En primer lugar, la entrevista está dirigida a la población de las zonas rurales donde el Estado y el JNIM compiten por el control efectivo del territorio. El mensaje insiste de forma recurrente en conceptos como la justicia, la seguridad, la resolución de conflictos o la administración local. Más que convencer a la población de un proyecto ideológico, intenta transmitir la idea de que la organización puede ofrecer un orden más eficaz que las instituciones estatales. Al mismo tiempo, el documento busca consolidar la cohesión interna del movimiento. Varias secciones están claramente orientadas a reforzar la moral de los combatientes, recordar la importancia de la disciplina y presentar las dificultades del conflicto como una prueba religiosa que debe afrontarse con paciencia. La entrevista actúa, por tanto, como una herramienta de liderazgo interno además de un instrumento de propaganda. También existe un mensaje dirigido a quienes combaten junto al Estado. En lugar de limitarse a calificarlos como enemigos, Abou Mahmoud les ofrece la posibilidad de abandonar las armas y desvincularse del Gobierno antes de advertirles de las consecuencias de seguir colaborando con él. Este tipo de mensajes forman parte de las operaciones psicológicas habituales en las insurgencias, cuyo objetivo no es únicamente derrotar militarmente al adversario, sino erosionar su voluntad de resistir. Finalmente, resulta especialmente significativo que el JNIM dedique parte de la entrevista a las organizaciones humanitarias y otros actores internacionales. Al presentar al grupo como un interlocutor capaz de garantizar la seguridad de quienes actúen con neutralidad, intenta proyectar la imagen de una autoridad con capacidad para gestionar el territorio, alejándose deliberadamente de la imagen de una organización exclusivamente armada. En conjunto, la entrevista refleja una evolución importante en la comunicación del JNIM. Su objetivo no parece ser movilizar una audiencia global ni lanzar amenazas contra Occidente. El esfuerzo principal se concentra en influir sobre quienes viven, trabajan o combaten en las zonas donde la organización aspira a consolidar su autoridad. Esa diferencia es fundamental para comprender el resto del documento. La legitimidad religiosa como fundamento del proyecto político Uno de los aspectos más significativos de la entrevista es que Abou Mahmoud no comienza hablando de operaciones militares, victorias en el campo de batalla o capacidad de combate. Su primer objetivo consiste en establecer la legitimidad del JNIM desde una perspectiva religiosa. No se trata de un recurso retórico, sino del pilar sobre el que construye todo el discurso posterior. A lo largo de la entrevista, el emir presenta la actuación del JNIM como una obligación derivada de la fe, vinculando la actividad de la organización a la defensa del islam y al cumplimiento de un mandato religioso. Desde esta perspectiva, el conflicto deja de ser una simple confrontación armada contra el Estado burkinés y pasa a interpretarse como una cuestión de obediencia a principios considerados superiores. Este planteamiento cumple varias funciones simultáneamente. En primer lugar, dota de coherencia ideológica a la organización y refuerza la cohesión entre sus miembros. En segundo lugar, dificulta que el conflicto pueda percibirse únicamente como una disputa política o territorial. Finalmente, sitúa al JNIM como una autoridad moral con capacidad para emitir juicios sobre la legitimidad del Estado, de las fuerzas de seguridad e incluso de quienes colaboran ","url":"https://www.sahelintel.com/el-jnim-ya-no-habla-como-una-insurgencia-la-estrategia-de-al-qaeda-para-gobernar-el-sahel/","source":"Sahel Intel","image":"","published":"2026-08-01T16:31:39+00:00","category":"terrorism","category_label":"Terrorism","region":"Sahel","country":"Burkina Faso","fatalities":0,"severity":"Moderate","severity_score":0},{"id":"cec364fcab44","title":"Somalia y la guerra interminable contra Al Shabaab: entre las victorias tácticas y el fracaso estratégico","summary":"La ofensiva impulsada por Hassan Sheikh Mohamud ha permitido recuperar la iniciativa militar frente a Al Shabaab. Sin embargo, la […]","body":"La ofensiva impulsada por Hassan Sheikh Mohamud ha permitido recuperar la iniciativa militar frente a Al Shabaab. Sin embargo, la evolución de la rama somalí de Al Qaeda, la fragilidad del Estado y la creciente regionalización del conflicto plantean una cuestión mucho más profunda: ¿ha sabido Al Qaeda adaptar su modelo de guerra híbrida con mayor rapidez que los Estados encargados de combatirla? Introducción Pocas guerras ilustran mejor la complejidad de derrotar a una insurgencia que el conflicto que, desde hace casi dos décadas, enfrenta al Estado somalí con Al Shabaab, la rama somalí de Al Qaeda. A pesar de las sucesivas ofensivas militares, del despliegue de fuerzas internacionales y de miles de millones de euros invertidos por la comunidad internacional, la organización continúa siendo el principal desafío para la estabilidad de Somalia y una de las mayores amenazas para la seguridad del Cuerno de África. Cuando Hassan Sheikh Mohamud regresó a la presidencia en mayo de 2022, lo hizo con la promesa de cambiar el rumbo de una guerra que parecía haberse estancado. A diferencia de sus predecesores, el presidente apostó por una estrategia integral que combinaba la presión militar sobre Al Shabaab con el aislamiento de sus fuentes de financiación, la movilización de los clanes locales y la consolidación de la presencia del Estado en los territorios recuperados. El objetivo no era únicamente expulsar a la insurgencia de determinadas regiones, sino impedir que pudiera regresar una vez concluidas las operaciones militares. Los primeros resultados fueron prometedores. El Ejército Nacional Somalí, apoyado por milicias comunitarias, fuerzas internacionales y ataques aéreos estadounidenses, recuperó decenas de localidades que durante años habían permanecido bajo el control de la organización. Por primera vez en mucho tiempo, comenzó a extenderse la percepción de que Al Shabaab podía estar perdiendo la iniciativa estratégica. Sin embargo, el desarrollo posterior del conflicto demostró que la realidad era mucho más compleja. Aunque la organización sufrió importantes reveses militares, logró adaptarse con rapidez, reorganizar sus estructuras y modificar su forma de operar. En lugar de desaparecer, evolucionó hacia un modelo de insurgencia más flexible, menos dependiente del control permanente del territorio y mucho más centrado en disputar al Estado su legitimidad política, institucional, económica y social. Ese cambio constituye, probablemente, el aspecto más relevante de la guerra en Somalia y uno de los fenómenos más importantes del yihadismo africano contemporáneo. Al Shabaab ya no busca únicamente controlar ciudades o ejecutar atentados de gran impacto. Como parte de la estrategia impulsada por Al Qaeda en el continente africano, la organización pretende ocupar progresivamente los vacíos de poder que el Estado es incapaz de llenar, sustituyendo sus funciones mediante sistemas propios de justicia, recaudación fiscal, seguridad y administración. Se trata del mismo modelo que, con sus particularidades, comienza a observarse en otras regiones del continente, especialmente en el Sahel, donde organizaciones afines siguen una lógica muy similar. Analizar la gestión de Hassan Sheikh Mohamud exige, por tanto, ir más allá del balance de victorias y derrotas militares. Obliga también a examinar hasta qué punto el Estado somalí ha conseguido consolidar los territorios recuperados, evaluar la eficacia de la ayuda internacional y comprender cómo la evolución de Al Shabaab se inserta en una estrategia regional mucho más amplia que conecta el Cuerno de África con el Sahel y, cada vez más, con la inestabilidad del mar Rojo. Porque la verdadera cuestión ya no es únicamente si Somalia puede derrotar militarmente a Al Shabaab, sino si el Estado somalí y sus aliados serán capaces de evolucionar tan rápido como lo ha hecho Al Qaeda. Mientras la organización ha aprendido las lecciones de los últimos veinte años y ha transformado profundamente su forma de hacer la guerra, buena parte de la respuesta internacional continúa anclada en estrategias concebidas para combatir al yihadismo de principios de siglo. Una estrategia basada en cuatro pilares Cuando Hassan Sheikh Mohamud regresó a la presidencia de Somalia en mayo de 2022, heredó un conflicto que había entrado en una preocupante fase de estancamiento. Durante años, las operaciones militares habían permitido recuperar localidades y debilitar temporalmente a la rama somalí de Al Qaeda, pero la organización terminaba reorganizándose y recuperando parte del terreno perdido. Aquella dinámica evidenciaba que la presión militar, por sí sola, resultaba insuficiente para derrotar a una insurgencia que había aprendido a adaptarse a cada ofensiva. Consciente de esa realidad, el presidente impulsó una estrategia más amplia que pretendía atacar simultáneamente las capacidades militares, económicas, políticas e ideológicas de la organización. El objetivo ya no consistía únicamente en expulsar a Al Shabaab de determinadas zonas, sino en impedir que pudiera reconstruir su influencia una vez concluidas las operaciones. El primer pilar fue el militar. El Gobierno incrementó la coordinación entre el Ejército Nacional Somalí, las fuerzas especiales entrenadas por socios internacionales y las milicias comunitarias Macawisley, formadas principalmente por miembros de los clanes locales. La participación de estas milicias representó una diferencia sustancial respecto a campañas anteriores, ya que aportaban un profundo conocimiento del terreno, mejores redes de información y una mayor capacidad para identificar a los combatientes de Al Shabaab entre la población. Paralelamente, el apoyo de Estados Unidos mediante ataques aéreos selectivos y el respaldo de la misión de la Unión Africana reforzaron las capacidades operativas del Ejército somalí. El segundo eje de la estrategia se centró en asfixiar las finanzas de la organización. Lejos de depender exclusivamente de actividades ilícitas, Al Shabaab había construido durante años un sofisticado sistema de recaudación basado en impuestos, extorsiones y gravámenes sobre el comercio y el transporte. El Ejecutivo intensificó la vigilancia sobre las transacciones financieras, aumentó la presión sobre empresas y operadores económicos que colaboraban con la organización y trató de reducir las fuentes de financiación que sostenían tanto su aparato militar como su estructura administrativa. El tercer pilar buscaba combatir la influencia ideológica de Al Shabaab. El Gobierno promovió campañas de comunicación destinadas a desacreditar el discurso de la organización y contó con el apoyo de destacados líderes religiosos somalíes para cuestionar la interpretación extremista del islam utilizada por el movimiento yihadista para justificar su violencia. El objetivo era disputar a Al Shabaab la legitimidad religiosa sobre la que había construido buena parte de su narrativa y reducir su capacidad de atraer nuevos apoyos entre la población. Por último, el Ejecutivo trató de consolidar la presencia del Estado en las zonas recuperadas. La experiencia de campañas anteriores había demostrado que expulsar a la insurgencia constituía únicamente el primer paso. Si las instituciones no lograban restablecer rápidamente la seguridad, la administración, la justicia y los servicios básicos, el vacío de poder terminaba siendo aprovechado por la propia organización para regresar. La consolidación institucional se convirtió así en el cuarto pilar de la estrategia y, al mismo tiempo, en su mayor desafío. Sobre el papel, el plan diseñado por Hassan Sheikh Mohamud representaba la estrategia más ambiciosa impulsada por un Gobierno somalí desde el inicio de la insurgencia. Sin embargo, su éxito dependía de un factor esencial: que las victorias militares pudieran transformarse en una presencia permanente y efectiva del Estado sobre el territorio recuperado. La experiencia demostraría que ese era precisamente el terreno donde comenzaba la verdadera batalla. Los éxitos iniciales La estrategia impulsada por Hassan Sheikh Mohamud comenzó ofreciendo resultados que pocos observadores esperaban. Apenas unos meses después de su llegada a la presidencia, el Gobierno somalí logró recuperar la iniciativa frente a la rama somalí de Al Qaeda mediante una ofensiva coordinada que combinó operaciones militares, movilización de los clanes y apoyo internacional. Por primera vez en años, la insurgencia se vio obligada a abandonar posiciones que había mantenido durante largo tiempo, alimentando la percepción de que el equilibrio del conflicto podía estar cambiando. Uno de los factores más decisivos fue la implicación de las milicias comunitarias Macawisley. Integradas principalmente por miembros de clanes locales, estas fuerzas surgieron como respuesta al creciente rechazo hacia los abusos cometidos por Al Shabaab, especialmente la extorsión sistemática, el reclutamiento forzoso y la imposición de tributos sobre comunidades ya castigadas por la","url":"https://www.sahelintel.com/somalia-y-la-guerra-interminable-contra-al-shabaab-entre-las-victorias-tacticas-y-el-fracaso-estrategico/","source":"Sahel Intel","image":"","published":"2026-07-09T18:54:38+00:00","category":"terrorism","category_label":"Terrorism","region":"Horn of Africa","country":"Somalia","fatalities":0,"severity":"Moderate","severity_score":0},{"id":"09d8078e15f1","title":"Mauritania y Chad frente a la crisis del Sahel: solidez estratégica, soberanía religiosa y guerra informativa","summary":"Mientras el Sahel central —compuesto por Malí, Burkina Faso y Níger— atraviesa una crisis de seguridad sin precedentes, marcada por […]","body":"Mientras el Sahel central —compuesto por Malí, Burkina Faso y Níger— atraviesa una crisis de seguridad sin precedentes, marcada por la insurgencia yihadista persistente y una inestabilidad institucional crónica, Mauritania y Chad aparecen como casos comparativos de mayor solidez estatal. A pesar de compartir realidades geográficas, desafíos demográficos y amenazas similares a las de sus vecinos, estos dos Estados han logrado sostener una posición comparativamente más estable. La clave de esta divergencia no radica únicamente en su capacidad militar, sino en su destreza para blindar el espacio ideológico frente a la infiltración extremista mediante una supervisión estatal persistente, una soberanía ejercida sobre el discurso religioso y, sobre todo, una lectura proactiva de la guerra informativa. La soberanía sobre el discurso religioso: un muro ideológico histórico Para comprender esta diferencia, conviene partir de una premisa esencial: el islam no constituye un bloque homogéneo, sino un espacio atravesado por múltiples tradiciones, escuelas e interpretaciones. En el Sahel, las prácticas religiosas locales han estado históricamente vinculadas a formas de islam tradicional adaptadas a las costumbres comunitarias. Frente a ellas, desde los años setenta del siglo XX, algunas corrientes rigoristas impulsadas o financiadas desde ciertos países del Golfo comenzaron a proyectarse como instrumentos de influencia religiosa exterior. Aunque en origen funcionaron como una forma de poder blando, con el tiempo favorecieron lecturas más restrictivas, puristas y politizadas de la religión. Con ello, contribuyeron a erosionar la autoridad de los referentes religiosos tradicionales. En el Sahel central —como en otras regiones de África—, la debilidad institucional ha permitido durante décadas —y aún permite en algunos contextos— que actores extranjeros financien mezquitas y madrazas donde se difunden interpretaciones religiosas más rigoristas. Ese proceso no produjo automáticamente radicalización, pero sí generó un terreno propicio para que ciertos predicadores cuestionaran a las autoridades religiosas tradicionales y se presentaran como fuentes alternativas de legitimidad. En ese contexto, la propaganda extremista encontró canales sociales desde los cuales justificar la violencia y facilitar el reclutamiento para la yihad. Mauritania y Chad, por el contrario, comprendieron que la religión no era solo una cuestión doctrinal, sino también un asunto de seguridad nacional y un campo decisivo de disputa narrativa. En Mauritania, esta orientación se ha mantenido a través de varios presidentes; en Chad, ha mostrado continuidad bajo Idriss Déby y su hijo Mahamat Idriss Déby. Mauritania (2005-2010): Tras sufrir ataques directos, como el de Lemgheity en 2005 , las autoridades comprendieron que la amenaza no residía únicamente en la capacidad operativa de los insurgentes, sino también en la narrativa religiosa y política que legitimaba la violencia. A partir de 2010 , impulsaron un programa de diálogo en prisiones en el que ulemas y expertos religiosos debatían con yihadistas detenidos para desmontar sus argumentos desde marcos doctrinales reconocibles para ellos. Esta estrategia funcionó como una forma de contra-propaganda: no solo buscaba reducir la reincidencia, sino reafirmar la autoridad del rito maliki y del islam tradicional mauritano frente a lecturas extremistas. Chad (2014-2015): Ante la expansión de Boko Haram y de grupos vinculados al Estado Islámico en África Occidental durante 2014-2015 , Chad reforzó una política de seguridad religiosa orientada a supervisar quién enseñaba, qué se predicaba y qué redes influían en esos discursos. Las autoridades chadianas entendieron que dejar la narrativa religiosa en manos de actores externos equivalía a ceder parte del control sobre la percepción pública y a permitir que el radicalismo construyera una base social capaz de derivar en formas de organización paramilitar. El pragmatismo de la desradicalización: neutralizar, no necesariamente convertir Un error común, visible también en muchas experiencias europeas de desradicalización —con resultados generalmente limitados salvo casos puntuales—, consiste en pensar que la desradicalización busca transformar por completo las creencias del individuo. Mauritania y Chad han desarrollado una aproximación más pragmática: distinguen entre el radicalismo como ideología y el terrorismo como conducta violenta. Ambos países han empleado técnicas de contra-propaganda y negociación teológica no con el fin de borrar las creencias del individuo radicalizado, sino con el objetivo táctico de neutralizar su capacidad de recurrir a la violencia. Mauritania, especialmente, ha desarrollado esta lógica desde 2010 : mediante diálogos en prisiones, el Estado buscó que individuos con ideologías extremas aceptaran límites prácticos a la acción armada. El individuo podía mantener convicciones radicales en su fuero interno, pero al ver cuestionado públicamente su discurso de odio mediante contra-argumentos religiosos, perdía parte de su capacidad de reclutar y de justificar la violencia. Lograr que un radical siga siendo radical, pero deje de actuar violentamente, constituye, en términos de seguridad nacional, un éxito relevante de la inteligencia estatal. La clave diferencial: la lectura de la propaganda Mientras Mauritania y Chad identificaron tempranamente que una parte central del conflicto se libraba en el terreno de las ideas y la propaganda, los países del Sahel central tendieron a subestimar el poder de la narrativa. Estos dos Estados supieron leer el escenario: comprendieron que los grupos yihadistas no buscan únicamente conquistar territorio, sino también influir en las percepciones, lealtades y expectativas de la población local. Mientras en Malí, Níger o Burkina Faso ciertos predicadores radicales pudieron actuar como altavoces de ideologías externas con escasos contrapesos institucionales, Mauritania y Chad intervinieron en el espacio comunicativo y religioso. No se limitaron a reaccionar ante los ataques; actuaron sobre las condiciones que permitían la difusión del discurso radical. Otros países, en cambio, dejaron que la propaganda extremista circulara con menos restricciones, permitiendo que esta se convirtiera en un lenguaje recurrente de la disidencia. Cuando esos Estados quisieron reaccionar, la narrativa radical ya estaba profundamente enraizada en algunas comunidades locales, haciendo que cualquier esfuerzo militar resultara mucho menos eficaz frente a una población previamente influida en el plano ideológico. El funcionamiento de los organismos de gestión Para un lector no especializado, es importante entender cómo operan estos organismos: no funcionan únicamente como mecanismos de censura, sino como estructuras estatales destinadas a preservar un marco religioso y nacional compartido frente a discursos extremistas. En Mauritania: Ministerio de Asuntos Islámicos: Supervisa el currículo religioso, la formación de imanes y los mensajes difundidos en las mezquitas para preservar un marco doctrinal compatible con la convivencia, la autoridad religiosa tradicional y la cohesión nacional. Consejo Superior de la Fatwa: Refuerza la autoridad religiosa institucional al ordenar el espacio de emisión de opiniones jurídicas, de modo que las fatwas con legitimidad pública procedan de marcos reconocidos y no de liderazgos radicales locales. Con ello, reduce el margen para que los llamados a la violencia sean presentados como mandatos religiosos válidos. En Chad: Consejo Superior de Asuntos Islámicos (CSAI): Actúa como órgano de interlocución entre las comunidades musulmanas y el Estado, articulando la supervisión religiosa oficial y ofreciendo un marco institucional para canalizar la autoridad islámica reconocida. De este modo, reduce el margen para que predicadores o financiadores externos impongan agendas doctrinales ajenas al marco nacional. Sistema de registro: Al exigir autorización estatal para ejercer como imán, el Estado dispone de una herramienta para identificar discursos radicales, supervisar redes de influencia religiosa y actuar antes de que ciertos predicadores transformen el mensaje religioso en una plataforma de movilización extremista. Conclusión: Los pilares de la solidez frente al caos Mauritania y Chad han demostrado que la estabilidad requiere algo más qué capacidad militar: exige autoridad moral, continuidad institucional y una lectura precisa de la guerra informativa. Mientras sus vecinos vieron cómo los vacíos de poder abrían espacio a discursos extremistas, estos dos Estados mantuvieron una soberanía sostenida en el terreno de las ideas: Soberanía religiosa: Al regular el discurso religioso autorizado, reducen el margen para que las narrativas radicales se presenten como “verdad” social ante la población. Gestión de la conducta radical: Han priorizado la neutralización de la violencia sobre la reforma ideológica, buscando que individuos con conviccione","url":"https://www.sahelintel.com/mauritania-y-chad-frente-a-la-crisis-del-sahel-solidez-estrategica-soberania-religiosa-y-guerra-informativa/","source":"Sahel Intel","image":"","published":"2026-07-03T15:23:43+00:00","category":"terrorism","category_label":"Terrorism","region":"Sahel","country":"Burkina Faso","fatalities":0,"severity":"Moderate","severity_score":0}],"history":[{"date":"2026-09-08","total":125,"terrorism":22,"conflict":63,"kidnapping":18,"fatalities":2896}]};
